BEGIN;

-- From alchemilla-mollis.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant alchemilla in autumn or spring, in sun or part shade in any decent garden soil — it''s one of the most reliable UK perennial ground cover plants, with soft pleated palmate leaves (40 cm wide) and frothy clouds of tiny lime-green flowers from June to August. Alchemilla is very hardy (RHS H7) and famously easy — tolerates almost any UK garden condition except deep dry shade. The leaves hold raindrops as silver beads in a magical visual effect that''s been admired in UK gardens for centuries. Cut the whole plant down to 10 cm after flowering to trigger fresh foliage and prevent prolific self-seeding (the plant can become a colony if let go). Self-seeds anywhere that''s bare soil — manage by deadheading and pulling unwanted seedlings.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy a young plant in autumn or spring", "text": "Container-grown plants are widely sold by all garden centres in 9 cm or 1 L pots. One plant is enough for a typical garden — it'll spread by self-seeding into a colony within 3–4 years. Spacing: 50 cm apart for ground-cover plantings; 1 m apart if you want individual specimen mounds."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in sun or part shade", "text": "Alchemilla tolerates anything from full sun to fairly deep shade. Best display: part shade with morning sun — flowers are most prolific, foliage stays fresh. Full sun: works well in moist soil; in dry sunny positions the foliage can scorch at the edges in summer. Deep dry shade: alchemilla survives but stays smaller and flowers less."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in any soil", "text": "Alchemilla's chief virtue: tolerance of poor and difficult soils. Heavy clay, sandy loam, chalk all work. Slightly moist is ideal but the plant survives drought well once established. The only soil it dislikes: waterlogged ground. No special preparation needed — alchemilla thrives in plain garden soil with a token mulch."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Watch the leaves catch raindrops", "text": "Alchemilla's signature visual effect. The pleated soft leaves are slightly water-repellent, and after rain or heavy dew each leaf holds a single large mercury-like bead of water at its centre. Medieval alchemists collected this alchemilla water believing it had magical properties (the genus name Alchemilla comes from alchemy). A border of alchemilla after rain is one of the great quiet pleasures of UK gardening."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Cut the whole plant down after flowering", "text": "The single most important husbandry rule. When the lime-green flower clouds start fading in late July or early August, cut the whole plant down to 10 cm above the ground. Three reasons: (1) prevents prolific self-seeding (a single un-cut alchemilla seeds hundreds of volunteers); (2) triggers a fresh flush of clean foliage for late summer and autumn; (3) tidies the plant after the slightly tatty post-flower stage."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Manage the self-seeding", "text": "Alchemilla is famously promiscuous. Cut flower-heads off BEFORE they shatter to prevent self-seeding entirely. Or let one or two plants seed and accept volunteers. Self-sown seedlings appear in any bare soil — paths, gravel, between paving stones, in the lawn. Most UK gardeners balance the colony — let a few seed, pull unwanted volunteers. The seedlings are easy to pull while small (March–April); harder once established (mature alchemilla has a deep root system)."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Use as cut flower or ground cover", "text": "Cut flower use: the lime-green sprays last well in water and brighten any mixed bouquet — particularly good with pinks and blues. Pick whole stems just as flowers fully open. Ground cover use: plant in mass alongside paths, under roses, in mixed borders. The pleated foliage suppresses weeds and softens hard edges. Classic UK pairings: alchemilla + nepeta + roses (a cottage-garden trinity)."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Divide every 3–4 years (or not at all)", "text": "Alchemilla is one of the few perennials that doesn't strictly need dividing — it stays vigorous for many years from a single plant. Division is useful if you want to spread the plant or refresh a tired specimen: lift in early autumn or spring, split with a spade into 4–6 sections, replant immediately. Self-sown seedlings are often easier than division for propagation."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I cut alchemilla back?", "answer": "After flowering in late July or early August — cut the whole plant down to 10 cm above the ground. Three reasons: (1) prevents prolific self-seeding (a major issue if not done); (2) triggers a fresh flush of clean foliage for late summer; (3) removes the slightly tatty post-flower foliage. The plant regrows in 2–3 weeks with fresh new leaves; a second light flush of flowers sometimes follows."},
    {"question": "Why is alchemilla seeding all over my garden?", "answer": "It's prolific self-seeder if not deadheaded. Each plant produces thousands of seeds that drop in any bare soil. The solution: cut the plant down to 10 cm right after flowering (late July) before seeds set. For unwanted seedlings: pull them while small (March–April); they're easy to remove young. Mature alchemilla has a deep root system that's harder to dig out. Many UK gardeners accept some self-seeding for the easy ground cover, just pulling volunteers from where they're unwanted."},
    {"question": "Why do alchemilla leaves hold raindrops as beads?", "answer": "Natural hydrophobic surface. The pleated alchemilla leaf has a slightly water-repellent surface and a concave shape that holds rainwater or heavy dew as a single large mercury-like droplet at the leaf centre. Medieval alchemists collected this alchemilla water (the source of the plant's genus name — derived from alchemy) believing it had magical properties. In the modern garden: simply one of the great quiet pleasures — a border of alchemilla after rain holds dozens of these silver beads."},
    {"question": "Will alchemilla grow in shade?", "answer": "Yes — it's one of the most shade-tolerant flowering perennials. Tolerates part shade and even fairly deep shade, though flower production reduces in deep shade. Best display: part shade with morning sun. Dry shade under trees: alchemilla survives where most plants fail, though stays smaller and flowers less. For shaded ground cover: alchemilla mixes well with hardy geranium (G. macrorrhizum), pulmonaria, and hostas."},
    {"question": "Can alchemilla be invasive?", "answer": "Yes by self-seeding, not by spread. Alchemilla doesn't run by rhizomes (no underground spread), but it produces enormous amounts of seed that germinate anywhere there's bare soil. An un-managed alchemilla can produce hundreds of seedlings annually, gradually colonising paths, gravel, lawn edges, and any bare patch. Manage with the post-flower cut-back (mid-July) — this prevents self-seeding entirely. The plant itself stays a tidy clump; the spread is only via seed."},
    {"question": "What does alchemilla pair well with?", "answer": "Cottage garden classics. (1) With roses: alchemilla's lime-green softens the strong rose colours and fills gaps at the base. (2) With nepeta (catmint): the lime-green-against-violet contrast is one of the great UK garden combinations. (3) With hardy geranium: any colour works — pink, magenta, blue, white. (4) With dark foliage: heuchera Palace Purple or Obsidian for dramatic contrast. (5) In bouquets: alchemilla makes the perfect bridging foliage between any flower colours."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'alchemilla-mollis';

-- From apple.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant apple trees from November to March (bare-root) or year-round (container), in any decent garden soil with reasonable drainage and sun for at least half the day. Apples are very hardy (RHS H6) and crop reliably across the UK. The two decisions that matter most are rootstock (which controls eventual size — M27 for patio pots, M9 for cordons, MM106 for half-standards, M25 for traditional standards) and pollination group (most apples need a partner from the same flowering group). UK heritage varieties — Egremont Russet, Discovery, James Grieve, Bramley — outperform supermarket favourites in flavour and disease resistance. Prune in winter for shape; thin fruit in June for size; net or pick promptly to beat codling moth.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose rootstock and variety", "text": "Rootstock controls eventual size: M27 (patio pot, 1.5 m), M9 (cordon or small bush, 2 m), MM106 (half-standard, 3–4 m), M25 (traditional standard, 5+ m). For UK conditions choose heritage varieties (Egremont Russet, Discovery, James Grieve) or modern disease-resistant cultivars (Red Falstaff, Saturn) over supermarket favourites that need spraying."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Check pollination group", "text": "Most apples need a pollinator from the same flowering group (1–7, where 3 and 4 are most common). Bramley is a triploid (needs two partners). A single apple tree on a small garden plot pollinates from neighbours' trees within 30 m; in a vacuum, pollination fails."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant", "text": "November to March (bare-root) is best; pot-grown can go in year-round. Dig a hole twice the rootball width. Set the graft union 5 cm above soil level (not below — burying the union defeats the rootstock's size control). Stake immediately to prevent rocking. Water in well."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Mulch and protect", "text": "Apply a 5 cm mulch of compost or wood chips, kept 5 cm clear of the stem. Protect the trunk with a spiral guard against rabbits and rodents."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Winter prune for shape", "text": "Each winter (December to February), prune to maintain an open goblet shape — remove crossing branches, water-shoots, and any branch growing into the centre. Aim for an open framework that lets light into the middle of the tree."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Thin fruit in June", "text": "After the natural June drop (when the tree sheds excess developing fruit in late June), thin further by hand. Leave 10–15 cm between fruits; remove the central king fruit on each cluster. Heavy crops left unthinned give masses of small poor-flavour apples and risk biennial bearing."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for codling moth", "text": "Pheromone traps in May–June help reduce codling moth (the maggot in supermarket apples). Encouraging tits and other small birds is the best biological control. Pick fruit promptly when ripe — windfalls left on the ground harbour next year's pest."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick when ready", "text": "Test ripeness by cupping a fruit and twisting upwards gently. If it comes away easily with the stem, it's ready. Different varieties ripen at different times from August (Discovery) to October (Bramley, Egremont Russet). Store late varieties in a cool dark place — they keep for months."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How tall does an apple tree get?", "answer": "Depends entirely on rootstock, not variety. M27 stays 1.5 m (patio); M9 reaches 2 m (cordon); MM106 is 3–4 m (half-standard); M25 reaches 5–8 m (traditional orchard tree). For small gardens, M9 or M27 trained as cordons or fans against a wall is the productive choice. The variety controls the flavour and ripening time; the rootstock controls the size."},
    {"question": "Do I need two apple trees for pollination?", "answer": "Almost always yes. A few apples are self-fertile (Queen Cox, Greensleeves) but most need a pollination partner that flowers at the same time (same group 1–7). If you have only space for one tree, check whether a neighbour has an apple within 30 m — bees travel that far. Crab apples make excellent universal pollinators and look beautiful too."},
    {"question": "What apple varieties grow best in the UK?", "answer": "Heritage British varieties beat supermarket favourites for flavour and disease resistance. Eaters: Discovery (early, sweet), James Grieve (mid, sharp-sweet), Egremont Russet (late, distinctive), Cox's Orange Pippin (classic but disease-prone). Cookers: Bramley (the standard), Lord Derby. Disease-resistant modern: Red Falstaff, Saturn, Topaz. For a small garden, plan for two or three trees with different ripening times for a long season."},
    {"question": "When and how should I prune my apple tree?", "answer": "Winter prune (December to February) for shape and structure: remove crossing branches, water-shoots, anything growing inwards. Summer prune (late July) for cordons and trained shapes only: cut new growth back to 3 leaves above the basal cluster. Don't prune in autumn or early spring (encourages disease). Don't lop tops off mature trees — invites disease and produces hundreds of water-shoots."},
    {"question": "What are the brown patches in my apples?", "answer": "Scab (Venturia inaequalis) — fungal, common in wet UK summers. Rake up fallen leaves to break the cycle (the fungus overwinters on leaf litter). Open up the centre of the tree by pruning for air circulation. Scab-resistant cultivars (Discovery, Red Falstaff, Saturn) suffer much less. The flesh is fine to eat — peel and remove the brown bits."},
    {"question": "How long do apple trees last?", "answer": "30–50 years for healthy trees on standard rootstocks; 15–25 years on dwarf rootstocks. The oldest productive apple trees in the UK are over 200 years old. Most trees become less productive after 30 years and worth replacing."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'apple';

-- From aquilegia.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow aquilegia direct in May–June, or plant young plants in spring or autumn, in sun or part shade in any decent garden soil — they''re easy short-lived perennials (3–4 years) that self-seed prolifically and persist as a self-renewing colony. Aquilegias are hardy (RHS H7) and produce nodding bell-shaped spurred flowers in May–June on stems 30–80 cm tall. The wild granny''s bonnet (Aquilegia vulgaris) has blue-purple flowers; named hybrids cover the full range from white through pink, lilac, deep purple, near-black, yellow, and bicolour. Cross-pollination is dramatic — if you have two different colour parents, the offspring seedlings show wild colour variation. Cut spent flower stems back after flowering to extend the season, or leave them for self-seeding. All parts mildly toxic if eaten in quantity — leave the seedheads alone if children or pets graze plants.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick the wild species or named hybrids", "text": "Wild granny's bonnet (Aquilegia vulgaris): the UK native (and widely naturalised) — blue, purple, white, or pink nodding bells on 60 cm stems. Self-seeds, hybridises freely, the easiest aquilegia. Long-spurred hybrids (McKana's Giants, Mrs Scott Elliott): larger flowers, longer back-curving spurs, range of bicolours. Often shorter-lived. Black Barlow and other Barlow series: doubled flowers without spurs, deep purple-black, distinctive. Crimson Star, Nora Barlow, William Guinness: named cultivars with specific colour patterns. Alpine species (A. flabellata, A. canadensis): smaller, more refined, suit rockeries. For a first aquilegia: plant A. vulgaris — it'll spread, hybridise, and give you a perpetual colony."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct or plant young", "text": "From seed: surface-sow in May–June (needs light to germinate). Direct-sow where you want the plants. Germination in 14–28 days. From plant: 1 L pots in spring or autumn. Aquilegias have long taproots and don't transplant well once mature — get them in young."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in sun or part shade, ordinary soil", "text": "Adaptable. Sun gives more flowers; part shade gives longer-lasting flowers and better foliage. Ordinary garden soil, ideally well-drained but moisture-retentive. Aquilegias tolerate clay if not waterlogged. Spacing 40 cm. Position: aquilegias suit cottage gardens, woodland edges, gravel gardens, mixed borders."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Accept the cross-pollination", "text": "Aquilegias are promiscuous. Plant two different colours within bee range (50 m+) and the offspring seedlings will be a riot of unpredictable colours — usually muddier than the parents, sometimes spectacularly new combinations. To preserve a specific colour: isolate one variety, or accept that the seedlings will revert to wild blue-purple within a few generations. Many UK gardeners welcome the colour lottery; some plant single-colour beds in distant corners."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Watch for aquilegia leaf miner", "text": "The main UK pest. Aquilegia leaf miner (a small fly) lays eggs on the leaves; larvae tunnel through the leaf creating winding white tracks. Cosmetic damage — doesn't kill the plant, doesn't significantly affect flowering, but ruins the foliage appearance. Two responses: (1) Pick off and bin badly affected leaves at first sight (don't compost — larvae overwinter in fallen leaves). (2) For severe infestations: cut affected plants down hard after flowering; the fresh regrowth is usually miner-free."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut spent stems for repeat or leave for seed", "text": "Two approaches, both valid. Cut spent flower stems at the base after the first flush fades (late June). The plant may produce smaller secondary stems with a few more flowers, and the foliage stays tidy. Leave the spent stems for the architectural seedheads (small upright capsules splitting open to release shiny black seeds) and the self-seeding that perpetuates the colony."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Accept the short lifespan", "text": "Aquilegias live 3–4 years on average. Don't be surprised when individual plants die out after their fourth flowering — this is normal. Self-seeding is the perpetuation strategy: let some plants set seed each year and the colony renews itself indefinitely without effort. Some hybrids are shorter-lived than the species — the long-spurred Mrs Scott Elliott and McKana types often last only 2–3 years."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Manage self-seeding", "text": "Aquilegia seedlings appear in spring around mature plants — often in surprising places (gravel paths, between paving). Pull unwanted seedlings while small; transplant desired ones to fill gaps (they move well as small plants but resent disturbance once their long taproot establishes). For a controlled colony: deadhead before seed sets each year. For a self-renewing cottage-garden look: let the colony self-manage, weeding out only crowded or unwanted seedlings."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Are aquilegias perennial?", "answer": "Yes, but short-lived perennials — 3–4 years average. Each individual plant grows from seed, flowers for 3–4 seasons, then dies. The colony perpetuates indefinitely because aquilegias self-seed prolifically — there's always a new generation coming through to replace dying parents. Don't be surprised when individual plants disappear after their fourth flowering; this is normal. To keep aquilegias indefinitely: let some plants set seed each year (don't deadhead all stems), or sow fresh seed every 2–3 years."},
    {"question": "Why do my aquilegias change colour over the years?", "answer": "Cross-pollination. Aquilegias hybridise freely between colours when planted within bee range of each other (50+ m). The offspring seedlings inherit a mix of parent characteristics, often emerging as muddier or unexpected colour combinations. Within 3–4 generations, mixed colours often revert to the dominant wild blue-purple of Aquilegia vulgaris. To preserve a specific colour: plant in isolation from other aquilegias, or deadhead religiously before any seed sets and replant from fresh seed of the chosen variety each generation."},
    {"question": "When do I plant or sow aquilegias?", "answer": "Two windows. Direct-sow seed in May–June (surface-sow, needs light). Plants flower the following year. Plant young plants (1 L pots) in spring (March–April) or autumn (September–October). Most aquilegias bought as young plants flower in their first or second season. Don't transplant mature plants — the long taproot resents disturbance and the plant often dies within a year of being moved. Source young plants or sow direct where you want the colony."},
    {"question": "What are those white squiggles on my aquilegia leaves?", "answer": "Aquilegia leaf miner damage — a small fly whose larvae tunnel through the leaf, creating winding white-grey tracks. Cosmetic damage — doesn't significantly harm flowering or plant health, but ruins foliage appearance. Most aquilegias have some leaf miner damage by late spring. Two responses: (1) Pick off badly affected leaves and bin (don't compost — larvae overwinter in fallen foliage). (2) For severe infestations, cut the whole plant down to 5 cm after flowering — fresh regrowth is usually miner-free for the rest of the season."},
    {"question": "Are aquilegias poisonous?", "answer": "All parts mildly toxic if eaten in quantity. Aquilegias contain cyanogenic glycosides and alkaloids that can cause stomach upset, nausea, vomiting, and (in large quantities) heart palpitations. Healthy adults: small contact is harmless; eating significant amounts requires medical attention. Children and pets: warn against eating any part. In practice: aquilegias are not particularly attractive to eat (bitter flavour), and poisonings are rare. The seeds inside the splitting capsules contain the highest concentration of toxic compounds. Don't worry about handling, but wash hands after deadheading."},
    {"question": "What's the best aquilegia for the UK?", "answer": "For naturalising and easy perpetuation: wild Aquilegia vulgaris — the UK native (and widely naturalised), self-seeds reliably, classic cottage-garden look. For named-cultivar interest: Black Barlow (deep purple-black double), Nora Barlow (pink-and-green double), William Guinness (deep purple with white edges). For long-spurred elegance: Mrs Scott Elliott hybrids — large flowers, range of bicolours, slightly shorter-lived. For a first aquilegia, plant the species A. vulgaris — it'll give a perpetual colony with minimum fuss."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'aquilegia';

-- From asparagus.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant asparagus crowns from March to April, bare-root, in a sunny well-drained bed prepared the previous autumn with plenty of well-rotted manure. Asparagus is very hardy (RHS H7) and one of the longest-lived garden crops — 15–20 years of cropping from a single planting. The catch is the wait: don''t cut any spears in year one (let the plants build up), light pick year two, full pick from year three onwards. Cut spears at ground level when 15–20 cm tall, from late April to mid-June only — stop cutting on the 21st June to let the plants build reserves for next year. All-male F1 cultivars (Gijnlim, Backlim, Mondeo, Pacific 2000) give larger spears than older mixed-sex varieties. The premium garden crop for patient growers.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose all-male F1 cultivars", "text": "Modern all-male F1s (Gijnlim, Backlim, Mondeo, Pacific 2000, Stewart's Purple) give 30–50% more crop than older mixed-sex varieties (Connover's Colossal, Martha Washington). All-male plants don't waste energy producing seed; bigger spears, longer cropping life. Worth the premium price."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the bed the previous autumn", "text": "Full sun, deep well-drained soil. Fork in two barrowloads of well-rotted manure or compost per square metre — asparagus is in this bed for 20 years and won't be re-fed easily. Asparagus hates waterlogged soil; if your ground is heavy, build a raised bed."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant crowns in spring", "text": "March to April, bare-root. Dig a trench 30 cm wide and 20 cm deep. Make a ridge of soil 5 cm high down the centre of the trench. Set crowns 30–45 cm apart along the ridge, draping the roots down both sides. Cover with soil to fill the trench level; water in."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Mulch annually", "text": "Each March or April, top-dress the bed with 5 cm of well-rotted compost or manure. Asparagus loves a deep mulch; it conserves moisture, feeds the crowns, and suppresses weeds."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Don't cut in year one", "text": "The hardest step. Let all the spears grow into tall ferny foliage in the first season. The plants need this year to build up the crown reserves. Cutting spears in year one stunts the bed for life."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Light pick in year two", "text": "In year two, cut a few spears (no more than 4 weeks of picking) and let the rest grow up to ferny foliage. From year three onwards, full picking from late April to 21st June."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut spears at ground level", "text": "Spears are ready when 15–20 cm tall and the tips are still tightly closed. Cut with a sharp knife 2 cm below soil level, taking care not to damage other developing spears. Pick daily during the peak in May."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "STOP picking on 21st June", "text": "The rule that protects the bed. After 21st June, let any further spears grow up to ferny foliage. This rebuilds the crown reserves for next year. Cutting later weakens the crowns and shortens the productive life of the bed."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Cut down foliage in autumn", "text": "When the ferny foliage yellows and dies back in October–November, cut it down to 5 cm above the ground. Compost the foliage (no diseases of concern). Top-dress with compost in spring; the crowns sprout fresh spears in April."},
    {"step": 10, "name": "Watch for asparagus beetle", "text": "Bright red and black beetles with their grey larvae strip the ferny foliage. Hand-pick in summer; encourage ladybirds. Severe infestations weaken the crowns over years; light infestations the plants tolerate."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When and how do I plant asparagus crowns?", "answer": "March to April, bare-root, in a sunny well-drained bed prepared the previous autumn with plenty of compost or well-rotted manure. Dig a trench 20 cm deep; make a central ridge; drape the crowns over the ridge with roots spread; cover. Plant 30–45 cm apart. Asparagus stays in the same bed for 15–20 years — site carefully and prepare the soil properly."},
    {"question": "When can I start picking my asparagus?", "answer": "Year one: no picking at all. Year two: very light picking for 4 weeks maximum. Year three onwards: full picking from late April to 21st June. The slow start is non-negotiable — cutting too early weakens the crowns and shortens the productive life of the bed."},
    {"question": "Why must I stop picking on 21st June?", "answer": "After mid-June, the plant needs to grow its full ferny foliage and rebuild crown reserves for next year. Cutting later than the longest day (21st June) weakens the crowns and shortens the productive life of the bed from 20 years to maybe 8–10. The rule isn't superstition; it's about leaving enough season for the plant to recover."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between all-male F1 and older asparagus cultivars?", "answer": "All-male F1 hybrids (Gijnlim, Backlim, Mondeo, Pacific 2000): only male plants in the seed, no energy wasted on producing seed; bigger spears; 30–50% more crop than older varieties; longer productive life. Older mixed-sex cultivars (Connover's Colossal, Martha Washington): smaller spears; female plants produce decorative red berries but waste energy on seed; still good but outperformed by F1s. Pay the extra for F1s."},
    {"question": "How long does an asparagus bed last?", "answer": "15–20 years productively with proper care (annual mulch, never cutting after 21st June, weed-free). Some old asparagus beds in walled gardens have cropped for 30+ years. Replace only when productivity drops below worthwhile levels; the crowns themselves can live 25+ years."},
    {"question": "What's the asparagus beetle and how do I control it?", "answer": "Crioceris asparagi — bright red-and-black adult beetle and grey-brown larvae that strip the ferny foliage in summer. Hand-pick adults and rub off the brown egg-clusters from stems. Encouraging ladybirds and parasitic wasps helps. Severe infestations weaken crowns over years; light damage the plants tolerate. Pyrethrum-based sprays are an organic-approved option for heavy infestations."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'asparagus';

-- From aubergine.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow aubergine seed indoors from late February to mid-March in a heated propagator. Aubergines are frost-tender (RHS H1c) and want consistent warmth above 18°C to flower and fruit — a greenhouse, polytunnel or conservatory is essential for reliable UK cropping. Choose F1 hybrids bred for cooler climates: Moneymaker (the UK classic), Bonica F1 (early, heavy cropper), Black Beauty (large dark fruit). Pinch out the growing tip at 30 cm to encourage branching. One healthy plant gives 5–8 fruit through July to October. Pick when shiny and firm — overripe fruit goes dull and bitter. Red spider mite is the main problem under glass; high humidity and weekly inspection are the defence. Don''t try outdoors in UK except a hot sheltered south coast.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow indoors with bottom heat", "text": "Late February to mid-March in a heated propagator at 21–25°C. Aubergines need 22–26 weeks from sowing to first ripe fruit; an early start is essential. Sow 2 seeds per 9 cm pot, 5 mm deep; thin to the strongest after germination."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose UK-suited F1 hybrids", "text": "Moneymaker (the long-running UK classic, reliable), Bonica F1 (early, heavy cropper), Black Beauty (large dark glossy fruit), Pinstripe (slim purple-and-white striped, ornamental as well as edible). Skip heritage cultivars from warmer climates."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pot on as they grow", "text": "9 cm → 1-litre → 5-litre or 7.5-litre final pot. Move on as soon as roots fill each container; aubergines check growth badly if root-bound. Pot on into rich peat-free compost with added grit for drainage."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant in final position by late May", "text": "Greenhouse border, polytunnel, or in 7.5-litre containers in the warmest greenhouse spot. Outdoor only in southern England in a south-facing sheltered position from June. Stake plants — they grow heavy and can topple."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pinch out for branching", "text": "At 30 cm, pinch out the growing tip to force side branches. After fruit starts to set, limit each plant to 4–6 developing fruit; remove later flowers to concentrate the plant's energy."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Feed and water", "text": "High-potash weekly liquid feed from first flowers. Consistent watering — aubergines drop flowers under water stress. Mulch the soil surface to hold moisture. Spray foliage with water on hot days to discourage red spider mite."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for red spider mite", "text": "The main greenhouse aubergine pest. Tiny mites suck sap; leaves go mottled then bronzed. Misting daily, ventilating well, and biological control (introduce Phytoseiulus persimilis) all help. Hot dry conditions encourage the mite; humidity discourages it."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick when shiny and firm", "text": "Fruit is ready when it reaches full colour, shiny skin, and firm flesh. Press gently — slight give is right; firm but yielding. Overripe fruit goes dull-skinned, soft, and bitter; under-ripe is hard and tasteless. Cut fruit off with secateurs leaving 2 cm of stem."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Can I grow aubergines outdoors in the UK?", "answer": "Marginally and only in southern England in a sheltered south-facing position, ideally in a black or dark pot that holds heat. Outdoor crops in the south of England give 2–4 fruit per plant in a good summer; in cool years, fewer or none. North of the Midlands, outdoor aubergines essentially don't ripen. Grow under cover."},
    {"question": "When should I sow aubergine seeds?", "answer": "Late February to mid-March in a heated propagator. Earlier (January) gives leggy plants by planting-out time; later (April) leaves too little growing season for fruit to ripen. Aubergines need 22–26 weeks from sowing to first ripe fruit; the maths require an early start."},
    {"question": "Why are my aubergine flowers dropping without setting?", "answer": "Water stress, cold, or low humidity. Aubergines drop flowers if they get dry, if the temperature falls below 18°C consistently, or in very dry air under glass. Water more consistently (mulch helps), keep night temperatures above 15°C, and mist plants in dry weather. Stable conditions are the key."},
    {"question": "What variety of aubergine grows best in the UK?", "answer": "Moneymaker is the UK long-running classic — reliable, productive, classic shape. Bonica F1 is an early-cropping modern alternative. Black Beauty is the standard large dark fruit. For containers, Pinstripe is compact and ornamental. Slim Japanese cultivars (Long Tom, Kermit) crop well in cooler greenhouses."},
    {"question": "When can I pick aubergines?", "answer": "From mid-July under glass; mid-August outdoors in southern England. Pick when fruit reaches full size, full colour, and shiny taut skin. Press gently — slight give means ripe. The first fruit usually ripens 18–20 weeks after sowing. Pick the lower fruit first; the plant keeps setting from the top."},
    {"question": "How do I store aubergines?", "answer": "Fresh: 4–7 days in a cool place (not the fridge — they suffer chill damage below 10°C). For longer storage, slice, salt, drain, then freeze in single layers; or roast, peel and freeze in batches; or make caponata. Aubergines don't dry well and don't pickle in interesting ways."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'aubergine';

-- From basil.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow basil seed indoors from late April to mid-May; don''t bother with outdoor sowings except on a sheltered south-coast windowsill. Basil is very frost-tender (RHS H1c) and hates cold wet soil — even a chilly summer night below 10°C checks growth. Grow in 1-litre pots on a sunny windowsill, in a greenhouse, or in a polytunnel; outdoor plants only thrive in pots in a sheltered south-facing position from June to early September. Pinch out flower spikes as they appear to keep the plant leafy. Pick young leaves regularly from the top; older leaves get bitter. Sweet (Genovese) basil is the kitchen standard; Thai basil for South-East Asian dishes; lemon basil for Mediterranean.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "Late April to mid-May. 9 cm pots of multi-purpose compost, 5–6 seeds per pot, lightly covered. 18–22°C on a warm windowsill or in a propagator. Germination in 7–14 days."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Thin to the strongest", "text": "Once seedlings are 5 cm tall, thin to 2–3 per pot. Don't transplant — basil hates root disturbance."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pot on or plant in containers", "text": "Pot on to a 2-litre pot when roots fill the smaller, or plant 3 seedlings per 30 cm container. Outdoors only after the last frost in a sheltered sunny position."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water carefully", "text": "Water at the base in the morning. Basil hates wet leaves and waterlogged roots — let the compost surface dry between waterings."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pinch out flowers", "text": "As soon as flower spikes appear, pinch them out. Flowering shuts down leaf production. Keep doing it through the season."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick from the top", "text": "Pick the top pairs of leaves regularly; the plant branches at each cut and produces more. Don't strip the lower leaves; they keep the plant alive."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Move under cover in autumn", "text": "From mid-September, night temperatures drop below basil's tolerance. Move pots indoors to extend the season to October or November."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow basil seed in the UK?", "answer": "Indoors from late April to mid-May; in a heated propagator from late March. Don't sow outdoors except on a sheltered south-coast bed in late May. Basil seedlings need 18°C minimum and hate cold wet soil — sowing too early gives leggy, stalled plants. Two or three pots indoors gives a household plenty."},
    {"question": "Can I grow basil outdoors in the UK?", "answer": "Only in pots, in a sunny sheltered south-facing position, from June to early September. The UK summer is borderline for basil — a cool wet July checks growth and a chilly August night below 10°C halts it. Greenhouse, polytunnel, or warm windowsill basil is more productive than outdoor in most of the UK."},
    {"question": "Why is my basil bolting (flowering)?", "answer": "Long days, heat stress, or the plant is mature. Bolting is normal — basil is an annual that's trying to set seed before frost. Pinch out the flower spikes as soon as they appear; this delays bolting by a few weeks. After several rounds of pinching, the plant will eventually flower regardless — that's the end of its useful life. Start a fresh sowing every 6 weeks if you want continuous supply."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between sweet, Thai, and lemon basil?", "answer": "Sweet (Genovese) is the standard pizza-and-pesto basil — broad leaves, classic basil flavour. Thai basil has smaller pointed leaves and a stronger aniseed note; the right basil for pho and Thai curries. Lemon basil has narrower leaves and a citrus-floral flavour; works in dressings and fish dishes. Sweet is the everyday; the others are worth a separate pot if you cook with them often."},
    {"question": "How do I store fresh basil?", "answer": "Treat cut stems like a small bouquet — stand in a glass of cold water on the kitchen counter, not in the fridge (cold blackens basil leaves). It keeps 4–5 days. For longer storage, blend whole leaves with olive oil and freeze in ice-cube trays. Drying ruins the flavour; freezing in oil preserves it."},
    {"question": "Can I grow basil from supermarket pots?", "answer": "Yes — the multi-stemmed pots at supermarkets are bunches of seedlings crammed together, struggling for root space. Tip the pot out, gently tease the seedlings apart, and pot them on individually (or in groups of 2–3) in fresh compost. Place on a sunny windowsill. Within two weeks they double in size and stop dying back."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'basil';

-- From bay.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant a bay laurel in a sunny sheltered spot, in a pot or in the ground, in well-drained soil — it''s a slow-growing Mediterranean evergreen shrub or small tree that gives you fresh bay leaves any time of year. Bay is moderately hardy (RHS H4) and survives most UK winters once mature, but young plants and pot-grown bays are vulnerable below -5°C and need winter protection. The classic UK use is a container-grown standard or pyramid on the doorstep — moveable, manageable, and decorative. Pick leaves any time; older darker leaves have stronger flavour than the bright fresh tips. Don''t confuse with cherry laurel (Prunus laurocerasus) which is toxic — true culinary bay has matt narrow leaves, faintly aromatic when crushed; cherry laurel has glossy broader leaves and contains cyanide compounds. Bay sucker (a pest causing curled leaves) is the main UK problem.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Know your laurel — true bay vs cherry laurel", "text": "This is critical. Bay laurel (Laurus nobilis): matt narrow oval leaves, faintly aromatic when crushed, small yellow flowers, dark berries — the kitchen herb. Cherry laurel (Prunus laurocerasus): glossy broader oval leaves, NO aroma, white flower spikes, red-to-black berries — used as hedging but TOXIC (contains cyanide compounds). Mistakes in identification can be serious. If in doubt, crush a leaf — bay has a distinctive aromatic smell; cherry laurel doesn't."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Buy a young plant from a reputable nursery", "text": "Garden centres sell bay in 3 L upwards. Topiary shapes (lollipops, pyramids, balls) cost more but save 3+ years of training. For a kitchen herb, a 1 m bush or smaller is fine — you won't use many leaves. Avoid cheap supermarket bay — often weak, poorly rooted, sometimes mislabelled."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in well-drained soil, in sun", "text": "Full sun ideal, light shade tolerated. Bay hates waterlogged soil — on clay, plant on a raised bed or grow in a pot. Spring planting (April–May) gives the best establishment; autumn planting risks winter loss on a young plant."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Container-grow for moveability and winter protection", "text": "The classic UK approach. A 40 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed 30% horticultural grit, in a sheltered sunny spot. Move under cover (unheated greenhouse, porch, garage with light) when severe cold is forecast — pot bays freeze faster than ground-planted ones. Refresh the top 5 cm of compost annually; repot every 3–4 years."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Feed lightly in spring", "text": "A handful of slow-release general-purpose fertiliser around the base in April. Pot bays need more regular feeding — liquid feed monthly through the growing season. Bay tolerates poor soils but a moderate feed keeps leaves dark and glossy."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Clip in early summer for shape", "text": "Bay tolerates shaping well — used in topiary for centuries. Clip lightly in early June to maintain shape (lollipop, pyramid, ball, or natural bush). For natural bushes, just remove crossing or dead branches in April. Sharp secateurs for individual leaf cuts; shears for hedging-style clipping."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick leaves any time", "text": "Bay is evergreen — pick fresh leaves year-round. Older leaves (1+ year old, dark green) have stronger flavour than fresh new growth (yellowish-green). Use 1–2 leaves per dish; bay is potent and overdoes easily. Dry surplus by spreading on a tray for 1–2 weeks in a warm dry place; dried leaves keep flavour for a year in a sealed jar."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for bay sucker", "text": "The main UK pest. Bay sucker (Trioza alacris) causes leaf edges to curl, thicken, and pale — a clear distortion. Heavy infestations weaken the plant and ruin leaf appearance. Pick off and bin curled leaves at first sight. Severe cases respond to a horticultural soap or oil spray (organic) or systemic insecticide (chemical). Good airflow and avoiding overcrowding reduce risk."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I tell bay from cherry laurel?", "answer": "Critical to get right — cherry laurel is toxic. Bay laurel (Laurus nobilis, the kitchen herb): matt narrow oval leaves, faintly aromatic when crushed, small yellow flowers in spring, dark berries in autumn. Cherry laurel (Prunus laurocerasus, the hedging plant): glossy broader oval leaves, no aroma when crushed, white flower spikes, red-to-black berries. The crushed-leaf test is reliable — bay smells distinctly herbal, cherry laurel smells of nothing or faintly of bitter almond (cyanide)."},
    {"question": "Will bay survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Hardy in most of the UK once mature (RHS H4) — survives down to about -10°C in a sheltered position. Young plants (under 3 years) and pot-grown bays are vulnerable below -5°C; they need protection in severe weather. Symptoms of cold damage: brown leaves in late winter that drop in spring; the plant usually re-shoots from the base if the roots survived. In cold gardens (Highlands, exposed Midlands), grow bay in a pot that can move under cover December–February."},
    {"question": "When do I prune a bay tree?", "answer": "Light shaping in early June is the main job — after the spring flush has hardened, before the second flush of growth. Use secateurs for individual leaf clipping (topiary), shears for hedge-style cuts. April: remove any crossing, dead, or frost-damaged branches. Don't prune late summer or autumn — soft regrowth won't harden before winter and will be frost-damaged. For renovation of an overgrown bay, hard-prune in late April; it tolerates cutting back into old wood."},
    {"question": "What's wrong with my bay's leaves — curled and yellowing at the edges?", "answer": "Almost certainly bay sucker (Trioza alacris), a small aphid-like insect that causes the characteristic leaf curl. Pick off and bin affected leaves at first sight. A horticultural soap or oil spray (organic) or pyrethrum-based control deals with active infestations. Improve airflow around the plant — bay sucker thrives in still, crowded conditions. Yellowing without curl is more likely nutrient deficiency on a pot-grown plant — top-dress with fresh compost and apply liquid feed."},
    {"question": "Can I grow bay from seed or cuttings?", "answer": "Cuttings are easier than seed. Take 10 cm semi-ripe cuttings in late summer (August), strip the lower leaves, push into gritty compost in a propagator with bottom heat (15–20°C). Roots form slowly — expect 8–12 weeks. Seeds are notoriously slow and unreliable; they take 6–12 months to germinate and seedlings grow at a glacial pace. Most UK gardeners buy a plant."},
    {"question": "How many bay leaves should I use in cooking?", "answer": "Two or three at most per dish. Bay is potent — too many leaves make a sauce or stew bitter and overly herbal. Always remove the leaves before serving (they're not edible whole — woody and sharp-edged when chewed). Older dark-green leaves have stronger flavour than fresh new tips; a year-old dried leaf is often more flavoursome than a fresh young one straight off the bush."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'bay';

-- From beech.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant beech bare-root from November to March, in well-drained soil, in sun or part shade — it''s the classic UK formal-hedge plant and a magnificent woodland specimen tree. Beech is very hardy (RHS H6) and tolerates almost any chalky or loamy soil except waterlogged or heavy clay. The signature feature for hedging: marcescence — beech holds its dead copper-brown leaves through winter (the spring growth pushes them off), giving year-round screening without needing an evergreen. Clip once a year in August for a perfect dense hedge. Slow first 2 years, then 30 cm per year once established. Green beech is the standard; copper (purple) beech (F. sylvatica Purpurea) is the dark-leaved ornamental form, often used as occasional accents within a green beech hedge. Doesn''t tolerate waterlogged soil — that''s the main UK failure mode.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick green or copper beech", "text": "Green beech (Fagus sylvatica species form): the classic UK formal hedge, fresh green leaves in spring, copper-brown in autumn, holds leaves through winter. The functional choice — cheaper and more available. Copper (purple) beech (F. sylvatica Purpurea / Atropurpurea): dark purple-bronze leaves all summer, deep copper-brown in winter. Used as occasional accents in a green hedge for variety, or as standalone specimens. Fern-leaved beech (F. sylvatica Aspleniifolia): cut-leaf foliage, ornamental specimen tree only — not for hedging. For hedging, green beech is the safe choice."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in well-drained soil — NOT clay", "text": "Beech's main failure mode in UK gardens is waterlogged or heavy clay soil. It wants free-draining loam or chalk; even improved clay struggles. Test before planting: dig a 60 cm hole, fill with water, leave 24 hours. If still standing water, beech won't thrive — plant hornbeam (Carpinus betulus) instead, which looks similar and tolerates heavy soils. Beech tolerates almost any pH provided drainage works."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March while dormant. Bare-root whips (60–90 cm) are very cheap (50p–£2 each) and establish faster than container plants for hedging. Spacing for hedges: 30 cm apart for a dense fine hedge, 45 cm for a more vigorous structure. For specimen trees: 5–8 m clear space; beech becomes a large tree (15–30 m) over decades. Water in deeply."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Allow slow establishment", "text": "First 2 years are slow — beech puts energy into roots before visible top growth. Don't despair if the hedge looks ragged in year 1 or 2; year 3 onwards it powers up. Mulch with leaf-mould or garden compost in a strip along the planted row each spring to conserve moisture and feed. Water through dry summers in the first 2 years; established beech tolerates drought."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Trim once a year in August", "text": "The classic UK beech hedge regime. August is the only clip needed. Earlier cuts disturb nesting birds and remove the spring flush; later cuts leave soft regrowth vulnerable to winter cold. Use shears or a powered hedge trimmer, taking the new growth back to just above the previous year's cuts. A neat batter (slight inward slope from base to top) keeps the lower parts well-furnished with leaves."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Welcome the marcescence — don't worry about brown leaves", "text": "Beech's signature winter trick. Unlike most deciduous plants, beech retains its dead copper-brown leaves through winter — a phenomenon called marcescence. The new spring growth pushes them off in March–April. This is feature, not failure: the brown leaves give year-round screening, attractive winter texture, and protect emerging buds from frost damage. Don't try to remove the brown leaves; let them fall naturally in spring."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Grow a beech specimen tree only if you have space", "text": "Beech becomes a massive tree — 20–30 m tall, 15 m crown spread, over 100–200 years. Don't plant a single beech in a small garden; it will dominate within 20 years and outlive everyone alive when planted. For specimen culture: 10 m clear space minimum, deep well-drained soil, no overhead competition. Beech avenues are stunning but suit estate-scale plantings only."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for honey fungus (the main long-term threat)", "text": "Honey fungus (Armillaria mellea) is the main UK long-term killer of established beech trees. Symptoms: leaves yellowing early, branches dying back, honey-coloured mushrooms at the base in autumn. No cure: affected trees usually die within 5–10 years of infection. Prevention: keep beech vigorous (mulch, no compaction), don't plant adjacent to known honey-fungus sites, plant resistant species (yew, holly) in known infected areas. Hedges are less vulnerable than specimen trees."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I clip a beech hedge?", "answer": "August — and only once a year. Earlier cuts disturb nesting birds (illegal during the bird breeding season under the UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981); later cuts leave soft regrowth that's killed by winter cold. August is the sweet spot: birds have finished, the spring flush has hardened, and the cut surfaces heal before autumn. Use shears or a powered hedge trimmer, cutting just above last year's pruning line. A neat hedge needs one annual clip; that's all."},
    {"question": "Why does my beech hedge keep its brown leaves through winter?", "answer": "That's normal — it's a feature called marcescence. Beech (and hornbeam, oak) hold their dead copper-brown leaves through winter; the new spring growth pushes them off in March–April. Three benefits of marcescence: (1) year-round screening even though beech is deciduous; (2) attractive winter colour and texture; (3) the dead leaves protect emerging buds from late frosts. Don't try to remove them — they'll fall naturally in spring. This is exactly why beech is the UK's favourite formal-hedge plant."},
    {"question": "My beech hedge isn't growing — what's wrong?", "answer": "Three usual causes. (1) Waterlogged soil: beech's biggest enemy. Check drainage — dig a 60 cm hole and fill with water; if it still holds water 24 hours later, beech won't thrive. Replace with hornbeam in wet soils. (2) First 2 years are slow: beech establishes roots first, then accelerates. Year 1–2 may look ragged; year 3 onwards normally powers up to 30 cm/year. (3) Drought stress on young plants: water deeply through dry summers in the first 2 years."},
    {"question": "Can I grow beech in clay soil?", "answer": "Not really — replace with hornbeam. Beech's main UK failure mode is waterlogged or heavy clay soil. Hornbeam (Carpinus betulus) looks almost identical (similar leaf shape, also holds dead leaves through winter via marcescence, takes clipping similarly) but tolerates clay and waterlogged conditions far better. If your soil holds water after 24 hours of testing, plant hornbeam instead of beech — you'll thank yourself in 10 years."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between green and copper beech?", "answer": "Same species, different leaf colour. Green beech (Fagus sylvatica species form) has fresh green leaves in spring, copper-brown in autumn and winter. Copper (or purple) beech (F. sylvatica Purpurea / Atropurpurea) has deep purple-bronze leaves all summer that turn deep copper in winter. Cultivation is identical. Often combined: planting one in five as a copper beech within a green beech hedge gives an attractive dappled-purple effect."},
    {"question": "Can I grow beech in a pot?", "answer": "Possible only as a young hedge whip or bonsai. A mature beech is too large for any container — it wants 5+ m of root space. Young beech whips can live in a 30 cm pot for 3–5 years while establishing, but must be planted out before they outgrow it. Beech bonsai: a centuries-old tradition; works because bonsai techniques restrict root growth artificially. For a permanent container shrub, plant box or yew instead of beech."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'beech';

-- From beetroot.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow beetroot seed direct from late March (under cloches) to July in successional batches every three weeks. Beetroot is hardy (RHS H4–H5) and one of the easiest UK vegetables — the seedlings come up reliably, the roots want little fuss, and a pull-young approach gives sweet tender beets in 10–12 weeks. What looks like a single seed is actually a cluster of 2–4 seeds, so thin seedlings to one per station. Boltardy is the standard for early sowings (truly bolt-resistant); Detroit Globe for main-crop; Chioggia for the striped slice. Pull when 5–7 cm across for the best flavour; once they get to tennis-ball size they coarsen. Store in damp sand for winter eating.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "Late March under cloches in southern England, mid-April outdoors elsewhere. Successional sowings every 3 weeks until early July give a continuous supply. 2 cm deep, 10 cm apart in rows 30 cm apart."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Soak seed (optional)", "text": "Soaking beetroot seed in tepid water for an hour before sowing speeds germination. Not essential but useful in cold spring soil."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin seedlings", "text": "Each beetroot seed is actually a cluster of 2–4 seeds — multiple seedlings come up in a tight clump. When seedlings are 5 cm tall, thin to the strongest one per station. The thinnings are edible (toss in salads)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Use Boltardy for early sowings", "text": "Cold-snap-after-sowing makes most beetroot bolt (run to seed) instead of forming a root. Boltardy is the cultivar bred specifically to resist this. For sowings before late April, choose Boltardy."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water in dry weather", "text": "Water deeply once a week if the weather is dry, especially as the roots are swelling. Drought makes them woody and small."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pull young", "text": "Pull when roots are 5–7 cm across (golf-ball to tennis-ball size) for the best flavour. Anything larger gets coarse and woody. Pull alternate plants from a row to give the rest room to keep growing."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Store for winter", "text": "Lift main-crop beets before the first hard frost. Twist (don't cut) the tops off 2 cm above the root — cutting bleeds them. Layer in damp sand in a wooden box; store in a frost-free shed. They keep until April."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow beetroot in the UK?", "answer": "Late March under cloches in southern England; mid-April to early May outdoors elsewhere. Successional sowings every 3 weeks until early July give continuous picking. Late July sowings of fast varieties crop into November. Avoid sowing into cold wet soil; beetroot wants soil above 8°C to germinate."},
    {"question": "Why are my beetroot plants bolting (flowering)?", "answer": "A cold spell after sowing triggers bolting — the plant thinks it's been through a winter. Choose Boltardy (the only truly bolt-resistant cultivar) for sowings before May. Other causes: drought stress, planting too close, or the variety isn't bolt-resistant. Once a plant bolts, pull it; the root will be small and woody."},
    {"question": "Why do I get multiple seedlings from one beetroot seed?", "answer": "Each beetroot seed is actually a corky cluster containing 2–4 individual seeds. They germinate as a tight clump. Once seedlings are 5 cm tall, thin to the strongest single seedling per station — use scissors to snip the unwanted ones at soil level rather than pulling (which disturbs the keeper). Monogerm varieties (single-seed clusters) exist (Boltardy is sometimes available as monogerm) and skip this step."},
    {"question": "How big should I let my beetroot grow?", "answer": "5–7 cm across — golf-ball to tennis-ball size — is the sweet spot for flavour and texture. Pull when they reach this size; smaller is sweeter but yields little; bigger gets woody and coarse. Pull alternate plants from a row first to give the remainder room to keep swelling for a second harvest a fortnight later."},
    {"question": "Can I grow beetroot in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — a 30 cm deep container grows 6–8 plants. Use peat-free multi-purpose compost; water consistently; thin to one per station. Container beetroot is sweeter than ground-grown because you control watering. Round varieties (Boltardy, Pablo F1) suit containers best."},
    {"question": "Are beetroot leaves edible?", "answer": "Yes — the leaves are essentially the same as chard (both are Beta vulgaris in different cultivar groups). Pick young leaves for salads; older leaves cook like spinach. Don't strip the plant though — the leaves are powering the root. Take an outer leaf or two per plant per week."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'beetroot';

-- From bergenia.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant bergenia in autumn or spring, in sun or part shade in almost any soil — it''s an exceptionally tough hardy evergreen perennial grown for the large rounded "elephant''s ear" leaves and dense clusters of pink, magenta, or white flowers in March–April. Bergenia is very hardy (RHS H7) and thrives in conditions that defeat most plants — including dry shade, poor soil, exposed sites, and coastal positions. The bold paddle-shaped leaves turn deep purple-red in winter (especially in colder positions), giving year-round structural interest. Bressingham White (white flowers, classic), Eric Smith (purple winter foliage), and Overture (compact, magenta flowers, RHS Award of Garden Merit) are popular UK choices. Cut spent flower stems off after blooming; remove tatty old leaves any time. Divide every 4–5 years.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick a bergenia for flower colour and foliage interest", "text": "Bressingham White: white flowers (rare in bergenia), glossy green leaves, the white-garden classic. Overture: deep magenta flowers, compact 30 cm, RHS Award of Garden Merit, the reliable modern choice. Eric Smith: pink flowers, leaves turn vivid purple-red in winter — outstanding winter foliage. Bressingham Ruby: deep ruby-red flowers, large leaves, vigorous. Silberlicht (Silver Light): white flowers ageing to pink, mid-sized. For a first bergenia: Overture — reliable, attractive, prize-winning. Eric Smith if you want maximum winter foliage colour."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in sun or part shade", "text": "Bergenias tolerate almost anything: full sun, part shade, deep shade, exposed sites, coastal positions, urban pollution. Best display: part shade with morning sun — flowers are biggest, foliage stays glossy. For winter foliage colour (the leaves turning purple-red): a position with some exposure to cold intensifies the colour change. Bergenia in deep warm shade stays green all winter."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in autumn or spring", "text": "Bergenia is tough but planting time matters. Autumn (September–October): establishes well before winter. Spring (March–April): most popular timing, plants are growing actively. Container-grown plants widely sold by garden centres in spring. Spacing: 40 cm apart — bergenia spreads slowly into ground-covering mats."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant in any soil", "text": "Bergenia's main virtue: tolerance of awful conditions. Heavy clay, dry chalk, poor sandy soil all work. Even dry shade under trees (where most plants fail) suits bergenia. The only soil it dislikes: waterlogged ground. No special preparation needed — bergenia thrives in plain garden soil with a token mulch of garden compost at planting."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Cut spent flower stems off after blooming", "text": "The main bergenia maintenance task. After flowering finishes in May, cut the flower stems off at the base with secateurs. This tidies the plant and prevents seed-set (which drains energy from leaf production). Don't cut foliage in spring beyond removing damaged old leaves — bergenia is evergreen and the leaves remain useful through summer."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Remove tatty old leaves anytime", "text": "Bergenia leaves are evergreen but individual leaves age over 2–3 years. Old leaves go brown at the edges, eventually turn entirely brown. Pull or cut these off at the base any time of year — they come away easily once they're past their useful life. Don't cut the whole plant down — fresh growth comes from the centre, and bergenia regrows slowly."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch winter foliage colour", "text": "The seasonal magic of bergenia. Through autumn and winter, the large paddle-shaped leaves of many varieties turn deep purple-red, burgundy, or wine — the colour intensified by frost. Eric Smith, Wintermärchen (Winter Fairy Tale), and Eden's Magic Giant are the best for winter colour. In spring, as new growth emerges, the older purple leaves gradually green again."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Divide every 4–5 years", "text": "Bergenia spreads slowly by underground rhizomes, gradually forming dense ground-covering mats. Every 4–5 years, in early autumn or early spring, lift the clump and divide with a sharp spade or knife into 4–6 sections. Replant the best outer pieces in fresh soil; compost the centre. Each section needs at least one growing point (the small dome at the crown) and roots. Free new plants for filling gaps."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Will bergenia grow in dry shade?", "answer": "Yes — bergenia is one of the few perennials that genuinely thrives in dry shade (the conditions under trees, beside walls, in north-facing rooty corners). The combination of dry + shade + poor soil defeats most plants but bergenia tolerates it without complaint. Best results: even in dry shade, mulch annually with garden compost and water in extreme drought; the plant survives without but grows more vigorously with basic help."},
    {"question": "Why are my bergenia leaves turning purple-red?", "answer": "Normal seasonal change. Many bergenia varieties have leaves that turn deep purple-red, burgundy, or wine in autumn and winter, often most intensely after frost. The colour change is a feature, not a problem — bergenia is grown for this winter foliage display as much as for the spring flowers. In spring, as new growth emerges, the older purple leaves gradually return to green. For maximum winter colour: choose varieties bred for it (Eric Smith, Wintermärchen, Eden's Magic Giant) and position with some cold exposure."},
    {"question": "When and how do I cut bergenia back?", "answer": "Minimal pruning needed. After flowering (May): cut the spent flower stems off at the base with secateurs. Any time of year: remove individual brown/tatty old leaves by pulling them off at the base (they come away easily once aged). Don't cut the whole plant down — bergenia regrows slowly from the centre and a complete cut-back sets the plant back a season. Every 4–5 years: lift, divide, replant fresh — the only major intervention bergenia needs."},
    {"question": "Why is bergenia called pigsqueak?", "answer": "A folk name from the leaf sound. When you rub a bergenia leaf between thumb and fingers, the slightly waxy surface squeaks — apparently like a pig. The name is more common in older UK gardening literature than in modern usage; elephant's ears (from the large rounded leaf shape) is the more popular contemporary common name. Both names refer to the same plant — Bergenia cordifolia and its hybrids."},
    {"question": "What's the best bergenia for the UK?", "answer": "For all-round reliability: Overture — RHS Award of Garden Merit, compact (30 cm), deep magenta flowers, vigorous, attractive. For winter foliage colour: Eric Smith — pink flowers, leaves turn vivid purple-red in winter. For white flowers: Bressingham White — classic for white-themed gardens. For deep colour: Bressingham Ruby — ruby-red flowers, vigorous, large-leaved. For dry shade ground cover: any large-leaved cultivar."},
    {"question": "Can I grow bergenia in pots?", "answer": "Yes — but pots aren't the obvious choice. Bergenia's chief virtue is its tolerance of difficult ground conditions; pots remove that advantage. A 30 cm pot of peat-free compost works fine, in part shade. Water more often than in-ground plants (pots dry quickly). Refresh top compost annually. Pot bergenia is useful for moving the winter foliage display closer to a doorstep or path, where the purple-red leaves can be appreciated. Repot every 3 years."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'bergenia';

-- From blackberry.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant blackberry canes bare-root from November to March, against a wall, fence, or sturdy post-and-wire system — modern cultivated varieties crop far more heavily than wild brambles and most are thornless. Blackberries are very hardy (RHS H6–H7), shrug off any UK winter, and one or two plants will give a household more fruit than they can eat from August to October. Train this year''s canes (the floricanes) to one side and next year''s growth (the primocanes) to the other so you can identify what to cut out after fruiting — that single rule prevents the bramble chaos most gardens fall into. Loch Ness, Loch Tay, and Triple Crown are the reliable thornless UK varieties; Bedford Giant is the heaviest cropper if you can tolerate thorns. Pick when berries are fully matt-black, never glossy.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose thornless or thorny", "text": "Thornless modern varieties are most UK gardeners' choice — easier to prune and pick, slightly less heavy-cropping than thorny equivalents. Loch Ness (the UK standard, semi-upright, fully thornless, late August–September). Loch Tay (earlier than Loch Ness, more compact). Triple Crown (huge berries, vigorous, late). Thorny varieties crop slightly heavier and often have better flavour: Bedford Giant (the heritage cropper), Ashton Cross (the wild bramble taste, refined). For one bush in a small garden, Loch Ness is the safe pick."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Build a training framework", "text": "Blackberries crop on two-year-old canes, so they need somewhere to go. Post-and-wire: three horizontal wires (60 cm, 90 cm, 120 cm) between sturdy posts, 3–4 m apart. Against a wall or fence: vine eyes and wire at the same heights. Tie the canes horizontally and at an angle as they grow — this is what separates a productive blackberry from a sprawling thicket."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March, while dormant. Set the rootball 5–7 cm below ground level. Cut the cane back to 25 cm at planting to encourage new growth from the base. Space 2.5–3 m apart for vigorous varieties; 2 m for compact ones like Loch Tay. They tolerate part shade — useful for a north or east wall."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Understand floricanes vs primocanes", "text": "The whole pruning system rests on this. Floricanes: last year's canes, which fruit this summer and then die. Primocanes: this year's new growth from the base, which will fruit next summer. A blackberry has both at once — old fruiting canes on one side of the wire system, new green canes on the other."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Train new canes to the unused side", "text": "As new canes (primocanes) emerge from the base from May onwards, train them to one side of the framework — tie them horizontally along the lower wires. The fruiting canes (floricanes) from last year occupy the other side. Keep them separate; the moment they tangle, the pruning advantage is lost."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Net against birds and squirrels from July", "text": "Blackbirds love blackberries. Net as the first berries colour from red to black in July. Squirrels will work harder than birds to defeat a net — tuck the mesh under and around posts. A walk-in fruit cage is the long-term answer if you have multiple soft fruit."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick when berries are matt-black", "text": "A truly ripe blackberry is matt-black with a slight bloom, never glossy. Glossy black means it's not fully ripe — the sugars haven't developed yet. The berry should pull off the white core with the lightest touch; if you tug, leave it another day or two. Pick over the plant every 2–3 days from late July through to early October."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Cut out fruited canes after harvest", "text": "Late September–October. Cut every floricane (the wood that just fruited) right down to the base. This is the entire pruning of a blackberry — drastic but mechanical. The new primocanes from this season then take over the framework for next year's crop. Tie them in tidily, shorten any whippy ends to 1.8 m. Done for the year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When are blackberries ready to pick?", "answer": "Late July to early October, depending on variety. The berry must be fully matt-black with a dusty bloom, never glossy. Glossy means not fully ripe — pick at that stage and it's tart and watery. A truly ripe berry pulls off the white core with no resistance; if you tug, it's not ready. Pick every 2–3 days through the season — berries ripen unevenly across each cluster."},
    {"question": "How do I prune a blackberry?", "answer": "One job, in late September or October: cut out every cane that fruited this summer (the floricanes) at the base. This year's new green canes (primocanes) become next year's fruiting wood. Tie them in tidily, shorten any over-long ones. That's the whole pruning system — there's no winter or summer pruning beyond this single cut-back after harvest."},
    {"question": "What's the best thornless blackberry variety?", "answer": "Loch Ness — the UK standard. Semi-upright (easier to train), fully thornless, very heavy cropping, late August through September, excellent flavour. For an earlier crop, Loch Tay (3 weeks earlier than Loch Ness, smaller bush). For huge berries, Triple Crown (vigorous, late, big bowls of jam). For deep flavour and tolerance of awkward sites, Reuben (a primocane-fruiting variety — crops on this year's wood, prune to ground every winter)."},
    {"question": "Can I grow a blackberry in a container?", "answer": "Not really — they're too vigorous. A blackberry wants 2–3 m of horizontal space and roots that can run. A compact variety like Loch Tay might survive in a 60 cm pot for a season or two but will give a fraction of the in-ground crop. For container-friendly cane fruit, plant raspberries or a hybrid like loganberry instead."},
    {"question": "How do I stop blackberries taking over the garden?", "answer": "Tip-rooting is the issue — the long arching primocanes will root where they touch the ground and form new plants. Two preventatives. (1) Train all canes onto the wire framework; never let cane tips trail on soil. (2) In autumn, walk the row and cut off the tip of any primocane that's near touching ground. If they've already rooted, dig out the new plants in winter or transplant them to fill gaps."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between a blackberry and a wild bramble?", "answer": "Same species (Rubus fruticosus agg.) — cultivated varieties are selections from the wild form, bred for bigger berries, better flavour, fewer or no thorns, and more compact growth. Wild brambles crop fine but with small thorny canes and inconsistent berry size. A cultivated Loch Ness will give you 5–10× the crop of a wild bramble in the same space, with zero thorns to navigate."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'blackberry';

-- From blackcurrant.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant blackcurrant bushes from November to March, bare-root, in deep moist soil with plenty of compost dug in. Blackcurrants are very hardy (RHS H6–H7) and one of the few soft fruits that genuinely thrive in the wetter, cooler parts of the UK. Plant 1.5 m apart, set the crown 5 cm below the soil surface to encourage strong new shoots from the base. Modern Ben varieties (Ben Hope, Ben Lomond, Ben Sarek) resist big-bud mite and don''t need a pollinator. Prune by cutting a third of the oldest stems to ground level each winter — fruit grows best on 2–3 year old wood. Net firmly against birds in June–July. A single mature bush gives 4–5 kg of fruit a year.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose modern Ben varieties", "text": "Ben Hope, Ben Lomond, Ben Sarek (compact, for small gardens), Ben Connan. All bred by the James Hutton Institute in Scotland for UK conditions; all resist big-bud mite and reversion virus; all self-fertile."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Full sun or light shade. Deep, fertile, moisture-retentive soil. Fork in well-rotted manure or compost."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant", "text": "November to March, bare-root. Dig a hole twice the rootball width. Set the crown 5 cm below the soil surface — this encourages multiple shoots from the base (stooling), which is what you want. Water in well."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Prune hard in year one", "text": "Cut all stems back to 5 cm after planting. This forces the plant to produce strong basal shoots in its first summer, the foundation of all future cropping."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Annual winter prune", "text": "Each winter (December to February), cut a third of the oldest stems (look black and woody) to ground level. New growth bears the heaviest crop on 2–3 year old wood. Open the centre of the bush for air circulation."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Mulch annually", "text": "Each March, top up with a 5 cm mulch of well-rotted compost or leaf mould. Blackcurrants are gross feeders and benefit from annual feeding."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Net against birds", "text": "From late May until harvest finishes. Use bird netting on a frame at least 30 cm above the foliage. Birds will strip a bush of every ripening berry given the chance."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick whole strigs", "text": "A strig is the cluster of berries on a single stem. Pick the whole strig once most berries are black and ripe; strip the berries off in the kitchen. Pick a single bush over 2–3 visits as the berries ripen progressively."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant blackcurrants in the UK?", "answer": "November to March, bare-root, while the bushes are dormant. Bare-root plants are cheaper and establish better than pot-grown. Plant on a frost-free day with the soil workable. If your soil is frozen or waterlogged, heel the plants in to a temporary trench until conditions improve."},
    {"question": "How do I prune a blackcurrant bush?", "answer": "Aim for an open-centred bush with shoots of mixed ages — 1, 2, and 3 years old. Each winter, cut a third of the oldest stems (look black and woody) to ground level. Keep the youngest, strongest shoots from this year's growth (look pale and smooth). This rotating renewal keeps the bush cropping heavily for 15+ years."},
    {"question": "My blackcurrant bush has small leaves and few berries — what's wrong?", "answer": "Possibly reversion virus, especially in older bushes (pre-1990 cultivars). Carried by big-bud mite. There's no cure — dig up affected bushes and replace with modern Ben varieties, which resist both. Other causes: lack of feeding (mulch annually with compost), poor drainage, or too much shade."},
    {"question": "Do I need to plant two blackcurrants for cross-pollination?", "answer": "No, modern blackcurrants are self-fertile — one bush will crop fully on its own. Plant a second only if you want more fruit. A single Ben Hope or Ben Lomond gives 4–5 kg of fruit at maturity."},
    {"question": "How long do blackcurrant bushes last?", "answer": "15+ years if well-pruned, well-fed, and free of reversion virus. Modern Ben varieties last longest. Replace bushes only when productivity drops below worthwhile levels, not on a schedule."},
    {"question": "How do I store a glut of blackcurrants?", "answer": "Freeze on trays then bag — they freeze better than most soft fruit, keeping shape and flavour for 12+ months. Blackcurrant jam, cordial, and cassis are the traditional preserves. They're also excellent in cooking with game and rich meats. The high vitamin C and pectin content make them one of the most useful preserved fruits."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'blackcurrant';

-- From bluebell.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant English bluebell bulbs "in the green" in spring (March–April with active foliage) or as dry bulbs in autumn (September–November) — they''re the iconic UK woodland flower, hardy native species (RHS H6–H7) that carpets ancient deciduous woods in violet-blue in late April and May. Don''t plant Spanish bluebell (Hyacinthoides hispanica) — the invasive imported species that hybridises with and threatens the UK native. Spanish and hybrid bluebells have upright flower stems and bells around the stem all sides; English bluebells nod to one side with bells hanging down one face of the stem, and have a sweet scent that hybrids lack. All English bluebells are legally protected from wild digging under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 — buy from reputable wildflower nurseries. Naturalises beautifully under deciduous trees.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Make sure you have English bluebells, not Spanish", "text": "Critical identification — the conservation issue. English bluebell (Hyacinthoides non-scripta, the UK native): stem nods to one side with bells hanging down one face; strong sweet scent; narrow leaves; deep violet-blue (occasionally white); pollen cream-white. Spanish bluebell (Hyacinthoides hispanica, invasive non-native): upright stem with bells around all sides; no scent; broader leaves; paler blue; pollen blue. Hybrid bluebells (H. × massartiana): intermediate, vigorous, the most invasive — threatens UK native populations by cross-pollinating. Many bluebells sold by garden centres are Spanish or hybrid — buy by Latin name from a reputable wildflower nursery (Naturescape, BBC Wild Flower Shop) for the English native."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Buy from a reputable wildflower nursery", "text": "The Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 prohibits digging up wild English bluebells. Specialist wildflower nurseries (Naturescape, BBC Wild Flower Shop, Plantlife, Crocus) sell propagated English bluebell bulbs and in the green plants. Don't buy from cheap supermarket bulb sales without confirming the species — these are often Spanish or hybrid bluebells."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in autumn or in the green in spring", "text": "Dry bulbs: plant September–November, 10 cm deep, pointed end up. Bulbs desiccate quickly — plant immediately on arrival. In the green: plant March–April (just after flowering, with active foliage attached) at the same depth the bulbs were originally growing. In the green plants establish better than dry bulbs."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant in damp, part to deep shade", "text": "English bluebells are deep-shade woodland plants — they thrive under deciduous trees where they get spring sunlight before canopy closes, then summer shade. Best position: under oak, beech, hazel trees; alongside shaded north-facing walls; in damp leafy soil. Soil: moisture-retentive, humus-rich, slightly acid. Don't plant in full sun — bluebells in sun are short-lived and lose vigour."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Let them naturalise undisturbed", "text": "English bluebells spread slowly by both seed and bulb division. An established colony should be left undisturbed for years — bluebells don't like being moved. Naturalisation takes 5–10 years from an initial planting to a noticeable drift, and 20+ years for a proper carpet. The investment is generational — but the result is one of the great UK garden displays."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Don't cut foliage early — same as daffodils", "text": "The bluebell rule is the bulb rule: leave leaves for 6+ weeks after flowering. English bluebell foliage dies back naturally in June or early July. Don't mow or strim any naturalised area until then. In borders: simply leave the yellowing leaves alone, tuck under emerging summer perennials if they look tatty."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Don't compost the foliage — bin it", "text": "Slight risk of spreading hybrid genes. English bluebell foliage may produce viable seed even after the flower stem appears spent. For genuine UK-native conservation gardens, bin the cut foliage (or compost it in a separate heap away from wild areas) rather than letting it disperse. For ordinary gardens, compost normally — the risk is minor outside conservation contexts."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Help the conservation effort", "text": "UK native bluebell populations are declining due to habitat loss and cross-pollination with Spanish bluebells. In your garden: (1) Plant only English bluebells from reputable suppliers. (2) Remove Spanish bluebells if you find them — dig out bulbs and dispose in bin (not compost) to prevent regrowth and cross-pollination. (3) Spread the word about Spanish vs English ID. (4) Support conservation charities (Plantlife). UK gardens hold a meaningful share of the world's English bluebell genetic diversity."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I tell English bluebells from Spanish?", "answer": "Three key tests. (1) Stem shape: English bluebell stems nod to one side with bells hanging down one face; Spanish bluebell stems are upright with bells around all sides. (2) Scent: English bluebells have a strong sweet honey-like scent; Spanish bluebells are scentless. (3) Pollen colour: cream-white in English; blue in Spanish. Hybrid bluebells (most common in UK gardens) are intermediate — slightly nodding, weakly scented, varied. Buy by Latin name (Hyacinthoides non-scripta) from a wildflower nursery to be sure."},
    {"question": "Are Spanish bluebells bad?", "answer": "Yes — they threaten UK native bluebell populations. Spanish bluebells (Hyacinthoides hispanica) escape gardens, naturalise into wild bluebell woods, and cross-pollinate with the native English bluebell producing fertile hybrids. The hybrids are more vigorous than the natives and gradually replace them. UK conservation guidance: remove Spanish bluebells from gardens within a few miles of native bluebell woods (most of the UK qualifies). Dig out the bulbs, bin (don't compost), replace with English bluebells from a reputable wildflower nursery."},
    {"question": "When do I plant bluebells?", "answer": "Dry bulbs: September to November, before soil cools, 10 cm deep. In the green plants: March to April (just after flowering, with active foliage attached) — establish more reliably than dry bulbs. Best windows in either case: avoid summer planting (drought stress) and deep winter (frozen soil). For mass naturalising, dry bulbs are more economical; for guaranteed establishment, in the green is more reliable."},
    {"question": "Will bluebells naturalise in my lawn?", "answer": "Better under trees than in open lawn. English bluebells are deep-shade woodland specialists — they thrive under deciduous trees where they get spring sunlight before canopy closes, then summer shade. In open lawns: too sunny, the plants are short-lived. Best for naturalising: damp shady corners under oak, beech, hazel trees, or against north-facing walls. The result, over 10–20 years: a spreading drift that gradually carpets the area."},
    {"question": "Why aren't my bluebells flowering?", "answer": "Three common causes. (1) Too sunny: English bluebells are deep-shade plants; in sunny positions they're short-lived and flower poorly. Move to a shadier spot. (2) Too dry: bluebells need moisture-retentive soil. Mulch with leaf-mould; water in extreme drought. (3) Foliage cut too early: same rule as daffodils — leaves need 6+ weeks after flowering. Patience also matters: newly-planted bluebells often take 2–3 years to settle and flower properly."},
    {"question": "Can I grow bluebells in pots?", "answer": "Possible but not ideal. English bluebells want naturalising conditions (deep shade, undisturbed soil, leaf-mould mulch year after year) — pots restrict this. A 30 cm pot of leaf-mould-enriched compost can hold 10–15 bulbs for a year or two; after that, performance declines. For long-term bluebells: plant in the garden under deciduous trees. Pot bluebells are better thought of as a short-term display ahead of garden planting."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'bluebell';

-- From blueberry.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant blueberry bushes in ericaceous compost in containers, or in soil only if it tests acid (pH 4.5–5.5) — most UK gardens have neutral or alkaline soil where blueberries simply will not thrive. Blueberries are hardy (RHS H5–H6) and crop reliably for 20+ years if soil chemistry is right. Plant two different cultivars for proper cross-pollination and a heavier crop. Water with rainwater wherever possible — UK tap water is alkaline and gradually pushes the soil out of the acid range. Net the ripening fruit against blackbirds from late June. Pick when each berry pulls off the stalk with the lightest touch — that''s the only fully-ripe test. Container culture in 40 cm pots of ericaceous compost is the practical UK approach for most gardens.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Test your soil first (or skip to containers)", "text": "Blueberries need soil pH 4.5–5.5 — sharply acid. Most UK gardens are pH 6.5–7.5, which kills blueberries slowly through iron deficiency. Buy a £5 soil pH meter or kit. If your soil isn't acid, don't fight it — grow blueberries in pots of ericaceous compost."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose 40 cm containers and ericaceous compost", "text": "For non-acid soils, a 40 cm (or larger) pot of peat-free ericaceous compost per bush. Pots dry out quickly — bigger is better. Plastic pots hold moisture better than terracotta. Drainage holes essential — blueberries hate waterlogging but also wilt the moment they dry."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pick two different cultivars", "text": "Most blueberries crop more heavily with a pollination partner of a different variety. Reliable UK pairings: Bluecrop (mid-season) + Patriot (early), or Duke (early) + Chandler (late). For container gardens, Top Hat is a dwarf form that pairs well with any standard cultivar. Some are listed as self-fertile but still crop better in a pair."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant the bushes", "text": "Container-grown blueberries plant any time of year; bare-root only from November to March. Set the rootball at the same depth as it was in the pot. Firm gently — blueberries are shallow-rooted, don't compact. Water in with rainwater."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water with rainwater only (if you can)", "text": "UK tap water is alkaline. Watering with tap water gradually raises the pH of pot compost and starves the blueberry of iron, leading to yellow leaves with green veins (lime-induced chlorosis). Collect rainwater in a butt and use it for every watering. In a long drought, tap water is better than letting the plant wilt — switch back to rainwater as soon as you can."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Feed with ericaceous fertiliser in spring", "text": "A handful of ericaceous slow-release fertiliser around the base in April, top up in early June. Never use general-purpose feed — it's the wrong nutrient ratio and often makes the compost less acid."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Net against birds before berries ripen", "text": "Blackbirds will strip a bush in a single morning the day the first berries turn blue. Drape fine mesh netting over the bush from late June, or build a fruit cage. Tuck the mesh under at the base — blackbirds find any gap."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Prune from year 3 onwards", "text": "First two years: leave it alone, let the bush establish. From year 3: in February, remove the oldest, thickest, darkest stems at the base (one or two each year). Keep the bush full of younger, more vigorous wood — that's what carries the heaviest crops. Never give it a haircut."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why aren't my blueberries fruiting?", "answer": "Two usual causes. Wrong soil pH: the leaves yellow with green veins and the plant sulks — get a pH meter and confirm the soil or compost is in the 4.5–5.5 range. Single bush, no pollination partner: most blueberries crop much better with a different cultivar nearby. Even self-fertile varieties (like Bluecrop) crop more heavily in a pair. A third possibility: nets gone on late and birds have eaten the developing fruit before you noticed."},
    {"question": "Can I grow blueberries in regular UK garden soil?", "answer": "Only if your soil tests acid (pH 4.5–5.5). Naturally acid soils in the UK are mostly limited to heath, moor, and woodland edges — Surrey heath, parts of Hampshire, the New Forest, much of Wales and west Scotland. Everywhere else, growing blueberries in the ground fails within 2–3 years as the plant runs out of iron. Containers of ericaceous compost are the practical answer for almost every UK garden."},
    {"question": "How many blueberry bushes do I need?", "answer": "Two minimum, of different cultivars, for proper cross-pollination and the heaviest crop. Three bushes gives you a longer picking window if you pick an early, mid, and late variety (e.g. Duke + Bluecrop + Chandler). A single bush will set some fruit but never as much as a pair."},
    {"question": "When do I prune a blueberry bush?", "answer": "February, from year 3 onwards. Younger bushes don't need pruning. Once established, take out one or two of the oldest, darkest, thickest stems at the base each February — the goal is to keep the bush full of younger wood that crops heavily. Never trim like a hedge; the fruit forms on the previous year's growth, so a haircut removes the entire crop."},
    {"question": "What's the best blueberry variety for UK gardens?", "answer": "Bluecrop is the reliable workhorse — vigorous, heavy-cropping, mid-season, hardy. Pair it with Patriot (earlier, large berries) or Duke (very early). For container growing or small spaces, Top Hat is a true dwarf (60 cm). Chandler has the biggest berries but ripens late. Avoid southern highbush varieties (rabbiteye types) — they need more summer heat than the UK provides."},
    {"question": "My blueberry leaves are yellow with green veins — what's wrong?", "answer": "Lime-induced chlorosis: the soil or compost has drifted alkaline and the plant can't take up iron. Three fixes, do all of them. (1) Switch to rainwater only. (2) Apply sequestered iron (sold as Sequestrene or similar) according to the pack. (3) If in a pot, top-dress with fresh ericaceous compost; in ground, fork in pine bark mulch and pine needle mulch to acidify. Recovery takes a season."},
    {"question": "When are blueberries ready to pick?", "answer": "July to September, depending on variety. The berry must be fully blue (no red shoulders), slightly soft, and most importantly should pull off the stalk with no resistance. If you have to tug, leave it another few days. A truly ripe blueberry has the sweetness; an under-ripe one tastes thin and tart. Pick over the bush every 3–4 days as berries ripen unevenly across the cluster."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'blueberry';

-- From borage.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow borage direct from April to June, in any sunny spot in ordinary garden soil — it''s one of the easiest hardy annuals to grow, with bristly cucumber-flavoured leaves and striking sky-blue star-shaped flowers from June through to October. Borage is reasonably hardy (RHS H4) and self-seeds prolifically: once you have one borage, you have borage for ever. The flowers are extraordinary for bees — bumblebees in particular work them obsessively — and edible (a Pimm''s classic, scattered on salads, frozen in ice cubes). The young leaves taste of cucumber and can be added to summer drinks and salads in small amounts, though the bristles make them rough on the tongue. Don''t transplant — long taproot resents disturbance, sow direct. Eat in moderation; borage contains low levels of pyrrolizidine alkaloids that can affect the liver in large quantities.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct, not in modules", "text": "Borage has a long taproot and resents transplanting. Sow seed direct where you want the plants to grow, in April–June. Germination in 7–14 days. If you must start indoors, use deep root-trainer cells and plant out before the roots reach the bottom."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pick a permanent home — you can't move borage", "text": "A sunny spot with ordinary soil. Borage tolerates poor soils, light shade, and dry conditions; it doesn't tolerate waterlogging. Spacing 45 cm — it grows to 60–90 cm tall and just as wide, and can flop sideways in rich soil or wind."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Accept that it will self-seed for years", "text": "A single borage plant produces hundreds of viable seeds and the next year's seedlings appear everywhere. Two strategies. (1) Welcome it — just weed out unwanted seedlings in spring and let the plant become a permanent semi-wild presence. (2) Cut flower heads off before seed sets (late September) — this drastically reduces but doesn't eliminate self-seeding."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Stake taller plants in exposed sites", "text": "On rich soil or in windy gardens, borage can flop sideways and look untidy. A pea-stake or grow-through plant support placed early lets the plant grow up through it and stay upright. In a wild corner, let it flop — it's part of the look."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick young leaves sparingly", "text": "Cucumber-flavoured but with bristly hairs that make the leaves rough on the tongue. Pick young leaves (the older ones get really hairy) and use sparingly: a few finely chopped in a summer salad, or whole in Pimm's. Avoid large quantities — see Step 8."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Use the flowers", "text": "The main reason most UK gardeners grow borage. The sky-blue star flowers are edible and ornamental — float on Pimm's, freeze whole inside ice cubes for cocktails and elderflower cordial, scatter on salads, candy with egg white and caster sugar for cake decoration. Pick whole flowers in the morning when fully open."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Let bees do the work", "text": "Borage is one of the top three UK garden plants for honey-bee and bumblebee forage. The flowers refill with nectar every two minutes, so a borage patch is in constant motion through summer. Even one plant tucked at the back of a veg garden upgrades pollination across nearby crops (tomatoes, beans, courgettes especially)."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Eat in moderation", "text": "Borage contains low levels of pyrrolizidine alkaloids — compounds that, in large quantities, can affect the liver. Small culinary use (a few young leaves, a handful of flowers) is traditional and fine for healthy adults. Avoid or use minimally: pregnant women, anyone with liver conditions, children. Don't take borage as a daily medicinal tea — that's the higher-dose use where the alkaloid issue applies."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why does my borage flop sideways?", "answer": "Three usual causes. (1) Rich soil — borage gets lush and floppy in heavy feeding. It actually grows tighter and more upright on poor soils. (2) Wind in an exposed garden — stake or grow through a plant support placed early. (3) End of season — old borage plants get long and sprawling after 4 months; cut them back to 15 cm in mid-summer for a second flush of compact growth, or just let them go to seed and rely on the next generation."},
    {"question": "Are borage flowers really edible?", "answer": "Yes — and they're one of the classic edible UK garden flowers. The sky-blue star-shaped flowers have a faint cucumber flavour and are completely safe in normal culinary quantities. Float on Pimm's (the traditional use), freeze whole inside ice cubes, scatter on salads, candy with caster sugar for cake decoration. The whole flower including the dark central cluster is edible — pick fully-open flowers in the morning when nectar is at its peak."},
    {"question": "How do I stop borage taking over my garden?", "answer": "Cut flower heads off before seeds set — late September is the critical window. A single un-deadheaded borage produces hundreds of seedlings. Even with diligent deadheading, some seeds will escape, so weed out unwanted seedlings in spring (they're easy to pull when small). For total control, dig out the parent plant before any flowers set seed — its long taproot needs the spade. Most UK gardeners accept a permanent borage presence and just manage the population by weeding seedlings each spring."},
    {"question": "Will borage survive a UK winter?", "answer": "No — it's an annual. Each plant lives one season (April-sown plants die in October, autumn-sown plants overwinter as small rosettes and bolt the following spring). But borage self-seeds so prolifically that the colony perpetuates indefinitely without any work from you. Hardy enough as seedlings: autumn-germinated seedlings survive most UK winters as small rosettes and flower earlier the following year than spring-sown plants."},
    {"question": "Can I dry borage flowers?", "answer": "Not really — they lose their colour and structure fast. The sky-blue fades to dusty mauve within days of picking. Better preservation methods: freeze whole flowers in ice cubes (perfect for cocktails), or candy with egg white and caster sugar (which sets the colour and the flower shape). Fresh flowers keep 1–2 days in a sealed container in the fridge."},
    {"question": "Is it true borage was the herb of courage?", "answer": "Yes — historic herbal lore, from at least the Greek period. Borage was traditionally added to wine to dispel melancholy and give courage; the phrase borage for courage appears in herbals from the Roman to the Tudor era. Modern science doesn't strongly support a specific stimulant effect, but borage is mildly diuretic and contains trace amounts of gamma-linolenic acid (GLA), with some adaptogenic claims. The courage reputation likely came from the cooling, refreshing effect on a hot day combined with wine."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'borage';

-- From broad-bean.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow broad bean seed in November for an early crop the following May, or in February to March for a summer crop. Autumn-sown beans need a hardy cultivar like Aquadulce Claudia; spring-sown can be anything. Broad beans are very hardy (RHS H4–H5) and shrug off a UK winter as small plants. They want a sunny spot with fertile soil; plant in double rows 20 cm between plants. Pinch the growing tip once the first beans set — that''s the part blackfly want, and removing it deters the worst of an infestation. Harvest from late May (autumn-sown) or late June (spring-sown), picking when the pods feel full but the beans inside are still bright green.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "November for autumn crop (Aquadulce Claudia or The Sutton only); February to mid-March for spring crop. 5 cm deep, 20 cm apart in double rows 25 cm apart."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Protect young seedlings (autumn sowings only)", "text": "Once 5 cm tall they overwinter outdoors fine in most of the UK. In exposed gardens or very cold winters, cover with a cloche or fleece. They want air and don't need warmth."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pinch out the blackfly target", "text": "Once the first beans set in May or June, pinch out the top 7 cm of each growing shoot. The tender tip is what blackfly want. Removing it deters infestation and pushes the plant's energy into beans."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Support", "text": "In a windy site, run two parallel strings 30 cm and 60 cm above the row, tied to canes at each end. The plants lean on the string and don't break."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water if dry", "text": "Water deeply at flowering. Drought at flowering reduces the pod count."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Harvest", "text": "Pick when pods are firm but the beans inside are still bright green and smaller than your thumbnail. Older beans get tough and floury. The pods snap rather than tear."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Should I sow broad beans in autumn or spring?", "answer": "Autumn-sown beans (November, hardy cultivars only) crop two to three weeks earlier than spring-sown, get going before blackfly arrives, and are out of the ground in time for a summer succession crop. Spring sowings are faster to set up and avoid the slug and waterlogging risks of overwintering plants. Most UK allotments do both."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between Aquadulce and other broad bean varieties?", "answer": "Aquadulce Claudia is the hardiest UK variety, bred specifically for autumn sowing — it shrugs off our winters as small plants. Bunyards Exhibition and Masterpiece Green Longpod are for spring sowing — bigger pods, sweeter beans, but less winter-hardy. The Sutton is a dwarf variety (45 cm) for windy sites and small gardens."},
    {"question": "How do I get rid of blackfly on broad beans?", "answer": "Pinch out the top 7 cm of each shoot once the first pods set. The tender tip is what blackfly want, and removing it is enough on its own most years. For a bad infestation, a spray of soapy water (a teaspoon of washing-up liquid in a litre of water) on a calm dry day knocks them back. Ladybirds and hoverflies eat them in volume."},
    {"question": "When can I pick broad beans?", "answer": "Late May to mid-July for autumn-sown crops; late June to August for spring-sown. Pick when pods are firm and full, but the beans inside are still bright green and no larger than your thumbnail. Older beans turn floury. The first pods at the base of the plant ripen first; work upwards over a couple of weeks of picking."},
    {"question": "Can I eat the whole pod?", "answer": "Very young broad bean pods, picked at 8–10 cm long before the beans inside are visible, can be cooked whole like french beans. After that the pod gets stringy and you want the beans alone. Pick the youngest pods straight from the plant and steam for 4–5 minutes."},
    {"question": "What can I do with the spent plants?", "answer": "Cut the plants at the base, leaving the roots in the ground. Broad bean roots fix nitrogen — they release it slowly as they decompose, feeding whatever follows. Top growth goes on the compost heap or as a green mulch around brassicas."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'broad-bean';

-- From broccoli.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow calabrese (the supermarket-standard broccoli) from March to June for summer to autumn cropping; transplant 30 cm apart in firm fertile soil. Broccoli is hardy (RHS H5) and reliable in UK conditions but heads bolt quickly in heat — successional sowings every 3 weeks keep the supply going. Cut the central head when buds are still tightly closed; side-shoots crop on for weeks afterwards if you keep picking. Net firmly against cabbage white butterflies through summer. Purple sprouting broccoli (the overwintering type, different cultivar group) is hardier (H6), sown in May and cropping the following February to April when little else is in season. Net pigeons in winter for sprouting types.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose the type", "text": "Calabrese (Marathon, Belstar): the standard green broccoli, large central head with smaller side-shoots, summer to autumn cropping. Purple sprouting (Red Spear, Rudolph, Cardinal): overwintering, no large head, many small purple-budded side-shoots, cropping February to April. Different sowing schedules — pick one type per bed."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "Calabrese: March to June in modules at 12–15°C; or direct from April. Successional sowings every 3 weeks for continuous summer–autumn cropping. Sprouting broccoli: May to early June only, for cropping the following spring."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prepare a firm fertile bed", "text": "Full sun, firm soil, slightly alkaline (lime if pH below 6.5). Fork in compost from the previous autumn but not fresh manure. Like Brussels sprouts, broccoli needs a settled bed."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Transplant when 10–15 cm tall", "text": "30 cm apart for calabrese, 60 cm for sprouting types (they get bigger). Firm the soil hard around each plant; water in well. Don't transplant in hot dry weather; choose a damp morning."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Net against cabbage white", "text": "From transplanting until October. Caterpillars strip a plant overnight. Net firmly on hoops with 5 mm enviromesh."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water and feed", "text": "Water deeply once a week in dry weather; broccoli stops heading if it dries out. A liquid feed in mid-summer keeps it going."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut central head when tight", "text": "For calabrese, cut the central head when the buds are still tightly closed and dark green. Once flower buds open into yellow flowers it's gone over. Cut 10 cm of stem; side-shoots will produce smaller heads from the leaf joints for 4–8 weeks afterwards."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick sprouting broccoli shoots continuously", "text": "For purple sprouting, cut shoots when the buds are still tight, from late February onwards. Cut at the base of each shoot just above a leaf joint. Picking is continuous through to April; the more you cut, the more it produces."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Re-net for pigeons over winter (sprouting types only)", "text": "Sprouting broccoli stands through winter. From October pigeons strip unprotected plants. Net with proper bird netting on hoops 30 cm above the foliage."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow broccoli in the UK?", "answer": "Calabrese (the standard green broccoli): March to June in modules; successional sowings every 3 weeks. Crops from July to October. Purple sprouting broccoli: May to early June only — these are biennials that crop the following February to April. Don't mix the timings; the two types are essentially different crops with different sowing windows."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between calabrese and purple sprouting broccoli?", "answer": "Both are Brassica oleracea var. italica but in distinct cultivar groups. Calabrese: one big central head, summer-autumn cropping, the supermarket standard. Purple sprouting: many small purple-budded shoots, late winter–early spring cropping (February–April), the allotment treasure. Sprouting types are hardier (H6 vs H5), much longer to mature (10+ months), but crop when nothing else is in season."},
    {"question": "My broccoli flowered before I could pick it — can I still eat it?", "answer": "The buds are edible up to the moment they open into yellow flowers; once flowering, the heads turn bitter and stringy. Catch heads before flowering by checking daily once they look full — the window is 2–3 days, easy to miss in a hot week. If the central head bolted, leave the plant — side-shoots from leaf joints often produce smaller usable heads."},
    {"question": "Why are my broccoli heads tiny?", "answer": "Most common causes: planted too close (give them 30 cm calabrese, 60 cm sprouting), water stress (broccoli stops heading in drought), nutrient-poor soil, or a calabrese variety unsuited to the UK climate. Choose F1 hybrids bred for UK conditions; water deeply through July–August; feed mid-summer."},
    {"question": "When can I pick purple sprouting broccoli?", "answer": "Late February to mid-April from a sowing the previous May. Cut shoots when buds are still tightly closed and purple. Continuous picking — the more you cut, the more shoots form. A single plant gives 6–8 weeks of picking. Cropping ends when the plant runs out of energy and bolts."},
    {"question": "How do I stop caterpillars on broccoli?", "answer": "Net firmly with 5 mm enviromesh on hoops from transplanting through October. Cabbage white butterflies lay tiny yellow eggs in clusters on the undersides of leaves; once hatched the caterpillars eat fast. Inspect weekly for eggs and rub them off. Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) spray is an organic-approved last resort."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'broccoli';

-- From brussels-sprouts.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow Brussels sprout seed from March to April indoors or in a seedbed; transplant 60 cm apart in firm fertile soil from June to July. Brussels sprouts are very hardy (RHS H5–H6) and one of the longest-season UK crops — sown in March you''re picking from October through to March the following year. Frost actually sweetens the sprouts; pre-frost picks taste rough. Net against cabbage white butterflies through summer and again against pigeons through winter. Stake firmly at planting — mature plants are top-heavy in autumn gales. Pick from the bottom up as sprouts mature; the leafy top stays on the plant until November to power growth.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "March to April. Indoors in modules at 12–15°C, or direct in a seedbed once soil is workable. Sow thinly; germination in 1–2 weeks."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare a firm fertile bed", "text": "Full sun, firm soil — Brussels sprouts hate loose ground because they topple later in life. Tread the bed firm; rake to a fine tilth. Brassicas prefer slightly alkaline soil (pH 6.5–7.0); lime if your soil is below 6.5."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Transplant when pencil-thick", "text": "June to July, when seedlings are 15–20 cm tall with 4–6 true leaves. Plant 60 cm apart in rows 75 cm apart. Bury the stem deeper than it was in the seedbed for stability. Firm the soil hard around each plant."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Net against cabbage white", "text": "From transplanting through October. Cabbage whites lay eggs on any brassica; caterpillars can strip a plant overnight. Use 5 mm enviromesh or fine butterfly netting on hoops."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Stake firmly", "text": "Tall varieties (Trafalgar, Diablo) reach 1 m and become top-heavy as sprouts develop. Bury the stem deep at transplant; tie to a stout cane from autumn onwards in exposed sites. Rocking damages the roots and reduces sprout size."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Don't pinch the top until November", "text": "The leafy top of the plant powers sprout development — keep it intact until at least November. Pinching the top earlier (topping) forces sprouts to mature simultaneously, which suits commercial growers but loses the long pick."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick from the bottom up", "text": "Sprouts mature first at the base of the stem. Snap the bottom sprouts off with a sharp downward twist when they're walnut-sized and tight. Work up the stem over weeks of picking. After cold frosts the flavour transforms — pre-frost sprouts are coarse; post-frost they're sweet."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Re-net for pigeons in winter", "text": "From October onwards, pigeons strip exposed brassicas. Re-tension or replace netting against pigeon damage. The cabbage white risk drops as butterflies die off; pigeon pressure peaks January–February."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow Brussels sprouts in the UK?", "answer": "March to April. Earlier (February) sowings tend to bolt; later (May) sowings don't have time to mature decent sprouts before winter. Transplant the seedlings to their final position in June or July when they're pencil-thick."},
    {"question": "Why are my Brussels sprouts loose and blown rather than tight?", "answer": "Loose soil at planting time. Brussels sprouts need a firm, settled bed — they're top-heavy plants that rock in wind, and any movement at the roots produces blown (loose, open-leaved) sprouts. Tread the bed firm before transplanting; firm the soil hard around each plant; stake from autumn onwards."},
    {"question": "Do Brussels sprouts really taste better after frost?", "answer": "Yes, scientifically. Frost triggers the plant to convert starches to sugars as an antifreeze response, sweetening the sprouts dramatically. Pre-frost sprouts (September) tend to taste rough and earthy; post-frost (November onwards) they're sweet. The reason Christmas sprouts always taste better than September ones."},
    {"question": "When do I pick Brussels sprouts?", "answer": "From October when the lower sprouts are walnut-sized and tight; pick through to March the following year. Snap the bottom sprouts off first; work upwards over weeks. A single plant gives 30–50 sprouts spread over 4–5 months of picking — far better value than a single-harvest commercial-style crop."},
    {"question": "What's the best variety for the UK?", "answer": "For uniform commercial-style crops, F1 hybrids (Diablo, Trafalgar, Brigitte) give predictable results. For better flavour and a longer picking window, heritage varieties (Bedford Fillbasket, Long Island Improved) crop over a wider date range with variable sprout size. Most allotment growers split between an F1 (reliability) and a heritage cultivar (flavour)."},
    {"question": "My sprouts have aphids inside them — what do I do?", "answer": "Mealy cabbage aphid (Brevicoryne brassicae) gets between the leaves of developing sprouts. Net against butterflies as standard; that doesn't stop aphids. For severe infestations, hose down the plants weekly with a soft soap spray (a teaspoon of washing-up liquid in a litre of water). Encouraging hoverflies and ladybirds (plant flowering dill, coriander, fennel near the brassica bed) is the long-term defence."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'brussels-sprouts';

-- From cabbage.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow cabbage seed across four overlapping seasons for year-round harvest: spring cabbage (sow July–August, harvest March–May), summer cabbage (sow March–May, harvest June–August), autumn cabbage (sow April–May, harvest September–November), winter cabbage (sow April–May, harvest December–February). Cabbages are hardy (RHS H5; winter cultivars H6) and reliable in UK conditions provided you net against cabbage white in summer and pigeons in winter. Plant 45 cm apart in firm fertile soil; firm the soil hard around each plant — like Brussels sprouts they sulk in loose ground. Cut when heads are firm to the touch. Winter cultivars (January King, Tundra) stand outdoors right through to spring; summer cultivars must be cut and used promptly or they split.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose by season", "text": "Spring (Pixie, Spring Hero): sow July–August, transplant September, harvest March–May. Summer (Hispi, Greyhound): sow March–May, harvest June–August. Autumn (Autoro, Marabel): sow April–May, harvest September–November. Winter (January King, Tundra, Holland Late): sow April–May, harvest December–February. Pick a season; sow accordingly."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "Modules at 12–15°C or a seedbed once soil is workable. Germination in 1–2 weeks. Successional sowings within each seasonal window every 3 weeks for continuous picking."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prepare firm fertile soil", "text": "Full sun, firm soil, slightly alkaline (lime if pH below 6.5). Fork in well-rotted compost from the previous autumn. Brassicas hate loose ground — they need firmness."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Transplant when 10–15 cm tall", "text": "45 cm apart for standard heading cabbages. Firm the soil hard around each plant; water in well. Spring cabbage plants overwinter in their final position; mulch them with straw in November in cold gardens."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Net against cabbage white", "text": "From transplanting through October. Cabbage whites lay on any brassica; caterpillars strip a plant overnight. Net firmly with 5 mm enviromesh on hoops."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water through dry spells", "text": "Cabbages need consistent moisture but tolerate drought better than cauliflowers. Water deeply once a week in dry weather; mulch with grass clippings or straw."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut summer cabbages promptly when firm", "text": "Cut when heads are firm to the touch and full-sized. Don't leave summer cabbages in the ground past their prime — they split, especially after rain. Cut the head off above the lower leaves with a sharp knife; sometimes the stump resprouts giving a second crop of small heads."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Stand winter cabbages in the ground", "text": "Winter cultivars (January King, Tundra) take cold and hold their shape outdoors right through to February or March. Cut as needed. Spring cabbages can be picked as spring greens (loose leaves) from December onwards if you don't want to wait for hearting."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Re-net pigeons in winter", "text": "From October, pigeons strip unprotected brassicas. Net winter cabbages with proper bird netting on hoops."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow cabbage in the UK?", "answer": "Depends on which season you want to harvest. Spring cabbage: July–August (transplant September; harvest the following March–May). Summer cabbage: March–May (harvest June–August). Autumn cabbage: April–May (harvest September–November). Winter cabbage: April–May (harvest December–February). Successional sowings within each window every 3 weeks for continuous picking."},
    {"question": "Why are my cabbages splitting open?", "answer": "Sudden heavy rain after dry weather, or simply leaving heads past their prime. The plant takes up water faster than the head can expand and the leaves split. Pick summer cabbages promptly when firm; for autumn and winter cabbages, the slow growth through cool weather rarely causes splits. To save a damaged head, cut immediately and use within a few days."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between summer, autumn, and winter cabbages?", "answer": "Summer cabbages (Hispi, Greyhound): fast-growing, tender loose-leaved or pointed types; don't store well; eat fresh. Autumn cabbages (Marabel, Autoro): firmer, larger, store 4–6 weeks. Winter cabbages (January King, Tundra): very hardy, dense heads that stand outdoors all winter; classic for storage and braising. Spring cabbages are pointed loose-headed types picked young as spring greens or hearted later."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between green, red, and savoy cabbage?", "answer": "All are Brassica oleracea var. capitata (same species, same general care). Green cabbage is the standard type. Red cabbage (Red Drumhead) has purple-red leaves with the same general care; stores particularly well. Savoy cabbage (technically Brassica oleracea var. sabauda — separate slug `savoy-cabbage` in this content set) has crinkled leaves and is hardier than green types. All net against the same pests."},
    {"question": "How do I stop cabbage root fly?", "answer": "Use a 15 cm cardboard collar around the base of each transplant (cut a slit in a cardboard disc and fit it round the stem). The fly lays eggs at soil level next to the stem; the collar prevents this. Brassica root collars made of carpet underlay or pond liner work too. Most damage is to younger plants; established plants tolerate some root damage."},
    {"question": "Can I grow cabbages in containers?", "answer": "Possible but underwhelming. Small varieties (Hispi, Greyhound) in 30-litre containers crop, but yield is modest and the heads tend to be smaller. Use peat-free compost; water consistently; feed weekly. Net against cabbage white even in containers. Use bed space if you have it."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'cabbage';

-- From carrot.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow carrot seed direct from late February under cloches, March to July outdoors — successional sowings every three weeks for a steady crop. Carrots want light, stone-free soil; heavy clay or stony ground gives forked roots. The dominant enemy is carrot fly (Psila rosae), so cover the bed with enviromesh or a 60-cm-tall fleece barrier from sowing onwards — the fly can''t fly higher than that. Thin seedlings to 5 cm apart and pull thinnings in the evening on a still day; the carrot scent attracts the fly. Carrots are hardy enough to tolerate light frosts (RHS H4) and main-crop varieties stored properly last all winter. Early varieties crop from late June; main-crops from August onwards.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Prepare the soil", "text": "Fork over a sunny bed; remove stones; rake to a fine tilth. Don't add fresh manure (it splits the roots) or lime. Sandy or sandy-loam soil is ideal — for heavy clay, use a deep raised bed of sieved compost."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "From late February under cloches; March to July outdoors. Make a 1 cm drill with the corner of a hoe. Sow seed thinly along the drill; cover lightly with soil; tamp down with the back of the rake."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Cover for carrot fly", "text": "From sowing onwards, drape enviromesh or 60-cm-tall fleece over hoops above the bed. Carrot fly can't fly higher than 60 cm; physically excluding them is the only fully reliable defence."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Thin seedlings", "text": "When seedlings are 5 cm tall, thin to 5 cm apart. Pull thinnings in the evening on a still day to avoid wafting the carrot scent into the air. Water after thinning to settle the remaining roots."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Keep weed-free", "text": "Hoe carefully between rows; carrots don't like root disturbance. Mulch with grass clippings or straw once seedlings are established to suppress weeds and hold moisture."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Harvest", "text": "Pull early varieties from late June; main-crops from August to first frost. Tug gently from the base of the foliage. Smaller is sweeter; over-large carrots get coarse."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Store", "text": "Lift main-crop carrots before the first hard frost. Cut foliage 2 cm above the root; layer in damp sand in a wooden box; store in a frost-free shed. They keep firm until April."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow carrot seed in the UK?", "answer": "Late February under cloches in southern England; mid-March to July outdoors across the UK; first sowing in early April further north. Carrots want soil above 7°C to germinate, so don't push them in too early. Successional sowings every three weeks from April to early July give a steady supply through summer and autumn. Late-July sowings of fast varieties crop into November."},
    {"question": "Why are my carrots forked?", "answer": "Forked roots mean the soil was too stony, too rich, or too compact. Carrots fork around obstructions or split when their growing tip hits stones. Don't add fresh manure or compost to the bed before sowing — high nitrogen makes them branch. For heavy clay, grow short or globe varieties (Parmex, Chantenay) in deep tilth or in containers of loose multi-purpose compost."},
    {"question": "How do I stop carrot fly?", "answer": "Cover the bed with enviromesh or 60-cm-tall fleece from sowing onwards — the fly can't fly higher than that. Thin seedlings in the evening on a still day; the bruised foliage attracts the fly. Sow Resistafly or Flyaway varieties for partial resistance, though no carrot variety is fully resistant. Companion planting with chives, leeks or onions confuses the fly but only modestly — netting is the only reliable defence."},
    {"question": "How do I know when carrots are ready?", "answer": "Push back the foliage with your fingers and look at the shoulder of the root. Early varieties are ready when 1–2 cm across, usually 10–12 weeks from sowing. Main-crops are ready from August onwards when 3–5 cm across. Don't wait too long — over-large carrots get woody and coarse."},
    {"question": "Can I grow carrots in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — short varieties (Parmex, Paris Market) do well in 30 cm pots or window boxes. Use sieved multi-purpose compost. Sow thinly, thin to 3 cm apart, keep consistently watered. Carrot fly affects container-grown carrots less because the bed is usually higher than 60 cm; raise containers on bricks to be safe."},
    {"question": "How do I store carrots through winter?", "answer": "Lift main-crops before the first hard frost. Cut foliage 2 cm above the root (don't twist; you'll wound them). Layer the roots in damp sand in a wooden box, ensuring they don't touch. Store in a frost-free shed or garage. They keep firm until April. Alternatively, leave them in the ground covered with a deep straw mulch — fine in southern England, risky north of the Midlands."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'carrot';

-- From catmint.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant catmint in spring, in full sun in well-drained soil — it''s an easy hardy perennial that forms loose mounds of grey-green aromatic foliage topped by spires of lavender-blue flowers from May through September. Catmint is hardy (RHS H5–H7) and one of the most reliable UK garden plants for pollinators — bees of all kinds work the flowers obsessively. Six Hills Giant is the workhorse cultivar (90 cm tall, vigorous), Walker''s Low is the mid-sized one (50 cm), and N. racemosa Walker''s Low is more compact still. Cut the whole plant back hard after the first flush in late June (the Chelsea Chop) for a longer second flush August–September. Cats do roll in it — catnip (Nepeta cataria) is the species that drives cats wild; ornamental catmint affects them too but less dramatically. A bee plant first, a cat plant second.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick ornamental or true catnip", "text": "Ornamental catmints (the garden classics — Nepeta × faassenii, N. racemosa, N. nervosa, N. govaniana): lavender-blue flowers, grey-green foliage, the showy border plants. Cats are mildly attracted but rarely destructive. Six Hills Giant: vigorous 90 cm tall, the UK workhorse. Walker's Low (despite the name, 50–60 cm): mid-sized, RHS Award of Garden Merit, the most popular UK garden choice. True catnip (Nepeta cataria): the species cats really respond to — used in herbal teas, grown more for cats than for ornament. Smaller white-pink flowers, weedier appearance. See separate catnip content if drafted."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in full sun and well-drained soil", "text": "Mediterranean origins: catmint wants 6+ hours direct sun and free-draining soil. Rich soil = flopping plants. On clay soils, fork in horticultural grit at planting. Tolerates poor sandy or chalky soils perfectly. Spacing 50 cm for medium varieties, 75 cm for Six Hills Giant."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in spring, after last frost", "text": "April to May. Container-grown plants are sold in 1–2 L pots from spring onwards. Plant at the same depth as the pot. Water in deeply, then leave alone — catmint hates overwatering once established. Spring planting gives time to root before its first winter."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't feed", "text": "Feeding makes catmint flop. Rich soil produces lush growth that collapses sideways and reduces flowering. No fertiliser at planting; no fertiliser during the season. A gravel mulch around the base keeps weeds down without adding nutrients. Stress and poor soil concentrate the essential oils and improve both flowering and bee-attractiveness."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Apply the Chelsea Chop in late June", "text": "The single most important husbandry technique. After the first flush of flowers fades (late June), cut the whole plant down to 10 cm above ground. Looks brutal but works: catmint regrows quickly and produces a second, longer flush of flowers from August through September. Without the Chelsea Chop, catmint flowers in May–June and then looks tatty for the rest of summer. With it: 4 months of flowering and tidy foliage."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Stake taller varieties or let them flop naturally", "text": "Six Hills Giant (90 cm) often flops sideways onto the ground in summer storms — a discreet grow-through plant support placed early lets it grow naturally. Many UK gardeners actually like the flopping habit at the front of a border (it softens path edges) and don't bother staking. Compact varieties (Walker's Low, Junior Walker) stay upright without support."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut to 10 cm in late autumn", "text": "After the second flush fades in September, cut the plant down to 10 cm above ground. Removes the spent stems and prepares the clump for winter dormancy. The plant returns each spring from the rootstock. Don't divide in autumn — wait for spring."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Divide every 3–4 years in spring", "text": "Catmint clumps gradually become congested and the centre dies out. In early spring (just as new growth emerges), lift the clump, split with a spade into 3–4 sections, replant the best outer pieces in fresh soil. Compost the dead centre. Free new plants — give the spares away. Easy and reliable propagation."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I cut back catmint?", "answer": "Twice a year for the longest display. First cut (the Chelsea Chop) in late June: cut the whole plant to 10 cm after the first flush of flowers fades. This triggers a second, longer flush August–September. Second cut in late autumn (after the second flush): trim to 10 cm to tidy for winter. Without the Chelsea Chop, catmint flowers once in May–June and looks tatty all summer; with it, you get 4 months of flowering."},
    {"question": "Will catmint attract cats to my garden?", "answer": "Yes — but typically less dramatically than true catnip. Ornamental catmints (Nepeta × faassenii, N. racemosa — the garden classics) contain nepetalactone (the compound cats respond to) but at lower concentrations than true catnip (Nepeta cataria). Most cats are mildly attracted, sniff the leaves, sometimes roll on the plant. Heavy play with it like a toy cat behaviour is more common with true catnip. For minimum cat interest: plant Junior Walker or N. nervosa — lower nepetalactone content. For maximum cat attraction: plant true catnip (Nepeta cataria)."},
    {"question": "Why is my catmint flopping over?", "answer": "Two usual causes. (1) Too rich soil: catmint flops in fertilised or compost-rich soil. Stop feeding, fork grit in next spring. (2) Vigorous cultivar in a small space: Six Hills Giant (90 cm) naturally flops in summer — that's its habit. For an upright plant, choose Walker's Low or Junior Walker (50–60 cm, naturally upright). Many UK gardeners accept the flopping habit at border fronts; it softens hard path edges."},
    {"question": "What's the best catmint variety for the UK?", "answer": "Walker's Low is the modern UK favourite — RHS Award of Garden Merit, 50–60 cm tall (despite the Low in the name), reliable hardy, flowers June–September with the Chelsea Chop. Six Hills Giant is the vigorous workhorse for larger spaces (90 cm). Junior Walker is a sterile compact form (30–40 cm) — doesn't self-seed, more upright. Avoid bedding-type Nepetas; they're often short-lived."},
    {"question": "Will catmint survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — fully hardy (RHS H5–H7). Catmint dies back to the rootstock each autumn and returns each spring. Survives any normal UK winter without protection. Established plants are essentially indestructible. Two cautions: (1) Don't divide in autumn (wait for spring). (2) Avoid waterlogged soil — winter wet on heavy clay can occasionally rot the crown."},
    {"question": "Are catmints good for wildlife?", "answer": "Outstanding — one of the top UK garden plants for pollinator diversity. The small two-lipped flowers are accessible to bees of all kinds (bumblebees, honeybees, solitary bees), hoverflies, butterflies, and moths. Long-tongued bumblebees work the flowers from the front; short-tongued bumblebees and honeybees visit too. A patch of catmint in full flower is a constant buzz from May through September. With the Chelsea Chop technique, you maximise pollinator value across the longest possible season."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'catmint';

-- From cauliflower.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow cauliflower from March to May for summer–autumn cropping, or in July–August for autumn–winter heading types. Cauliflower is hardy (RHS H4–H5) — the least hardy of the common brassicas — and famously demanding: any check to growth (drought, transplant shock, nutrient gap) ruins the curd. Plant in firm fertile soil 60 cm apart; water deeply through summer; never let it dry out. Mini cultivars (Igloo, All The Year Round) crop in 14 weeks for small gardens; standard heading types take 20+ weeks. Tie outer leaves over the curd as it develops to shade and keep it white. Net against cabbage white and pigeons. Heads damaged by hard frost — winter cauliflowers crop only as far north as the Midlands reliably.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose by season", "text": "Summer / autumn cauliflowers (Snowball, All The Year Round, Igloo mini): sow March–May, harvest July–November. Autumn / winter cauliflowers (Aalsmeer, Walcheren Winter, Galleon): sow July–August, harvest the following March–May. Different sowing windows; pick one type per bed."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "Modules at 12–15°C or a seedbed once soil is workable. Don't sow direct in cold wet soil; seedlings rot. Successional sowings every 3 weeks for continuous summer–autumn cropping."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prepare firm rich soil", "text": "Full sun, firm soil, slightly alkaline (lime if below pH 6.5). Fork in well-rotted compost. Cauliflowers are heavy feeders and hate poor soil."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Transplant when 10–15 cm tall", "text": "60 cm apart for standard heading types, 30 cm for mini cultivars. Firm the soil hard around each plant; water in well. Choose a damp morning; don't transplant in dry heat."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Net against cabbage white", "text": "From transplanting until October. Cauliflowers are top of the cabbage white menu; netting is non-negotiable."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water consistently", "text": "The single most important step. Cauliflowers need uninterrupted growth — any drought check (even 4–5 days dry) ruins the curd. Water deeply twice a week through July and August; mulch with grass clippings to hold moisture."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Tie outer leaves over the curd", "text": "When the curd is the size of a tennis ball, tie 3–4 outer leaves loosely over it with twine. Excludes light, keeping the curd white and tender. For coloured cultivars (Romanesco, purple, orange) skip this step — colour development needs light."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Cut when curd is firm and tight", "text": "Standard heads are ready when the curd is full size, firm, and still tightly packed. Once buds start to separate (ricing) the curd is going over. Cut early in the morning while the curd is cool; store in the fridge."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Net pigeons over winter (winter cauliflowers)", "text": "For July-sown overwintering types, re-net against pigeons from October. They strip unprotected brassicas through winter."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow cauliflower in the UK?", "answer": "Depends on type. Summer / autumn cauliflowers: March to May (modules); successional sowings every 3 weeks. Crops July–November. Winter cauliflowers (overwintering, also called heading broccoli): July to August. Crops the following March–May. Don't try to combine types in one sowing window — they're essentially different crops."},
    {"question": "Why are my cauliflower heads so small?", "answer": "Most likely: drought check during growth, poor soil, or a cultivar too vigorous for UK conditions. Cauliflowers need consistent moisture and fertile soil — any check to growth ruins the curd. Water deeply twice a week through summer; fork in well-rotted compost before planting. Mini cultivars (Igloo) are more forgiving than full-size standards if you can't guarantee consistent watering."},
    {"question": "My cauliflower curd has turned yellow or grey — what happened?", "answer": "Yellow: light exposure during development. Tie outer leaves over the curd from tennis-ball size onwards to shade it. Grey or brown patches: boron deficiency or fungal damage. Boron is added by a light dressing of borax (1 g per square metre) before planting. Fungal grey is usually from prolonged wet weather on a mature head; cut and use; doesn't affect the next crop."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between standard, mini, and Romanesco cauliflower?", "answer": "Standard heading cauliflower (Snowball, Walcheren): full-size white curds, 20+ weeks from sowing, the supermarket standard. Mini cauliflower (Igloo, Lateman): small heads, 14 weeks, suited to small gardens and successional sowing. Romanesco (Veronica): striking lime-green geometric curds, longer season, slightly more cold-tolerant; technically a different cultivar group but grown the same way."},
    {"question": "Can I grow cauliflower in containers?", "answer": "Mini cultivars (Igloo) in 30-litre containers work. Standard heading types don't have enough root space — they head poorly in containers. Use peat-free compost; water consistently; feed weekly. Net against cabbage white even in containers — they'll find them."},
    {"question": "When and how do I harvest cauliflower?", "answer": "Cut when the curd is full size, firm, and still tightly packed. Once buds start to separate (the curd looks rice-grained instead of smooth) it's going over and the texture coarsens. Cut early morning. Heads keep 7–10 days in the fridge; for longer storage, blanch and freeze florets."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'cauliflower';

-- From celeriac.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow celeriac seed indoors from February to March at 18°C; plant out from late May 30 cm apart in rich, moisture-retentive soil. Celeriac is hardy (RHS H4) and one of the longest-season UK crops — sown in February, you''re lifting from October onwards. Same demanding needs as its celery cousin: constant moisture, rich soil, full sun for half the day. From mid-summer, remove the outer leaves and side shoots as they sprawl outwards — this redirects the plant''s energy into swelling the root. Lift from October; smaller earlier lifts have the best flavour. In milder gardens celeriac stands in the ground under straw mulch through to February. Monarch and Prinz are reliable UK cultivars; Brilliant for big roots.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow indoors with bottom heat", "text": "February to March at 18°C. Surface sow (the tiny seeds need light to germinate); mist regularly to keep the surface moist. Germination 2–3 weeks. Don't sow direct — celeriac needs a long head start."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prick out into 9 cm pots", "text": "When seedlings have two true leaves, prick out individually. Keep above 15°C minimum; cool checks at this stage cause bolting later."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prepare rich moist soil", "text": "Full sun, deep fertile soil with plenty of organic matter. Fork in well-rotted manure or compost — celeriac is a hungry crop that grows from a tiny seedling to a fist-sized root over 30+ weeks."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Harden off and plant out", "text": "After the last frost (late May south, early June north). 7–10 days hardening off. Plant 30 cm apart in rows 38 cm apart. Set plants at the same depth they were in the pot; don't bury the crown."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water deeply and consistently", "text": "Celeriac wants constant moisture from planting through to harvest. Water deeply twice a week in dry weather; mulch heavily with grass clippings or straw. Drought gives small woody roots."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Remove outer leaves and side shoots from July", "text": "As the plant develops, the outer leaves spread out and side shoots emerge from the base. Remove them progressively from July onwards. This redirects energy into swelling the root rather than leaf growth."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Feed and lightly earth up", "text": "Liquid feed monthly. As the root swells above the soil surface, draw a little earth up around it (light earthing-up — not like potatoes or trenching celery). Keeps the developing root tender."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Lift from October", "text": "Lift roots when fist-sized (10–15 cm across). Smaller earlier lifts (golf-ball size) have sweeter flavour; bigger later lifts give more flesh. Use a fork; trim foliage to 5 cm above the crown; brush off soil."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Store or stand in the ground", "text": "Store: layer in damp sand in a frost-free shed; keeps until March. Stand in ground: in milder gardens (southern England, mild south coast), celeriac holds in the ground under a thick straw mulch through winter; lift as needed. Cold-garden growers lift before deep frost."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow celeriac in the UK?", "answer": "February to March indoors at 18°C. The lead time is long — 28–32 weeks from sowing to first lift. Don't sow earlier or later: earlier and the plants get leggy before plant-out; later and the roots don't have time to swell before autumn."},
    {"question": "Why are my celeriac roots small and woody?", "answer": "Drought stress, the dominant cause. Celeriac needs constant moisture across its long season — even occasional drying checks the root development. Water deeply twice a week from planting through to harvest; mulch heavily. Other causes: poor soil (fork in plenty of compost), cold check at seedling stage causing bolting, or simply lifting too early."},
    {"question": "Should I remove the outer leaves on celeriac?", "answer": "Yes, from July onwards. As the plant matures, the outer leaves and side shoots spread sideways from the crown. Remove them progressively (cut at the base with a sharp knife). The plant's energy then redirects into swelling the root rather than producing more leaves. Don't remove the central rosette of young leaves — they continue powering growth."},
    {"question": "When and how do I harvest celeriac?", "answer": "From October to February, depending on size. Lift when roots are fist-sized (10–15 cm across) — that's the prime size. Smaller earlier lifts have sweeter, milder flavour; bigger lifts give more flesh but get coarser. Use a fork to lift; trim foliage to 5 cm; brush off soil. Don't wash until you use it."},
    {"question": "Can celeriac stand outdoors through winter?", "answer": "Yes, in southern England and most of the Midlands with a thick straw or bracken mulch. The roots themselves are hardy (RHS H4); the foliage dies in the worst weather but regrows. North of the Midlands or in cold gardens, lift in November and store in damp sand in a frost-free shed. Highlands gardens lift before October."},
    {"question": "Why does celeriac take so long to grow?", "answer": "It's a slow biennial root that needs a long season to swell from a tiny seedling to a usable root. Plant size scales with day length and temperature — the long UK summer suits it, but the indoor head start in February is essential. There's no fast cultivar; the variety choice is between earlier-maturing types (Monarch) and bigger-rooted later types (Brilliant)."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'celeriac';

-- From celery.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow celery seed indoors from February to April at 18°C; plant out from late May after the last frost. Celery is half-hardy (RHS H3–H4) and demanding — it needs constantly moist, rich soil, full sun for half the day, and protection from slugs. Two main types: self-blanching (Galaxy, Loretta) which need no earthing-up and crop summer to autumn, and trenching (Solid White, Giant Pink) which require deep planting and earthing-up but give classic long white stems for autumn–winter cropping. Bolting in heat above 25°C ruins the crop, so choose bolt-resistant cultivars and water deeply through summer. Pick whole heads when the stems are firm and full-sized; trenching types stand into November with frost protection.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H3',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose self-blanching or trenching", "text": "Self-blanching (Galaxy, Loretta, Lathom Self-Blanching): no earthing-up needed, crop July–October, plant in close blocks to shade each other. Easier for first-time growers. Trenching (Solid White, Giant Pink, Solid Pink): plant in a trench and earth up through summer for blanched white stems; classic flavour and texture; crops September–November."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow indoors with bottom heat", "text": "February to April at 18°C in modules or seed trays. Surface sow (don't cover — celery needs light to germinate); mist regularly. Germination is slow (2–3 weeks) and patchy."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prick out into 9 cm pots", "text": "When seedlings have two true leaves, prick out into individual 9 cm pots. Keep above 15°C minimum; cool temperatures (below 10°C) at this stage cause bolting later."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Harden off and plant out", "text": "After the last frost (late May south, early June north). 7–10 days of hardening off. Plant 25–30 cm apart for self-blanching blocks; for trenching types, plant 30 cm apart in a trench 30 cm deep."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water consistently and heavily", "text": "Celery is a swamp plant by ancestry — it wants constant moisture. Water deeply twice a week through summer; mulch heavily. Drought stress is the most common cause of stringy, bitter, bolted celery."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Feed every 2 weeks", "text": "A liquid feed (balanced or slightly nitrogen-rich) every 2 weeks. Celery is a heavy feeder and won't make full-size stems on poor soil."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Earth up trenching types", "text": "For trenching cultivars, draw soil up around the stems from August onwards as they grow. By September, only the top 10 cm of leaves should show. The covered stems blanch white, becoming tender and sweet. Self-blanching types skip this step."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Protect from slugs", "text": "Slugs love celery seedlings. Apply nematodes before planting out; beer traps near the bed; hand-pick at dusk. Once stems are 30 cm tall, slug pressure drops."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Harvest before frost", "text": "Pick whole heads from late July (self-blanching) or September (trenching). Cut the entire plant at ground level; the stub doesn't resprout. Trenching celery stands through mild autumn frosts under fleece; self-blanching is killed by the first frost."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow celery in the UK?", "answer": "February to April indoors at 18°C with bottom heat. Don't sow direct; celery seed is tiny and germination is slow and unreliable in outdoor conditions. The long lead time (12–16 weeks from sowing to plant-out) means an early start matters. Don't sow earlier than February or you'll have leggy plants by planting-out time."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between self-blanching and trenching celery?", "answer": "Self-blanching: plant in close blocks (25 cm apart); the plants shade each other, partially blanching the stems naturally; no earthing-up. Crops July–October. Less work; smaller crop; killed by frost. Trenching: plant in a trench 30 cm deep; earth up from August; covered stems blanch white; classic flavour and texture. Crops September–November; more work; stands light frosts."},
    {"question": "Why is my celery stringy and bitter?", "answer": "Drought stress, the dominant cause. Celery needs constant moisture — even brief drying gives stringy stems and bitter flavour. Water deeply twice a week; mulch heavily; never let the bed dry out. Other causes: bolting (from cold check during seedling stage or summer heat), poor soil (celery is a heavy feeder), or simply over-mature plants."},
    {"question": "How do I stop celery bolting?", "answer": "Avoid cold checks at seedling stage — keep plants above 15°C until they have 4–5 true leaves. Choose bolt-resistant cultivars (Galaxy, Loretta). Water deeply through summer; mulch heavily. Once a plant bolts (sends up a tall flower stem), the stems get woody and bitter — pull and use what's still palatable."},
    {"question": "Can I grow celery in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes, but it's demanding. Use a deep (40 cm+) container; rich peat-free compost; water daily in summer; feed weekly. Self-blanching types in close groups of 4–6 plants per 40-litre container work. The risk is drying out — container celery needs religious watering."},
    {"question": "When do I harvest celery?", "answer": "Self-blanching types from July–October once the heads are full-sized. Trenching types from September–November once the stems are well-blanched white. Cut the whole head at ground level with a sharp knife; the plant doesn't resprout. Pick before the first hard frost (self-blanching) or before deep frost (trenching)."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'celery';

-- From chard.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow chard seed direct from April to July, in moist, fertile soil with full sun or light shade. Chard is hardy (RHS H4–H5) and far more reliable for UK summer leaf-cropping than true spinach — it doesn''t bolt the same way and crops continuously from June through to the following spring with light winter protection. Bright Lights cultivar gives stems in white, yellow, orange and red; Fordhook Giant is the heavy-cropping white-stemmed standard; Rhubarb chard has deep red stems. Each seed is a cluster of 2–4 — thin to one per station 30 cm apart. Pick outer stems and leaves continuously from 8 weeks after sowing. Slugs the main pest; otherwise one of the easiest crops in any UK kitchen garden.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "April to July. 2 cm deep, 30 cm apart in rows 40 cm apart. Each beetroot/chard seed is a cluster of 2–4 — multiple seedlings come up per station."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Thin to one per station", "text": "When seedlings are 5 cm tall, thin to the strongest single seedling per station. Use the thinnings in salads."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Water consistently", "text": "Chard wants steady moisture but tolerates dry spells better than spinach. Water deeply once a week in summer; mulch with grass clippings to hold moisture."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Protect from slugs", "text": "The young plants are slug-vulnerable. Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita); beer traps; hand-picking at dusk. Established plants with tough leaves are mostly slug-proof."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick outer leaves continuously", "text": "From 8 weeks after sowing. Twist or cut outer stems at the base; the centre keeps producing for months. A single April sowing crops from June right through to the following spring."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Mulch for winter cropping", "text": "Apply a 5 cm mulch of straw or compost around plants in October to protect against frost. Most of the UK will keep cropping outdoors all winter; in colder gardens, cover with cloches or fleece for the worst weather."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pull bolting plants in year 2", "text": "In its second spring, chard bolts (sends up flower stems). The bolting shoots are themselves edible as a sort of broccoli. After bolting the plant dies — replace from a fresh April sowing."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow chard in the UK?", "answer": "April to July outdoors; March under cloches in southern England. A single April sowing usually crops continuously from June through to the following March or April when the plant bolts. Mid-summer sowings (July) crop from autumn through winter and into the following spring."},
    {"question": "Is chard the same as Swiss chard or perpetual spinach?", "answer": "Same species, all Beta vulgaris subsp. cicla. Swiss chard usually refers to the named cultivars with coloured stems (Bright Lights, Rhubarb chard, Fordhook Giant). Perpetual spinach is a green-stemmed type sold as a spinach alternative — same plant, marketed for the spinach-substitute angle. They're all the same family and grow the same way."},
    {"question": "Why does chard not bolt like spinach?", "answer": "Different species, despite the names. True spinach (Spinacia oleracea) is hard-wired to bolt as summer days lengthen. Chard (Beta vulgaris subsp. cicla) is a biennial — it grows in year one, bolts in year two. So a spring-sown chard gives leaf for 8–10 months before bolting, where spinach gives 4–6 weeks at best."},
    {"question": "How long do chard plants last?", "answer": "About 12–14 months — sown in April, they crop from June, through the winter, and bolt the following March or April. After bolting, replace with a fresh sowing. For continuous supply with no gap, sow a second batch in July; the older plants bolt as the new ones are coming online."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between the coloured chards?", "answer": "Mostly aesthetic. Bright Lights and Five Colours give a rainbow of white, yellow, orange, pink and red stems. Rhubarb chard has uniform deep red stems. Fordhook Giant has thick white stems and is the heaviest-cropping. Flavour differences are subtle; coloured stems tend to be slightly milder; white is more robust. Bright Lights wins on looks in an ornamental kitchen garden."},
    {"question": "Can I grow chard in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Excellent for containers. A 30 cm deep pot grows 3–4 plants. Use peat-free compost; water consistently; feed monthly through summer. The coloured cultivars (Bright Lights) make beautiful patio plants. Slug pressure is much lower in elevated containers than in the ground."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'chard';

-- From chervil.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow chervil direct from March to September, in part shade in moist soil — it''s a hardy annual/biennial that loves cool damp conditions and bolts straight to flower in heat or full sun. Chervil is hardy (RHS H6) — sown in late summer it overwinters as small plants and gives an early-spring flush, while spring-sown chervil crops from May to early summer before bolting. Don''t transplant — long taproot resents disturbance; sow direct or use deep modular cells. Pick young feathery foliage continuously for the delicate aniseed-parsley flavour that defines French cooking — chervil is one of the classic fines herbes alongside parsley, tarragon, and chives. Use fresh always — chervil loses flavour completely when dried, and even cooking destroys the delicate aroma. Add at the very end of cooking, just before serving.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct, not in modules", "text": "Chervil has a long taproot and resents transplanting. Sow seed direct where you want the plants to grow, in moist soil. If you must start indoors, use deep root-trainer cells (10+ cm deep) and plant out before roots reach the bottom. Standard module trays don't give enough depth — transplants bolt to flower within 2 weeks."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow successionally, March to September", "text": "Chervil bolts quickly in heat — successional sowing is the only way to keep tender foliage going. Three windows for the best supply: (1) Early spring sowings (March–April) for May–June cropping. (2) Skip the hot midsummer months (June–early August) — chervil bolts immediately. (3) Late summer sowings (mid-August–September) for autumn cropping AND overwintered plants that crop early next spring (March–April)."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in part shade and moist soil", "text": "Chervil's defining preference: cool, damp, part shade. Full sun + dry soil = instant bolting and tough bitter leaves. Ideal positions: north-facing borders, woodland edges, under deciduous shrubs (after their leaves emerge), shady allotment corners. Moisture-retentive soil enriched with garden compost. Tolerates clay if not waterlogged."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Sow shallow and thin", "text": "Surface-sow or barely cover seed (1 cm max — chervil needs light to germinate). Germination is slow for chervil — 14–21 days at 10–15°C, slower in cold soil. Be patient before assuming the seed has failed. Thin seedlings to 20 cm apart when they have 3–4 true leaves."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water consistently", "text": "Chervil bolts in dry conditions. A weekly soak in dry spells keeps the leaves tender and the plant in leaf-production mode. Mulch with leaf-mould or fine compost in spring to conserve moisture. Pot-grown chervil needs daily watering in summer."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick continuously from 6 weeks", "text": "From 6 weeks after sowing, pick whole stems just above a side-shoot rather than picking individual leaves. The plant branches and re-grows. Strongest flavour is on the youngest growth — pick the tips, leave the older lower leaves which get tough and bitter. Continuous picking + watering = continuous fresh foliage until the plant bolts."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Use fresh, never dried", "text": "The most important chervil rule. Chervil's delicate aniseed-parsley flavour is in volatile oils that fade dramatically with drying — dried chervil tastes of nothing. Even cooking destroys the flavour — add chervil at the very end, after the heat is off, just before serving. Classic uses: omelettes, fish, asparagus, herb butter, fines herbes mix (with parsley, tarragon, chives). Chop just before adding."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Let some plants flower for self-seeding", "text": "Chervil produces small white umbel flowers when it bolts. Let the late-summer/early-autumn plants flower and self-seed — chervil establishes self-seeding colonies in shady corners (like wild chervil naturally does in UK woodland). The self-sown seedlings appear the following spring and give the easiest, most reliable continuous chervil supply. Cut flower stems before seed sets if you want to limit spread."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why does my chervil bolt so fast?", "answer": "Heat + dry soil + full sun. Chervil is a cool-season annual that bolts immediately in summer. Four strategies for longer cropping: (1) Sow in part shade, not full sun. (2) Sow in cool months (March–April for spring, August–September for autumn/winter) — skip June and July entirely. (3) Keep the soil consistently moist — chervil bolts in drought. (4) Use successional sowing every 3 weeks during the cool months. Most UK gardeners grow chervil for spring and autumn use; summer chervil supply is hard to maintain."},
    {"question": "When can I pick chervil?", "answer": "From 6 weeks after sowing, before the plant flowers. Pick whole stems at the base of new growth — the plant branches and re-grows. Strongest flavour is on the youngest tender shoots; older leaves get tough and bitter. Stop picking once the plant bolts — flowering chervil has tough stems and bitter leaves. Year-round cropping is possible in southern UK with autumn-sown plants that overwinter and give March–April leaves before going to seed."},
    {"question": "Can I dry chervil?", "answer": "No — chervil loses almost all flavour when dried. The delicate aniseed-parsley aroma is in volatile oils that fade dramatically with any drying method. Two preservation options. (1) Freeze fresh chervil chopped into ice cubes with water or olive oil — keeps reasonably well for 3 months. (2) Plant successional sowings for fresh use across most of the year. Don't bother with dried chervil from shops — it's flavourless. Fresh chervil is the only chervil worth using."},
    {"question": "What does chervil taste like?", "answer": "Delicate aniseed-parsley — a refined, sweet, slightly aromatic version of parsley with a clear anise note. Much softer than tarragon (which has a stronger aniseed punch). The flavour is delicate enough to be used in larger quantities than most herbs — a tablespoon of fresh chopped chervil per dish rather than a teaspoon. Cooking destroys the flavour — add chervil at the very end, just before serving. Classic uses: omelettes (the French herb-omelette star), fish sauces, asparagus, herb butter, the fines herbes mix."},
    {"question": "When and how do I sow chervil for year-round use?", "answer": "Three sowing windows. (1) March–April: direct sow for May–June cropping. (2) August–September: direct sow for autumn cropping AND plants that overwinter and give early-spring leaves in March–April. (3) Successional sowing every 3 weeks during the cool months extends each window. Skip June and July entirely — chervil bolts immediately in heat. Sow shallow (chervil needs light to germinate), in part shade, in moist soil."},
    {"question": "Can chervil grow in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and pots make slug control and part-shade positioning easier. Use a 25–30 cm pot of peat-free compost. Sow direct into the pot (don't transplant). Position in part shade — under a north-facing windowsill, or in a shady corner of the garden. Water consistently to keep the compost evenly moist. Pot chervil bolts faster in hot weather than ground-planted; sow successionally and accept the shorter individual-pot lifespan. One pot lasts about 6 weeks before bolting."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'chervil';

-- From chicory.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow chicory direct from May to July, in sun in well-drained soil — it''s a hardy perennial (RHS H7) grown as an annual or for autumn-lifted roots that are forced indoors over winter to produce pale tender "chicons" of bitter-sweet leaves. Three main types serve different uses: forcing chicory (lift roots in November, force indoors for white chicons in January–March — the Belgian witloof tradition); heading chicory / radicchio (red Italian types like Palla Rossa that head up like cabbage in autumn); and leaf chicory (sugarloaf and asparagus types cropped for whole heads or cut-and-come-again leaves). All three share a slightly bitter flavour that''s a love-it-or-hate-it characteristic — milder when blanched (the forcing technique), full-strength when grown to full size and colour. Snail-magnet outdoors — protect young plants.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick the type that matches your use", "text": "Forcing chicory (witloof, Belgian endive): root-forming type sown for autumn-lifted roots that are forced indoors over winter for blanched white chicons in January–March. Classic dish: braised chicons with ham. Variety: Witloof Zoom. Heading chicory / radicchio: cabbage-like heads in red-and-white or solid red in autumn. Varieties: Palla Rossa (rose-coloured), Treviso (elongated red), Chioggia (round red). Leaf / sugarloaf chicory: less bitter, cropped for whole loose heads or cut-and-come-again leaves. Varieties: Pan di Zucchero (sugarloaf, mild), Catalogna (asparagus chicory, eaten as cooked greens). For a first chicory: Palla Rossa heading type — most rewarding visual return."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, May to July", "text": "Late-spring to early-summer sowing. Earlier sowings bolt; later sowings don't size up before winter. Direct-sow in shallow drills 1 cm deep, 30 cm between rows. Germination in 7–14 days. Forcing chicory: sow in late May–early June for autumn root harvest. Heading chicory: sow in June–July for autumn heads. Leaf chicory: sow successionally May–August for continuous cropping."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to type-specific spacing", "text": "Forcing types: thin to 15 cm — fat single tap roots are the goal. Heading types: thin to 25–30 cm — needs space to form a tight cabbage-like head. Leaf types: thin to 15–20 cm or grow as a thicker cut-and-come-again row."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water consistently in dry spells", "text": "Chicory bolts to flower in drought stress, and heading types fail to form proper heads in dry conditions. Weekly deep watering in summer dry spells. Mulch with garden compost to conserve moisture. Heading chicory needs consistent moisture from sowing through to autumn maturity."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Force witloof roots from November", "text": "The Belgian classic. In late October–November, lift fully-grown chicory roots from the bed. Trim the leaves down to 2–3 cm above the crown and trim the root to 15–20 cm long. Pack roots upright in a bucket or box of moist sand or compost, with just the crowns showing. Cover completely with a black bin to exclude all light (essential for blanching). Keep at 10–15°C in a shed, cellar, or unheated greenhouse. Chicons form in 3–5 weeks: tight pale yellow-white spear-shapes 12–15 cm long. Snap chicons off at the base; replace cover; second chicons may form a few weeks later."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Harvest heading chicory in autumn", "text": "Palla Rossa and other heading types: from late September onwards, cut whole heads when they feel firm and tight (like a small loose cabbage). The colour intensifies in cold weather — heads sown for autumn become more deeply red after a few light frosts. Some types overwinter outdoors for spring cropping in southern UK; in cold areas, harvest before December and store cool (2–3 weeks)."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Use the bitterness as a feature", "text": "Chicory's slight bitterness is the point, not a defect. Balancing flavours: chicory pairs perfectly with sweet, rich, or fatty ingredients — orange, honey, blue cheese, walnuts, bacon, butter, cream. Cooking reduces the bitterness — braised chicons in stock are milder than raw radicchio salad. Blanching (forcing under dark cover) reduces bitterness most of all — that's why witloof chicons taste so different from sun-grown leaves."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Protect from slugs and snails", "text": "Chicory is a slug-magnet in its young seedling stage. Apply at sowing: wool pellets along the row, or nematodes watered into the soil. Hand-pick slugs in the evening after rain. Mature plants resist slug damage better but young rows can be shredded overnight. Heading types are particularly vulnerable when the head is forming — slugs hide inside the developing heart."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I force chicory for winter chicons?", "answer": "The Belgian witloof tradition. Sow Witloof Zoom or similar forcing variety in late May. Let the plants develop tap roots through summer. In late October–November, lift the roots; trim leaves to 2 cm, trim root to 15–20 cm. Pack roots upright in a bucket of moist sand/compost, just the crowns showing. Cover completely with a black bin or thick black plastic to exclude all light. Keep at 10–15°C for 3–5 weeks. The pale chicons (tight 12–15 cm spears) form; snap off at the base. Second chicons may follow on remaining root."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between chicory and endive?", "answer": "Same family, different species, different uses. Chicory (Cichorium intybus): hardy perennial (annual in cultivation), upright growth, bitter flavour, three main types — forcing/witloof, heading/radicchio, leaf/sugarloaf. Endive (Cichorium endivia — see separate endive slug): hardy annual, flat or curly rosette growth, milder bitterness, autumn salad crop. Endive's two types are curly (frisée) and flat-leaved (escarole). Both are bitter salad greens but chicory tends to be sharper and more often cooked."},
    {"question": "Why is my chicory so bitter?", "answer": "Bitterness is the point of chicory. All chicory is bitter to some degree — that's why it's used as a counter-flavour to sweet, rich, fatty ingredients. To reduce bitterness: (1) Blanching — grow under dark cover (the witloof forcing method) for the mildest leaves. (2) Cooking — braising reduces bitterness significantly. (3) Choose mild varieties — Pan di Zucchero (sugarloaf) is the mildest. (4) Pair with sweet/rich flavours — orange, honey, butter, cheese, walnuts cut through the bitterness."},
    {"question": "When do I harvest chicory?", "answer": "Depends on type. Forcing chicory: harvest the roots in late October–November, then force indoors over winter for chicons in January–March. Heading chicory (radicchio): cut whole heads from late September onwards when firm and tight. Leaf chicory (sugarloaf, asparagus): cut-and-come-again from 6 weeks after sowing, or harvest whole heads at maturity. Asparagus chicory (Catalogna): cook the young flower stems as well as the leaves."},
    {"question": "What's the best chicory variety for the UK?", "answer": "For a first chicory experience: Palla Rossa radicchio — rose-coloured cabbage-like heads in autumn, dramatic visual return, classic Italian salad use. For Belgian witloof tradition: Witloof Zoom — bred for forcing chicons. For milder eating: Pan di Zucchero (sugarloaf) — large pale heads with less bitterness than red types. For something different: Treviso — elongated burgundy-red leaves, beautiful in autumn salads."},
    {"question": "Can I grow chicory in a pot?", "answer": "Possible but not optimal. Chicory has a deep taproot and wants 30+ cm of root space. Pot chicory: use a 30 cm pot, choose leaf or heading types (forcing chicory needs proper-sized roots for chicons). Water consistently. Pot heading chicory makes smaller heads than ground-grown but works for small gardens. For forcing: pot culture isn't ideal — the roots don't bulk up enough for substantial chicons. Better in the ground for that traditional Belgian winter crop."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'chicory';

-- From chilli.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow chilli seed indoors from January to early March — earlier than sweet peppers because hot chillies (Capsicum chinense like Habanero and Scotch Bonnet) need 25+ weeks from sowing to ripe fruit. Chillies are frost-tender (RHS H1c) and want consistent warmth, so a greenhouse, polytunnel, or sunny windowsill is essential in UK conditions. Self-pollinating, so a single plant in a pot crops well. Easy UK varieties: Apache (mild, compact, very prolific), Hungarian Hot Wax (medium), Jalapeño (medium, classic), Cayenne (hot but long season). Pick green for fresh use; leave on the plant to turn red, orange or yellow to develop full flavour and heat. Bring plants indoors in October for a second year — chillies are technically perennials.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow early indoors", "text": "January to early March in a heated propagator at 25°C. The hotter the variety, the earlier you need to sow: C. annuum (jalapeño, cayenne) from February; C. chinense (habanero, scotch bonnet, naga) from January. Germination 2–4 weeks."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose by heat and season length", "text": "For UK conditions: Apache (mild, dwarf, very productive), Hungarian Hot Wax (medium, easy), Jalapeño M (medium, classic, prolific), Cayenne Long Slim (hot but long season), Numex (medium, big fruits). For brave UK growers in a warm greenhouse: Habanero, Scotch Bonnet, Jamaican Hot. Skip Carolina Reaper and similar superhots unless you have a heated greenhouse."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pot on through spring", "text": "Move seedlings to 9 cm pots once they have 4 true leaves; then 1-litre at 15 cm tall; then final 5-litre pots from May. Don't let chillies get root-bound — they sulk and check growth permanently."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Final position by late May", "text": "Greenhouse / polytunnel border, conservatory, or warm sunny windowsill. Outdoor pots in sheltered south-facing positions from June only — and even then, indoor positions out-produce outdoor by 2-3x."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Feed and water", "text": "Weekly high-potash liquid feed from first flowers. Consistent watering — let the surface dry between waterings; chillies hate waterlogging. Higher heat varieties (habanero) actually develop hotter fruit under slight water stress."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Self-pollinating but help with a brush", "text": "A gentle tap of each flowering plant or a soft paintbrush stroked across the flowers improves fruit set, especially in greenhouse crops with poor air movement."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick green or wait for colour", "text": "Most chillies are edible green; flavour and heat develop fully only when the fruit ripens to red, orange or yellow (depending on cultivar). Picking green keeps the plant cropping longer; leaving fruit to colour gives the full flavour. Most UK growers do both."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Overwinter as a perennial", "text": "Chillies are technically tender perennials. Bring plants indoors in October, cut back hard, water sparingly, keep above 10°C. They restart in spring 4–6 weeks earlier than new seedlings — year-two plants give earlier and bigger crops."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow chilli seeds in the UK?", "answer": "January to March, depending on variety. Hot chillies (Capsicum chinense — habanero, scotch bonnet, naga) need the longest season, so sow January in a heated propagator. Medium chillies (jalapeño, cayenne, hungarian hot wax) sow February. Mild dwarf cultivars (Apache, NuMex Twilight) can wait until March. Earlier sowings give leggy plants by planting-out time; later sowings won't ripen before winter."},
    {"question": "Why are my chillies not turning red?", "answer": "Time. Chillies start green and turn to their final colour (red, orange, yellow, purple-brown) over the final 2–3 weeks before they're fully ripe. If your fruit is staying green into October, the plant didn't have enough warmth and time. Move it indoors to a sunny windowsill — fruit ripens off the plant for several more weeks if temperatures stay above 15°C."},
    {"question": "How hot is hot? What variety should I grow?", "answer": "Mild (Scoville 0–10,000): Apache, Sweet Hungarian, NuMex Twilight, Padron, Anaheim. Medium (10,000–100,000): Jalapeño, Cayenne, Hungarian Hot Wax, Tabasco. Hot (100,000–350,000): Habanero, Scotch Bonnet, Bird's Eye, Lemon Drop. Very hot (350,000+): Ghost Pepper, Naga Viper, Carolina Reaper. Start with medium varieties; UK conditions favour them and they're more usable in everyday cooking."},
    {"question": "Can I grow chillies on a windowsill?", "answer": "Excellent for windowsill growing. Compact varieties (Apache, NuMex Twilight, Prairie Fire) reach only 40 cm and crop heavily in 5-litre pots. A sunny south-facing windowsill gives a full crop. Larger varieties need bigger pots and ideally a greenhouse, but if you have a warm conservatory or south-facing windowsill with room for a 7.5-litre pot, even habaneros work."},
    {"question": "Can I save chilli plants over winter?", "answer": "Yes — chillies are tender perennials. In October, cut back the plant to a 30 cm stump, bring indoors to a frost-free spot (above 10°C, ideally above 15°C). Water very sparingly through winter. The plant restarts in March 4–6 weeks earlier than fresh seedlings, giving a bigger second-year crop. Pruning hard is the key."},
    {"question": "How do I store a glut of chillies?", "answer": "Fresh: 2 weeks in the fridge. Drying: thread on string and hang in an airy dry place — works well for thin-walled varieties (cayenne, bird's eye). Freezing: whole or chopped, freeze on trays then bag. Pickling: in vinegar and salt. Chilli oil and hot sauce are the artisan options for a big glut."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'chilli';

-- From chives.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant chives from a young clump in spring or autumn, in sun or light shade, in ordinary garden soil — they''re one of the easiest perennial herbs and a single clump gives years of pickings. Chives are hardy (RHS H5–H6) and die back to the ground in winter, returning each spring. Snip leaves at the base with scissors and they regrow within days; cut whole flushes through the summer. The pink-purple pompom flowers in May–June are edible (pull apart for sprinkling on salads) and a magnet for bees. Cut the whole clump back hard to 5 cm in mid-summer for a fresh flush of tender leaves. Divide every 3–4 years to keep it vigorous. Chives partner well with carrots (the onion scent confuses carrot fly) and roses (deter aphids).',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy a young clump in spring", "text": "Easier and faster than growing from seed. Garden centres sell small clumps in spring from £3–5; one clump is enough for a household and will multiply quickly. If you want garlic chives (Allium tuberosum — different species, flat leaves, white star flowers, garlicky flavour), buy those separately."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in sun or light shade", "text": "Chives tolerate most UK soils provided they don't sit waterlogged. Best in moisture-retentive soil that doesn't dry out; light shade in southern England keeps them lush in midsummer. Plant the clump at the same depth it was in the pot. Space clumps 25 cm apart if planting more than one."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pick by snipping at the base with scissors", "text": "Cut whole stems at the base (1 cm above soil) — don't shear the tips like a haircut, which leaves brown stubs. The clump regrows from the base within a few days. Pick little and often through spring and summer. Use fresh; chives don't dry well (they lose flavour) but freeze fine in ice cubes with water."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Let some flowers bloom for bees, then cut back hard", "text": "Chive flowers (pink-mauve pompoms) appear in May–June. They're beautiful, edible (pull apart and scatter on potato salad or eggs), and an outstanding bee plant. After flowering, cut the entire clump back to 5 cm — this triggers a fresh flush of tender leaves for the rest of summer."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water in dry spells", "text": "Chives sulk and brown off in long dry summer spells. A weekly soak in droughts (or daily for potted plants) keeps the leaves tender. Lush, bright green leaves = good moisture. Yellow-tipped leaves = too dry or the clump needs dividing."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Divide every 3–4 years", "text": "After 3–4 years a clump gets congested and the centre starts to die out. In early spring or autumn, lift the whole clump, split it with a spade into 4–6 sections, replant the best outer pieces, compost the dead centre. Free new plants and rejuvenated original."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Grow garlic chives alongside for a longer season", "text": "Garlic chives (Allium tuberosum) start later (July), flower later (August–September with white star flowers), and have flat strap-like leaves with a definite garlic flavour. Pair them with regular chives and you have chive harvest from May through to October."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Companion plant near carrots and roses", "text": "The onion scent of chives confuses carrot root fly — plant chives around carrot rows. Chives also deter aphids on roses; a clump at the base of each rose bush is the classic English cottage-garden pairing. Don't plant near peas or beans (alliums slow legume growth)."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I keep chives producing tender leaves all summer?", "answer": "Two things. (1) Snip whole stems at the base with scissors, never shear the tops — base-cutting triggers regrowth, top-shearing leaves brown stubs. (2) Cut the entire clump down to 5 cm in mid-summer after the flowers fade — this resets the clump and produces a fresh flush of tender new growth for the rest of summer. Without this mid-summer reset, the leaves get tough and yellow."},
    {"question": "Are chive flowers edible?", "answer": "Yes, and good. The pink-mauve pompom flowers in May–June are edible — pull individual florets apart and scatter on salads, eggs, or potato dishes. Mild onion flavour, beautiful colour. Chive flower vinegar (steep flowers in white wine vinegar for a week) turns a stunning pink. Pick flowers just after opening; older ones get tough and fade in colour."},
    {"question": "Can I grow chives in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — perfect for pots. A 25 cm pot is enough for a clump. Water more often than in-ground plants (every 2–3 days in summer); chives in pots dry out quickly and brown off. Divide and re-pot every 2 years (faster than in-ground, because pots get congested quicker). A pot on a kitchen windowsill or doorstep is the most-used chive position in UK gardens."},
    {"question": "How do I divide chives?", "answer": "Early spring or early autumn. Lift the whole clump with a fork. Cut or pull it into 4–6 sections, each with roots and several growing points. Replant the best outer sections at the same depth in fresh soil; compost the dead congested centre. Water in. Free plants — give the spares to neighbours."},
    {"question": "Do chives come back every year?", "answer": "Yes — they're a perennial. Chives die back to the ground in late autumn (leaves go yellow and disappear) and reappear from the bulbs the following March–April. The clump gets bigger each year. They survive any UK winter without protection (RHS H5–H6). In a mild year you might see a few green leaves through winter; in a hard year they retreat fully underground."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between chives and garlic chives?", "answer": "Different species, different flavour, different season. Chives (Allium schoenoprasum): hollow tube-like leaves, mild onion flavour, pink pompom flowers in May–June. Garlic chives (Allium tuberosum): flat strap-like leaves, definite garlic flavour, white star-shaped flowers in August–September. Grow both — they complement each other and extend the picking season from May right through to October."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'chives';

-- From clematis.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant clematis in spring or autumn, with the crown 7–10 cm BELOW the soil surface (deeper than the pot) — this is the single most important planting rule, and it protects against the devastating clematis wilt disease that kills shallowly-planted plants. The pruning group matters enormously: Group 1 (flowers on old wood — montana, alpina) needs almost no pruning; Group 2 (flowers on old AND new wood — most large-flowered hybrids) gets a light tidy in February; Group 3 (flowers on new wood — viticella, late large-flowered, C. tangutica) gets cut back hard to 30 cm in February. Mismatch the pruning group and you''ll cut off all the flowers. Plant in a sunny position with roots in shade, head in sun — a paving slab or low planting at the base shades the roots. Most clematis are hardy (RHS H5–H6).',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick a clematis matched to your scale and season", "text": "Vigorous spring climbers (Group 1): C. montana (3–10 m, May, white/pink), C. alpina (2–3 m, April, nodding bells), C. armandii (4–6 m evergreen, April, scented white). Large-flowered summer hybrids (Group 2): Nelly Moser (pink-striped), Henryi (white), Niobe (deep red), The President (purple) — 2–3 m, large saucer flowers May–July. Viticellas and late large-flowered (Group 3): Etoile Violette, Polish Spirit, Madame Julia Correvon, Princess Diana — 2–4 m, smaller flowers but longer season (June–September). The most reliable group for UK gardens. Texensis types (Group 3): tulip-shaped flowers, the only red-flowered group. For a first clematis: pick a Group 3 viticella — easy pruning, disease-resistant, prolific."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant 7–10 cm below the soil — DEEPER than the pot", "text": "The single most important clematis rule. Standard advice for most plants is plant at the same depth as the pot. For clematis, plant 7–10 cm deeper — the crown should sit below soil level. Why: clematis wilt is a fungal disease that attacks stems above the crown; deep planting means new shoots can emerge from below soil if the upper stems are infected, saving the plant. Without deep planting, a clematis-wilt attack often kills the entire plant."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in sun with roots in shade", "text": "The classic clematis paradox: top growth wants 4–6 hours sun for flowering, but roots want cool moist conditions. Three ways to shade the roots: (1) a paving slab or large stone at the base; (2) low planting partners (hardy geraniums, lady's mantle) growing over the root zone; (3) a thick organic mulch around the base. Without root shading, clematis sulks, flowers poorly, and dries out in summer."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Know your pruning group", "text": "The most important clematis knowledge. Pruning the wrong group at the wrong time removes all the flowers. Group 1 (flowers on previous year's wood — montana, alpina, armandii): minimal pruning — just remove dead/damaged branches AFTER flowering (June). Group 2 (flowers on both old and new wood — large-flowered hybrids like Nelly Moser): light tidy in February, then a second light prune after first flowering for repeat blooms. Group 3 (flowers on current year's wood — viticellas, late large-flowered): cut all stems down to 30 cm above ground in February. If unsure, check the cultivar name and look up its group; getting this right is the difference between a flowering plant and disappointment."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Give a sturdy support", "text": "Clematis climb by twining leaf petioles around thin supports. Strong wire trellis, vine eyes and wires, or a sturdy obelisk are essential. Fences need wire or trellis added — clematis can't grip flat wood. Don't expect clematis to climb a bare post or tree trunk — they need the thin support to grip. The support also needs to bear weight; mature montana types can weigh 30+ kg."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Feed and water generously", "text": "Clematis are hungry and thirsty. In spring: a thick mulch of garden compost or well-rotted manure around the base, plus a handful of slow-release general-purpose fertiliser. Through summer: deep weekly watering in dry spells — clematis flop and drop flowers when stressed. Pot-grown clematis need much more frequent watering. Feed with liquid tomato food every 2 weeks during flowering for the best blooms."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Manage clematis wilt", "text": "Clematis wilt (Calophoma clematidina) is the main UK clematis disease. Symptoms: stems suddenly wilt and turn black, often after first flowering, sometimes the whole plant collapses overnight. Three responses. (1) Cut affected stems to below the wilt point (often to ground level); the plant usually re-shoots from the deep-planted crown. (2) Keep watering — the roots are usually fine. (3) For severe repeated wilt, the resistant Group 3 viticella types and species clematis are far less affected — replace the susceptible large-flowered Group 2 hybrid with a viticella."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Cut to the ground every 3–5 years (renovation)", "text": "For an overgrown, tangled, bare-at-base mess: clematis tolerate radical renovation. In February, cut every stem down to 30 cm above the ground. The plant resprouts vigorously from the crown — full flowering normally returns the following year. This works for all three groups, though Group 1 will skip a flowering season; Groups 2 and 3 usually flower the same year on new growth."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I prune a clematis?", "answer": "Depends entirely on the pruning group — and getting it wrong removes all the flowers. Group 1 (montana, alpina, armandii — flowers on previous year's wood): prune AFTER flowering, just removing dead/damaged stems. Group 2 (large-flowered hybrids like Nelly Moser, Niobe, Henryi): light tidy in February, then a second light prune after first flowering. Group 3 (viticellas, late large-flowered hybrids, C. tangutica): cut to 30 cm above ground in February. If you don't know your group: check the cultivar name in a clematis catalogue or RHS database. If buying a new clematis, the label should state the pruning group."},
    {"question": "Why did my clematis suddenly die overnight?", "answer": "Almost certainly clematis wilt (Calophoma clematidina) — a fungal disease that causes stems to suddenly wilt and turn black, often after the first flowering. Three steps. (1) Cut all affected stems back hard to below the wilt point (often to ground level). (2) Keep watering and feeding — the deep-planted crown is usually fine. (3) The plant will usually re-shoot from below the soil within 4–6 weeks (which is why deep planting matters so much). For chronic wilt problems: replace susceptible Group 2 hybrids with disease-resistant Group 3 viticellas."},
    {"question": "Why is my clematis not flowering?", "answer": "Five common causes. (1) Wrong pruning — cutting at the wrong time removes flowering wood. Identify the group. (2) Too young — most clematis take 2–3 years to settle and flower properly. (3) Hot dry roots — shade the root zone, mulch, water deeply. (4) Not enough sun on the top — needs 4–6 hours direct sun for flowering. (5) Wrong feed — high nitrogen produces leaves not flowers; switch to tomato food for blooms."},
    {"question": "What's the best clematis variety for the UK?", "answer": "For a first or only clematis, pick a Group 3 viticella: easy pruning (cut to 30 cm in February), disease-resistant, prolific from June to September. Etoile Violette (deep violet, vigorous), Madame Julia Correvon (wine red), Polish Spirit (deep purple, AGM), Princess Diana (texensis, tulip-shaped pink). For a big spring climber: Montana var. rubens (pink) or Montana Elizabeth (pale pink, scented). Large-flowered hybrids are striking but prone to wilt; pick named cultivars carefully."},
    {"question": "Why do I need to plant a clematis so deep?", "answer": "Wilt protection. Clematis wilt is the most common UK clematis disease, attacking stems just above the crown. If the clematis is planted at pot depth, all the stems are vulnerable and the whole plant can die. Plant 7–10 cm deeper so the crown sits below soil level — new shoots can then emerge from below ground if upper stems are wilted, saving the plant. This single planting trick prevents 90% of clematis losses in UK gardens. Apply to every clematis you plant."},
    {"question": "Can I grow clematis in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — Group 3 viticellas are best. A 45 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed with garden compost and slow-release fertiliser. Plant 7–10 cm deeper than the pot the clematis came in (same deep-planting rule applies). Water consistently — pot clematis dry out fast and are quick to flop. Feed with tomato food fortnightly during flowering. Repot or top-dress every 2 years. Avoid vigorous montanas (too big) and large-flowered hybrids prone to wilt — viticellas are the pot-friendly group."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'clematis';

-- From coriander.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow coriander seed direct from late March to August in successional batches every three weeks. It bolts fast in heat — UK summer is exactly the wrong time for leaf coriander, so sow in spring and again in autumn for cool-season cropping. The leaf (cilantro to Americans) is what most home gardeners want; the seed is the spice and gathers naturally once the plant bolts. Coriander is hardy (RHS H4) and tolerates light frost as young plants. Choose slow-bolting cultivars (Calypso, Confetti) for any summer sowings. Sow thinly into well-drained soil; pick leaves from the bottom of the plant before flower stems appear. One plant gives leaf for about three weeks, so plant successionally.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "Late March to August in successional batches every 3 weeks. 1 cm drill, sow thinly, cover lightly. Coriander hates root disturbance; sow where it's to grow rather than transplanting."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose slow-bolting cultivars for summer", "text": "Calypso, Confetti, Cilantro (the variety, not the herb). These run to seed weeks later than standard coriander in hot weather."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to 5 cm", "text": "When seedlings have 2 true leaves, thin to 5 cm apart. Use the thinnings — even small coriander leaves are useful in the kitchen."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Provide afternoon shade in summer", "text": "From late June, coriander bolts in heat. Plant under taller crops or use shade netting. Cool-season sowings in autumn produce the longest pick of the year."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick from the bottom", "text": "Pick whole stems from the base of the plant. The plant continues producing from the centre until it bolts."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Let some plants run to seed", "text": "For coriander seed, let one or two plants bolt and flower. Wait until the seed heads turn brown, then cut whole plants and dry under cover for a fortnight. Shake seeds out into a tray."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Sow again for autumn cropping", "text": "Mid-August to mid-September sowings produce leaf coriander through to first frost in November."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why does my coriander always bolt?", "answer": "Coriander bolts fast in heat — anything above 25°C and long summer days triggers it within weeks. UK summer is the worst time for leaf coriander. Sow early (March–April) and late (August–September) instead of June–July. Choose slow-bolting cultivars (Calypso, Confetti) for any summer sowings. Once a plant bolts you can't stop it; pull it and re-sow."},
    {"question": "When should I sow coriander seed in the UK?", "answer": "Late March under cloches in southern England; April outdoors. Successional sowings every 3 weeks until early June, then skip July (too hot), and resume in August for an autumn crop. Hardy varieties can be sown into September for winter cropping under cloches. Coriander wants soil above 10°C to germinate."},
    {"question": "Is coriander an annual or biennial?", "answer": "Annual — grows from seed, flowers, sets seed, dies, all in one season. Most plants bolt and die in 8–12 weeks. The seed they set is the spice (called coriander seed in the UK; Americans call the seed coriander but the leaf cilantro). Save your own seed by letting a few plants bolt and harvesting when the heads turn brown."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between coriander and cilantro?", "answer": "Same plant. Coriander is the UK name and refers to both leaf and seed. Cilantro is the American name for the leaf only (they call the seed coriander). Some recipes specify coriander leaf to disambiguate. UK-grown plants are always called coriander."},
    {"question": "Can I grow coriander in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — and it's often easier than outdoors because you can move pots into shade in hot weather to delay bolting. A 30 cm pot grows enough for a household. Successional sowings every 3 weeks keep the supply going. Indoor windowsill sowings work year-round but the leaves are weaker-flavoured than outdoor."},
    {"question": "When is coriander ready to harvest?", "answer": "Leaf: from about 4 weeks after sowing, when stems are 15 cm long. Pick whole stems from the base. Seed: 12–16 weeks after sowing, when the seed heads have turned brown and the seeds rattle when shaken. Cut whole plants, hang under cover to dry for a fortnight, then thresh."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'coriander';

-- From cosmos.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow cosmos seed indoors from late March to early May for transplanting, or direct outdoors from late May. Cosmos is a frost-tender annual (RHS H1c) and one of the most reliable, prolific cut flowers in any UK garden — a single plant produces dozens of stems from July to first frost. Plant 45 cm apart in any decent soil with sun for most of the day; rich soil actually reduces flowering, so don''t over-feed. Deadhead religiously — cosmos stops flowering as soon as seed heads form. Purity (pure white), Sensation Mix (mixed pink, white, magenta), Apricot Lemonade (apricot to lemon shades) and Versailles series (long stems) are the easy starters. Self-seeds modestly in mild gardens.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow indoors or direct", "text": "Late March to early May indoors in modules at 18°C for transplanting. Late May direct outdoors. Don't sow earlier than this; cosmos seedlings hate cold and grow leggy on a windowsill. 5 mm deep, 2 seeds per cell or per station outdoors."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Don't over-feed the soil", "text": "Cosmos flowers best in average-to-poor soil. Rich soil and high nitrogen push leaves at the expense of flowers — the cosmos in a heap of leaves with no blooms failure mode is almost always over-feeding. Don't dig in manure before planting; standard garden soil is fine."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant out after the last frost", "text": "Mid-May in southern England; early June further north. Harden plants off for 7–10 days. Space 45 cm apart in a sunny spot. Stake taller varieties (Sensation, Purity) early — they reach 1.2 m and topple in wind."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Pinch out the tip", "text": "When plants are 30 cm tall, pinch out the growing tip to encourage branching. This doubles the eventual flower count."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water modestly", "text": "Water in well at planting; then only in genuinely dry weeks. Cosmos prefers slightly dry conditions to bog-rich ones."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Deadhead religiously", "text": "Cut spent flowers back to the next bud or side-shoot. Cosmos stops flowering the instant seed pods form — so deadheading is non-negotiable. Pick for the vase regularly too; it's a cut-and-come-again cut flower."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Save seed in autumn", "text": "Let the last flowers of the season go to seed. Cut the dried seed heads in October; rub the seeds out into a paper envelope; store in a cool dry place. Cosmos comes true from seed (it's not F1) — Sensation, Purity, and other open-pollinated cultivars give the same flowers next year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow cosmos in the UK?", "answer": "Late March to early May indoors in modules; late May direct outdoors. Don't sow earlier — cosmos hates cold soil and damp germination conditions. A single sowing of 8–12 plants gives flowers from July through first frost in October."},
    {"question": "Why is my cosmos producing leaves but no flowers?", "answer": "The soil is too rich. Cosmos flowers on lean, average-to-poor soil; rich, manured beds give all leaf and no bloom. Switch to a high-potash feed (or no feed at all) and stop watering except in genuine drought. The plant should look slightly under-cared-for to flower its best."},
    {"question": "How tall do cosmos grow?", "answer": "Standard cultivars (Sensation, Purity) reach 1.2–1.5 m — back of a border. Versailles series 1 m, good for cutting and middle borders. Dwarf cultivars (Sonata, Cosmic, Apollo) reach only 45–60 cm — ideal for containers and edges. Cosmos sulphureus (orange cosmos) is a different species, shorter and more heat-loving."},
    {"question": "Can I grow cosmos in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Excellent for containers. Dwarf varieties (Sonata, Cosmic Orange) in 30-litre pots; standard varieties in larger 50-litre containers with staking. Use peat-free compost without added feed (avoid the cosmos in leaf without flowers trap). Water consistently; deadhead daily for the long run."},
    {"question": "When does cosmos start and stop flowering?", "answer": "First flowers from mid-July onwards, depending on sowing date. Peak August through to October. Stops at the first hard frost, which kills the plant outright. With a March indoor sowing and an aggressive deadheading regime, you get 14–16 weeks of continuous picking."},
    {"question": "Does cosmos self-seed?", "answer": "Modestly. Open-pollinated cultivars (Purity, Sensation) drop seed in autumn that germinates in spring in mild gardens, particularly on bare soil. Self-sown seedlings are unpredictable in colour (especially from Sensation Mix) but often grow more vigorously than sown-and-planted-out plants. F1 hybrids don't reliably self-seed."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'cosmos';

-- From courgette.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow courgette seed indoors from late April, or direct from late May once the soil is warm. Plant out after the last frost: mid-May in southern England, early June in the north. Courgettes are frost-tender (RHS H1c) and need warmth to set fruit. Space them 90 cm apart in rich, deep soil with plenty of compost dug in — they''re hungry plants. From late July a single plant produces a fruit every two or three days; pick them at 15 cm before they balloon into marrows. The UK summer suits them perfectly, which is why they''re the easiest crop to over-grow. Watch for powdery mildew in dry weather.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow seed indoors", "text": "Late April to early May. Set one seed on its side in a 9 cm pot of multi-purpose compost. 18–22°C on a windowsill or in a propagator. Germination in 5–7 days."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pot on if needed", "text": "If the roots fill the pot before it's safe to plant out, pot on to a 1-litre pot. Don't let courgette seedlings get pot-bound — they sulk and check growth."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Fork compost or well-rotted manure into the bed in early May. Courgettes are hungry plants; the richer the better. A sunny sheltered position is essential."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Harden off and plant out", "text": "Harden plants off for 7–10 days. Plant out from mid-May in southern England, early June in the north. Space 90 cm apart for trailing varieties, 60 cm for bush. Water in well."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water and feed", "text": "Water at the base every few days; never on the leaves. A weekly high-potash feed from the first flowers helps fruit set. Mulch with grass clippings to hold moisture."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick young and often", "text": "From late July, check daily. Pick when 15 cm long — about every two or three days at the peak. A week away on holiday guarantees marrows."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for mildew", "text": "Powdery mildew (white dusty patches on leaves) is common from August onwards, especially in dry weather. Mulch heavily, water at the base, remove affected leaves. The plants usually crop through it anyway."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow courgette seeds in the UK?", "answer": "Late April to early May indoors; late May direct outdoors in southern England. Don't sow too early — courgette seedlings hate cold and grow leggy on a windowsill. Two plants per gardener is usually enough; one for a single person. They produce more than you'll know what to do with."},
    {"question": "Why are my courgette flowers not setting fruit?", "answer": "Either you've only got male flowers (which open first, on long stalks, with no bulge at the base), or the female flowers aren't being pollinated. Male flowers appear 1–2 weeks before females — wait, both will come. If females drop without setting, the weather is too cool or wet for pollinators; hand-pollinate by transferring pollen from a male flower with a small paintbrush."},
    {"question": "How big should I let my courgettes grow?", "answer": "15 cm is the ideal length — tender and full of flavour. Over 20 cm the texture coarsens; over 30 cm they're marrows, which need long cooking and stuffing to be palatable. Pick every two or three days during the peak; miss a week and you'll have marrows whether you wanted them or not."},
    {"question": "Can I grow courgettes in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — bush varieties (Defender, Patio Star) do well in 30-litre containers. Use rich compost; water consistently; feed weekly with a high-potash liquid feed. Place in a sunny sheltered spot. Yields are lower than ground plants but you'll still get more fruit than you need from one plant."},
    {"question": "How do I treat powdery mildew on courgettes?", "answer": "Remove the worst affected leaves to improve airflow. Water only at the base of the plant, never the foliage. Mulch heavily to retain soil moisture — mildew is worse when roots are dry. A milk spray (1 part milk to 9 parts water, weekly) helps; cultivar Defender has good mildew resistance."},
    {"question": "Are courgette flowers edible?", "answer": "Yes — pick male flowers (long thin stems and no fruit forming), stuff or batter and fry. Pick in the morning when they're fully open. Picking male flowers doesn't reduce fruit production; you only need a few to pollinate the females. Female flowers can be eaten with the small courgette attached."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'courgette';

-- From crocus.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant crocus corms in autumn (September–November) at 5–7 cm deep in well-drained soil — they''re hardy spring-flowering bulbs that produce vivid cup-shaped flowers in February–March, one of the earliest UK garden colours. Crocuses are hardy (RHS H6–H7) and split into spring-flowering types (the popular garden species — C. tommasinianus, C. chrysanthus, C. vernus Dutch hybrids) and autumn-flowering types (C. sativus = saffron, C. speciosus = autumn crocus, plant in summer for autumn bloom). For UK lawn naturalising, "Tommasinianus" is unbeatable — small, vigorous, mouse-and-squirrel-resistant, spreads into drifts. Let foliage die back naturally before mowing — same rule as snowdrops. Squirrels dig and eat crocus corms (especially larger Dutch hybrids); protect with chicken wire or grow squirrel-resistant C. tommasinianus.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick spring or autumn flowering type", "text": "Spring crocus — the popular garden choice, plant autumn for February–March flowers. Tommasinianus (C. tommasinianus): small, vigorous, mouse-and-squirrel-resistant, the best for lawn naturalising. Chrysanthus types (snow crocus): early (Feb), small flowers in white/yellow/blue. Dutch hybrids (C. vernus): bigger flowers (March), large goblet shape — but more squirrel-vulnerable. Autumn crocus — plant in late summer for September–October flowers. Speciosus (C. speciosus): blue-violet, large flowers, late September–October. Saffron crocus (C. sativus): purple with vivid orange-red stigmas (the spice saffron), late October–November. For a first crocus: Tommasinianus for lawn naturalising, or Chrysanthus types in pots and borders."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant corms in autumn (spring-flowering types)", "text": "September to November for spring crocus, before soil cools below 5°C. Plant 5–7 cm deep, 5 cm apart for natural drifts, pointed end up. In lawns: lift small squares of turf, scatter corms, replace turf — within a year you can't see the disturbance. In borders: plant in groups of 15–20 minimum for visual impact (a single crocus is invisible)."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant autumn-flowering types in late summer", "text": "Saffron crocus and autumn crocus: plant corms in July or August for September–October flowering. Same depth (5–7 cm) and spacing. Don't confuse autumn-flowering crocus with autumn crocus (Colchicum) — different plant entirely with much larger flowers and broader strap-like leaves later in spring."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Pick a well-drained sunny position", "text": "Crocuses want full sun and sharp drainage — the chief enemy is wet-rot in waterlogged soil. Position: sunny lawns, well-drained borders, gravel gardens, rockeries. Avoid: clay that holds water, deep shade (flowers don't open in shade). For lawn use: the lawn dries out reasonably quickly after rain."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Protect against squirrels (and mice)", "text": "Squirrels dig and eat crocus corms, especially larger Dutch hybrid types. Three defences. (1) Plant squirrel-resistant species: Crocus tommasinianus is largely ignored by squirrels (the corms don't smell appealing). (2) Chicken wire over plantings: bury wire mesh 5 cm below the soil surface; flowers grow through, squirrels can't dig. (3) Plant deeper: 10 cm deep instead of 5–7 cm — squirrels rarely dig that far."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Let foliage die back naturally", "text": "Same rule as snowdrops and daffodils: don't cut, mow, or tidy crocus foliage until it dies back naturally in May. The grass-like leaves photosynthesise to fuel next year's flowers. In lawns: delay mowing the crocus area until mid-May. Skipping this kills the colony within 2–3 years."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Bee plant for late-winter pollinators", "text": "The other reason to plant crocuses: bees. Early-emerging queen bumblebees depend on crocus and other late-winter flowers for the first nectar. A patch of crocuses on a sunny February day is a constant buzz of bumblebee activity. Tommasinianus and Dutch hybrids are particularly bee-attractive. Don't deadhead or disturb during flowering — let the bees work."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Harvest saffron (if growing C. sativus)", "text": "The world's most expensive spice from your own garden. Each Crocus sativus flower contains three thread-like deep orange-red stigmas — the spice saffron. Harvest: on dry mornings just after the flowers open, pick the whole flower into a tray, then carefully pluck out the three orange stigmas from each. Dry the stigmas on paper in a warm dry place for 2–3 days. Yield: 100–200 flowers produce about 1 g of dry saffron (worth ~£10). A bed of 200 corms produces enough saffron for a year's risottos."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I plant crocus?", "answer": "Spring-flowering crocuses: plant corms in September to November, before soil cools below 5°C. Autumn-flowering crocuses (including saffron): plant corms in July or August for autumn bloom the same year. For lawn naturalising: late September is ideal — soil is still warm but autumn rains have started. Plant 5–7 cm deep, 5 cm apart, in groups of 15–20 minimum for visual impact."},
    {"question": "Why are squirrels digging up my crocuses?", "answer": "Squirrels eat crocus corms — particularly the larger Dutch hybrid types. Three defences. (1) Plant squirrel-resistant species: Crocus tommasinianus has corms that squirrels rarely touch; the standard for lawn naturalising. (2) Chicken wire under planting: bury 1 cm mesh wire 5 cm below the corms; flowers grow through, squirrels can't dig down. (3) Plant deeper: 10 cm instead of 5–7 cm — squirrels rarely excavate that deep."},
    {"question": "When do I cut crocus foliage down?", "answer": "Never — let it die back naturally in May. The grass-like leaves photosynthesise to fuel next year's flowers; cutting them early kills the colony within 2–3 years. In lawns: delay mowing the crocus area until at least mid-May, by which time leaves have yellowed and collapsed. In borders: leave the yellowing foliage; tuck it gently under emerging perennials if it looks tatty."},
    {"question": "Will crocuses naturalise in my lawn?", "answer": "Yes — exactly where they thrive. Lawn crocuses are one of the great UK garden classics. Best species for lawn naturalising: Crocus tommasinianus — small, vigorous, mouse-and-squirrel-resistant, multiplies by both seed and bulb division. Plant in autumn: lift small squares of turf, scatter corms, replace turf. Don't mow until mid-May. Over 5–10 years the colony spreads into a drift."},
    {"question": "How do I grow my own saffron?", "answer": "Plant Crocus sativus corms in July–August, 10 cm deep, in full sun in well-drained soil. Flowers appear in late October–November. Harvest: pick the whole flower as it opens, carefully pluck out the three orange-red stigmas. Dry on paper in a warm dry place for 2–3 days. Yield: 100–200 flowers per gram of dried saffron. A 200-corm bed produces about 1 g per year — enough for several saffron dishes. Best crop in dry sunny UK summers (which precondition the corms)."},
    {"question": "Can I grow crocuses in pots?", "answer": "Yes — pots showcase crocuses brilliantly. Use a 25 cm pot of peat-free compost with grit added for drainage. Plant 20–30 corms per pot, 5 cm deep. For spring crocus: plant in October, leave outside in a cool spot through winter. Move to a sunny porch or doorstep in February for the flower display. Watering: don't waterlog. Replant in the garden after the foliage dies back in May — pot crocuses rarely flower a second year in the same compost."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'crocus';

-- From cucumber.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow cucumber seed indoors from mid-April; plant out after the last frost into a greenhouse or polytunnel border (or sheltered south-facing outdoor spot for ridge types). Cucumbers are frost-tender (RHS H1c) and split sharply by type: greenhouse / indoor cucumbers (Carmen, Telegraph, Femspot — all all-female F1 hybrids) want consistent warmth above 18°C and produce long smooth fruits; ridge / outdoor cucumbers (Marketmore, Burpless Tasty Green) tolerate UK summer outdoors in southern England, with shorter prickly fruits. Water consistently — drought makes cucumbers bitter. Feed weekly with high-potash from first flowers. Pick fruit young (15–20 cm) and often; leaving fruit on the plant shuts down further flowering. Powdery mildew is the main disease in late summer.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose your type", "text": "Indoor / greenhouse types (Carmen F1, Telegraph, Femspot, Petita): all-female F1 hybrids — no male flowers means no pollination, no bitter fruit, no seeds. Long smooth fruits. Need 18°C+ consistently. Outdoor / ridge types (Marketmore, Burpless Tasty Green, Crystal Apple): hardier, tolerant of cooler conditions, shorter spiny fruits, need a sunny sheltered spot. Don't mix the two — outdoor types need bee pollination; indoor types are bred to avoid it."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow seed indoors", "text": "Mid-April. Set seeds on edge in 9 cm pots of multi-purpose compost, 2 cm deep, 1 seed per pot. 18–22°C in a propagator or warm windowsill. Germination in 5–7 days."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pot on once or twice", "text": "Move to 1-litre pots when roots fill the smaller; then to final position. Don't let cucumber seedlings get pot-bound — they check growth and never fully recover."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant in final position", "text": "Greenhouse / polytunnel: late April to mid-May into border soil or 7.5-litre containers. Train up vertical strings or canes. Outdoor: after last frost (mid-May south, early June north) into sheltered south-facing position. Plant on a slight mound for drainage."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Train indoor types vertically", "text": "Indoor cucumbers crop on vertical strings. Tie the main stem to a string from floor to greenhouse roof; pinch out side-shoots above the second leaf; remove tendrils and male flowers (if any appear on supposedly all-female F1s)."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Feed and water", "text": "Weekly high-potash feed from first flowers. Water consistently — irregular watering is the single biggest cause of bitter cucumbers. Water deeply 2–3 times a week; never let the soil dry out; mulch heavily."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for powdery mildew", "text": "White dusty patches on leaves from late summer onwards. Worse in dry-after-wet conditions. Remove affected leaves; improve airflow; spray weekly with diluted milk (1 part to 9 parts water) on a calm dry day. Cultivars like Marketmore have some natural resistance."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick young and often", "text": "Pick when fruit is 15–20 cm for ridge types, 25–30 cm for greenhouse types. Pick every 2–3 days during the peak — leaving fruit on the plant shuts down further flowering. A single greenhouse plant can give 30–40 cucumbers across a season; outdoor 15–20."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow cucumber seeds in the UK?", "answer": "Mid-April indoors for both greenhouse and outdoor types. Greenhouse plants go into the border or pots in late April–early May; outdoor plants go out after the last frost (mid-May south, early June north). Don't sow direct outside — cucumbers hate cold wet soil."},
    {"question": "Greenhouse or outdoor cucumbers — what's the difference?", "answer": "Greenhouse types (Carmen F1, Telegraph, Femspot): all-female F1 hybrids bred to fruit without pollination. No seeds, no bitterness, long smooth fruit. Need consistent warmth above 18°C; greenhouse / polytunnel / conservatory only. Outdoor / ridge types (Marketmore, Burpless Tasty Green): hardier; pollinated by bees; shorter spinier fruit; some seeds; can be slightly bitter if stressed. Tolerate UK outdoor conditions in southern England. Pick one type per growing area."},
    {"question": "Why are my cucumbers bitter?", "answer": "Inconsistent watering — the dominant cause. Cucumbers stressed by drought (even brief) produce cucurbitacin, the compound that makes them bitter. Water deeply 2–3 times a week; mulch heavily; never let the soil dry out. Other causes: cold weather (under 15°C), pollination of greenhouse all-female types by stray male flowers (remove any male flowers in greenhouse types), or simply a stressed plant."},
    {"question": "Why are my cucumber leaves going yellow with white patches?", "answer": "Powdery mildew (Podosphaera xanthii) — the most common late-season cucumber disease in UK greenhouses and outdoor crops. Worse in dry-after-wet weather. Remove the worst-affected leaves; improve airflow (ventilate the greenhouse, space plants further apart); water at the base only. A milk spray (1:9 milk:water) weekly helps. Resistant cultivars (Marketmore More 76, Vorgebirgstrauben) suffer less."},
    {"question": "How do I prune indoor cucumbers?", "answer": "Train the main stem up a vertical string. When the main stem reaches the top of the support, pinch out the growing tip. Side-shoots: pinch out at 2 leaves above the first developing cucumber to focus energy on fruit. Male flowers: remove any that appear on supposedly all-female F1 hybrids (they cause pollination and bitter seedy fruit). Tendrils can be left or removed for tidiness."},
    {"question": "Can I grow cucumbers in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — bush varieties (Bush Champion, Picolino) and dwarf indoor types (Petita) in 30-litre containers work. Use rich peat-free compost; water daily in summer; feed weekly. Train climbing types up a tall stake or wigwam. Container plants need more attention to watering than ground plants — they dry out fast."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'cucumber';

-- From daffodil.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant daffodil bulbs in autumn (September–November), at three times their height deep, in sun or part shade in well-drained soil — they''re the quintessential UK spring bulb, hardy (RHS H6–H7), and one of the easiest perennial bulbs to naturalise. Daffodils flower from late February to April depending on variety, with the bulk in March–April. Leave foliage for at least 6 weeks after flowering — the leaves photosynthesise to fuel next year''s bulb. Cutting them too early is the #1 cause of "blind" daffodils that don''t flower. Don''t tie or knot the foliage — old gardening practice, but reduces photosynthesis. Daffodils are largely pest-free thanks to alkaloid toxicity (narcissus fly is the only significant problem). Squirrels and mice don''t eat daffodil bulbs (unlike crocus). One of the best UK lawn-naturalising bulbs.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick a daffodil type for your use", "text": "For naturalising in lawns and woodland: smaller, more vigorous types — Tete-a-Tete (very early, 15 cm dwarf), February Gold (early, mid-size), Thalia (white, mid-late, multi-headed). Narcissus poeticus var. recurvus (Pheasant's Eye) — UK heritage choice, late, fragrant. For border display: large-cupped Dutch hybrids — King Alfred (classic yellow), Mount Hood (pure white), Ice Follies (white with yellow cup), Pink Charm (white with pink cup). For pots and patios: Tete-a-Tete, Jetfire, February Gold — compact, dramatic. For scent: Thalia, Pheasant's Eye, paperwhites (forced indoors). For a first daffodil: Tete-a-Tete — vigorous, naturalising, plant-anywhere reliable."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant bulbs in autumn", "text": "September to November, before soil cools below 5°C. Don't delay past early December — bulbs that go in too late may flower late or skip a year. Plant 3× bulb height deep (typically 12–15 cm for full-size bulbs). Plant pointed end up, blunt end down. Spacing: 10 cm apart for full-size; 5 cm for dwarf types."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in sun or part shade in well-drained soil", "text": "Daffodils are forgiving about position. Best: full sun for the most flowers; part shade tolerated; deep shade reduces flowering significantly. Soil: any well-drained garden soil — clay needs grit; sand is fine. Avoid waterlogged ground — daffodils rot in winter wet. For lawn naturalising: damp meadow grass works perfectly."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Naturalise in lawns and under trees", "text": "Daffodils are one of the best UK lawn-naturalising bulbs. Method: lift small squares of turf, scatter bulbs (12 cm deep), replace turf. Or use a bulb planter for individual placement. Scatter randomly rather than in neat rows — clusters of 7–15 bulbs spaced 30 cm apart look most natural. Best lawns: relaxed meadow-style lawns under deciduous trees. Don't naturalise in formal lawns that need early mowing."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Don't cut foliage for at least 6 weeks after flowering", "text": "The single most important husbandry rule. After the flowers fade, leave the leaves alone for at least 6 weeks (longer is better) until they yellow naturally. The leaves photosynthesise to refill the bulb for next year's flowers. Cutting them too early is the #1 cause of blind daffodils that produce only leaves and no flowers. In lawns: don't mow daffodil areas until mid-June."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Don't tie or knot the foliage", "text": "Common old practice — but wrong. Tying or knotting daffodil leaves to tidy the garden after flowering reduces photosynthesis and starves next year's bulb. The flowering reduction can be 30–50% if leaves are routinely tied. Modern advice: simply leave the yellowing leaves alone; tuck them gently under emerging perennials if they look tatty."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Deadhead but leave the foliage", "text": "After flowers fade, snap the spent flower head off (with finger and thumb) at the base of the seed-pod. Why deadhead: stops the plant putting energy into seed production (which it would do at the expense of bulb fattening). Don't deadhead in lawn or woodland naturalising plantings — let some plants set seed for new colonies. Don't cut the stems — leave them with the leaves for photosynthesis."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for narcissus fly (the main pest)", "text": "The only significant UK daffodil pest. Symptoms: bulbs that fail to flower, may produce only spindly distorted leaves, or rot when lifted. Cause: narcissus fly larvae eat the bulb interior. Prevention: deep planting (12–15 cm) — flies struggle to reach deeper bulbs. For affected colonies: lift bulbs in summer, inspect, bin any that feel soft or have entry holes. The good news: most UK garden daffodils never get narcissus fly."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I plant daffodils?", "answer": "September to November — the earlier in this window, the better. Soil should still be warm (above 5°C) but autumn rains should have started. In southern UK: September is the ideal window. In Scotland and the north: September–October. Don't delay past early December — late-planted bulbs often flower late or skip the first year. Plant 3× bulb height deep (12–15 cm for standard bulbs), pointed end up."},
    {"question": "Why aren't my daffodils flowering?", "answer": "Almost certainly because the foliage was cut too early. Daffodils need their leaves for at least 6 weeks after flowering to photosynthesise and refill the bulb. Cutting or mowing too soon produces blind bulbs the following year. Fix going forward: leave foliage until it yellows naturally (typically mid-June); don't tie or knot leaves; deadhead spent flowers but never cut stems and leaves. Recovery: blind daffodils usually flower again 1–2 years after proper management resumes."},
    {"question": "When do I cut daffodil foliage down?", "answer": "When it has yellowed and collapsed naturally — typically late May to mid-June, 6+ weeks after the last flowers. Don't cut, mow, or tie before then — the leaves are still working to fuel next year's flowers. In lawns: skip mowing daffodil areas until mid-June at earliest. In borders: simply leave the yellowing leaves alone; they'll collapse and decompose naturally."},
    {"question": "Will daffodils naturalise in my lawn?", "answer": "Yes — daffodils are one of the best UK lawn-naturalising bulbs. Best types for lawns: smaller vigorous varieties — Tete-a-Tete, February Gold, Thalia, Pheasant's Eye. Method: lift small turf squares in autumn, scatter bulbs 12 cm deep, replace turf. Don't mow until mid-June. Over 5–10 years a small initial planting spreads by both seed and bulb division into impressive drifts. Best lawns: damp meadow-style lawns under deciduous trees."},
    {"question": "What's the best daffodil for the UK?", "answer": "For naturalising: Tete-a-Tete — early (Feb–Mar), dwarf (15 cm), vigorous, multi-headed; the most-planted UK daffodil. For classic display: King Alfred type yellow daffodils, classic trumpet shape. For scent: Thalia (multi-headed white) or Pheasant's Eye (late, intensely fragrant, UK heritage). For pots: Tete-a-Tete, Jetfire — compact and dramatic. Avoid heavy double-flowered varieties for general garden use — they often flop in rain."},
    {"question": "Can I grow daffodils in pots?", "answer": "Yes — pots showcase daffodils brilliantly. Use a 30 cm pot of peat-free compost with grit for drainage. Plant 10–15 bulbs in October, 12 cm deep. Move the pot under cover in deep winter if temperatures drop hard; bring out in early spring for flower display. After flowering: leave the pot somewhere out of the way for the leaves to die back (6+ weeks); replant bulbs in the garden in autumn for next year — pot daffodils rarely flower a second year in the same compost."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'daffodil';

-- From dahlia.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant dahlia tubers from April under cover or after the last frost outdoors (mid-May to early June depending on region). Dahlias are frost-tender (RHS H3) but the tubers themselves can overwinter in mild south-coast soil under a thick mulch; everywhere else, lift them in November after the first frost blackens the foliage and store dry. The UK summer suits dahlias perfectly — cool nights, long days, plenty of rain. Slugs are the dominant problem on young shoots; protect with nematodes, copper rings, or hand-picking at dusk. Many forms — dinner-plate, decorative, cactus, pompon, single — and dahlias make exceptional cut flowers. Deadhead through summer to keep the flowers coming. Bishop of Llandaff, Café au Lait, and Karma series are reliable UK starters.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H3',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H3',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy tubers or pot-grown plants", "text": "Tubers from January onwards from garden centres and seed merchants. Or buy pot-grown plants from May. Tubers are cheaper; pot-grown plants give an earlier crop and skip the early-spring care."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pot tubers up from April", "text": "Plant tubers in 2-litre pots of multi-purpose compost in a frost-free greenhouse or porch from April. Eye facing up; cover with 5 cm compost; water sparingly until shoots appear. This head-start gives flowers from July rather than late August."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prepare a sunny rich bed", "text": "Full sun, fertile, well-drained soil. Fork in well-rotted manure or compost. Dahlias are hungry; a poor bed gives weak plants and few flowers."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant out after last frost", "text": "Mid-May in southern England; early June further north. Harden plants off for 7–10 days first. Space 60 cm apart for medium varieties, 1 m for dinner-plate types. Stake at planting (in afterwards damages tubers)."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Protect young shoots from slugs", "text": "Slugs love emerging dahlia shoots. Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) to warm moist soil before planting. Beer traps, copper rings on the bare soil around each plant, hand-picking at dusk in damp weather. Once shoots are 30 cm tall, slug pressure drops."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pinch out the growing tip", "text": "When plants are 30 cm tall, pinch out the central growing tip to encourage branching and more flowers. Don't pinch dinner-plate varieties — you want the show-stopping single bloom on each stem."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Feed and water", "text": "Water deeply twice a week through July and August. Weekly high-potash liquid feed from the first buds. Mulch heavily."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Deadhead continuously", "text": "Cut spent flowers back to the next bud. Deadheading is the single biggest factor in long flowering — dahlias stop blooming if seed heads form. Cut for vases too; the more you cut, the more they flower."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Lift tubers in November", "text": "After the first frost blackens the foliage, cut stems back to 10 cm, lift tubers with a fork, brush off soil, label, and store dry in a frost-free shed in trays of dry sand or vermiculite. In mild south-coast gardens, you can leave them in the ground under a thick mulch of bracken or straw."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I plant dahlia tubers?", "answer": "Tubers can go into pots indoors from April; into the ground outdoors after the last frost — mid-May in southern England, early June further north. Don't plant tubers direct outside in cold wet soil before mid-April; they rot. The indoor head-start from April gives flowers from July; direct-outdoor planting gives flowers from late August onwards."},
    {"question": "Should I lift my dahlia tubers in winter?", "answer": "Depends where you live. Mild south-coast (Cornwall, south Devon, southern Pembrokeshire) and well-drained chalky gardens: leave them in the ground under a 10 cm mulch of bracken or straw. Everywhere else: lift them after the first frost blackens the foliage, store dry in a frost-free shed. Tubers rot in cold wet soil through winter."},
    {"question": "How do I stop slugs eating my dahlias?", "answer": "Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) to warm moist soil two weeks before planting out. Beer traps near the bed. Hand-pick at dusk in damp weather. Copper rings on the bare soil around each plant. Once shoots are 30 cm tall, slug pressure drops dramatically — the worst window is the first 4 weeks after emergence."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between dahlia types?", "answer": "Dinner-plate (decorative giant): 20+ cm blooms, one per stem (Café au Lait, Kelvin Floodlight). Decorative: medium blooms, classic form. Cactus: narrow spiky petals (Black Narcissus, Nuit d'Été). Pompon / ball: small round blooms, very long-lasting in vases. Single / collerette: open daisy-like, excellent for pollinators (Bishop of Llandaff, Mystic Spirit). For pollinators, choose singles; for cut flowers, choose decoratives and pompons; for show, dinner-plates."},
    {"question": "Why are my dahlias growing leaves but no flowers?", "answer": "Too much nitrogen, not enough sun, or the plant is too young. Fresh manure or high-nitrogen fertiliser pushes leaves at the expense of flowers. Switch to a high-potash feed from June. Make sure the plant gets at least 6 hours of direct sun. New tubers planted late may not flower until late August; nothing wrong with the plant."},
    {"question": "Can I grow dahlias in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — compact and patio varieties (Karma series, Gallery series, Dahlegria) suit 30-litre containers and crop heavily. Larger types want larger pots (50+ litres). Water container dahlias daily in summer; feed weekly with high-potash liquid. Containers can be moved into a shed or greenhouse over winter to overwinter the tubers in situ."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'dahlia';

-- From dill.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow dill direct from April to July, every 3 weeks, in sunny well-drained soil — it''s a hardy annual that bolts to seed quickly in heat so successional sowing is the only way to keep fresh foliage going through summer. Dill is moderately hardy (RHS H3–H4) and tolerates spring frosts but not hard winter cold. Don''t transplant dill — it has a long taproot and resents being moved; sow direct where you want it to grow, or use deep modular cells. Pick young feathery foliage for fish dishes, soft cheese, and dressings; let later sowings flower and set seed for pickling (dill seed has a stronger, more aniseed flavour than the leaf). Dill is a magnet for hoverflies and parasitic wasps — invaluable in any veg garden. Don''t plant near fennel: they cross-pollinate and produce muddy-flavoured hybrids.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H3',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct, not in modules", "text": "Dill has a long, fragile taproot and resents transplanting. Either sow seed direct where you want it to grow, or use deep modular cells (10+ cm deep — root-trainer cells work well) and plant out before the roots circle the bottom. Standard module trays don't give enough depth."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "First sowing in April, then successional", "text": "Sow the first seeds in mid-April (mid-March in mild southern gardens, early May in cold areas). Then sow a new short row every 3 weeks until late July. This is the only way to keep tender foliage coming — each individual plant bolts to flower within 6–8 weeks of sowing in warm weather."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Sun and well-drained soil", "text": "Full sun, ordinary garden soil. Heavy or wet soils encourage damping off and root rot. On clay, sow into a raised bed or large container of multipurpose compost. Don't enrich the soil — dill flowers and bolts faster on rich ground."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Sow shallow and thin", "text": "Surface sow or barely cover seed (1 cm max). Germination in 7–14 days at 15–20°C. Thin seedlings to 20 cm apart when they have 2–3 true leaves. Crowded plants bolt sooner and produce thinner foliage."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick young foliage from 6 weeks", "text": "Cut whole stems just above a side-shoot rather than picking individual leaves. The plant branches and re-grows for several cuts. Once flower buds form, leaf production stops and flavour weakens — let those plants run to seed for pickling, and rely on younger successional sowings for fresh foliage."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Let some plants flower for hoverflies", "text": "The yellow umbel flowers in July–August are extraordinary for hoverflies and parasitic wasps — both major aphid predators. Letting a few plants flower per dill patch turns it into a biological pest-control hub. Most gardeners let the late-summer plants flower (since they're going to seed anyway)."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Harvest seed for pickling", "text": "Dill seed has a stronger, more aniseed flavour than the leaf — essential for dill pickles, gravlax, rye bread. Cut flower heads as the seeds turn brown but before they shatter (early September). Hang upside-down inside a paper bag for 1–2 weeks; seeds drop into the bag as they dry. Store in a sealed jar."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Don't plant near fennel", "text": "Dill and fennel cross-pollinate (both in the carrot family) and produce hybrid plants with muddy flavour. Keep them at opposite ends of the garden, or pick one or the other. Dill also doesn't mix well with carrots (same family, attracts the same pests)."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why does my dill bolt so quickly?", "answer": "Dill is an annual with a short window of leaf production — typically 6–8 weeks from sowing before it starts flowering. Successional sowing every 3 weeks is the only way to keep tender foliage available all summer. Bolt-resistant varieties exist (Dukat is the standard, holds leaf production 2–3 weeks longer than generic dill, and won't run to seed in average UK conditions). Heat triggers bolting — late-spring sowings bolt faster than early or autumn ones."},
    {"question": "Can I transplant a dill seedling?", "answer": "Not really. Dill has a long taproot that resents disturbance. Transplants typically bolt within 2–3 weeks of being moved (the stress signals go to seed now). Two workarounds. (1) Sow direct where you want the plants — easiest by far. (2) Use deep modular cells (root-trainer or 10+ cm) and plant out before roots reach the bottom. Standard module trays don't give enough depth."},
    {"question": "When do I pick dill?", "answer": "Leaves: from 6 weeks after sowing, before flower buds form. Cut whole stems just above a side-shoot for re-growth. Flowers: in July–August, used in some pickle recipes (the whole flowering head goes into the jar). Seeds: in early September, as flower heads brown but before they shatter. The leaf, flower, and seed all have slightly different flavours and uses."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between dill leaf and dill seed?", "answer": "Dill leaf (the feathery foliage, called dill weed in old recipes): mild, fresh, slightly anise — fish, eggs, soft cheese, dressings, gravlax. Dill seed: stronger, more pungent, more aniseed — pickled cucumbers, rye bread, vinegars, spiced fish. Most recipes specify which form; don't substitute equal amounts of one for the other."},
    {"question": "Can I grow dill in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and it's often easier than in-ground. Use a deep (30+ cm) pot to give the taproot room. Sow direct into the pot. Water consistently — pot-grown dill bolts faster in dry conditions. A pot on a sunny doorstep gives you a fresh handful of foliage from June to September. One pot's worth lasts about 4 weeks before bolting; sow a fresh pot every 3 weeks."},
    {"question": "Why can't I plant dill near fennel?", "answer": "They cross-pollinate and produce hybrid seedlings the following year (from any self-sown seed) with muddy, neither-quite-fennel-nor-dill flavour. Pollen drifts between umbels of the two species easily. The fix: grow them at opposite ends of the garden (10 m+ apart), or pick one or the other. Cutting flower heads before they set seed avoids the hybrid problem entirely."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'dill';

-- From echinacea.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant echinacea in spring, in full sun in well-drained soil — it''s a North American prairie perennial that flowers from July to October with large daisy-like pink-purple petals around a prominent spiky orange-brown central cone. Echinacea is hardy (RHS H5–H6) and increasingly grown in UK gardens as a late-summer/autumn highlight in prairie-style and pollinator-focused planting. The orange-coppery central cone gives it the common name coneflower — and the spent seed cones make outstanding winter structure if left through the dormant season (goldfinches eat the seeds). The species E. purpurea is the safest UK choice; the brightly-coloured Big Sky series and similar bred hybrids (orange, yellow, red) can be short-lived (1–3 years) on heavy or wet soils. Avoid feeding — like most prairie plants, echinacea flowers better in poor soil.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Start with the species, not the hybrids", "text": "Species form Echinacea purpurea (the wild prairie type): pink-purple ray petals around a coppery cone, 80–120 cm tall, RHS H5–H6, the long-lived reliable choice for UK gardens. White Swan and Magnus are popular reliable cultivars close to the species. Big Sky series and similar hybrids (orange, yellow, red, double-flowered) are bright but often short-lived in the UK (1–3 years), particularly on heavy or wet soils. Start with the species or a close cultivar; experiment with the bright hybrids only once you've established conditions are right."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in full sun and very well-drained soil", "text": "Echinacea is a prairie plant — it wants 6+ hours of full sun and free-draining soil. The main UK failure mode is wet winters on heavy soils: the crown rots and the plant disappears. On clay: fork in plenty of horticultural grit at planting, or grow in a raised bed. On sandy, chalky, or stony soils it thrives. Spacing 40–50 cm."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in spring, after the last frost", "text": "Mid-April to late May. Container-grown plants are sold in 1–2 L pots from April. Plant at the same depth they were in the pot. Water in deeply, then leave alone — echinacea hates overwatering once established. Spring planting gives the plant a full growing season to establish before its first winter, which is critical for survival."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't feed", "text": "Echinacea flowers better in poor soil. Rich soil and fertiliser produce lush growth that flops, blocks airflow, and reduces flowering. No fertiliser at planting; no fertiliser during the season. A 3 cm gravel or coarse compost mulch around the base keeps weeds down without adding nutrients."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Stake taller plants in exposed sites", "text": "E. purpurea species form is typically self-supporting at 80–120 cm. Taller hybrids (some reach 1.2–1.5 m on rich soil) may flop in summer storms — a discreet grow-through plant support placed early lets them grow naturally without spoiling the look. Most UK echinaceas don't need staking in sheltered gardens."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Deadhead for repeat or leave for structure", "text": "Two approaches, both valid. Deadhead after first flush (cut spent flower stems back to a side bud) for more, smaller secondary flowers extending the season. Leave seedheads intact through autumn and winter for the architectural spent cones — they're stunning rimed with frost, and goldfinches feed on the seeds through October–February. Many UK gardeners do half and half: deadhead some plants, leave others for wildlife and winter structure."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut back hard in early spring, not autumn", "text": "Don't cut echinacea down in autumn — the dead stems and seedheads protect the crown from winter wet and frost. Cut to 10 cm in late February or early March, just before new growth emerges. The spent stems provide winter habitat for overwintering insects and structural interest in the bare garden."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Divide every 3–4 years for vigour", "text": "Echinacea clumps gradually become congested and the centre dies out. Divide in early spring (just as new growth emerges) every 3–4 years: lift the clump, split into 3–4 sections with a spade or two forks back-to-back, replant the best outer pieces in fresh soil. Don't divide in autumn — the disturbed roots may not survive the wet winter that follows."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why did my echinacea disappear after the first winter?", "answer": "Wet soil + heavy clay = crown rot. Echinacea is a prairie plant from well-drained North American soils. The single biggest UK failure mode is winter wet — the crown sits in waterlogged soil and rots. Four prevention strategies. (1) Plant in well-drained soil: fork in horticultural grit at planting on clay soils, or use a raised bed. (2) Don't cut the plant down in autumn: dead stems shed water and protect the crown. (3) Plant in spring, not autumn: gives a full season to establish. (4) Choose the species or Magnus / White Swan — the bright hybrid colours (orange, yellow, red) are notoriously short-lived in UK conditions."},
    {"question": "When do I cut echinacea back?", "answer": "Late February or early March — NOT autumn. The dead stems and seedheads protect the crown from winter wet and frost; cutting in autumn exposes the plant to crown rot. Leave the spent flower stems through winter for structural interest (frost-rimed cones are stunning) and bird food (goldfinches eat the seeds). Cut to 10 cm in late winter, just before new growth emerges."},
    {"question": "Why are my echinacea flowers floppy or short-lived?", "answer": "Too rich soil, too much shade, or recent transplant. Echinacea flowers best in poor sandy soil with 6+ hours sun. Fix: stop any feeding, fork grit into the soil if heavy, move to a sunnier position if currently shaded. Newly-planted echinacea also takes 1–2 years to settle and produce its best flowering — be patient through years 1–2; year 3 is usually peak."},
    {"question": "What's the best echinacea for the UK?", "answer": "Stick close to the species for long life. Magnus (large flat-faced pink flowers, RHS Award of Garden Merit), White Swan (white with orange cone), and the basic species form Echinacea purpurea are all long-lived UK perennials. Avoid the bright hybrids (Big Sky series orange/yellow/red, double-flowered varieties) unless you have ideal drainage — they're often short-lived (1–3 years) and disappointing for the price."},
    {"question": "Can I grow echinacea in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and on heavy soils, it's the better option. A 40 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed 30% horticultural grit, in full sun. The pot advantage: perfect drainage protects against winter crown rot. Water consistently in summer; don't overwater in winter (move the pot under a porch in deep winter if possible). Repot every 2 years in fresh compost. Pot echinacea flowers as well as in-ground plants if drainage is right."},
    {"question": "Are echinaceas good for wildlife?", "answer": "Outstanding — multiple groups visit. The flat-faced large flowers are particularly accessible to butterflies (small tortoiseshells, peacocks, painted ladies), hoverflies (top aphid predators), bees of all kinds, and moths. The seedheads in autumn and winter feed goldfinches — leave the spent flowers intact to feed the birds and provide winter structure. A patch of echinacea is one of the most useful late-summer pollinator and winter-bird plantings you can include in a UK garden."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'echinacea';

-- From elder.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant elder bare-root from November to March, in sun or part shade, in almost any soil — it''s a vigorous UK native shrub or small tree (3–6 m) that thrives in hedgerows, copses, and gardens with minimal care. Elder is very hardy (RHS H7) and produces frothy cream flower-heads in June (the source of elderflower cordial and champagne) followed by black berries in September (elderberry wine, syrup, jelly). Cooked berries only — raw elderberries cause stomach upset; cooking destroys the offending glycosides. The ornamental Black Lace cultivar has near-black foliage and pink-tinted flowers — a dramatic foliage shrub even if you never harvest. Cut hard back in winter for the strongest fresh growth and biggest flower-heads, but at the cost of fruit. Self-seeds via bird-dropped berries and from suckers — manage where it appears or it colonises gradually.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick green-leaf or ornamental form", "text": "Common elder (Sambucus nigra): the UK native — green leaves, frothy cream flowers, black berries in autumn. The functional choice for flowers and fruit harvest. Grows 3–6 m, can be trained as a single-stemmed tree or multi-stem shrub. Black Lace (S. nigra Eva): near-black deeply-cut feathery foliage, pink-flushed flowers, dramatic in mixed borders. Less productive than the species but ornamental year-round. Aurea (golden-leaved): bright yellow-green foliage, classic UK garden form. Madonna: variegated green and cream leaves, ornamental but weak-growing. All four are Sambucus nigra cultivars and share cultivation requirements."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March while dormant. Bare-root plants establish faster than container-grown and are much cheaper. Plant at the same depth the plant was in the nursery; water in deeply. Elder tolerates almost any soil except waterlogged ground, sun or part shade, exposure or shelter — it's a hedgerow toughie."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Allow space — 3 m radius minimum", "text": "Mature elder reaches 4–6 m wide and 3–6 m tall depending on pruning. Don't plant against a wall or path edge. The branches are brittle and break in autumn storms when laden with fruit — give it space to fall harmlessly. Multi-stem shrub forms are typical; train to a single trunk for a small ornamental tree if preferred."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Choose your pruning style", "text": "For flowers and fruit: prune lightly in winter, removing only dead, diseased, or crossing branches, and shorten the longest stems by a third. This preserves the older wood that bears the heaviest flowering and fruiting clusters. For dramatic foliage (especially Black Lace, Aurea): cut hard back to 30 cm above the base each February (coppicing). This sacrifices flowers and fruit but produces enormous, vivid leaves — the foliage-statement use of ornamental cultivars."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Harvest elderflowers in June", "text": "Pick flower-heads when the small individual flowers have just opened (the white shimmer is at peak), in dry weather, late morning after dew has evaporated. The musky-sweet scent is at its strongest then. For elderflower cordial: 30 heads, 1.5 kg sugar, 2 lemons (sliced), 50 g citric acid, 1.5 L boiling water — pour over, leave 24 hours, strain, bottle. Keeps 6 months in the fridge."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Harvest elderberries in September", "text": "Pick the entire umbel cluster when the berries are fully black and the whole head droops with weight. Snip the stem with secateurs. Strip the berries off the stems with a fork over a bowl (the stems contain toxic glycosides — discard them). Use straight away or freeze on trays for later cordial, wine, or syrup making."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "NEVER eat elderberries raw", "text": "Important. Raw elderberries (and the stems, leaves, and bark of elder) contain cyanogenic glycosides that cause nausea, vomiting, and stomach upset. Cooking destroys the glycosides — heat the berries to a boil for at least 30 seconds before any use. Cordials, syrups, jellies, wines all involve cooking that makes the berries safe. Don't eat them straight off the bush, however good they look."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Manage self-seeding", "text": "Elder is bird-distributed — robins, blackbirds, thrushes eat the berries and deposit seed widely. New elder plants appear in hedges, fence corners, and garden edges within 3–4 years. Pull young seedlings while small (1–2 years old) or let one establish as part of a wildlife corner. Old elders sucker from the base — dig out unwanted suckers in winter."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Are elderberries safe to eat?", "answer": "Only cooked — never raw. Raw elderberries, leaves, stems, and bark contain cyanogenic glycosides (sambunigrin and related compounds) that release small amounts of cyanide in the body, causing nausea, vomiting, and stomach upset. Cooking destroys these compounds — heating the berries to a simmer for at least 30 seconds makes them completely safe. Elderberry cordials, syrups, jellies, wines all involve cooking that removes the issue. Strip berries off the stems before cooking (stems contain higher concentrations of glycosides than the berries themselves)."},
    {"question": "When do I prune an elder?", "answer": "Late winter (February). Two pruning approaches depending on your goal. For flowers and fruit: light prune — remove dead/diseased/crossing branches, shorten longest stems by a third. This preserves the older wood that flowers and fruits heavily. For dramatic ornamental foliage (especially Black Lace, Aurea): hard-prune (coppice) to 30 cm above ground each February. This sacrifices flowers and fruit but produces the most vivid, large foliage. You can't have both maximum flowers AND maximum foliage on the same plant in the same year."},
    {"question": "How do I make elderflower cordial?", "answer": "The classic UK summer recipe. Pick 30 fresh flower-heads on a dry morning, just after the flowers have fully opened (shake gently — pollen will fall, that's a good sign). Combine in a large bowl with 1.5 kg granulated sugar, 50 g citric acid (chemist or brewing shops), 2 lemons sliced, and 1.5 L just-boiled water. Stir to dissolve sugar. Cover and steep 24 hours. Strain through muslin into sterilised bottles. Keeps 6 months in the fridge, longer frozen in ice cubes. Dilute 1:5 with cold water or sparkling wine."},
    {"question": "What's the best elder variety for a UK garden?", "answer": "For productive use (flowers + fruit): plant common elder (Sambucus nigra species form). Vigorous, heavy cropping, and you can take fresh growth from hedgerows if a garden plant isn't ready. For ornament: Black Lace is the modern UK standard — deeply-cut near-black foliage and pink-flushed flowers, dramatic in mixed borders. Aurea for yellow foliage. Both ornamental forms can be productive if pruned lightly, but they're chosen for foliage first."},
    {"question": "Will elder take over my garden?", "answer": "Slowly, but yes — it's a hedgerow coloniser. Two routes. (1) Birds eat the berries and deposit seed widely; new elder plants appear in hedges, fence corners, garden edges. (2) Established elders sucker from the base, gradually widening the clump. Manage both: pull young seedlings while small (1–2 years old, before the deep roots set), and dig out unwanted suckers in winter. Many UK gardeners welcome a wild-corner elder for the wildlife value; just keep it out of the cultivated borders."},
    {"question": "Can I grow elder in a pot?", "answer": "Possible but not ideal. Elder is a vigorous shrub that wants 3 m of root space; even a 60 cm pot holds it back significantly. Pot elders grow as compact shrubs (1.5–2 m) and produce fewer flowers/fruit. If you really want a pot elder, choose the smaller-growing ornamental forms (Black Tower is more upright and compact than Black Lace) and feed/water consistently. Repot every 3 years."},
    {"question": "Is elder good for wildlife?", "answer": "Outstanding. Elderflowers feed a huge range of bees, hoverflies, and other pollinators. Elderberries feed at least 20 UK bird species in autumn. The dense foliage provides nesting habitat. Older elders develop hollow stems that solitary bees use as nesting sites. A wild-corner elder is one of the most wildlife-valuable plants you can include in a UK garden — both productive for the kitchen and important for the local ecosystem."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'elder';

-- From endive.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow endive direct from May to August, in sun or part shade in moist soil — it''s a hardy annual bitter salad green grown for autumn cropping, with two main types: curly endive (frisée) with deeply-cut feathery leaves, and escarole (flat-leaved Batavian) with broader spoon-shaped leaves. Endive is hardy (RHS H4–H5) and crops most reliably in autumn — earlier sowings bolt to flower; later sowings overwinter in mild gardens. Blanch the heart by tying the outer leaves together two weeks before harvest, or covering the centre with a dinner plate — the inner leaves blanch pale yellow and become much less bitter. Distinct from chicory (different species — Cichorium intybus — see separate slug). Less bitter than chicory, more mainstream-friendly. Slug-magnet outdoors — protect young plants.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick curly or escarole", "text": "Curly endive (frisée): deeply cut feathery leaves, paler interior, classic French bistro salad — Tres Fine Maraichere or Frenzy. Escarole / Batavian endive: broader smoother spoon-shaped leaves, easier to clean, milder bitterness — Pancalieri, Bubikopf, Cornet de Bordeaux (hardy enough for overwintering). Both grow under identical conditions; escarole is the more forgiving for UK first-timers."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, May to August", "text": "Cool-season preference. Earlier than May: plants bolt in summer heat. Later than August: don't size up before winter. Direct-sow in shallow drills 1 cm deep, 30 cm between rows. Germination in 7–14 days at 15°C. For autumn cropping: sow late July to mid-August. For winter under cover: sow late August to early September."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to 25–30 cm apart", "text": "Once seedlings have 4–5 leaves, thin to one plant every 25–30 cm. Don't crowd — overcrowded endive forms smaller, looser, more bitter heads. Eat the thinnings in salads (small mild-bitter young leaves)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water consistently", "text": "Endive bolts to flower in drought and becomes much more bitter under stress. Weekly deep watering in dry spells. Mulch with garden compost to conserve moisture. Consistent moisture from sowing through to harvest is the key to milder-flavoured endive."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Blanch the heart two weeks before harvest", "text": "The classic technique that transforms endive. Two weeks before you want to harvest, gather the outer leaves together over the heart and tie loosely with twine, OR cover the centre with an upturned dinner plate / black flowerpot. The inner leaves blanch pale yellow and lose much of their bitterness — the classic creamy heart of a French bistro salad. Blanching takes 7–14 days; check progress by peeking under the cover."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Harvest whole heads in autumn", "text": "From mid-September onwards, cut whole heads at ground level when they're properly sized (30 cm diameter for escarole, smaller for frisée). The blanched heart is the prize — pale, creamy, mild. The unblanched outer leaves are still edible but more bitter — use sparingly or cook them."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Overwinter for spring cropping", "text": "Cornet de Bordeaux and other hardy escarole varieties survive UK winters in mild gardens (southern England, sheltered Midlands) without protection, cropping in February–March before bolting in late spring. In colder areas: a cloche or low polytunnel extends the cropping window. Light frost actually improves flavour by reducing bitterness."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Protect from slugs throughout", "text": "Slugs love endive at every stage. Apply wool pellets along sowing rows; nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) watered into the soil for ongoing control. Hand-pick slugs in the evening after rain. The blanched heart inside its cover is particularly vulnerable — slugs hide inside, undetected, and eat the prize while it forms. Lift the cover briefly every 2–3 days to check for and remove slugs."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What's the difference between chicory and endive?", "answer": "Same family, different species, different uses. Endive (Cichorium endivia, this plant): hardy annual, rosette of curly (frisée) or flat (escarole) leaves, milder bitterness, autumn–winter salad crop. Chicory (Cichorium intybus — see separate chicory slug): hardy perennial (grown as annual), upright growth, sharper bitterness, three main types (forcing/witloof, heading/radicchio, leaf/sugarloaf). Endive is the milder, more salad-friendly cousin; chicory tends to be sharper and more often cooked."},
    {"question": "How do I blanch endive?", "answer": "Two methods, both effective. Method 1 (gathering): when the head is well-developed (3–4 weeks before expected harvest), gather the outer leaves up over the heart and tie loosely with twine. The inner leaves blanch pale yellow over 7–14 days. Method 2 (covering): place an upturned dinner plate, black flowerpot (with the drainage hole covered), or piece of slate over the centre of the plant. Same blanching effect over 7–14 days. Check inside every 2–3 days for slugs."},
    {"question": "When can I harvest endive?", "answer": "Mid-September to late autumn for most types — escarole and overwintering varieties extend the season into February–March in mild gardens. Cut whole heads at ground level when they're well-developed and the heart has been blanched. The harvest window is 2–3 weeks for each plant — after that, leaves get tougher and the plant starts bolting. Stagger sowings to spread the autumn supply."},
    {"question": "Why is my endive so bitter?", "answer": "Two causes. (1) Drought stress — endive grown in dry conditions becomes much more bitter. Water consistently. (2) No blanching — un-blanched endive (the green outer leaves) is much sharper than the blanched pale heart. Blanch the heart for 7–14 days before harvest by gathering outer leaves or covering with a plate. Cool weather and light frost also reduce bitterness — autumn endive tastes milder than summer-stressed plants."},
    {"question": "Can endive survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Hardy varieties yes, in mild gardens. Cornet de Bordeaux is the standard overwintering escarole — survives most UK winters in southern England, sheltered Midlands, and Wales without protection. In colder areas: a cloche or low polytunnel keeps the plant cropping through winter. Light frost actually improves the flavour by reducing bitterness — overwintered endive in February–March is often the best of the year."},
    {"question": "Can I grow endive in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — a 30 cm pot gives a single head. Use peat-free compost, water consistently, place in part shade in summer (delays bolting). Blanch the heart with an upturned plate or pot. Pot endive is useful for slug control (raised off the ground, easier to monitor and protect) and moveable positioning (move to a sheltered spot for winter cropping). One pot per head; sow a fresh pot every 3 weeks for continuous supply."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'endive';

-- From fennel-bulb.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow Florence fennel seed direct from April to July; bulbs swell in cool moist weather and bolt in heat or cold check, so timing matters more than for most crops. Florence fennel is half-hardy (RHS H3–H4) and the bulb-forming form distinct from herb fennel (same species, different cultivar group, same slug needs to be disambiguated — herb fennel under separate slug `fennel-herb`). Plant 30 cm apart in rich moist soil; thin from clusters; earth up the developing bulb to keep it tender and white. Pick bulbs when they''re fist-sized (8–12 cm across) — checking weekly once they start to swell. The classic UK bolting risk: cool dip after planting, hot summer, or simply growing the wrong cultivar — Romanesco and Mantovano are the most bolt-resistant.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H3',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct from April", "text": "Sow direct from late April through to July. Florence fennel hates root disturbance — direct sowing or careful module-raising only. 1 cm deep drill; sow thinly; cover lightly. Successional sowings every 3 weeks for continuous picking."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose bolt-resistant cultivars", "text": "Romanesco, Mantovano, Finale, and Perfection are bred to resist bolting. Older cultivars (Zefa Fino) bolt readily in UK conditions. Bolt-resistant is the difference between a crop and a wasted bed."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to 30 cm", "text": "When seedlings have 4 true leaves, thin to 30 cm apart in rows 45 cm apart. Eat the thinnings (small fennel plants are tender in salad)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water consistently", "text": "Florence fennel bulbs split or bolt under drought stress. Water deeply twice a week from planting through to harvest; mulch with grass clippings or straw."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Earth up the developing bulb", "text": "Once the bulb is the size of a golf ball, draw soil up around it loosely (3–5 cm), keeping the top exposed. Earthing-up keeps the bulb white, tender, and stops it going green and tough."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Watch for bolting", "text": "Cool checks (cold weather after planting), drought, or simply day-length sensitivity in older cultivars can trigger bolting (early flowering stem). Bolt-resistant cultivars help. Pull bolted plants; the bulb stops developing once flowering starts."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick when fist-sized", "text": "Cut bulbs at ground level with a sharp knife when fist-sized (8–12 cm across) — checking weekly once swelling starts. Smaller is more tender; bigger gets fibrous. The cut stump sometimes resprouts giving secondary shoots that can be picked young."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Save feathery foliage", "text": "The feathery aniseed-flavoured foliage above the bulb is edible too — chop into salads, fish dishes, or use as a garnish. Pick lightly through the season; pick heavily when you harvest the bulb."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow Florence fennel in the UK?", "answer": "Late April to mid-July direct outdoors. Don't sow earlier — cold weather triggers bolting later. Successional sowings every 3 weeks until early July give continuous picking from July to October. Late-July sowings of bolt-resistant cultivars can crop into November in mild south-coast gardens."},
    {"question": "Why does my Florence fennel bolt before forming a bulb?", "answer": "Three usual causes. Cold check after sowing triggers the plant to think it's been through a winter; the response is to bolt. Sow into warm soil only (above 12°C). Drought stress triggers bolting as a survival response. Bolt-prone cultivar — older varieties (Zefa Fino) bolt readily in UK conditions; choose bolt-resistant modern cultivars (Romanesco, Mantovano, Finale)."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between Florence fennel and herb fennel?", "answer": "Same species (Foeniculum vulgare), different cultivar groups. Florence fennel (var. azoricum): grown for the swollen leaf-base bulb; annual; eaten as a vegetable. Herb fennel (var. dulce or wild form): grown for the feathery foliage and aromatic seeds; perennial; reaches 2 m+; ornamental as well as edible. Both produce identical-tasting foliage and seeds; only the bulb-forming form gives the fleshy white root vegetable."},
    {"question": "Can I grow Florence fennel in containers?", "answer": "Yes — a 25 cm deep container grows 4–6 plants. Use rich peat-free compost; water consistently (containers dry out fast); thin to one per station. Container plants need watering daily in summer to avoid the drought-bolting trap."},
    {"question": "When are Florence fennel bulbs ready to harvest?", "answer": "When they're fist-sized — 8–12 cm across at the widest point. Check the bed weekly once swelling starts; the window from perfect to starting to bolt is short, often just a week or two. Cut at ground level with a sharp knife. Smaller young bulbs are more tender for salads; larger ones suit roasting and braising."},
    {"question": "Can I eat the feathery green foliage?", "answer": "Yes — the fronds above the bulb are edible and intensely aniseed-flavoured. Pick lightly through the growing season; chop into salads, mix into mayonnaise for fish, sprinkle over roasted vegetables. The flowers and seeds (if you let the plant bolt) are also edible. The whole plant is essentially the same as herb fennel; the bulb is just the extra prize."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'fennel-bulb';

-- From fennel-herb.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant herb fennel from a young plant in spring, or sow seed direct in April–June, in a sunny well-drained position — it''s a tall, elegant perennial (1.5–2 m) with feathery foliage and yellow umbel flowers in mid to late summer. Herb fennel is hardy (RHS H4–H5) and dies back to ground each winter, returning each spring. Bronze fennel (Foeniculum vulgare ''Purpureum'') is the popular ornamental form — the same plant in dark copper-bronze foliage that lifts a herbaceous border. Don''t confuse with Florence fennel (annual bulb-forming form — separate slug fennel-bulb). Herb fennel self-seeds prolifically — cut flower heads off in autumn to limit spread. Don''t plant near dill: they cross-pollinate. Pick young foliage for fish dishes; harvest seeds in late summer for cooking. Excellent for bees, butterflies, and hoverflies — top wildlife herb.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose green or bronze fennel", "text": "Green fennel (F. vulgare — the species): classic kitchen herb, feathery green foliage, yellow umbels, height 1.5–2 m. The functional choice. Bronze fennel (F. vulgare Purpureum): identical to green in cultivation but copper-bronze foliage. A border highlight as well as a kitchen herb — most gardeners grow it for both. Both are perennial. Florence fennel (the annual form grown for bulbs) is a different beast — see separate fennel-bulb content."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct or plant young", "text": "From seed: surface-sow direct in April–June where you want the plant. Germination in 10–14 days. From plant: a 1–3 L pot in spring. Don't transplant a mature fennel — long taproot, resents disturbance. One plant gives a household more herb than they can use."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in sun, well-drained soil", "text": "Full sun. Tolerates poor soils — actually prefers them; rich soil produces lush growth that flops without support. Doesn't tolerate waterlogging. Spacing 60 cm if planting more than one — it grows wide as well as tall."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Stake if exposed", "text": "The flower spikes (1.5–2 m tall) can blow over in summer storms, particularly on rich soil or in windy gardens. A single stake at the base, with a loose tie at 1 m, holds the plant elegantly without trussing it. Don't stake low — it ruins the airy look."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick young foliage through summer", "text": "Cut feathery leaves and young stems for fish, salads, dressings. Pick from May through August. Once flowering starts (July–August) leaf production slows; pick younger fennel plants for tender foliage and let the mature one go to flower and seed."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Don't plant near dill (or carrots)", "text": "Dill and fennel cross-pollinate and produce muddy-flavoured hybrid seedlings the next year. Keep them at opposite ends of the garden (10+ m). Don't plant near carrots either — same family, similar pests. Fennel is generally a poor companion plant and best given its own corner of the garden."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Harvest seed in late summer", "text": "The yellow umbel flowers in July–August develop into seed heads through September. Cut when seeds are fully formed and starting to turn brown, but before they shatter. Hang upside-down inside a paper bag for 2 weeks. Seeds drop into the bag. Use for fish, sausages, bread, and the strong-flavoured tea."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Cut to ground in autumn and limit self-seeding", "text": "After the last seed harvest, cut all stems down to 5 cm above ground. This tidies the plant and removes any remaining seed heads that would self-sow. Fennel self-seeds aggressively if flower heads are left — be ready to weed out seedlings the following spring or wear gloves when handling them (some people are sensitive)."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Is herb fennel the same as Florence fennel?", "answer": "Same species, different selections. Herb fennel (Foeniculum vulgare) is a perennial grown for leaves, seeds, and ornamental value — 1.5–2 m tall, dies back annually. Florence fennel (F. vulgare var. azoricum) is an annual form selected for swollen leaf bases (bulbs), grown like celery. The two should be kept apart in the garden — they can cross-pollinate. For fennel for cooking, be clear which you want."},
    {"question": "Will herb fennel survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — hardy in most of the UK (RHS H4–H5). The above-ground growth dies back in late autumn and the plant retreats to its rootstock, returning in spring. A mature plant survives temperatures down to about -12°C. In very cold or wet gardens a mulch over the crown after autumn cut-down protects from frost-heaving."},
    {"question": "My fennel is taking over the garden — how do I control it?", "answer": "Cut flower heads off before they set seed is the answer. Fennel self-seeds prolifically, and within 2–3 years a single plant becomes a colony. In autumn, cut every umbel before the seeds shatter (or hand-collect for kitchen use). Weed out spring seedlings while small — the taproot makes large fennel seedlings hard to remove. For full removal, dig out the parent plant in autumn; new shoots from root fragments may appear for 2 years afterwards."},
    {"question": "When do I harvest fennel seed?", "answer": "September, as the green seeds turn brown but before they shatter and self-seed across the garden. Cut whole umbels with stems attached and hang upside-down inside a paper bag in a dry place for 2 weeks. The seeds drop into the bag as they fully dry. Sieve to remove stalks. One mature plant yields 100–200 g of seed."},
    {"question": "Can I grow herb fennel in a pot?", "answer": "Possible but awkward. Fennel has a long taproot and wants 1.5+ m of vertical space. A 40 cm pot can hold a young plant for 2–3 years before it gets root-bound and weak. Cut the plant down hard each autumn to keep size manageable. Better to plant in the ground at the back of a sunny border where it can run its full lifespan (10+ years)."},
    {"question": "Why are bees and butterflies all over my fennel?", "answer": "Because herb fennel is one of the top 10 UK garden plants for pollinator and beneficial-insect diversity. The flat yellow umbel flowers are accessible to short-tongued bees, hoverflies, parasitic wasps, and butterflies that struggle with tubular flowers. Hoverflies and parasitic wasps in particular are major aphid predators — a fennel patch upgrades the pest control across the whole garden."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'fennel-herb';

-- From foxglove.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow foxglove direct from May to July, or plant young plants in autumn or spring, in light to medium shade in moist soil — they''re a UK native biennial that produces a rosette of leaves in year one and spectacular spires of tubular flowers in year two before dying. Foxgloves are hardy (RHS H7) and self-seed prolifically, perpetuating themselves indefinitely once established. All parts are highly toxic — the source of the heart drug digoxin; never eat any part, wash hands after handling, and warn children. Bumblebees adore the tubular pink-purple-white flowers and disappear inside them. Cut the main spike off after flowering to encourage smaller side-spikes (extending the season into August), or leave it to set seed if you want self-sown next-generation plants. Excellent at the back of a border or in a wild woodland edge.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose the wild species or a named cultivar", "text": "Wild foxglove (Digitalis purpurea species): the UK native — pink-purple spires with darker spots inside the bell, 1.2–1.5 m tall, biennial, self-seeds heavily. The reliable choice for woodland edges and naturalistic plantings. Named cultivars: Sutton's Apricot (peach-coloured), Pam's Choice (white with maroon throat), Camelot series (more compact, partly perennial). The Camelot series often flowers in year one — worth knowing if you want quick results. Perennial foxgloves: Digitalis lanata, D. grandiflora are true perennials with smaller yellow flowers — separate species, longer-lived."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct in late spring/early summer", "text": "May to July is the ideal sowing window. Surface-sow seed where you want the plants — foxglove seed is dust-fine and needs light to germinate (don't cover). Press gently into damp soil. Germination in 14–21 days. Thin seedlings to 30 cm apart by autumn."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in light shade in moist soil", "text": "Foxgloves thrive at woodland edges, north-facing borders, and dappled-light positions. Full sun works but plants are smaller and shorter-lived. Deep shade reduces flowering. Moisture-retentive soil enriched with leaf-mould is ideal; tolerates clay if not waterlogged. Plant young plants 30 cm apart."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Understand the biennial cycle", "text": "Year one: a rosette of large soft leaves at ground level, no flowering. Year two: a single tall flower spike (1.2–1.5 m) rises in May–June, blooms June–July, then sets seed and the parent plant dies. The trick to continuous foxgloves: sow or plant new young rosettes every spring so there's always a flowering generation and a developing generation."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Stake taller spikes in exposed sites", "text": "The 1.2–1.5 m flower spikes can blow over in summer storms, especially when fully loaded with flowers. A single discreet cane and soft tie at 60 cm gives support without spoiling the natural look. In sheltered gardens, staking isn't needed."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut the main spike for side-spikes (or let it seed)", "text": "For continuous flowering: once the main spike has gone over in July, cut it off at the base. The plant produces smaller secondary spikes from below, extending the flowering season into August. This shortens its life slightly but more than doubles flower production. For self-seeding: leave the main spike intact through August. Seeds form in capsules along the spent stem, ripen, and scatter to perpetuate next year's generation. You can shake the dry stem over chosen areas in September to direct the colony."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "All parts are highly toxic", "text": "Important. Every part of foxglove contains cardiac glycosides (digoxin, digitoxin) — the same compounds used in cardiac medicine. Eating any quantity can cause irregular heartbeat, nausea, vomiting, and in severe cases death. Even handling can transfer the compounds to your eyes via your hands, causing irritation. Wash hands after handling, especially after deadheading or cutting back. Warn children — the flowers are visually tempting but absolutely not to be touched without supervision. Don't plant near children's play areas."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Embrace the self-seeding", "text": "The easiest way to keep foxgloves indefinitely. Let one or two plants set seed each year and the colony perpetuates itself — new rosettes appear in autumn where the seed fell. Manage the population: thin seedlings to 30 cm apart in spring; transplant excess seedlings to fill gaps (they move well as small rosettes); pull all unwanted seedlings before they get large. A managed foxglove population gives reliable display year after year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Are foxgloves poisonous?", "answer": "Yes — all parts are highly toxic to humans, dogs, cats, and grazing animals. The plant contains cardiac glycosides (digoxin and digitoxin) — the same compounds used to make heart medicines. Eating any part can cause irregular heartbeat, nausea, vomiting, blurred vision, and in severe cases cardiac arrest. Even handling can transfer compounds to your eyes. Three precautions. (1) Wash hands after handling. (2) Warn children — flowers are visually tempting. (3) Avoid planting near vegetable gardens (the leaves can be confused with comfrey or borage by inexperienced foragers). The toxicity doesn't reduce in dried plant material."},
    {"question": "Do foxgloves come back every year?", "answer": "Most are biennial — flower in year two, then die. But foxgloves self-seed prolifically, so an established planting perpetuates indefinitely without effort. Each year there will be year-one rosettes (no flowers) and year-two flower spikes. For continuous display: let one or two plants set seed each year (don't deadhead them) and the colony self-renews. Perennial foxgloves: Digitalis lanata, D. grandiflora are true perennials with smaller yellow flowers — separate species, longer-lived. The popular Camelot series of cultivars often flower in year one and live 2–3 seasons."},
    {"question": "When do I plant or sow foxgloves?", "answer": "Two windows. Direct sow seed May–July for plants that produce a rosette in year one and flower the following May–July. Plant young plants (1 L pots) in autumn (September–October) or spring (March–April) for either rosettes that flower the following year, or established plants that may flower the same year (especially Camelot series). The summer sowing window matches how foxgloves naturally self-seed in UK woodlands."},
    {"question": "Why didn't my foxgloves flower this year?", "answer": "Almost certainly because they're in year one of the biennial cycle. Foxgloves form a rosette of leaves in year one (no flowers) and the tall flowering spike in year two. Be patient — they'll flower next May–July. If you want year-one flowering, plant the Camelot series of cultivars which are bred for first-year bloom. Other causes of no flowers: deep shade (move to lighter position), or very dry soil (water in extended droughts)."},
    {"question": "How do I keep foxgloves going year after year?", "answer": "Three approaches, all easy. (1) Self-seeding: let one or two plants set seed each year — they'll perpetuate the colony indefinitely. (2) Direct sow new seed every summer in May–July; in 2 years you'll have a continuous cycle. (3) Plant new young plants every autumn or spring — buy from a garden centre or grow from seed. Most UK gardeners do a combination: let some plants self-seed, sow extra seed where you want new colonies, and occasionally buy a special cultivar to add colour variety."},
    {"question": "Are foxgloves good for wildlife?", "answer": "Outstanding for bumblebees. The long tubular flowers are specifically adapted for large bumblebees (especially queens), who push their way inside to reach the nectar at the bottom of the tube. Buff-tailed bumblebee and garden bumblebee are the main UK visitors — you'll hear them buzzing inside the flowers. Foxgloves are also a useful larval food plant for the foxglove pug moth. Don't grow foxgloves near beehives though — the small honeybees can get trapped inside the deep flowers."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'foxglove';

-- From french-bean.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow French bean seed direct from late May after the last frost; indoors in modules from late April for an earlier crop. French beans are frost-tender annuals (RHS H1c) and self-pollinating so reliable in cooler UK summers when runner beans fail to set. Two types: bush (45–60 cm, no support needed) and climbing (2 m+, wigwam or cane required). Plant 15 cm apart for bush forms; one plant per cane for climbers. Pick young pods (15–18 cm) before the beans inside swell — pods left on the plant shut down further flowering. Bush varieties: Tendergreen (reliable), Cosse Violette (purple), The Prince (heritage). Climbing: Cobra (the standard), Blue Lake, Hunter. A single climbing plant gives 6+ weeks of picking from late July to October.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose bush or climbing", "text": "Bush varieties (Tendergreen, Cosse Violette, The Prince): 45–60 cm tall, no support needed, crop in flushes. Best for small gardens and containers. Climbing varieties (Cobra, Blue Lake, Hunter): 2 m+, need a wigwam of canes, crop continuously for 6+ weeks. Better yield per square metre."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow indoors or direct", "text": "Indoors in modules from late April for an earlier crop. Direct outdoors from late May once soil is warm (above 12°C). 5 cm deep, 2 seeds per station (thin to strongest). Don't sow before mid-May outdoors — cold wet soil rots the seed."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Build the support (climbers only)", "text": "Wigwam of bamboo canes 2.5 m tall, 5–6 canes around a 1 m diameter circle. Sink each cane 30 cm into the ground for stability. Erect the support before planting."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant out after last frost", "text": "Mid-May south, early June north. Indoor-raised plants harden off for 7–10 days first. Bush forms: 15 cm apart in rows 45 cm apart. Climbers: one plant at the base of each cane."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water and mulch", "text": "Water deeply once a week from flowering onwards. Mulch with grass clippings or straw to hold moisture. French beans are reasonably drought-tolerant before flowering but pod set drops sharply in dry weather."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick young and often", "text": "From late July. Pick pods when 15–18 cm long and pencil-thin, before the beans inside swell. Pods left to bulge shut down further flowering. Pick every 2–3 days at the peak; climbing varieties give 6+ weeks of continuous cropping with this regime."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for pollination problems", "text": "French beans are self-pollinating (unlike runner beans, which need bumblebees) so they set in cool weather where runners fail. But hot dry weather above 27°C can drop flowers — water deeply and mist plants in the cool of the morning."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow French beans in the UK?", "answer": "Indoors in modules from late April for an earlier crop; direct outdoors from late May once soil is warm. Don't sow direct before mid-May — French bean seed rots in cold wet soil. Successional sowings every 3 weeks until early July give continuous picking from late July through to October."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between French beans and runner beans?", "answer": "Different species. French bean (Phaseolus vulgaris): self-pollinating; sets in cool weather; eaten as whole pods or shelled beans; both bush and climbing forms. Runner bean (Phaseolus coccineus): bee-pollinated; flowers drop in heat without pollinators; classic UK climbing bean. French beans are more reliable in poor summers; runner beans give bigger pods. Grow both for variety and security."},
    {"question": "Bush or climbing French beans?", "answer": "Bush for small gardens, containers, and where you can't stake. Crop in flushes — pick over 3–4 weeks then declines. Climbing for bigger yields per square metre, longer picking season (6+ weeks), and where you have space for a 2.5 m wigwam. Climbing varieties also give better flavour (longer in the pod)."},
    {"question": "How do I get a continuous supply of French beans?", "answer": "Successional sowings every 3 weeks from late April (indoors) through early July (direct). Each sowing crops for 3–4 weeks. Pick every 2–3 days at the peak — picking maintains flowering; pods left to bulge shut it down. A single climbing-type sowing crops for 6+ weeks of continuous picking."},
    {"question": "Why are my French bean flowers dropping without setting?", "answer": "Most likely hot dry weather (above 27°C) — the flowers drop before they pollinate. Water deeply; mulch heavily; mist plants in the cool of the morning. Less commonly, very cold weather (below 10°C nights) checks growth. French beans are self-pollinating so unlike runner beans they don't need bumblebees, but they still need the right conditions."},
    {"question": "Can I save French bean seed?", "answer": "Yes — let some pods mature fully on the plant in late summer. When the pods turn brown and the beans inside rattle when shaken, pick the pods; dry on a tray for a week; shell out the beans; store in a paper envelope. French beans come true from seed (open-pollinated cultivars). Each pod has 6–10 beans — a single plant yields plenty for next year's sowing."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'french-bean';

-- From french-tarragon.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant French tarragon from a young plant in spring — never from seed. True French tarragon doesn''t set viable seed, so any tarragon seed on sale is Russian tarragon, a related plant with almost no flavour. French tarragon is moderately hardy (RHS H4) and may struggle in cold wet UK winters on heavy soils; gritty, well-drained soil and a sunny sheltered spot are essential. Pick young shoots and leaves from May through September for the classic aniseed-tinged flavour of béarnaise sauce, chicken dishes, and herb butters. The plant gets leggy and loses vigour after 3–4 years — divide or take cuttings each spring to maintain young, strong-flavoured stock. Cut to the ground in autumn after the foliage yellows; mulch the crown in cold gardens. A single plant gives a household more tarragon than they can use.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy a plant, NEVER seed", "text": "This is the single most important rule. True French tarragon doesn't produce viable seed. Any packet labelled tarragon seed is Russian tarragon (Artemisia dracunculoides) — a related plant that looks similar but has almost no flavour. To be sure you have French tarragon, buy a plant in spring from a reputable herb nursery (Jekka's Herb Farm, Pepperpot, Hartley Botanic, or a quality garden centre that names the plant correctly). One plant lasts you for years."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in spring, in gritty well-drained soil", "text": "April–May, after the last frost. Full sun, sharp drainage — tarragon hates wet feet, especially in winter. On clay soils, plant on a raised bed or fork in plenty of horticultural grit. A south-facing spot against a wall gives the warmth and dry winter conditions French tarragon needs."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Grow in a pot if your soil is heavy or your garden is cold", "text": "A 25 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed 50/50 with horticultural grit, in a sunny sheltered spot, often gives better results than open ground in marginal UK gardens. Move the pot under cover (porch, conservatory, unheated greenhouse) in deep winter to protect the crown from waterlogging."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't feed", "text": "French tarragon flavour is best in poor, dry conditions. Rich soil and feeding produce lush growth that flops, lacks aniseed punch, and is more prone to rot."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick young shoots from May", "text": "Cut the top 10 cm of young stems with their leaves attached. The plant branches and re-grows. Strongest flavour is on the young growth in early summer (May–June); midsummer growth gets coarser. Use fresh — tarragon loses much of its flavour when dried. Freeze excess in ice cubes or steep in white wine vinegar for tarragon vinegar."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut hard in mid-summer for fresh growth", "text": "A hard trim in mid-July (cut to 10 cm above the base) triggers a fresh flush of strongly-flavoured young leaves for late summer cooking. Without this, the plant grows leggy and loses flavour intensity by August."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut to the ground in autumn and mulch", "text": "After the foliage yellows in October–November, cut all stems to 2 cm above ground. Mulch the crown with bracken, horticultural fleece, or a 10 cm pile of dry compost — French tarragon's main winter risk is rotting from wet, not freezing from cold. In cold wet gardens this mulch is the difference between survival and loss."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Divide or take cuttings every 3 years", "text": "French tarragon loses vigour and flavour after 3–4 years. Division: in early spring, lift the plant and split it into pieces with roots attached. Cuttings: in early summer, take 8 cm tip cuttings of soft new growth, push into gritty compost, keep in a cool shaded spot — root in 4–6 weeks. Either way, plant the best young pieces in a fresh spot; compost the exhausted parent."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why can't I grow French tarragon from seed?", "answer": "Because true French tarragon doesn't produce viable seed. It's a sterile cultivar selected centuries ago for its strong aniseed flavour. Any packet of tarragon seed you'll find online or in shops is Russian tarragon (Artemisia dracunculoides) — a related but inferior plant with almost no flavour. Russian tarragon is hardier and bigger, but cooks who have grown it almost always rip it out within a year and buy a French plant."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between French and Russian tarragon?", "answer": "French: Artemisia dracunculus var. sativa — narrow grey-green leaves, strong distinctive aniseed flavour, won't set seed, moderately hardy (H4), needs gritty well-drained soil. Russian: Artemisia dracunculoides — broader greener leaves, larger plant, hardier (H6+), grows from seed easily, almost no flavour. There's no contest for cooking — French is the only one worth growing. Cooks describe Russian tarragon flavour as grass or wet hay."},
    {"question": "Will French tarragon survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes in southern England and Wales, often in the Midlands, marginally further north. Hardy to about -10°C in well-drained soil, but the main UK risk is winter wet rotting the crown. Three protection strategies. (1) Plant in gritty soil; on heavy clay, grow in a pot. (2) Cut hard and mulch the crown in autumn (10 cm of bracken, fleece, or dry compost). (3) Move pots under cover in December–February. A plant that survives one winter usually survives several."},
    {"question": "Why has my French tarragon gone weak and tasteless?", "answer": "Three usual causes. Too old: French tarragon loses vigour after 3–4 years — divide it or take cuttings to start fresh. Too rich: tarragon flavour intensifies in poor soil — stop feeding, fork in grit. Wrong tarragon: confirm it's French by Latin name (A. dracunculus sativa, not A. dracunculoides). Russian tarragon is what most people complain about — even at peak vigour it has almost no flavour."},
    {"question": "Can I dry French tarragon?", "answer": "Not really — it loses most of its flavour. Tarragon's volatile oils don't dry well. Two alternatives. Tarragon vinegar: stuff a bottle with fresh sprigs and fill with white wine vinegar — ready in 2 weeks, lasts for years. Frozen tarragon: chop fresh leaves, pack into ice cube trays with water, freeze. Drop a cube into a sauce. Either method captures the flavour better than drying."},
    {"question": "When should I cut tarragon back?", "answer": "Twice a year for kitchen use. A hard cut to 10 cm in mid-July triggers a fresh flush of strongly-flavoured leaves for late summer cooking. A final cut to 2 cm above ground in late October when foliage yellows, followed by a winter mulch over the crown. Don't prune in spring beyond removing dead stems — let the new growth establish before any cutting."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'french-tarragon';

-- From garlic.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant garlic cloves in autumn — mid-October to mid-November for hardneck varieties; spring planting (February to early March) is possible for softneck. Autumn-planted hardnecks need 4–6 weeks of cold below 10°C to bulb properly, which the UK winter delivers reliably. Plant cloves 5 cm deep, 15 cm apart, in a sunny well-drained bed with the pointed end up. Garlic is very hardy (RHS H6) and shrugs off frost. Harvest from mid-June to late July when half the leaves have yellowed and started to droop. Dry the bulbs in an airy place for two weeks before storing. UK-grown garlic stores until the following spring.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose your cloves", "text": "Buy seed garlic, not supermarket cloves (which may not be UK-suited or virus-free). Hardneck varieties (Music, Carcassonne Wight) for autumn; softneck (Solent Wight, Cristo) for spring."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Mid-October. Fork over a sunny spot; rake to fine tilth; don't add fresh manure. Garlic prefers slightly alkaline soil — a light dusting of garden lime if your pH is below 6.5."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant", "text": "Mid-October to mid-November for autumn planting; February to early March for spring planting. Push individual cloves 5 cm deep, pointed end up, 15 cm apart in rows 30 cm apart. Cover."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Mulch", "text": "Apply a 2–3 cm mulch of compost or leaf mould after planting. Keeps the bed warm in winter and weed-free in spring."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Weed", "text": "Hand-weed throughout the growing season. Garlic hates competition. Don't hoe close to the cloves; you'll damage the developing bulb."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Remove scapes (hardnecks only)", "text": "In late May to June, hardneck garlic sends up a curling flower stalk (the scape). Snap it off when young — the plant's energy then goes into the bulb. The scapes themselves are edible and excellent in stir-fries."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Harvest", "text": "When half the foliage has yellowed and the lower leaves are dying back (mid-June to late July depending on cultivar and region). Loosen with a fork; pull gently. Don't yank — the necks snap and the bulb won't store."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Cure", "text": "Lay the whole plants in a single layer in an airy frost-free shed or under cover for 2–3 weeks. Once the foliage is fully dry and papery, trim roots and necks (leave 5 cm of neck), brush off loose soil. Don't wash. Store in mesh bags or plaited in a cool dry place."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant garlic in the UK?", "answer": "Mid-October to mid-November for hardneck varieties (the most flavourful and the best UK-adapted). February to early March for softneck varieties. Don't plant in mid-winter — cold wet soil rots the cloves before they root. Hardneck garlic needs 4–6 weeks of cold below 10°C to form a proper bulb, which is why autumn planting is the UK default."},
    {"question": "Can I plant supermarket garlic cloves?", "answer": "You can, but the results are unpredictable. Supermarket garlic may be a variety unsuited to the UK climate (Chinese garlic, for example, is bred for warmer winters), it may carry viruses, and the bulbs may be too small for good results. Buy seed garlic from a UK supplier — the cost is small and the harvest is reliably better."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between hardneck and softneck garlic?", "answer": "Hardneck (Music, Carcassonne Wight, Lautrec Wight) has a stiff central stem (the neck), bigger cloves, stronger flavour, and produces edible flower scapes in late spring. Less storage life — usually until December. Softneck (Solent Wight, Cristo, Albigensian Wight) has flexible necks (can be plaited), smaller cloves, milder flavour, stores until the following spring. Hardneck is better suited to autumn planting in the UK."},
    {"question": "Why are my garlic bulbs small?", "answer": "Most common causes: planted too late, soil too rich (high nitrogen makes leaves not bulbs), too much competition from weeds, or not enough cold over winter. Hardneck needs at least 4–6 weeks below 10°C — a very mild winter can result in single-clove rounds instead of segmented bulbs. Plant at the right time, weed religiously, and choose a UK-adapted variety."},
    {"question": "When is garlic ready to harvest?", "answer": "Mid-June to late July for autumn-planted hardneck; July to August for spring-planted softneck. Watch the foliage — when about half has yellowed and the lower leaves are turning brown, the bulbs are ready. Lift one as a test; the cloves should be clearly defined inside the papery wrapping. Don't wait until all the foliage dies back — the wrapping deteriorates and the bulb won't store."},
    {"question": "How do I store garlic?", "answer": "Cure the harvested plants (whole, with roots and leaves still attached) in an airy frost-free shed for 2–3 weeks until the foliage is fully dry and papery. Trim roots and cut the stem 5 cm above the bulb. Don't wash. Store in mesh bags, woven trays, or plaited (softneck only) in a cool dry well-ventilated place. Hardneck keeps 4–6 months; softneck keeps 8–10 months."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'garlic';

-- From german-chamomile.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow German chamomile direct from March to May, in a sunny well-drained spot in poor-to-ordinary soil — it''s an easy hardy annual grown for the daisy-like flowers used in classic chamomile tea. German chamomile is moderately hardy (RHS H4) and grows to 60 cm tall, producing small white-petalled flowers with bright yellow domed centres from June through September. Don''t confuse with Roman chamomile (Chamaemelum nobile) — the perennial creeping form used for chamomile lawns; same flowers, different plant, different uses. Pick flowers when the white petals start to reflex backwards from the yellow centre (peak essential oil), dry on trays in a warm dry place for 1–2 weeks, store in a sealed jar. German chamomile self-seeds so a single sowing perpetuates indefinitely. Excellent for bees and hoverflies. Avoid in pregnancy and if allergic to other daisy family plants (asters, ragweed).',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick German over Roman for tea", "text": "German chamomile (Matricaria recutita): annual, upright 60 cm, hollow yellow flower centre, the standard for tea — the flowers harvested commercially. The choice if you want a herb-tea crop. Roman chamomile (Chamaemelum nobile): perennial, creeping 15 cm, solid yellow flower centre, traditional for chamomile lawns and seats. Flowers are smaller, less productive, and milder-flavoured but still usable. The choice if you want a fragrant lawn. For tea, plant German. They look similar — don't substitute."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct from March to May", "text": "Surface-sow seed direct where you want plants to grow — German chamomile needs light to germinate, so don't cover. Press lightly into damp soil. Sow in March–May for summer flowering, or in September for an autumn-sown crop that overwinters as small plants and flowers earlier the following year."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Choose a sunny, well-drained spot", "text": "Full sun. Poor or ordinary soil — rich soil produces lush growth and fewer flowers. German chamomile grows wild on disturbed sandy soils, so it tolerates almost anything that drains. Spacing 20–30 cm after thinning."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Thin seedlings to 20 cm", "text": "Germination in 7–14 days. Thin to one plant every 20 cm when seedlings have 2–3 true leaves. Overcrowded plants flower poorly and are prone to mildew in damp summers."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick flowers at the right stage", "text": "The critical detail. Pick flowers when the white petals are still flat or have just started reflexing downwards away from the yellow centre — this is peak essential oil concentration. Younger flowers (petals upright or flat) have less aroma; older flowers (petals fully reflexed, going brown) have started to drop their essential oils. Pick in dry weather, mid-morning after dew has evaporated."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Dry on trays", "text": "Spread freshly-picked flowers in a single layer on trays or muslin in a warm dry airy place — an airing cupboard, kitchen ceiling, or a low-heat dehydrator works well. After 1–2 weeks the flowers should feel papery and crumble easily. Store in a sealed jar away from light."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Brew the tea", "text": "Standard ratio: 1 tablespoon dried flowers (or 2 tablespoons fresh) per cup of boiling water. Steep covered for 5 minutes (covering keeps the essential oils in). Strain. Honey complements wonderfully. Traditional uses: calming before sleep, soothing for upset digestion, gentle anti-inflammatory."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Let it self-seed for next year", "text": "German chamomile self-seeds prolifically — once you have it, you have it forever in that spot. Let the last flowers go to seed (don't pick or deadhead in late September) and a new generation appears the following spring. To stop self-seeding, deadhead all flowers before they set seed."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What's the difference between German and Roman chamomile?", "answer": "Different species, different growth habits, same characteristic chamomile flowers. German chamomile (Matricaria recutita): annual, upright 60 cm, hollow yellow flower centre — this is the chamomile of tea bags and commercial drying. Roman chamomile (Chamaemelum nobile): perennial creeping 15 cm, solid yellow flower centre — used for fragrant chamomile lawns, with smaller and slightly less productive flowers. For tea: plant German. For a lawn: plant Roman (the Treneague non-flowering cultivar is the lawn standard)."},
    {"question": "When and how do I pick chamomile flowers for tea?", "answer": "When the white petals are just beginning to reflex (curve backwards) away from the yellow centre — peak essential oil concentration. Pick in dry weather, mid-morning after dew has evaporated. Use scissors or your fingernails to nip the flower head off below the petals. Spread in a single layer on trays and dry in a warm dry airy place for 1–2 weeks; store in a sealed jar away from light. One plant produces enough flowers for several pots of tea per season; six plants gives a year's supply."},
    {"question": "Will chamomile come back every year?", "answer": "German chamomile is annual — each plant lives one season and dies. But it self-seeds so prolifically that the colony perpetuates indefinitely without effort: a single un-deadheaded plant produces hundreds of viable seeds. Roman chamomile (the lawn species) is a true perennial that returns from the rootstock each spring. If your chamomile is creeping low and feathery, it's Roman; if it's upright 60 cm and dies after flowering, it's German."},
    {"question": "Can I drink chamomile tea every day?", "answer": "For most healthy adults, yes — it's one of the safest herbal teas. Traditional UK use is daily and well-tolerated. Avoid or consult a doctor if you're pregnant, on blood-thinning medication (warfarin), allergic to other daisy-family plants (asters, ragweed, marigolds), or about to undergo surgery (within 2 weeks). Some people develop hay-fever-style allergies to chamomile pollen. Children: small quantities of weak tea are traditional but check with your pharmacist for very young ones."},
    {"question": "My chamomile is getting mildew — what's wrong?", "answer": "Crowded planting + damp summer + still air. Thin plants to 20–30 cm apart for good airflow. Water at the base, not over the foliage. Avoid feeding (lush growth is mildew-prone). Mildewed plants can still be cropped (mildew is on the leaves, not the flowers) but flower production drops. Pull badly affected plants and rely on the self-sown seedlings the following year."},
    {"question": "Is chamomile good for my garden?", "answer": "Yes — multiple benefits. The flowers are excellent for bees and hoverflies (especially syrphid flies, which are major aphid predators). German chamomile is also called the plant physician in old herbals because growing it near other plants is supposed to make them more vigorous and disease-resistant — likely an effect of the beneficial insects it attracts. Plant a patch in or near the veg garden for pollinator and pest-control benefits."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'german-chamomile';

-- From globe-artichoke.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant globe artichoke offsets in spring, in full sun in deep well-drained soil — they''re handsome perennial plants (1.5 m tall) grown for the immature flower buds, which are eaten before they open. Globe artichokes are moderately hardy (RHS H4) and survive most UK winters with a thick crown mulch; in cold or wet gardens they may die. Green Globe and Violetto di Chioggia are the popular UK varieties. Plant offsets (small rooted shoots from the base of established plants) rather than seed — seed-grown plants are variable and often spiny. Cut buds when fully formed but still tightly closed — once the scales open it''s too late, the bud has become a flower. Replace every 4–5 years by planting fresh offsets; established plants get less productive. Architectural enough to earn a place even if you never harvest.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose offsets over seed", "text": "Seed-grown plants are variable — some produce small spiny buds with poor flavour. Offsets (rooted side-shoots taken from established plants) come true to the parent variety and crop reliably. Buy offsets from a specialist herb/veg nursery (Pennard Plants, Marshalls, RHS Wisley shop) in March–April. One plant produces 4–6 buds per year at peak; allow 2–3 plants for a household."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pick a UK-suitable variety", "text": "Green Globe: the UK standard — vigorous, large green buds, reliable cropping, hardy enough for most gardens. The safe choice for a first artichoke. Violetto di Chioggia: smaller purple-green buds, attractive ornamental, slightly less hardy. Imperial Star: bred to crop in year 1 from seed — useful if buying seed (rare) rather than offsets. Gros Vert de Laon: heritage variety, large green-purple buds, decent UK performance."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in deep, well-drained, sunny soil", "text": "Globe artichokes want deep fertile soil that holds moisture but doesn't waterlog. Full sun essential — 6+ hours direct sun for proper cropping. On heavy clay: build a raised bed or fork in plenty of grit and well-rotted manure before planting. Spacing 1 m apart, in rows 1.5 m apart — these are big plants (1.5 m tall and 90 cm wide at maturity)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant offsets in spring", "text": "April or early May, after the worst frosts have passed. Set offsets at the same depth they were growing (the small rosette of leaves at the soil surface). Water in deeply. Mulch with a 5 cm layer of well-rotted manure around (not on) the crown."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Don't expect a crop in year 1", "text": "Year 1: leaf growth only — let the plant establish, remove any flower buds that form (they exhaust a young plant). Year 2: first proper crop — 4–6 buds per plant in June–August. Years 3–5: peak cropping. Year 5+: declining — replace by planting fresh offsets taken from the parent."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut buds when fully formed but tightly closed", "text": "The harvest window is narrow. Cut buds when they're fully expanded to maximum size but still tight — the scales should still be pressed closely together. Cut with secateurs, leaving 5–10 cm of stem attached. Once the scales start to open (the bud begins becoming a flower), it's past the eating stage — the choke (the fuzzy bit at the centre) has developed and is inedible. Let those open buds become flowers — they're stunning purple thistle-flowers and excellent for bees."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Mulch the crown in autumn for winter protection", "text": "Globe artichoke's main UK weakness is cold wet winters. In November, cut spent stems down to 30 cm, then pile a 15–20 cm deep mulch of bracken, straw, or dry leaves over the crown to protect from frost. Remove the mulch in spring once new growth appears (March–April). In very cold gardens (Highland Scotland, exposed sites), grow in a pot that moves to a cold greenhouse for winter."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Take offsets every spring to perpetuate", "text": "Year 3 onwards: lift offsets from the base of established plants each spring. Use a sharp spade to slice down through the soil, separating a young side-shoot with its own roots from the parent crown. Replant immediately. Use the offsets to: (1) replace declining parent plants (year 5+), (2) expand the bed, (3) give to friends. This is how globe artichokes have been propagated for centuries."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I harvest globe artichokes?", "answer": "June to August in most UK gardens — but only from year 2 onwards. Year 1 plants should have any flower buds removed early to let the plant establish; cropping starts in year 2. Cut buds when fully formed but tightly closed — the scales should still be pressed together, the bud feels firm. Use secateurs, leaving 5–10 cm of stem. Once the scales start to spread (the bud is becoming a flower), it's too late — the choke inside has developed."},
    {"question": "Will globe artichokes survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Hardy in most UK gardens with proper protection (RHS H4) — survives down to about -10°C with a thick crown mulch. Main UK risk is cold wet winters on heavy soils: the crown rots. Protection regime: cut spent stems to 30 cm in November, pile 15–20 cm of dry mulch (bracken, straw, leaves) over the crown, remove mulch in spring. In very cold gardens (Highland Scotland): grow in a large pot that moves to a cold greenhouse for winter."},
    {"question": "How many globe artichokes will one plant produce?", "answer": "4–6 buds per year at peak (years 3–5). Year 1: zero (let it establish). Year 2: 1–3 buds. Years 3–5: full cropping (4–6 buds). Years 5+: declining (3–4 buds). For a household: plant 2–3 globe artichokes spaced 1 m apart for a regular weekly harvest from June through August during peak years. Each bud is a single artichoke serving."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between globe artichoke and Jerusalem artichoke?", "answer": "Completely different plants — and the names mislead. Globe artichoke (Cynara cardunculus var. scolymus, this plant): a thistle relative grown for the unopened flower buds. Jerusalem artichoke (Helianthus tuberosus — see separate jerusalem-artichoke slug): a sunflower relative grown for the underground tubers. The Jerusalem name is a corruption of the Italian girasole (sunflower); they have no botanical or culinary connection to globe artichokes despite sharing the word."},
    {"question": "Why are my globe artichoke buds small or spiny?", "answer": "Three causes. (1) Seed-grown plant: seedling artichokes are variable — many produce small spiny buds with poor flavour. Replace with named-variety offsets (Green Globe, Violetto di Chioggia). (2) Too young or too old: year-2 plants produce smaller buds than peak (years 3–5); year-5+ plants decline. Plant fresh offsets every few years. (3) Drought stress: artichokes shut down in dry weather, producing small tough buds. Water deeply weekly in dry spells."},
    {"question": "Can I grow globe artichokes in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and in cold gardens it's often the only way. Use a 50 cm deep pot of peat-free compost mixed with garden compost. Position in full sun. Advantage: the pot can move to a cold greenhouse or sheltered porch for winter protection. Disadvantages: pot plants are smaller and crop less (2–4 buds vs 4–6); they need more regular feeding (monthly liquid feed) and watering. Repot every 2–3 years; refresh top compost annually. Lift offsets from pot plants the same way as ground plants."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'globe-artichoke';

-- From gooseberry.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant gooseberry bushes bare-root from November to March, in sun or light shade, in any decent garden soil — they''re one of the easiest UK soft fruits and a long-lived workhorse (15+ years from a single planting). Gooseberries are very hardy (RHS H6), shrug off any UK winter, and crop reliably with modest care. Prune to a goblet shape with an open centre to let air and sun in, which keeps mildew at bay. Watch for gooseberry sawfly from late April — the caterpillars can strip a bush in a week. Modern mildew-resistant varieties like Invicta, Hinnonmaki Red, and Greenfinch make gooseberries far easier than 30 years ago. Wear thick gloves when pruning: the thorns are vicious. Pick green for cooking from late May, red and sweet for dessert from July.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose dessert vs culinary, thorn vs thornless", "text": "Dessert gooseberries are sweet enough to eat raw when fully ripe — Hinnonmaki Red, Captivator (nearly thornless), Whinham's Industry. Culinary are tart, for cooking — Invicta (mildew-resistant powerhouse), Careless. For a single bush, Hinnonmaki Red is the all-rounder: dessert when ripe, culinary when green. Thornless varieties exist (Pax, Captivator) but are less vigorous; the thorny ones crop heavier."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March, while the bush is dormant. Bare-root is cheaper and establishes faster than container-grown. Plant on a slight mound to prevent waterlogging. Spacing: 1.5 m apart for standard bushes; 30 cm for cordons; 1 m for fan-trained against a wall."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Train to a goblet shape", "text": "The classic gooseberry shape: an open-centred bowl on a short clear stem (15 cm). Pick 5–6 main branches angled outward and upward from the centre, removing anything that crosses inward. Open centre = air circulation = less mildew = easier picking despite the thorns."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Summer prune in late June", "text": "Cut all the new sideshoot growth back to five leaves in late June. This exposes the developing fruit to sunlight (which sweetens them), keeps the bush manageable, and encourages spur formation for next year's crop."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Winter prune for renewal", "text": "February. Shorten the previous summer's main shoots by half, cut back the sideshoots already shortened in June to two buds, remove any old wood (more than 4 years old) at the base, take out anything crossing into the centre. Don't be timid — gooseberries fruit on spurs on 2–3 year-old wood, so renewing the framework keeps cropping heavy."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Watch for sawfly from late April", "text": "Gooseberry sawfly is the dominant pest. The caterpillars (pale green with black spots) appear from late April and can strip a bush bare in 5–7 days. Check under the leaves of the lowest branches every 2–3 days from late April. Pick caterpillars off by hand at first sign — it's quick when you catch them early. A bad infestation can be controlled with a pyrethrum spray."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Net against birds and squirrels", "text": "Bullfinches strip the fruit buds in winter (the most damaging — no buds, no crop). Blackbirds and starlings take the fruit from July. Net the bush from December if you have bullfinches around, and again as fruit ripens. A walk-in fruit cage solves both problems permanently."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick green and red across the summer", "text": "Late May–June: pick the largest green berries for cooking (gooseberry fool, gooseberry crumble, gooseberry jam). This thins the crop and lets remaining fruit grow bigger. Late June–July: leave the rest to ripen fully — dessert varieties turn red/yellow/green-yellow and become sweet enough to eat off the bush. Wear thick gloves."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I prune a gooseberry bush?", "answer": "Two prunes a year. Late June (summer prune): cut new sideshoots back to five leaves to expose fruit and improve air flow. February (winter prune): shorten main shoots by half, cut sideshoots back to two buds, remove any wood older than 4 years. The goal is an open goblet shape with a clear centre."},
    {"question": "My gooseberry leaves are being stripped — what's eating them?", "answer": "Gooseberry sawfly caterpillars — pale green with small black spots, often the lowest leaves first. They appear from late April and can defoliate a bush in a week. Check under the leaves every 2–3 days from late April. Hand-pick at first sight; for a bad infestation, a pyrethrum-based spray applied late evening (after pollinators have stopped flying). Pheromone traps don't work for sawfly."},
    {"question": "What's the best gooseberry variety for a UK garden?", "answer": "Hinnonmaki Red for a one-bush garden — dessert when fully ripe, culinary when green, good crop, decent mildew resistance. Invicta for the heaviest crops and best mildew resistance (culinary). Captivator for a nearly thornless option (dessert, red, smaller crop than thorny varieties). Avoid old varieties like Whinham's Industry if mildew is a problem in your area; the modern ones are bred to resist it."},
    {"question": "What's the white powder on my gooseberry leaves?", "answer": "American gooseberry mildew — historically a real problem, much less so with modern resistant varieties. If you have an older susceptible variety: prune to open up the centre for air flow, water at the base not the foliage, clear fallen leaves in autumn, consider sulphur sprays in early spring. Long-term, replace with Invicta or another resistant cultivar."},
    {"question": "Are gooseberries hard to grow?", "answer": "No — they're one of the easiest soft fruits in UK gardens once planted. The hardest parts are pruning (which is unforgiving until you've done it twice) and dealing with sawfly (which needs vigilance from late April). Gooseberries are extremely cold-hardy, tolerate light shade better than most fruit, and don't need a pollination partner — a single bush will crop happily."},
    {"question": "How do I know when gooseberries are ripe?", "answer": "For cooking: pick the largest hard green berries from late May. They'll soften and sweeten with cooking. For eating raw: leave dessert varieties on the bush until they turn fully red (or yellow-green for green dessert types), feel slightly soft, and pull off the bush with light pressure. A truly ripe gooseberry is nothing like the tart cooking ones — it has a grape-like sweetness."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'gooseberry';

-- From hardy-geranium.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant hardy geraniums in spring or autumn, in sun or shade, in almost any soil — they''re the most reliable and forgiving perennials for UK gardens, with species adapted to every condition from full sun to dry shade to woodland. Rozanne is the most popular of all (RHS Plant of the Centenary 2013) — clear violet-blue saucer flowers from May until first frost on a sprawling 50 cm mound. Hardy geraniums (RHS H5–H7) are not the same as pelargoniums (often called geraniums in garden centres) — pelargoniums are tender summer bedding; hardy geraniums are fully winter-hardy perennials. Cut the whole plant back by half after the first flush in late June to trigger a second flush. Don''t confuse with the cranesbill weed — wild herb-robert (G. robertianum) self-seeds across gardens; pull young seedlings before they spread. Easy, beautiful, indestructible.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick a hardy geranium that matches your conditions", "text": "Sun / mixed border: Rozanne (violet-blue, May–first frost, 50 cm sprawling) is the standout. Brookside (rich blue, more upright). Patricia (magenta, vigorous). Dry shade: G. macrorrhizum (Album, Spessart) — bombproof ground cover, fragrant foliage, light pink flowers, the toughest of all. Woodland edge: G. phaeum Samobor (deep maroon, dark leaf markings), G. nodosum (lavender, evergreen). Cottage / informal: G. × oxonianum Wargrave Pink (clear pink, prolific), G. pratense (the wild meadow cranesbill, deep blue, 1 m). Avoid pelargoniums (the red/pink summer bedding pots labelled geranium) — different genus, tender, different plant entirely."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in spring or autumn", "text": "Container-grown plants are sold year-round; best planting windows are March–May or September–October. Plant at the same depth they were in the pot. Spacing 40–60 cm depending on cultivar vigour. Water in deeply."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Match the soil and exposure to the species", "text": "One of the great virtues of hardy geraniums: there's a species for every UK garden condition. Most tolerate ordinary garden soil with reasonable drainage. Sun-loving (Rozanne, pratense, sanguineum): full sun, well-drained. Shade-loving (macrorrhizum, phaeum, nodosum): part to full shade, even dry shade. Front of border (cinereum, dalmaticum): compact, full sun. Check the specific cultivar before planting."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Cut hard back in late June for a second flush", "text": "The key husbandry technique for most hardy geraniums. After the first flush of flowers fades in late June, cut the whole plant back by half to two-thirds (some gardeners cut to ground level). The plant resprouts within 2 weeks with fresh foliage and a second flush of flowers lasting into autumn. Rozanne is the exception — it flowers continuously from May to first frost without needing the chop, though it still benefits from a light tidy mid-summer."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Don't feed (much)", "text": "Hardy geraniums flower best in ordinary unimproved garden soil. Feeding tends to produce more foliage at the expense of flowers, and softer growth more prone to flopping. A spring mulch of garden compost is sufficient. No liquid feeding required."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Stake the tallest species", "text": "Most hardy geraniums sprawl elegantly without support. Two exceptions: G. pratense (the meadow cranesbill — 1 m, flops after rain) and tall hybrids like Patricia (90 cm) benefit from a grow-through plant support placed in spring. Rozanne sprawls intentionally — let it weave through neighbours rather than staking."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Divide every 4–5 years in spring", "text": "Hardy geranium clumps gradually become congested and the centre dies out. In early spring, lift the clump, split with a spade into 3–6 sections, replant the best outer pieces in fresh soil. Don't divide in autumn for borderline-hardy species (cinereum types). G. macrorrhizum is happy with division any time it's growing."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pull wild herb-robert seedlings", "text": "Wild herb-robert (Geranium robertianum) is a UK native annual cranesbill that self-seeds aggressively — small pink flowers, deeply-cut reddish leaves, smells unpleasant when bruised. Often appears in gardens with mature geraniums or in shady corners. Pull young seedlings while small (March–May) before they set seed. Not toxic and quite pretty in a wild corner, but aggressive in cultivated borders. Don't confuse with the desirable garden cranesbills."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Are pelargoniums and hardy geraniums the same plant?", "answer": "No — completely different plants, often confused. Pelargoniums (often sold as geraniums in garden centres, especially the red and pink summer bedding pots) are tender perennials from South Africa — they don't survive UK winters outdoors. Hardy geraniums (true Geranium species — the cranesbills) are fully winter-hardy perennials that die back each autumn and return in spring. Quick test: if it's a bright red flowering pot plant for sale in May–June as bedding, it's a pelargonium. If it's labelled with a Geranium species or cultivar name (Rozanne, macrorrhizum, etc.) and sold as a perennial, it's a hardy geranium."},
    {"question": "When do I cut back a hardy geranium?", "answer": "Late June for most varieties — and again in late autumn. After the first flush of flowers fades (typically late June), cut the plant back by half to two-thirds to trigger a second flush of flowers and fresh foliage. Rozanne is the exception — it flowers continuously May–first frost and needs only a light tidy mid-summer. Late autumn (after second flush): cut to 10 cm to tidy the plant for winter. The plant returns each spring from the rootstock."},
    {"question": "What's the best hardy geranium for the UK?", "answer": "Rozanne — Plant of the Centenary 2013, violet-blue saucer flowers from May to first frost, vigorous 50 cm sprawling habit, ideal mid-border. For dry shade: G. macrorrhizum — bombproof ground cover, fragrant foliage, light pink flowers. For woodland edge: G. phaeum Samobor — deep maroon flowers, dark leaf markings. For meadow / cottage style: G. pratense — the wild meadow cranesbill, deep blue. Hardy geraniums have a species for every UK condition."},
    {"question": "Will hardy geraniums grow in shade?", "answer": "Yes — some are shade specialists. G. macrorrhizum (and cultivars Album, Spessart, Czakor) is the toughest dry-shade ground cover available — works under trees and in north-facing positions where most plants fail. G. phaeum (mourning widow) and G. nodosum thrive in deeper shade with regular flower production. G. sylvaticum is happy at woodland edges. Rozanne and sun-loving species (pratense, sanguineum) need at least 4 hours direct sun for best flowering."},
    {"question": "Why is my hardy geranium not flowering well?", "answer": "Three common causes. (1) Too shaded for the species (sun-lovers in shade flower poorly). (2) Needs dividing: clumps over 4–5 years old get congested and flower less — divide in early spring. (3) No mid-summer chop: many varieties flower once in June and look tatty unless cut back in late June for a second flush. Rozanne specifically: should flower continuously — if it's not, check sun levels and clump age."},
    {"question": "Are hardy geraniums good for wildlife?", "answer": "Very — particularly for bees and hoverflies. The open saucer-shaped flowers are accessible to most pollinators. Bumblebees, honeybees, solitary bees, hoverflies all work the flowers. G. pratense (meadow cranesbill) is the most pollinator-rich — naturalises in meadow plantings and supports a wide range of bees. Hardy geraniums also provide dense ground cover habitat for insects and small wildlife at ground level. A hardy geranium border is a quietly productive pollinator garden."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'hardy-geranium';

-- From hazelnut.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant hazelnut bushes bare-root from November to March, in sun or part shade, in well-drained soil — they''re a vigorous UK native shrub or small tree (4–6 m), traditional source of cobnuts and filberts, and equally valuable as a coppice plant for poles and beanpoles. Hazel is very hardy (RHS H6), thrives in almost any soil except waterlogged ground, and pollinates by wind in February when the lambs-tail catkins shed pollen onto the tiny red female flowers. Plant at least two different varieties for cross-pollination — most cobnuts and filberts are not self-fertile. Grey squirrels are the dominant pest — they take nuts unripe in August, before you''d think to harvest. Kentish Cob and Cosford are the classic UK varieties. Coppice every 7–10 years for harvest of straight 2 m hazel poles (peas, beans, garden structures).',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose nut-cropping or coppice form", "text": "Nut-cropping: plant named varieties for big yields of edible nuts. Kentish Cob (the UK standard cobnut), Cosford (medium oval nut, easy to crack), Butler (modern, heavy cropping), Halle's Giant (very large filbert nut). Plant 2–3 different varieties for cross-pollination. Coppice form: plant the wild UK species (Corylus avellana unselected) for traditional coppicing — cut to ground every 7–10 years for straight 2 m hazel poles useful for pea/bean sticks, garden structures, hurdles. Grown on a coppice cycle, the same stool produces poles for 200+ years."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant at least two for pollination", "text": "Hazels are wind-pollinated and most varieties are self-incompatible — they need pollen from a different cultivar. Kentish Cob + Cosford is the classic UK pairing — both crop heavily and pollinate each other. Butler + Ennis is the modern combination. A single hazel bush flowers and produces catkins but sets few or no nuts. The pollination range is wide (50+ m), so a neighbour's hazel may already be doing the job."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March while dormant. Bare-root plants establish faster and cheaper than container-grown. Spacing 4–5 m apart for nut-cropping bushes; 1.5 m for coppice rows. Plant at the same depth as the nursery; water in. Hazel tolerates almost any soil — chalky, acid, sandy, clay (provided drainage), exposed or sheltered."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Train as a bush or a stool", "text": "Bush form for nut-cropping: maintain 6–8 main stems rising from near ground level, removing suckers and crossing branches each winter. Mature bushes 3–5 m tall by 4 m wide. Coppice stool for poles: cut every stem to the ground (a stool 10–15 cm above soil) every 7–10 years in winter. Each stool re-grows a thicket of straight new poles. Productive stools last 200+ years."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Light prune in winter for nut-cropping bushes", "text": "February is the only pruning window — hazel flowers in February (the catkins) and pruning later removes the flowers and the crop. Each winter: remove dead, diseased, crossing branches; thin overcrowded growth to maintain the bush shape; shorten over-vigorous shoots. Keep the centre open for airflow."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Defend against squirrels — the dominant pest", "text": "Grey squirrels strip unripe nuts in August, before they've fully matured. This is the single biggest reason garden hazels fail to crop. Three defences. (1) Pick nuts as soon as they reach full size and the husks start to brown (early August) — even if not fully ripe, they'll dry indoors. (2) Net the bush with fine mesh during August (works for small hazels). (3) Live-trap squirrels under licence (UK law restricts release after capture — humane dispatch only). Walk-in fruit cages keep them out permanently."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick nuts in August–September", "text": "Cobnuts (Kentish Cob) ripen from early August. The husk that surrounds each nut turns brown at the edges as the kernel inside reaches full size. Pick nuts before they fall — fallen nuts get eaten by wildlife within hours. Strip the husks off, spread the nuts on a tray to dry in a warm dry place for 2–3 weeks. Store in a dry, cool, vermin-proof place. They keep 6+ months."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Coppice cycle for hazel poles (if grown for wood)", "text": "Every 7–10 years in winter (December–February), cut every stem on the stool down to 10–15 cm. The stool re-shoots vigorously the following spring with a thicket of straight young poles. After 3–4 years they're useful for pea/bean sticks (1.5 m), after 7 years for beanpoles and structural canes (2.5 m), after 10 years for hedge laying. Stagger coppicing across multiple plants if you want a continuous supply."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Do I need more than one hazel tree?", "answer": "Yes — almost always. Most cobnut and filbert varieties are self-incompatible (their own pollen doesn't fertilise their own flowers). You need at least two different varieties planted within 50 m for cross-pollination. Kentish Cob + Cosford is the classic UK pairing. A single hazel will produce catkins (the dangling male flowers) and small red female flowers, but sets few or no nuts. The wind moves pollen between trees in February, so a neighbour's hazel might be doing the pollination work already."},
    {"question": "Why isn't my hazel producing nuts?", "answer": "Three usual causes. (1) Squirrels take them unripe in August — by the time you check in September, the bush appears barren but the nuts were taken weeks earlier. Net the bush or pick at full size in early August even if not fully ripe. (2) No pollination partner: most varieties need another variety nearby. (3) Too shaded: hazels tolerate part shade but heavy shade reduces flowering and cropping. Move the bush, or thin overhead canopy."},
    {"question": "When are hazelnuts ready to pick?", "answer": "Early to mid-August for cobnuts (full-size kernel, husks browning at the edges, but nut still slightly soft). Late August to September for filberts (the longer husks fully cover the nut). The squirrel test: if grey squirrels are taking them, they're getting too ripe to leave on the tree. Pick from the bush rather than waiting for fallen nuts (which are eaten within hours of falling). Spread on a tray to dry for 2–3 weeks in a warm dry place before storing."},
    {"question": "What's the best hazelnut variety for the UK?", "answer": "Kentish Cob is the UK standard cobnut — large round-oval nuts, reliable heavy cropping in southern England, the traditional commercial choice. Cosford is the classic pollination partner — medium oval nuts, easy to crack, sweet flavour. Butler is the modern heavy-cropping choice. For ornamental use: Contorta (Harry Lauder's walking stick — see separate harry-lauders-walking-stick slug) is grown for twisted stems, not nuts. Atropurpurea has purple foliage but cropping is light."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between a cobnut and a filbert?", "answer": "Both are forms of Corylus avellana, distinguished by the length of the husk relative to the nut. Cobnut: husk shorter than the nut, leaving the nut tip visible (e.g. Kentish Cob, Cosford). Filbert: husk longer than the nut, fully covering it (e.g. Kentish Filbert, Halle's Giant, most American varieties). Both crop similarly; filberts often have a slightly sweeter flavour but are less commercially common in the UK."},
    {"question": "How do I coppice a hazel?", "answer": "Cut every stem on the stool to 10–15 cm above ground, in winter (December–February). The plant re-shoots vigorously from the cut stool the following spring with a thicket of straight new poles. After 3–4 years, poles are pea-stick thickness (1.5 m, 2 cm diameter); after 7 years, beanpole thickness (2.5 m); after 10 years, hurdle weaving and hedge-laying material. Hazels live 100–200 years on a continuous coppice cycle — by far the most sustainable garden carpentry material UK gardens produce."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'hazelnut';

-- From hellebore.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant hellebores in autumn or spring, in part to full shade in moisture-retentive soil enriched with leaf-mould — they''re hardy evergreen perennials that flower from December to April when little else is in bloom, making them indispensable for UK winter gardens. Hellebores are very hardy (RHS H7) and long-lived (15+ years from a single planting). Cut all the OLD leaves down to the ground in late autumn (November) — this is the single most important husbandry rule, showcasing the flowers when they emerge and removing the foliage that carries hellebore leaf-spot fungus. "Lenten rose" types (Helleborus orientalis and modern hybrids) are by far the most popular — flower in nodding clusters of cream, white, pink, plum, near-black, and yellow. All parts toxic if eaten — wear gloves when handling.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick the right hellebore type", "text": "Lenten rose (Helleborus orientalis and hybrids — H. × hybridus): the most popular UK choice, flowers February–April, vigorous clumps 45 cm tall, huge range of flower colours. The safe first choice. Christmas rose (Helleborus niger): white outward-facing flowers December–February, slower-growing, more demanding (drainage essential), shorter (30 cm). Stinking hellebore (Helleborus foetidus): UK native, pale green flowers with red rims, January–April, architectural foliage. Corsican hellebore (Helleborus argutifolius): pale green flowers, marbled bluish-grey leaves, taller (90 cm), more sun-tolerant. For a first hellebore: Lenten rose hybrid (Helleborus × hybridus) — easy, reliable, dramatic."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in part to full shade", "text": "Hellebores are woodland-edge plants. Position under deciduous trees, alongside north-facing walls, in light or full shade. Avoid full sun — leaves scorch and plants sulk. Avoid waterlogged ground but moisture-retentive soil is essential. Spacing: 50 cm apart for ordinary cultivars, 70 cm for vigorous mature plants."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Enrich the soil with leaf-mould and compost", "text": "Hellebores want deep, fertile, moisture-retentive soil with leaf-mould or garden compost worked in. At planting: fork in a 10 cm layer of leaf-mould or well-rotted manure. Annual top-dressing: apply 3–5 cm of leaf-mould or compost around (not on) the crown each autumn. Hellebores feed heavily once established."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant in autumn or spring", "text": "Best time: autumn (September–October) when soil is still warm but rains return, OR spring (March–April) after the worst frosts. Don't plant in dry summer — establishment is poor. Container-grown plants are widely sold by garden centres and specialist nurseries (Ashwood, Hetzler) — buy named varieties for specific flower colours."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Cut all old leaves down in late autumn", "text": "The single most important husbandry rule. In late October or November, before flowers emerge, cut every leaf down to the crown with secateurs. Two reasons: (1) the flowers emerge in winter and are showcased without competition from tatty older leaves; (2) hellebore leaf-spot fungus (Microsphaeropsis hellebori) carries on the old leaves — removing them breaks the disease cycle. Wear gloves (toxic sap) and bin the leaves (don't compost — fungal carry-over)."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Don't transplant once established", "text": "Hellebores have deep fibrous root systems that resent disturbance. Mature plants are hard to move — the move often sets the plant back 2 years. Plan the position carefully at planting; for division, choose young plants (3–5 years old) and divide in late summer with care."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for hellebore leaf-spot", "text": "The main UK hellebore disease. Symptoms: dark brown-black blotches on leaves and stems, often appearing late winter or spring; can disfigure flowers. Prevention: cut all old leaves down in November (the main control); improve airflow by thinning crowded plantings; clear all leaf debris from around plants. Treatment: pick off affected leaves at first sight; severely-affected plants can be cut to the ground in spring after flowering and will re-shoot clean."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Handle with gloves — all parts toxic", "text": "All parts of hellebores are toxic — they contain cardiac glycosides and alkaloids. Don't eat any part. Skin contact: the sap can irritate skin (cause rash in sensitive people); wear gloves when cutting old leaves, deadheading, or handling root divisions. Children and pets: warn against handling. Keep in mind when positioning hellebores near play areas or vegetable gardens."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I cut hellebore leaves down?", "answer": "Late October or November, before the flowers emerge in winter. Cut every old leaf down to the crown with secateurs. Two reasons this is critical: (1) the winter flowers are properly displayed against bare ground rather than tatty old foliage; (2) it breaks the cycle of hellebore leaf-spot fungus, which overwinters on old leaves. Wear gloves — hellebore sap can irritate skin. Bin the cut leaves, don't compost (fungal carry-over)."},
    {"question": "Why aren't my hellebores flowering?", "answer": "Three common causes. (1) Too young — newly-planted hellebores take 2–3 years to settle and flower properly. Be patient. (2) Too sunny — hellebores need part to full shade; full sun causes stress and poor flowering. Move to a shadier spot in autumn. (3) Too dry — drought stress reduces flowering. Mulch annually with leaf-mould, water deeply in dry summer spells. Bonus cause: deer or rabbit damage to flower buds in some rural gardens — net during bud development."},
    {"question": "Are hellebores poisonous?", "answer": "Yes — all parts are toxic. Contain cardiac glycosides and protoanemonin (a skin irritant). Eating any part causes nausea, vomiting, irregular heartbeat, and in large quantities can be serious. Skin contact: sap irritates skin in sensitive people — wear gloves when cutting old leaves, dividing, or deadheading. Pets and children: warn against contact. The toxicity is well-documented but actual poisonings are rare because the plant is so bitter — most accidental tasters spit it out immediately."},
    {"question": "Will hellebores grow in deep shade?", "answer": "Yes — they're one of the few flowering perennials that genuinely flourish in deep shade. Hellebores tolerate north-facing positions, dense overhead canopy from deciduous trees, even spots that get almost no direct sunlight. Best display: under deciduous trees where they get spring sunlight before the canopy closes. In full shade: hellebores still flower well but the colours can be slightly muted compared to part-shade plants."},
    {"question": "What's the best hellebore variety for the UK?", "answer": "For a first hellebore: any Helleborus × hybridus — modern hybrid Lenten roses are vigorous, easy, and come in a huge colour range from white through pink to plum to near-black. Buy from a specialist nursery (Ashwood, Hetzler, Phedar) for the best colour variety. For winter flowers: Christmas Rose (H. niger) for December–February white flowers, but slower and more demanding. For architectural foliage: stinking hellebore (H. foetidus) — UK native, dramatic deeply-cut leaves."},
    {"question": "Can I grow hellebores in pots?", "answer": "Yes — useful for showcase positioning and slug-control. A 35 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed with leaf-mould, in part shade. Pot advantage: move the pot to display position when in flower (porch, doorstep); return to shady corner after flowering. Disadvantage: pot hellebores get root-bound within 3 years and need repotting or dividing. Watering: more important than in-ground — pots dry faster. Don't let hellebores sit in saucers of water (rots roots) but never let pots dry to bone."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'hellebore';

-- From heuchera.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant heuchera in spring or autumn, in part shade in well-drained soil — they''re evergreen perennials grown primarily for the bold rounded foliage in extraordinary colours (lime, gold, orange, red, deep purple, near-black, and silver-marbled forms). Heucheras are hardy (RHS H5–H6) and produce wiry stems of small bell-shaped flowers (mostly pink, cream, or red) in early summer, though they''re foliage plants first. Vine weevil is the dominant UK pest — larvae eat the roots and can kill plants overnight; treat with nematodes in autumn. The bewildering range of foliage colours comes from intensive modern breeding (the "Coral Bells" wave of 2000s–2020s). Replant every 3 years — heuchera crowns rise out of the ground as old roots die back; bury the rising crown or split and replant to keep plants vigorous.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick by foliage colour", "text": "Lime / chartreuse: Lime Marmalade, Citronelle — bright glow in shady positions. Orange / amber: Marmalade, Amber Waves — autumn fire colour all year. Deep purple / near-black: Obsidian, Forever Purple, Palace Purple — dramatic dark accents. Silver-marbled: Silver Scrolls, Northern Exposure Silver — striking variegation. Red: Cherry Cola, Fire Alarm — warm winter colour. For a first heuchera: Palace Purple — the original purple-leaved variety, vigorous and reliable."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in part shade in well-drained soil", "text": "Heucheras are woodland-edge plants preferring part shade — morning sun and afternoon shade is ideal. Full sun: leaves scorch and colour fades; many varieties get crispy. Deep shade: foliage colours mute, plants stretch and flower poorly. Soil: well-drained but moisture-retentive; clay needs grit forking in. Don't waterlog — root rot is the second-biggest heuchera killer after vine weevil."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in spring or autumn", "text": "Best windows: spring (March–April) for establishment ahead of summer, OR early autumn (September) for autumn–winter foliage display. Avoid summer planting — heat stress combined with root disturbance often kills new heucheras. Container-grown plants widely sold by garden centres in the popular foliage colours."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Watch for and treat vine weevil", "text": "The single biggest UK heuchera problem. Symptoms: plant collapses despite watering; lifted plant has eaten root system, often just a stump remaining. Prevention: nematodes (Steinernema kraussei) watered into the soil in autumn (August–October) and again in spring (March–April) if needed. Pot-grown heucheras are particularly vulnerable; treat all new pot purchases on arrival. Adult weevils (small grey-brown beetles) chew notches in leaf edges from May–August — cosmetic damage, but a sign of larvae below."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Bury or split rising crowns every 3 years", "text": "Heuchera's unique problem. As old roots die and new roots form above them, the crown gradually rises out of the soil — sometimes 5–10 cm above ground level by year 3. Two fixes. (1) Bury the rising crown: scrape soil away, position the crown lower, refill with fresh soil and a leaf-mould mulch. (2) Split and replant: lift the whole plant, divide with a sharp knife into 2–4 sections (each with leaves and roots), replant each at proper depth."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut flower stems off (or leave for wildlife)", "text": "Heuchera flowers in early summer (May–July) — small bell-shaped flowers on wiry 60 cm stems. For foliage focus: cut flower stems off as they emerge — energy goes to leaf production. For wildlife: let flowers bloom — small bees and hoverflies visit them. For seed-saving: leave a few stems to set seed for self-sown volunteers (though hybrids don't come true from seed)."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Don't cut foliage back in autumn", "text": "Heuchera is evergreen — keep foliage on through winter. In late winter (February), tidy by removing any dead or damaged leaves with secateurs; the plant resumes growth in March. Don't hard-prune — heucheras don't regrow well from a complete cut-back. Mulch annually with leaf-mould around (not on) the crown each autumn."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Use modern hybrids — but accept some shorter lifespan", "text": "The huge range of modern foliage colours comes from 21st-century breeding (Terra Nova nurseries especially). Many modern hybrids are stunning but slightly less robust than older cultivars — they may live 4–6 years vs 10+ for traditional varieties. Palace Purple (1980s introduction) remains one of the longest-lived; bright modern oranges and limes may need replacing more often. Plan for replacement as part of the gamble for spectacular colour."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why is my heuchera dying?", "answer": "Almost certainly vine weevil. Larvae eat the roots underground, often invisibly, until the plant collapses despite watering. Lift the affected plant: if the root system is reduced to a chewed stump with small cream C-shaped larvae visible, it's vine weevil. Treatment: nematodes (Steinernema kraussei) watered into the soil; for the affected plant, often too late — replace and treat the surrounding bed. Prevention going forward: apply nematodes in autumn and spring as preventative."},
    {"question": "Why is my heuchera rising out of the ground?", "answer": "Normal heuchera behaviour. As old roots die back at the base and new roots form higher up the crown, the plant gradually rises out of the soil — sometimes 5–10 cm above ground level after 3 years. Two fixes. (1) Bury the rising crown: scrape away soil, position the crown lower, refill with fresh soil. (2) Split and replant: lift the plant, divide into 2–4 sections, replant each at proper depth. Either approach should be done every 3 years to keep heucheras vigorous."},
    {"question": "Will heucheras grow in deep shade?", "answer": "Yes — but with colour and flower compromises. Heucheras tolerate part shade and full shade better than full sun. In deep shade: foliage colours mute (oranges and reds fade towards green), plants stretch slightly, flowering reduces. Best display: part shade (4–6 hours dappled or morning sun) — colours intensify, plants stay compact, flowering is reliable. For deep shade: pick the most vivid varieties (the brightest oranges/limes) to compensate for the colour muting."},
    {"question": "What's the best heuchera for the UK?", "answer": "For a first plant: Palace Purple — the original purple-leaved heuchera (1980s), vigorous, reliable, long-lived, dramatic. For lime/chartreuse: Lime Marmalade — glows in shade. For autumn fire: Marmalade — orange-amber leaves. For deep purple-black: Obsidian or Forever Purple. For silver: Silver Scrolls. For mass planting: pick 3–4 contrasting foliage colours and group them — heucheras work brilliantly in trios and quartets."},
    {"question": "When do I plant heucheras?", "answer": "Spring (March–April) or early autumn (September) is best. Spring planting gives the plant time to establish before its first summer; autumn planting establishes through cool wet weather before frost. Avoid summer planting — heat plus root disturbance often kills new heucheras. Container-grown plants can technically be planted any time but spring/autumn give the best survival rates. Treat new plants with nematodes at planting as vine weevil prevention."},
    {"question": "Can I grow heucheras in pots?", "answer": "Yes — pots showcase the foliage colours beautifully. Use a 25–30 cm pot of peat-free compost with some grit. Drainage is critical — heucheras hate waterlogged compost. The pot disadvantage: heightened vine weevil risk (potted heucheras are top of weevil's hit list). Treatment: apply nematodes to every new pot purchase, then again each autumn and spring. Repot every 2–3 years, refreshing compost and burying the rising crown. Pot heucheras live 3–5 years before needing replacement."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'heuchera';

-- From holly.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant holly bare-root from November to March (or container-grown any time of year), in sun or shade, in any well-drained soil — it''s an iconic UK native evergreen that takes almost any conditions, from coastal exposure to woodland shade to urban pollution. Holly is very hardy (RHS H6), slow-growing (15–30 cm per year), and long-lived (centuries). Holly is dioecious — male and female on separate plants, and only female plants bear the red Christmas berries. You need a male holly within bee range (~50 m) to fertilise the females. The variety names are deliberately confusing: Golden King is a female; Silver Queen is a male. Buy on stated sex rather than name. Tolerates clipping for topiary and hedges. The classic Christmas wreath holly is Argentea Marginata or J.C. van Tol (self-fertile — rare exception, the safe choice for solo planting).',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Decide what you want — hedge, specimen tree, or berries", "text": "Holly hedge: plant 60 cm apart in autumn or spring, will form a dense impenetrable hedge in 5–7 years. Tolerates clipping and shade. Specimen tree: plant a single holly, allow 3–4 m space, grows to 5–15 m over decades. Berries for Christmas: needs a female variety AND a male pollinator within bee range. The exception: J.C. van Tol is self-fertile and produces berries solo — the safe choice if you only have space for one."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Understand the male/female trap", "text": "Holly is dioecious — male and female flowers on separate plants. Only female plants bear berries. The variety names are notoriously misleading from a sex perspective. Examples: Golden King is female (yes — the queen is the king). Silver Queen is male. Pyramidalis is female. Ferox Argentea is male. Always buy on stated sex, not assumed sex from the variety name. Garden centre labels should specify M or F."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant one male per 5–6 females (or pick self-fertile)", "text": "For berries, you need pollination. Within 50 m, a single male holly pollinates 5–6 females (bees move the pollen). If you have a neighbour with holly, theirs may already be doing the job. If buying just one holly and you want berries, choose J.C. van Tol or Pyramidalis Fructu Luteo — the very few self-fertile varieties that produce berries solo."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant in any soil, sun or shade", "text": "Holly's main appeal is its toughness. Sun, part shade, or full shade: all work, though sun gives the heaviest berry crop. Any soil except waterlogged: chalk, acid, sand, clay all tolerated. Coastal exposure and urban pollution: holly tolerates both better than most evergreens. Best planting time: late autumn or early spring for bare-root; any time for container."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Allow slow establishment", "text": "Holly is slow — 15–30 cm growth per year, even in good conditions. Don't expect a productive specimen for 5+ years. First-year planting can look dispiriting; the plant is putting energy into roots, not visible growth. Patience is the rule. Water through dry spells in the first 2 summers; established hollies tolerate drought well."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Clip in late summer for hedges or topiary", "text": "Holly tolerates clipping and shaping well — used in formal hedges, topiary, and standards. Clip in August (avoid earlier — disturbs nesting birds; avoid later — soft regrowth gets frosted). One annual clip is enough for hedges. Topiary needs two clips a year (early June and late August). Wear thick gloves — the spines are no joke."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut Christmas holly carefully", "text": "For the traditional Christmas berried branches: cut in early December (before birds have stripped the berries). Take whole branches from the inner parts of the plant — visible scars on the outside ruin the appearance for years on slow-growing holly. Strip lower leaves from the cut stems, soak in a bucket of water overnight, store cool until needed (will keep 2–3 weeks). Don't store dry — leaves go brown quickly."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Manage berries vs birds", "text": "Holly berries are a major UK winter bird food — blackbirds, fieldfares, redwings, thrushes will strip a tree in cold weather. For Christmas wreaths, cut before late December or birds will have taken them. To prefer wildlife over wreaths, plant a holly in a position you don't need for cuttings. Holly berries are mildly toxic to humans (cause vomiting if eaten in quantity) — birds metabolise the saponins fine, but warn children not to eat them."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why doesn't my holly have berries?", "answer": "Almost always one of two reasons. (1) It's male: only female hollies bear berries, and variety names mislead (Golden King = female, Silver Queen = male). Check the sex of your plant — wait for spring flowering, look at the flowers (females have a green central knob that becomes the berry; males have only stamens). (2) No pollinator within range: females need a male holly within 50 m for bees to transfer pollen. Your single female + no nearby male = no berries. Fix: plant a male variety, or replace with self-fertile J.C. van Tol."},
    {"question": "Can I plant just one holly and get berries?", "answer": "Yes, but only with self-fertile varieties — and they're rare. J.C. van Tol is the standard self-fertile UK choice: dark green spineless leaves, generous red berries solo, the safe one-plant option. Pyramidalis Fructu Luteo is self-fertile and produces yellow berries. All other named UK varieties need a male pollinator within ~50 m."},
    {"question": "When and how do I prune a holly?", "answer": "Late summer (August) — after nesting birds have finished, before soft regrowth that won't survive winter. For hedges: one annual clip in August keeps the shape. For topiary or standards: two clips a year (June + August). Renovation of overgrown hollies: holly tolerates hard pruning — cut back to old wood, it will re-shoot, though the process takes 2–3 years to look respectable again. Wear thick gauntlets — the spines puncture deeply."},
    {"question": "How fast does holly grow?", "answer": "Slowly — 15–30 cm per year on established plants, less in the first 2–3 years while roots establish. From a 1 m sapling: a 2 m holly takes 5 years, a 3 m holly takes 10 years, a 5 m specimen takes 20+ years. Faster growth comes from full sun, moisture-retentive soil, and shelter from cold winds. Hedge plants grow faster than free-standing specimens (the competition pushes them up). Patience is the rule."},
    {"question": "Are holly berries poisonous?", "answer": "Mildly toxic to humans, especially children. Holly berries contain saponins that cause stomach upset, vomiting, and diarrhoea if eaten in quantity. A child eating 2–3 berries may have mild upset; 10+ requires medical attention. Toxic to dogs and cats too, in similar quantities. Birds metabolise the saponins fine and depend on holly berries as winter food. Warn children not to eat the berries — they look temptingly Christmas-bright. The leaves are not poisonous beyond the obvious spine danger."},
    {"question": "What's the best holly variety for the UK?", "answer": "For berries from a single plant: J.C. van Tol — self-fertile, spineless leaves, generous red berries. For a striking specimen: Golden King (variegated yellow-edged leaves, female) paired with any male holly. For traditional Christmas holly: Argentea Marginata (silver-edged leaves, female) plus a male nearby. For hedging: the wild Ilex aquifolium species form, much cheaper than named varieties, very tough and dense."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'holly';

-- From hosta.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant hostas in spring or early autumn, in part or full shade in moist soil — they''re foliage plants first, prized for their large architectural leaves in shades of blue-green, lime, gold, and variegated combinations. Hostas are hardy (RHS H6–H7) and disappear underground each winter, returning each spring. Slugs and snails are the unavoidable problem — they shred hosta leaves and will defeat a hosta unless actively defended. Blue-leaved varieties (Halcyon, Krossa Regal, Sum and Substance) have a waxy bloom that slugs find harder to eat. Heavy mulch with sharp grit, wool pellets, copper rings, or nematode treatment are the four main UK slug controls. Lavender-purple tubular flowers appear on tall stems in July–August (often cut off to focus the plant on foliage). One of the few perennials happy in deep dry shade once established.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick by leaf colour, size, and slug-resistance", "text": "Blue-leaved varieties are the slug-resistant heroes — waxy bloom on the leaves makes them harder to eat. Halcyon (60 cm, classic mid-blue), Krossa Regal (1 m tall, blue-green vase shape), Sum and Substance (massive yellow-gold, 1.2 m wide, vigorous and slug-resistant). Gold/yellow-leaved: Sum and Substance (lime-gold), August Moon (bright gold) — vivid colour in shady corners. Variegated: Patriot (green with white margin), Francee (deep green with crisp white edge), Frances Williams (blue-green with cream margins). Most variegated hostas attract more slug attention than blue-leaved. Small / front of border: Blue Mouse Ears (15 cm tiny blue), Mighty Mouse. For a first hosta in slug country: Halcyon or Sum and Substance."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in part to full shade", "text": "Hostas are shade specialists. Most prefer dappled shade under deciduous trees, part shade (3–4 hours morning sun), or deep shade (under conifers, north-facing positions). A few cultivars tolerate more sun — the yellow-gold types (August Moon, Sum and Substance) colour better with 3–4 hours direct sun. Full sun on a hosta scorches the leaves brown at the edges by July — avoid."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in moisture-retentive soil", "text": "Hostas want deep, fertile, moisture-retentive soil enriched with leaf-mould or garden compost. They tolerate clay (better than most perennials), but dislike thin sandy soils that dry out. Plant in spring (March–May) or early autumn (September), so roots establish before extreme conditions. Spacing 60–100 cm depending on cultivar size — hostas need room to develop their architectural form."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Apply slug control before leaves emerge", "text": "The single most important hosta husbandry. Slugs and snails strip emerging hosta leaves in March–April, ruining the plant before it's fully open. Apply slug control BEFORE growth starts (late February–early March). Four UK-effective controls: (1) Wool pellets scattered around the base — slugs dislike crossing the rough fibres; effective and organic. (2) Sharp grit / crushed eggshells as a 5 cm mulch around the crown. (3) Nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) watered into the soil from March — biological control, very effective for 6 weeks. (4) Copper rings or tape around individual plants in pots."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Mulch generously each spring", "text": "A 5 cm mulch of garden compost or leaf-mould around the crown each spring conserves moisture, suppresses weeds, and gradually improves the soil. Keep the mulch 5 cm clear of the emerging crown (don't smother the new shoots). Combine the mulch with slug control (wool pellets, grit) for double effect."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water through dry spells", "text": "Hostas wilt dramatically and shed leaves in summer drought. A deep weekly soaking in dry weather keeps the foliage in good condition. Pot-grown hostas need daily watering in summer. Yellow leaves at the base mid-summer = drought stress; the plant returns next spring but loses display this season."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut flower stems off (or let them flower)", "text": "Hostas produce tall stems of tubular lavender, purple, or white flowers in July–August. Some gardeners cut the flower stems off as buds form — the plant focuses energy on foliage instead of flowers, leaves stay larger and more intense. Others let them flower — bees enjoy the trumpets, and the white-flowered species (Royal Standard, Honeybells) are evening-scented. Personal preference; both approaches work."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Cut foliage down after frost", "text": "Late October–November, after the first hard frost has blackened the leaves, cut all foliage to 5 cm above the ground. Clear away the dead leaves to remove slug egg-laying habitat — leaving rotting hosta foliage in place is the easiest way to build a thriving slug population over winter. Compost the leaves elsewhere in the garden."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I stop slugs eating my hostas?", "answer": "Combine multiple controls — no single method is 100% effective. Apply before growth emerges in late February: (1) Wool pellets scattered around the base — organic, effective, recommended. (2) Nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) watered in from March — biological control, kills slugs underground; reapply every 6 weeks. (3) Sharp grit / crushed eggshells as a 5 cm mulch. (4) Copper tape around the base of pots. (5) Choose slug-resistant cultivars — blue-leaved varieties with thick waxy leaves (Halcyon, Krossa Regal, Sum and Substance) are eaten less. Hand-pick slugs in the evening after rain — old-fashioned but effective. Avoid metaldehyde slug pellets — they're banned in the UK and harm wildlife."},
    {"question": "Will hostas grow in deep shade?", "answer": "Yes — they're one of the few perennials that genuinely flourish in deep shade. Hostas tolerate north-facing positions, dense overhead canopy from deciduous trees, even spots that get almost no direct sunlight at all. The variegated and gold varieties sometimes lose colour intensity in very deep shade — they brighten the foliage with 2–3 hours dappled sun. Pure green and blue-leaved varieties are the deepest-shade specialists. For dry deep shade (under conifers), water consistently and mulch annually."},
    {"question": "When do I plant or divide hostas?", "answer": "Spring (March–May) is the best window for both planting new and dividing existing clumps. Early autumn (September) also works in southern UK. Don't divide in summer (too stressful) or deep winter (frozen roots can't establish). Division technique: lift the whole clump, cut into 4–6 sections with a sharp spade, each section with eyes (growth points) and roots. Replant immediately, water well. Divide every 4–5 years to maintain vigour, or simply leave clumps to develop into magnificent specimens over decades."},
    {"question": "Why are my hosta leaves looking torn and ragged?", "answer": "Slug damage is the answer 95% of the time. Slugs (and snails) feed at night, particularly in damp weather, and shred the leaves with characteristic ragged-edge holes. Apply the slug control measures listed above. Other causes: hailstorm damage (early summer, distinctive small clean holes), wind shred at exposed sites (tatty edges along the leaf veins), or scorch from too much sun (brown crispy edges, not ragged holes). The slug pattern is unmistakable once you've seen it."},
    {"question": "What's the best hosta variety for the UK?", "answer": "Halcyon is the modern UK favourite — mid-blue leaves, 60 cm wide, slug-resistant, RHS Award of Garden Merit. Sum and Substance for impact — massive yellow-gold leaves (1.2 m wide), surprisingly slug-resistant despite the bright colour, takes more sun than most. Patriot (green with white margins) for classic variegation. Francee if you want pure crisp white-edged elegance. For small spaces: Blue Mouse Ears — tiny blue clusters, ideal in pots."},
    {"question": "Can I grow hostas in pots?", "answer": "Yes — and pots make slug control much easier. A 30–40 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed with garden compost. Position in part shade. Pot slug controls work better: copper tape around the rim, raised off the ground on pot feet, slugs can't easily access. Water daily in summer (pots dry out fast). Feed with general-purpose liquid fertiliser monthly. Repot every 2–3 years, dividing if needed. Hostas in pots stay smaller than ground-planted ones but display the architectural foliage beautifully."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'hosta';

-- From hydrangea.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant hydrangeas in spring or autumn in moist, fertile soil with part-shade or sun for half the day. Hydrangeas are hardy (RHS H5) and one of the most rewarding flowering shrubs for UK gardens. Hydrangea macrophylla (mophead and lacecap) is the classic — flower colour shifts with soil pH (acid = blue, alkaline = pink). H. paniculata (Limelight, Vanille Fraise) gives huge cone-shaped flower heads regardless of pH. H. arborescens (Annabelle) produces basketball-sized white flowers on new growth. Each type has different pruning needs — and most hydrangea failures are bad pruning, not bad sites. Water deeply through summer; mulch heavily; feed in March. Pick fresh for vases or dry the heads for winter arrangements.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Identify your hydrangea type", "text": "Mophead and lacecap (H. macrophylla); paniculata (H. paniculata); arborescens (H. arborescens); oakleaf (H. quercifolia); climbing (H. anomala subsp. petiolaris). Each needs different pruning, and confusion is the commonest reason for poor flowering."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in a partly-shaded spot", "text": "Morning sun, afternoon shade is ideal in most of the UK. Full sun is fine in cool maritime gardens. Avoid deep shade — flowering reduces sharply. Macrophylla types tolerate more sun than paniculata in the south."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in moist, fertile soil", "text": "Fork in well-rotted compost or leaf mould. Hydrangeas drink heavily — moisture-retentive soil is essential. Heavy clay improved with grit works; light sandy soil needs lots of organic matter to hold water."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Adjust soil pH for mophead colour (if you want)", "text": "H. macrophylla mophead flower colour shifts with soil pH. Acid soil (pH 4.5–5.5): blue flowers. Alkaline soil (pH 7+): pink. To turn pink mopheads blue, apply aluminium sulphate or specialist hydrangea blueing agent twice a year. To turn blue to pink, add lime. Some cultivars (Vibrania) are bred for set colours and don't shift. White mopheads stay white regardless."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Prune by type", "text": "Macrophylla (mophead/lacecap): light prune in March. Cut spent flowers back to the next fat pair of buds; remove weak or crossing stems at the base; that's all. Paniculata: cut back hard each March — to a permanent framework 30 cm above the ground. Flowers on this year's growth. Arborescens (Annabelle): cut all stems to 30 cm each March. Flowers on this year's growth. Oakleaf and climbing: light prune only after flowering; flowers on last year's wood."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water deeply through summer", "text": "Hydrangea leaves wilt dramatically in drought — the plant looks like it's dying. Water deeply twice a week through July and August. Mulch heavily with compost to hold moisture. Containers need daily watering in hot weather."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Feed in March", "text": "A balanced shrub feed in March. Ericaceous formula if you're maintaining blue mophead colour (the soil chemistry stays right longer). Don't over-feed — pushes leaves at the expense of flowers."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Dry flowers for arrangements", "text": "Pick mature flower heads in autumn when they've turned papery (usually September–October). Hang upside down in a dry place for 2 weeks. Dried hydrangea heads keep their shape and colour for 1–2 years."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I make my hydrangea flowers blue (or pink)?", "answer": "Only works on H. macrophylla mophead and lacecap types. For blue: soil pH must be acid (4.5–5.5). Add aluminium sulphate or specialist hydrangea blueing agent twice a year (March and September). Ericaceous compost in pots. For pink: soil pH alkaline (7+). Add garden lime annually. White-flowered cultivars (Annabelle, Limelight, Vanille Fraise) don't change colour regardless of pH. Paniculata and arborescens types are not pH-sensitive."},
    {"question": "When and how should I prune my hydrangea?", "answer": "Crucial — depends entirely on type. Macrophylla (mophead/lacecap): light prune in March, removing only spent flowers and weak stems. Flowers on last year's wood, so heavy pruning loses next year's flowers. Paniculata: hard prune each March to a permanent framework 30 cm above ground. Flowers on this year's wood, so heavy pruning is what makes it flower. Arborescens (Annabelle): all stems to 30 cm each March. Wrong pruning for type is the single biggest cause of hydrangea problems."},
    {"question": "Why is my hydrangea not flowering?", "answer": "Most likely: wrong pruning for the type, or late frost damage to flower buds in spring. Macrophylla pruned hard like a paniculata loses all flower buds. Other causes: too much shade, too little water, over-feeding with nitrogen, or a young plant not yet established. Patience for new plants — they often skip a year while settling in."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between mophead, lacecap, and paniculata hydrangeas?", "answer": "Mophead (macrophylla): rounded heads of densely-packed sterile flowers — the classic. Pink, blue, white. Flower on last year's wood. Lacecap (macrophylla): flat heads of small fertile flowers ringed by larger sterile ones. Same plant, different flower form. Paniculata: cone-shaped flower heads — Limelight (lime-green), Vanille Fraise (white turning pink). Flower on this year's wood; pruned hard. Arborescens (Annabelle): basketball-sized round white heads. Flower on this year's wood; pruned hard."},
    {"question": "Can I grow hydrangeas in containers?", "answer": "Yes — H. macrophylla compact cultivars (Cityline series, You & Me, Endless Summer) and dwarf paniculatas (Bobo, Little Lime) suit large containers. Use 40-litre pots minimum, peat-free compost (ericaceous if maintaining blue mopheads), water daily in summer. Container hydrangeas need feeding more than ground plants — a slow-release granular feed in March and again in June."},
    {"question": "How do I dry hydrangea flower heads?", "answer": "Pick mature heads in autumn when they've turned slightly papery to the touch — usually September or October. The petals should feel papery, not soft and fresh. Cut stems 30–40 cm long; remove leaves; hang upside down in a cool dark airy place for 2 weeks. Dried hydrangeas keep their shape for 1–2 years; colours fade but the overall effect is beautiful in winter arrangements."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'hydrangea';

-- From hyssop.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant hyssop from a young plant in spring, in full sun in well-drained gritty soil — it''s a hardy Mediterranean semi-shrubby perennial that gives narrow grey-green leaves and dense spires of small two-lipped flowers in deep blue (or pink/white) from July to September. Hyssop is hardy (RHS H5) and stays semi-evergreen through normal UK winters; the dense low (40–60 cm) habit makes it useful as a low formal hedge in traditional knot gardens. Outstanding for bees — one of the top UK garden plants for honey-bee and bumblebee diversity, working the flowers constantly through high summer. The strong minty-bitter flavour is too pungent for most modern UK kitchens but historically used in stuffings, monastic liqueurs (Chartreuse), and herbal medicine. Cut hard back in spring (April) to keep compact. Replace every 4–5 years.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose flower colour and use", "text": "Hyssop comes in three flower colours, all the same species and same cultivation needs. Blue hyssop (Hyssopus officinalis species form): deep violet-blue spikes — the classic, most striking, most popular. Pink hyssop (Roseus): soft pink — a softer cottage-garden look. White hyssop (Albus): white — works well in white-themed plantings or as contrast. Anise hyssop is a different plant entirely (Agastache foeniculum) — see separate anise-hyssop slug."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Buy a young plant in spring", "text": "Buy a 1 L plant from a herb nursery (Jekka's, Pepperpot, RHS Wisley) in April–May. From seed is possible — sow indoors February–March, germinate at 18°C, prick out and plant out after last frost. Seed-grown plants reach productive size in year 2. One plant is enough for kitchen use; for a low hedge, space 30–40 cm apart in a row."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in full sun and well-drained soil", "text": "Mediterranean origins: hyssop wants 6+ hours direct sun and sharp drainage. Rich or wet soil = winter crown rot. On heavy soils: plant on a raised bed, fork in plenty of horticultural grit, or grow in a pot. Sandy, chalky, or stony soils suit it perfectly. Tolerates a wide pH range. Spacing 40 cm (or 30 cm for hedging)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't feed", "text": "Feeding makes hyssop flop. Rich soil produces lush growth that collapses sideways, gets mildewy, and reduces flowering. No fertiliser at planting; no fertiliser during the season. A gravel mulch around the base keeps weeds down without adding nutrients. Stress concentrates the essential oils and the bee-attractiveness."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick leaves sparingly for the kitchen", "text": "Hyssop's flavour is strong, minty, slightly bitter, with eucalyptus edges — too pungent for most modern UK cooking. Traditional uses: a leaf or two in stuffings for game and rich meats, fish, lamb. Modern uses: lavender-style baking (small quantities only). Monastic uses: Chartreuse, Bénédictine, and other herbal liqueurs. Herbal tea: a few leaves for a strong infusion. Don't use lots — hyssop dominates any dish."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut hard back in spring", "text": "April after the last frost: cut the whole plant back by half to two-thirds, into soft greenish wood (avoid cutting into the old grey woody base — it rarely re-shoots). This keeps the bush compact, encourages dense new growth, and triggers strong flowering for July–September. Don't prune in autumn — soft regrowth won't survive winter. A light trim after flowering tidies the plant."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Use as a low formal hedge or edge", "text": "Hyssop's dense low habit (40–60 cm) makes it excellent for low formal hedging in knot gardens, parterres, and herb beds. The classic Tudor knot-garden combination is hyssop with box, lavender, and santolina. For hedging: plant 30 cm apart in a row, clip lightly twice a year (June and after flowering) to maintain shape. The deep blue flowers in midsummer add a stunning belt of colour through the formal lines."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Replace every 4–5 years", "text": "Hyssop gradually gets woody and bare-based after about 4–5 years. Take 8 cm semi-ripe cuttings in late summer (August) to start replacement plants: strip lower leaves, push into gritty compost, root in 4–6 weeks. Plant out next spring. Self-seeding: hyssop sometimes self-seeds in gritty conditions — accept the volunteers as replacements, or pull them if you want to control the colony."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Is hyssop the same as anise hyssop?", "answer": "No — completely different plants, different families. Hyssop (Hyssopus officinalis): the herb on this page — semi-shrubby Mediterranean perennial, narrow leaves, blue spike flowers, strong minty-bitter flavour. Anise hyssop (Agastache foeniculum): a North American perennial in the mint family, broader leaves, lavender flower spires, sweet aniseed-mint flavour, used for tea. Both are good bee plants but genuinely different. Buy by Latin name to be sure. See separate anise-hyssop slug."},
    {"question": "Will hyssop survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — hardy in most UK gardens (RHS H5), staying semi-evergreen through normal winters. Main failure mode: cold WET winters on heavy soils rot the crown. Prevention: plant on well-drained or gritty soil; on clay, plant on a raised mound or grow in a pot. Established plants survive temperatures down to about -15°C in dry conditions. The above-ground growth may lose some leaves in a hard winter but the plant returns from the woody base in spring."},
    {"question": "What does hyssop taste like?", "answer": "Strong, minty, slightly bitter, with eucalyptus and camphor edges. It's a polarising flavour — wonderful in small quantities in stuffings, game dishes, and herbal liqueurs (Chartreuse, Bénédictine); overpowering in most modern cooking. Most UK home cooks find hyssop too strong for everyday use. Try it once in a stuffing for roast lamb or game bird before deciding whether to grow it for the kitchen. As a bee plant and ornamental it earns its place even if you never use it culinarily."},
    {"question": "When does hyssop flower?", "answer": "July to September, with peak flowering in late July and August. The deep blue (or pink/white) spike flowers attract enormous numbers of bees, hoverflies, and butterflies — hyssop is one of the top UK garden plants for pollinator diversity in high summer. Each spike produces flowers for 4–6 weeks; the whole plant flowers continuously for 2 months. For maximum flowering: hard-prune in April, don't feed, and don't deadhead during the flowering period (it slows production)."},
    {"question": "Can I grow hyssop in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and on heavy soils, it's the better option. A 25 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed 30% with horticultural grit, in full sun. The pot advantage: perfect drainage protects against winter crown rot. Don't feed. Water only when the surface is bone dry. Refresh top compost annually. Pot hyssop lives 4–5 years and works well as a patio bee plant. A 30 cm pot with three young plants of mixed colours makes a striking ornamental container."},
    {"question": "Is hyssop good for bees?", "answer": "Outstanding — one of the top 10 UK garden plants for pollinator diversity. The small two-lipped flowers are accessible to bumblebees, honeybees, solitary bees, hoverflies, and butterflies. The dense spike form means each plant has hundreds of small flowers open at once during July–August. Beekeepers sometimes plant hyssop near hives for the strong nectar flow. A hyssop bed in flower is a constant buzz of bee activity. The plant's traditional medicinal reputation pales next to its modern wildlife garden value."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'hyssop';

-- From jerusalem-artichoke.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant Jerusalem artichoke tubers from February to March, 12 cm deep and 30 cm apart, in any sunny corner of the garden. Jerusalem artichokes are very hardy (RHS H7), shrug off any UK winter, and crop reliably with minimal care — one of the easiest perennial vegetables. The plants grow tall (2–3 m), produce small yellow sunflower-like blooms in late summer, and form a wall of foliage that''s useful as a temporary screen. Lift tubers from October through to March. Contain the bed: Jerusalem artichokes spread aggressively from any tuber left in the ground and quickly become invasive — sink slates or a barrier 30 cm deep around the bed. The fartichoke digestive effect from inulin is real; introduce them gradually if new to your diet.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose tubers from a nursery (or a supermarket)", "text": "Buy seed tubers from a seed merchant in late winter, or use supermarket Jerusalem artichokes for a free start (they're not treated against sprouting). Fuseau is a smoother knobble-free cultivar; the wild type is more knobbly but more vigorous."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plan for the long term", "text": "Jerusalem artichokes are perennial and quickly spread by any tuber left in the ground. Contain the bed with a sunk barrier (paving slabs 30 cm deep, or pond-liner stapled to wooden posts) at planting. Site them where the 2–3 m tall foliage won't shade other crops — back of the plot or as a temporary windbreak."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant tubers", "text": "February to March. Dig a trench 12 cm deep; set tubers 30 cm apart with shoots facing up; cover with soil; firm in. No special soil prep needed — they thrive in poor ground."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Forget about them", "text": "Through summer, Jerusalem artichokes need almost no care. The plants grow 2–3 m tall and shade out weeds underneath. Light watering in extreme drought helps the tubers swell but isn't usually necessary."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Stake or earth up in exposed sites", "text": "Tall stems can blow over in autumn storms. In exposed gardens, stake firmly, or earth up around the base in mid-summer for stability. Falling stems break easily; broken stems reduce tuber size."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut foliage when blackened", "text": "The first hard frost blackens the foliage. Cut stems down to 10 cm above the ground — the tubers are now ready to lift as needed through winter."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Lift through autumn–winter", "text": "From October onwards, lift tubers with a fork as you need them. They store far better in the ground than out. Lift carefully — any tuber left behind starts a new plant next year. By March, dig over the bed thoroughly to remove every fragment if you're rotating crops."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Save tubers for next year's planting", "text": "Reserve 10–12 of the best tubers for replanting in February. Store in damp sand in a cool shed if you must, but in-ground storage works for most UK gardens."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant Jerusalem artichoke tubers?", "answer": "February to March, when soil is workable. Earlier is fine in mild gardens; later works but reduces the eventual crop size. They sprout once soil temperatures rise; emergence is usually from April. A single planting gives crops indefinitely if you save a few tubers each year for replanting."},
    {"question": "Will Jerusalem artichokes take over my garden?", "answer": "Yes, if uncontained. Any tuber left in the ground starts a new plant next year, and they spread aggressively. Contain the bed with a sunk barrier (paving slabs 30 cm deep, pond-liner stapled to posts, or a galvanised steel edging strip). If you've inherited a sprawling bed, dig the entire area over in March, sieving the soil to remove every tuber fragment."},
    {"question": "Do Jerusalem artichokes really cause flatulence?", "answer": "Yes — the tubers contain high levels of inulin, a fructan that humans don't digest but gut bacteria ferment, producing gas. Effects vary by individual. Introduce them gradually if new to your diet; cooking doesn't reduce the effect; some people develop tolerance over time. The fartichoke nickname is real-world experience, not folklore."},
    {"question": "When can I harvest Jerusalem artichokes?", "answer": "From October (after the first frost blackens the foliage) through to March. Lift only what you need each time — they store far better in the ground than out. The flavour is best after the first hard frost."},
    {"question": "How big do Jerusalem artichoke plants get?", "answer": "Very tall — 2–3 m from a single tuber, with a 30–50 cm spread. The yellow sunflower-like flowers appear in late summer (the plant is in the Helianthus genus, related to sunflowers). They make a good temporary windbreak or a screen for less-attractive parts of the plot."},
    {"question": "How long does a Jerusalem artichoke bed last?", "answer": "Indefinitely if you save tubers each year. Productivity stays high for 5–10 years; after that, tubers can get smaller and the bed benefits from being lifted, the soil refreshed with compost, and replanted with the best saved tubers. Move them to a fresh bed every 7–10 years to break any soil pest or disease build-up."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'jerusalem-artichoke';

-- From kale.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow kale seed from mid-April to early June in a seedbed or modules; transplant to a sunny spot 45 cm apart from June onwards. Kale is very hardy (RHS H5–H6) and crops right through a UK winter — the leaves actually sweeten after frost. Pick outer leaves continuously from October to April; the plant keeps producing new growth from the centre. Cavolo Nero (black kale) is the classic Italian for Tuscan beans and kale crisps; Red Russian is more tender; Curly Kale shrugs off the worst weather. Pigeons are the main winter problem — net the plants between October and March or the birds will strip them. Slugs and cabbage white in summer; pigeons in winter; otherwise easy.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow", "text": "Mid-April to early June in modules or a seedbed. Cover lightly; water in. Germination in 7–10 days. Don't sow too early — kale that bolts before winter is a wasted crop."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Transplant", "text": "From late June to early August, when seedlings have 4–5 true leaves and are 10–15 cm tall. Plant 45 cm apart for full-size plants, 30 cm for smaller types. Water in well."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Net against cabbage white", "text": "From transplanting until mid-October. Cabbage whites lay eggs on any brassica; caterpillars strip a plant overnight. Use 5 mm enviromesh or fine butterfly netting on hoops."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Stake tall varieties", "text": "Cavolo Nero and Walking Stick kale can reach 1 m and topple in autumn winds. Bury the stem deep at transplant; tie to a cane if the site is exposed."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick outer leaves continuously", "text": "From October onwards, pick the outer leaves regularly. Take 2–3 per plant per week. The plant keeps producing new growth from the centre. The lower stem becomes bare over time; this is normal."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Re-net against pigeons in winter", "text": "From October onwards, pigeons strip unprotected kale to skeletons. Net firmly with enviromesh or proper pigeon netting on hoops 30 cm above the plants. Re-tension after windy nights."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Take spring shoots", "text": "In March to April, kale plants bolt — they produce tender, broccoli-like flower shoots before the actual flowers open. These are the best part of the kale year; cut them like sprouting broccoli when the buds are still tight."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow kale in the UK?", "answer": "Mid-April to early June. Earlier than April risks bolting before the plant has matured; later than June and the plants won't be big enough by autumn to crop through winter. Successional sowing isn't usually needed — a single sowing in May gives plants that produce from October until they bolt the following spring."},
    {"question": "Why are pigeons stripping my kale?", "answer": "Winter food shortage. From October onwards, woodpigeons hit any unprotected brassicas and can reduce a plant to a stump overnight. Net firmly with enviromesh or pigeon netting supported on hoops 30 cm above the plants — they peck through anything touching the foliage. Re-tension after windy nights."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between cavolo nero, curly kale, and Red Russian?", "answer": "Cavolo Nero (also called Tuscan kale or black kale) has long dark blue-green strap leaves, slightly bitter, holds up to long cooking — the classic for Italian beans-and-kale dishes. Curly Kale has tightly ruffled leaves, sweeter after frost, the hardiest of the three. Red Russian is tender with serrated red-tinged leaves, the best raw in salads but less winter-hardy than the others. Grow all three for variety."},
    {"question": "When do I harvest kale?", "answer": "From October when plants have substantial leaves, until the plant bolts in March or April. Pick outer leaves continuously rather than cutting the whole plant — the centre keeps producing through winter. After cold frosts the flavour sweetens noticeably; pre-frost leaves are coarser and slightly bitter."},
    {"question": "Does kale really taste better after frost?", "answer": "Yes — frost triggers the plant to convert starches into sugars as an antifreeze response, sweetening the leaves. The difference is real and noticeable in November-onwards leaves versus October leaves. It's the reason December kale tastes good and August kale tastes bitter."},
    {"question": "Can I grow kale in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Dwarf varieties (Dwarf Green Curled, Petit Posy crosses) do well in 40-litre containers. Full-size cavolo nero in containers is possible but underwhelms — kale wants deep roots. Containers on hard ground need rope-and-stake stability in autumn winds. Net against pigeons the same as ground plants."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'kale';

-- From kohlrabi.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow kohlrabi direct from April to August, in sun in well-drained moisture-retentive soil — it''s a fast-growing brassica (8–10 weeks from seed to harvest) grown for the swollen above-ground "globe" that forms at the stem base. Kohlrabi is hardy (RHS H4) and an autumn-sown crop overwinters in mild gardens for spring harvest. Azur Star (purple) and Korist (green) are popular UK varieties. Pick when the globe is tennis-ball sized (7–10 cm diameter) — larger globes get woody. The flavour is mild, sweet, faintly cabbagey — like a cross between turnip and cabbage stalk, eaten raw in salads (peeled and grated, or thinly sliced) or cooked in stir-fries and stews. A less-grown UK crop that deserves wider use — fast, easy, productive in small spaces, and the purple varieties are ornamental.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick variety by colour", "text": "Azur Star: purple skin, white flesh, looks dramatic in the kitchen garden. Mild sweet flavour. The popular UK choice. Korist: green-skinned, slightly larger, very reliable — the green standard. Olivia: green-skinned modern variety, good bolt resistance. Superschmelz: massive heritage variety (can reach 8 kg!) — but stays tender even at large size. For the curious gardener. For a first crop: Azur Star purple — visual interest plus reliable mild flavour."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, April to August", "text": "Spring sowings (April–June): crop in summer. Late-summer sowings (July–August): crop in autumn–winter. Direct-sow in shallow drills 1 cm deep, 30 cm between rows. Germination in 7–14 days. Kohlrabi doesn't transplant well as mature seedlings (taproot disturbance triggers premature bolting); sow direct or use modules and plant out very young."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to 20–25 cm apart", "text": "Once seedlings have 3–4 leaves, thin to one plant every 20–25 cm. Don't crowd — overcrowded kohlrabi produces small globes. Use the thinnings in salads (very young leaves and small stems)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water consistently for tender globes", "text": "The single most important husbandry rule. Kohlrabi grown in dry conditions develops tough, woody, hot-tasting globes. Water deeply once or twice a week in dry weather. Mulch with garden compost to conserve moisture. Consistent moisture is more important than total water volume — fluctuating wet/dry conditions cause splitting and woody texture."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Harvest when tennis-ball sized", "text": "Pick when the globe is 7–10 cm diameter — tennis-ball size or slightly smaller. At this size: tender, sweet, mild. Bigger than 10–12 cm: globes get woody, fibrous, and develop the hot bitter flavour of overgrown turnips. Cut with a sharp knife just below the globe — the rest of the plant can stay in the ground briefly but won't regrow (the swollen stem is the whole crop)."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Use the leaves too", "text": "Kohlrabi leaves are edible — like a softer cabbage. Young leaves (from the centre of growing plants): salad or stir-fry. Older leaves (when harvesting the globe): chop and cook like spring greens, kale, or any other brassica leaf. Don't waste them — they're often as good a crop as the globe."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for cabbage white and slugs", "text": "Cabbage white butterfly: caterpillars on leaves April–September. Cover with insect mesh from sowing through the cropping window. Slugs: love kohlrabi seedlings. Apply wool pellets and nematodes; protect young plants with crushed eggshells or copper rings. Pigeons: occasionally eat seedlings; mesh covers exclude them."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Sow successionally for continuous supply", "text": "A single kohlrabi sowing matures over 2–3 weeks then needs harvesting before the globes get woody. Sow a fresh row every 3 weeks from April to August for continuous supply. For winter use: an August sowing gives autumn cropping (October–November) and the plants survive mild winters; in cold gardens, harvest before December and store in a cool shed (lasts 2–3 months wrapped in newspaper)."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What does kohlrabi taste like?", "answer": "Mild, sweet, faintly cabbagey — like a cross between turnip and cabbage stalk but milder and sweeter than either. Young tender globes: crunchy, juicy, mild — eat raw, peeled and grated into salads, thinly sliced for slaw, sticks for dips. Cooked: holds shape in stir-fries, roasts, soups, and stews. Overgrown globes (12+ cm): woody and bitter — fit only for soup-stock or compost. Pick at the right size for the best flavour."},
    {"question": "When can I harvest kohlrabi?", "answer": "8–10 weeks after sowing, when the globe is 7–10 cm diameter (tennis-ball size). Don't wait longer — kohlrabi globes go woody fast once they pass peak size. Check size regularly from week 6 onwards. Cut with a sharp knife just below the globe. Use within a week for best flavour and texture, or store in a cool shed for 2–3 months (autumn-harvested globes)."},
    {"question": "Why is my kohlrabi tough or woody?", "answer": "Three causes. (1) Picked too late — globes over 10–12 cm diameter go woody. Pick at tennis-ball size. (2) Drought stress during growth — kohlrabi needs consistent moisture. Water deeply weekly, mulch to conserve moisture. (3) Old variety — heritage kohlrabi varieties can get woody at smaller sizes than modern bred-for-tenderness types like Olivia and Korist."},
    {"question": "Can kohlrabi survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes in mild gardens, with protection in cold ones. August-sown kohlrabi crops in October–November. Hardy globes survive light frosts in the ground; in cold or wet areas, harvest before December and store in a cool shed wrapped in newspaper (2–3 months at 2–5°C). Superschmelz and overwintered varieties are bred for cold-hardiness and may stand in the ground all winter in southern England."},
    {"question": "What's the best kohlrabi variety for the UK?", "answer": "Azur Star (purple) — most popular, reliable, attractive, mild sweet flavour. Korist (green) — the workhorse for consistent cropping. Olivia — modern green variety with excellent bolt resistance for summer sowings. Superschmelz — heritage variety that grows enormous (up to 8 kg) but stays tender — interesting if you have space. Most UK gardens benefit from a purple-green mix for visual interest."},
    {"question": "Why isn't kohlrabi more popular in UK gardens?", "answer": "Mostly habit and unfamiliarity. Kohlrabi is widely grown in central and eastern Europe (Germany, Czech Republic, Hungary) where it's a kitchen staple, but never caught on as a UK supermarket vegetable. It's actually one of the easier UK kitchen-garden crops: fast (8–10 weeks), productive in small space, attractive (especially purple varieties), versatile (raw or cooked), and largely free of UK-specific pests and diseases. Modern food trends are bringing it back — it deserves a place in any UK veg plot."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'kohlrabi';

-- From lambs-lettuce.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow lamb''s lettuce direct from August to October, in sun or part shade in well-drained soil — it''s a very hardy winter salad green (RHS H7) that fills the gap when most other salads are dormant, cropping through autumn, winter, and into spring. Lamb''s lettuce forms small flat rosettes of soft mild oval leaves with a faintly nutty flavour. Outdoors it crops through frost and snow — one of the few salad greens that genuinely produces useable leaves in January and February. Sown in spring it bolts almost immediately in warm weather; this is fundamentally an autumn-winter-spring crop. Cut whole rosettes when leaves are 5–7 cm long — pick individual leaves and the plant disappoints. Self-seeds modestly. Distinct from lettuce (different family entirely).',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Understand the season — autumn–winter–spring only", "text": "Lamb's lettuce is fundamentally a cold-season crop. Sowings between April and July bolt to flower within weeks of germination. The useful sowing window is August to October for autumn–winter–spring cropping. March–April sowings of overwintered plants give a final spring flush before bolting in May. Don't try to grow lamb's lettuce in summer."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, August to October", "text": "Direct-sow in shallow drills 1 cm deep, 15–20 cm between rows. Germination is slow (10–21 days) and needs cool soil — autumn-sown lamb's lettuce often germinates better than spring sowings. Thinly sown rows crop better than crowded ones. Thin to 10 cm spacing once seedlings have 2–3 leaves."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pick a UK-suitable variety", "text": "Vit: the UK workhorse — vigorous, large rosettes, very hardy. Verte de Cambrai: traditional French variety, smaller darker rosettes, classic flavour. Coquille de Louviers: heritage variety, tightly-spiralled rosettes, good cold tolerance. For a first crop: Vit — reliable and easy."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Site in part shade for the warmer end of the season", "text": "August–September sowings benefit from light shade — full sun in warm weeks accelerates bolting. October sowings and later: full sun is fine. For winter cropping outdoors: any sunny or part-shaded position; lamb's lettuce shrugs off frost and snow on the leaves."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Crop in autumn, then continuously through winter", "text": "From October onwards, cut whole rosettes (not individual leaves) at 5–7 cm when they're well-formed. Snip at the base with scissors or a sharp knife. Don't bother picking individual leaves — they're small, and removing them leaves a damaged rosette that doesn't regrow well. Whole-rosette harvest is the right technique."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Continue cropping through frost and snow", "text": "Lamb's lettuce keeps producing through normal UK winters — slow growth in December–February, but the rosettes don't die back. Frost-rimed leaves taste mildly sweeter after a cold night. In hard frost: protect with a cloche or fleece, but most leaves are usable even without protection. Snow cover acts as natural insulation; brush snow aside to harvest."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Spring flush before bolt", "text": "Overwintered plants put on a final flush of growth in March–April before bolting to flower in May. The spring flush is the easiest cropping of the year — fast growth, no bolting yet, the largest rosettes. By late April or early May, plants start bolting; flowering stems mean leaves get tough. Pull bolted plants, replace with summer crops in the same row."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Let some plants flower for self-seeding", "text": "Bolted lamb's lettuce produces tiny pale-blue flowers in clusters. Let one or two bolted plants flower and set seed — the small grey-brown seeds drop and produce volunteer plants the following autumn. Or save seed in summer by cutting flower stems before shatter and threshing into a paper bag. Free re-sowing for next winter without buying packets."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow and harvest lamb's lettuce?", "answer": "Sow August to October for autumn–winter–spring cropping. Don't sow March–July — plants bolt almost immediately in warm weather. Harvest from October through to April, peaking in October–November and again in March–April with slow steady cropping through December–February. Cut whole rosettes at 5–7 cm rather than picking individual leaves."},
    {"question": "Will lamb's lettuce survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — it's one of the hardiest winter salads (RHS H7). Survives normal UK winters in the open, including frost and snow. Frost-rimed leaves taste sweeter. In hard winters (-10°C and below), cover with a cloche or fleece for protection — but most years no protection is needed. Snow cover acts as natural insulation. Lamb's lettuce is one of the few culinary crops that genuinely produces leaves in January and February."},
    {"question": "What does lamb's lettuce taste like?", "answer": "Mild, faintly nutty, with a soft buttery texture. Much less sharp than rocket or watercress; far milder than mustard greens or chicory. The flavour is delicate — pairs well with sharp citrus dressings, sweet beetroot, walnuts, blue cheese, smoked salmon. Classic French use: mâche salad with hot bacon dressing and warm croutons. UK gardeners: think of lamb's lettuce as a less aggressive substitute for rocket through winter when rocket is bolting or absent."},
    {"question": "Can I grow lamb's lettuce in pots?", "answer": "Yes — perfect for pots and windowboxes. A 25 cm pot of peat-free compost holds 10–15 rosettes. Sow in September, water consistently. Pot lamb's lettuce gives the same winter cropping as in-ground plants with the convenience of moving the pot to a sheltered position in hard weather. Three pots in rotation gives continuous winter salad. For a cloche-protected pot: place a glass cloche or inverted plastic bottle over the pot in hard frost."},
    {"question": "Is lamb's lettuce the same as corn salad?", "answer": "Yes — different names, same plant. Lamb's lettuce (UK common name), corn salad (older UK name, traditional), mâche (French name, often used in UK food writing). All three names refer to Valerianella locusta. The corn in corn salad refers to grain fields where the plant naturally self-seeds as a winter annual weed — hence corn in the British sense of cereal grains, not American sweetcorn."},
    {"question": "Why do my lamb's lettuce rosettes stay small?", "answer": "Three usual causes. (1) Wrong season: summer-sown plants bolt before sizing up. Sow August–October only. (2) Crowded planting: thin seedlings to 10 cm; overcrowded plants stay small. (3) Hard winter conditions: rosettes grow slowly in December–February — that's normal. Spring flush (March–April) brings the largest rosettes of the year. Patience is the rule; lamb's lettuce isn't a fast-growing crop."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'lambs-lettuce';

-- From lavender.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant lavender in spring or autumn as young plants in 9 cm pots; seed is possible but slow and unreliable. Lavandula angustifolia (English lavender — Hidcote, Munstead) is fully hardy in most of the UK (RHS H5) and the most reliable choice for gardens. L. × intermedia (lavandins — Grosso, Phenomenal) are H4 and bigger, with higher oil yield. L. stoechas (French / Spanish lavender, with the rabbit-ear bracts) is H3 and risky outside sheltered southern coastal gardens. Choose full sun and sharp drainage — lavender hates wet feet far more than it hates cold. Prune lightly after flowering, never into old bare wood. A well-sited English lavender lasts 8–12 years. Bees and butterflies adore it.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose by species", "text": "Lavandula angustifolia (English lavender) — H5, the UK standard: Hidcote (compact, deep purple), Munstead (vigorous, light purple). L. × intermedia (lavandins) — H4, bigger and more fragrant: Grosso, Phenomenal, Edelweiss. L. stoechas (French/Spanish) — H3, distinctive with rabbit-ear bracts but only reliable in sheltered south. Pick by your local climate."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose a sunny well-drained spot", "text": "Full sun, sharp drainage. South-facing aspect, raised beds, gravel gardens, alongside paving. Mediterranean conditions in miniature. Lavender on heavy clay in a wet UK winter rots; on poor sandy soil in full sun it thrives."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in spring or autumn", "text": "March–May or September–early October. Container-grown plants in 9 cm pots establish best. Dig the hole twice the rootball width; add grit to heavy soil; don't bury the stem below its original level."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water in, then leave alone", "text": "Water deeply once at planting. Established lavender is drought-tolerant and resents watering — overwatering is the second-most-common killer after winter wet. In containers, water only when the surface is dry."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Prune lightly after flowering", "text": "Late July to early August. Shorten flower stems and leaves by 5–7 cm. Never cut into old bare wood — lavender rarely regrows from it, and you'll kill or permanently disfigure the plant. Annual pruning keeps the bush dense and prevents legginess."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Take cuttings in summer", "text": "Softwood cuttings from non-flowering shoots in June–July root readily in gritty compost. Insert 10 cm cuttings around the edge of a pot, water in, cover loosely with a plastic bag. Roots in 6 weeks. Insurance against losing a parent plant."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Replace every 8–12 years", "text": "Even well-pruned lavender eventually gets woody, threadbare, and bare at the base. Have replacement cuttings ready before the parent fails. English lavender lasts longest; lavandins typically 6–8 years; French/Spanish 4–5 years in the UK."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Which lavender is best for the UK?", "answer": "Lavandula angustifolia Hidcote or Munstead — English lavender, fully hardy (H5), reliable in almost any UK garden with good drainage. Both flower beautifully, attract pollinators, and last 8–12 years. For bigger plants and higher oil yield, L. × intermedia (Grosso, Phenomenal) is H4 and works in most of the UK except the coldest gardens. Avoid French / Spanish lavender (L. stoechas) except in sheltered south-coast gardens — it's H3 and risky."},
    {"question": "Why is my lavender dying?", "answer": "Almost always waterlogged roots. Lavender hates cold wet feet — heavy clay holding water around the crown in winter kills more plants than frost itself. Symptoms: sudden collapse after wet spells, browning needles from the bottom up, dieback at the base. Move to a better-drained site, or replace and improve drainage with grit. Other causes: cutting into old bare wood (no regrowth), age (8–12 years for English lavender), or French/Spanish cultivars planted somewhere too cold."},
    {"question": "When and how should I prune lavender?", "answer": "Annually, late July to early August, immediately after flowering. Use shears or secateurs to shorten the flowering stems and a few centimetres of leafy growth — leave the plant looking like a tight green dome. Never cut into the woody base of the plant: lavender rarely regrows from old bare wood and you can kill or permanently disfigure it. Don't skip pruning; neglected plants get leggy and bare at the centre within 3–4 years."},
    {"question": "Can I grow lavender in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and it can be the better option in heavy-soil gardens where ground drainage is poor. Use a 30–40 cm terracotta pot (clay breathes better than plastic), peat-free compost mixed with 30% grit, and a south-facing position. Water modestly through summer; let the compost surface dry between waterings. In winter, move to a sheltered spot — pots are less hardy than ground plants because the rootball can freeze through."},
    {"question": "How long does a lavender plant last?", "answer": "English lavender (L. angustifolia): 8–12 years if well-sited and well-pruned. Lavandins (L. × intermedia): 6–8 years. French/Spanish (L. stoechas): 4–5 years in UK conditions. After this, plants get woody, threadbare, and bare in the centre; replace before they look terrible. Take cuttings each summer from a healthy 5-year-old plant to have replacements ready."},
    {"question": "Are all lavenders edible?", "answer": "Culinary lavender is Lavandula angustifolia — sweetest fragrance, used in lavender shortbread, herbes de Provence, lavender honey. Lavandins (L. × intermedia) are stronger and more camphor-like — fine for soap and essential oil, less pleasant in food. French/Spanish (L. stoechas) is technically edible but pungent. For cooking, choose English lavender, harvest just before flowers open fully, dry on trays in a dark airy place."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'lavender';

-- From leek.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow leek seed from March to April indoors or in a seedbed; transplant 15 cm apart into deep dibbed holes in June or July. Leeks are very hardy (RHS H6) and crop from October right through to April, sitting in the ground through the worst of a UK winter without protection. Drop the seedlings into 15 cm-deep holes made with a thick dibber and water in but don''t fill the holes with soil — the gap blanches the lower stem white. Choose Musselburgh (the heritage UK standard), Toledo or Bandit (overwintering F1s). Watch for allium leaf miner from spring — netting from March stops it; otherwise the maggots tunnel into the shanks.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "March to April. Indoors in modules at 12–15°C, or direct into a seedbed once soil is workable. Sow thinly. Germination in 2–3 weeks."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Full sun, deep fertile soil, fork in well-rotted compost. Leeks need a long growing season and plenty of feeding."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Transplant when pencil-thick", "text": "June to July. Seedlings should be 15–20 cm tall and pencil-thick. Trim the leaf tips back by a third (reduces water loss while roots re-establish)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Drop into dibbed holes", "text": "Use a thick dibber or rake handle to make 15 cm-deep holes, 15 cm apart in rows 30 cm apart. Drop one seedling into each hole. Water in but don't fill the hole with soil — the air gap is what blanches the lower stem white."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Net against allium leaf miner", "text": "From March until the crop is lifted. Phytomyza gymnostoma is a relatively new pest (first UK sighting 2002, now widespread) — the larvae tunnel into stems leaving them slimy and unusable. Enviromesh or fine netting stops the adult fly laying."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Earth up later for more blanching", "text": "Optional, for show-quality long white shanks. Draw soil up around the stems through autumn. Most allotment growers skip this; the dibbed holes give enough blanching for everyday eating."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Lift through winter", "text": "From October onwards, lift as needed with a fork (don't pull — the roots snap off). Frost actually improves the flavour. The crop sits happily in the ground until April."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I sow leek seed?", "answer": "March to April indoors or in a seedbed. Transplant to their final position in June or July when seedlings are pencil-thick. Earlier sowings (February under glass) can be lifted as early as September; later sowings (May) crop from December through to April."},
    {"question": "Why do you drop leeks into holes without filling them?", "answer": "The 15 cm-deep air pocket around the lower stem is what blanches it white. If you fill the hole with soil, you get a thinner, less-blanched leek with grit working into the leaf joints. Leave the hole open after planting; just water it in. Soil naturally falls in over the next few weeks as the plant grows."},
    {"question": "What's allium leaf miner and how do I stop it?", "answer": "Phytomyza gymnostoma — a small fly whose larvae tunnel into leek (and onion, garlic, chive) stems, leaving them slimy and unusable. First recorded in the UK in 2002 and now widespread. The only reliable defence is netting with 5 mm enviromesh from March until lifting. Two generations a year — adults active March–May and September–November."},
    {"question": "What variety of leek is best for the UK?", "answer": "Musselburgh has been the UK standard since the 1830s — reliable, hardy, fat shanks, good flavour. Modern alternatives: Toledo and Bandit (overwintering F1s with excellent cold tolerance), Oarsman (early season), Apollo (mid-season). For overwintering through the worst weather, choose an F1 bred specifically for it."},
    {"question": "Can I grow leeks in containers?", "answer": "Possible but limited. A deep (40 cm+) container holds 4–6 leeks if you really want them. Yield is modest and you lose the blanching depth the dibber method gives in the ground. Use the bed space if you have it."},
    {"question": "When are leeks ready to harvest?", "answer": "October onwards, when shanks are at least 2 cm thick. They keep cropping right through winter and into April. Lift as needed — they store far better in the ground than out of it. By May they bolt and become woody; lift any remaining before then."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'leek';

-- From lemon-balm.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant lemon balm from a young plant in spring, in sun or part shade, in any ordinary garden soil — it''s an easy, vigorous, lemon-scented perennial that thrives almost anywhere and dies back to ground each winter. Lemon balm is hardy (RHS H5–H6) and reliably returns each spring. Treat it like mint — contain it. Lemon balm spreads by both runners and self-seeding, and an unchecked plant takes over a herb bed within 3 years. Sink barriers around it or grow in a pot. Pick fresh leaves for herbal tea (the classic use — calming, lemony, lovely with honey), salads, dressings, and fish dishes. Cut the whole plant back to 5 cm in mid-summer for a fresh flush of tender new growth. The yellow-leaved Aurea cultivar is more ornamental and slightly less vigorous than the species.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy a young plant in spring", "text": "Lemon balm grows easily from seed but a single plant gives a household more than enough lemon balm — and seed-grown plants are slower than divisions or pot-grown specimens. Buy a 9 cm or 1 L plant in April–May."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Contain it, like mint", "text": "Lemon balm is in the same family as mint (Lamiaceae) and shares mint's invasive tendencies. Two containment strategies. (1) Grow in a pot — 30 cm minimum, stood on a paving slab so runners can't escape into surrounding soil. (2) Sink a barrier in the ground — 30 cm of paving slab or pond liner around the planting area. Without containment, lemon balm spreads aggressively by runners and self-sown seedlings, and is hard to remove once established."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in sun or part shade", "text": "Adaptable. Full sun for strongest lemon scent; light shade for tender leaves and better growth in dry summer. Ordinary soil — no need to enrich. Spacing 60 cm if planting more than one (it grows fast)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Pick fresh from spring through autumn", "text": "Cut whole stems or pinch individual leaves any time from April onwards. The plant branches and re-grows. Strongest lemon flavour and scent is in early summer just before flowering; midsummer growth gets coarser and less aromatic. Pick over the plant continuously to keep it producing tender shoots."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Cut back hard in mid-summer", "text": "The single most important husbandry. In late June or early July, cut the whole plant down to 5 cm above ground. This (1) removes the white flower spikes before they set seed, (2) prevents the leggy mid-summer slump when older growth gets tatty and aphid-prone, (3) triggers a fresh flush of tender new leaves for late summer. Without this cut, lemon balm gets ragged from July onwards."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut all flower stems before they set seed", "text": "Lemon balm self-seeds prolifically — a single un-deadheaded plant produces hundreds of seedlings the following year, often colonising paths and gravel. Cut flower stems off as buds form (mid-June) for total control, or right after flowering (late July) before seed develops."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Make lemon balm tea", "text": "The classic use. Pick a handful of fresh leaves (or 1 tbsp dried), pour over boiling water, steep 5 minutes, strain. A traditional UK calmer for nervous tension and disturbed sleep — herbalists have used it for centuries. Fresh leaves give a bright lemon flavour; dried leaves are subtler but easier to keep through winter. Honey complements lovely."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Divide every 3–4 years", "text": "Lemon balm clumps get woody and bare at the centre after 3–4 years. Lift in early spring, split with a spade into 4–6 sections, replant the best outer pieces in fresh soil. Compost the dead centre. The plant comes back rejuvenated, with the bonus of spare plants for friends."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Will lemon balm survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — fully hardy (RHS H5–H6). The above-ground growth dies back to the crown each autumn and the plant returns from the rootstock each March–April. Survives any normal UK winter without protection. In a mild winter you might see a few green leaves at the base; in a hard one it disappears completely until spring. Established plants are essentially indestructible."},
    {"question": "Is lemon balm invasive?", "answer": "Yes — treat it like mint. Spreads aggressively by underground runners AND by prolific self-seeding. An unchecked plant becomes a colony within 2–3 years and is hard to remove once established. Two essential controls. (1) Grow in a pot or sunken-barrier bed — never directly in the open border without containment. (2) Cut flower stems off before seed sets — mid-June for full prevention. If you've inherited an invasion, dig out parent plants and weed seedlings rigorously for 2 years; new shoots will keep appearing from root fragments."},
    {"question": "When do I cut lemon balm back?", "answer": "Late June or early July for the main hard cut — to 5 cm above ground. This produces a fresh flush of tender leaves for late summer, prevents seeding, and stops the plant looking tatty. Continuously through summer for the kitchen — pick individual stems whenever you want lemon balm. Don't cut in autumn beyond removing dead stems after the foliage dies back; let the plant retreat to its rootstock for winter."},
    {"question": "What does lemon balm tea do?", "answer": "Mild calming effect — herbalists have used it for centuries for nervous tension, anxiety, and disturbed sleep. Lemon balm contains rosmarinic acid and citral, with documented mild sedative and anti-anxiety properties (studies are small but consistent). The tea is gentle, lemony, and pleasant — not a strong medicine but a useful daily calmer. Not for everyone: lemon balm interacts with thyroid medication, so people with thyroid conditions should consult a doctor before regular use."},
    {"question": "Can I grow lemon balm indoors?", "answer": "Yes — on a sunny windowsill in a 20 cm pot. It's one of the easier herbs to keep indoors year-round. Water consistently (don't let the pot dry out), pick regularly to keep growth tender. The plant won't get as large or vigorous as an outdoor specimen — divide and re-pot in fresh compost every spring. Fluorescent grow lights help in winter when natural light is weak."},
    {"question": "How do I dry lemon balm?", "answer": "Pick on a dry morning just before flowering (peak essential oil). Cut whole stems, bunch 6–10 together, hang upside-down in a warm dark airy place (an airing cupboard works well) for 1–2 weeks. Once leaves crumble easily, strip from the stems and store in a sealed jar away from light. Dried lemon balm keeps its lemon flavour for about 6 months, then fades — better to dry small batches throughout summer than one large batch."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'lemon-balm';

-- From lettuce.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow lettuce seed from late March outdoors, or from February indoors for transplanting — and keep sowing every two to three weeks until early August for a continuous supply. Lettuce is half-hardy (RHS H3–H4) so summer varieties bolt in heat above 25°C; choose Cos and butterhead types for cool seasons, looseleaf and iceberg for summer. Plant out 25 cm apart in moist, fertile soil with light shade if you can — pure sun in July is too much. The biggest enemy is slugs; the second is birds pulling out seedlings. Pick outer leaves of looseleaf types regularly to keep new growth coming; hearting types cut whole when firm.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H3',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow indoors or direct", "text": "Indoors from February in modules under glass; direct from late March outdoors. Successional sowings every 2–3 weeks until early August give a continuous crop. Sow thinly — lettuce seed is light and easy to over-sow."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Thin or transplant", "text": "When seedlings are 5 cm tall with two true leaves, thin to 25 cm apart for full-size heads, 15 cm for cut-and-come-again looseleaf types. Water in well."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Protect from slugs", "text": "Slugs love lettuce above all else. Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) to warm moist soil two weeks before transplanting. Beer traps near the bed help. Check at dusk in damp weather and hand-pick. Copper rings around modules in the early stages."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water and mulch", "text": "Lettuce wants consistent moisture. Water in the morning, never in the evening (slug-attractor). Mulch with grass clippings to retain moisture and suppress weeds."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Provide afternoon shade in summer", "text": "From late June onwards, lettuce bolts in heat above 25°C. Plant under taller crops (sweetcorn, beans) or use shade netting in really hot weeks. Cool-season cultivars survive heat better than hot."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick or cut", "text": "Looseleaf types: pick outer leaves regularly, the centre keeps producing for weeks. Hearting types (Cos, butterhead, iceberg): cut the whole head at the base when firm. Pick in the cool of the morning for crispest leaves."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Choose bolt-resistant varieties for summer", "text": "For July to August sowings, use Lobjoits Green Cos, Webbs Wonderful, Reine de Glace, or Salad Bowl mixes. Standard butterheads bolt in summer heat."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow lettuce in the UK?", "answer": "From late February indoors under glass for transplanting in April; from late March outdoors. Continue sowing every 2–3 weeks until early August for continuous picking through autumn. Hardy winter varieties (Winter Density, Winter Imperial) sown in September overwinter under cloches for an early spring crop."},
    {"question": "Why is my lettuce going to seed (bolting)?", "answer": "Hot weather (above 25°C), long days, water stress, or the variety isn't bolt-resistant. Bolted lettuce gets bitter, then sends up a flowering stem. Once bolting starts you can't stop it. Pick the whole head, use what's still palatable, and choose a bolt-resistant cultivar for the next sowing."},
    {"question": "How do I stop slugs eating my lettuce seedlings?", "answer": "Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) to warm moist soil two weeks before transplanting — they kill slugs from inside. Beer traps catch a useful number. Hand-picking at dusk on damp evenings reduces the population. Copper rings on pots and modules in the early stages. Slug pellets approved for organic use (ferric phosphate) are a last resort."},
    {"question": "Looseleaf or hearting lettuce — which is easier?", "answer": "Looseleaf (Salad Bowl, oak-leaf types) is much easier and more forgiving. Pick outer leaves and the plant keeps producing for weeks; you don't have to time a single harvest perfectly. Hearting types (Cos, butterhead, iceberg) need to be cut whole when firm — pick the wrong week and they bolt. Mix both in a salad bed."},
    {"question": "Can I grow lettuce in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Excellent for containers. A 30 cm pot grows 6–8 looseleaf plants. Use peat-free multi-purpose compost; water daily in summer. Containers can be moved to shade in hot weather, which helps with bolting. Cut-and-come-again types are the easiest container crop in any season."},
    {"question": "When is lettuce ready to harvest?", "answer": "Looseleaf: start picking outer leaves about 4–6 weeks after sowing, when leaves are 10 cm long. Hearting types: when the head feels firm to the touch and is showing colour at the heart — usually 8–10 weeks from sowing. Cut just above the lowest leaves; the stump sometimes resprouts."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'lettuce';

-- From lovage.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant lovage from a young plant in spring, or sow seed direct in late summer, in sun or part shade in any decent garden soil — it''s a very hardy perennial that grows large (2 m tall when flowering) and gives strong celery-and-parsley flavour to soups, stocks, and salads. Lovage is very hardy (RHS H7) and the leaves are one of the first usable greens of the year, appearing in March. One plant is enough for a household — lovage is too big and too strongly flavoured to want more. Cut foliage hard back in mid-summer for a fresh flush of tender leaves; the older summer foliage gets coarse and intensely strong. An old-fashioned UK herb less grown today, but unbeatable for stocks and stews. Self-seeds modestly but doesn''t take over.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Plan for a big plant", "text": "Lovage grows BIG. A mature lovage reaches 1.5–2 m tall when flowering, with a 1 m wide clump of foliage at the base. Allow at least 1 m² of space; don't plant near small or delicate neighbours. Position: back of a border, the back of the herb garden, a wild corner near the kitchen door, an unused 1 m² of allotment."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant a young plant or sow seed", "text": "From plant (easier): a 1–3 L pot from a herb nursery (Jekka's Herb Farm, Pepperpot, RHS Wisley shop) in March–May. One plant is enough. From seed (cheaper, slower): sow direct in late summer (August–September) when the seed is fresh — lovage seed loses viability quickly. Surface-sow, press gently, germinates in 14–28 days. Seed-grown plants take a year longer to reach full productivity."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in sun or part shade, moisture-retentive soil", "text": "Lovage prefers deep, moisture-retentive, fertile soil but tolerates anything from light shade to full sun and most soil types except waterlogged. Mulch annually with garden compost or well-rotted manure to feed the deep taproot. Spacing isn't an issue for one plant; if planting multiple (most people don't), space 1 m apart."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't transplant once established", "text": "Lovage has a long deep taproot that resents disturbance. Plant young and leave it where it is — mature lovage is hard to move successfully. For dividing: lovage can be divided in early spring by carefully splitting the crown with a sharp spade, but the operation often sets the plant back for a year."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick young leaves from March", "text": "Lovage is one of the first usable greens of the year — leaves appear from early March and are tender, flavour-packed, ready for use immediately. Pick the youngest tender leaves through spring and early summer; midsummer foliage gets coarse and intensely flavoured (so strong it dominates dishes — use sparingly)."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut foliage back hard in mid-summer", "text": "The key husbandry rule. Cut the whole plant down to 15 cm above ground in mid-July (after flowering or as flower stems form). This (1) removes the coarse summer foliage, (2) triggers a fresh flush of tender young leaves for late summer and autumn cropping, (3) keeps the clump tidy. Without the mid-summer chop, lovage looks tatty from August onwards and the leaves are too strong to use."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cut flower stems off for leaves OR let it flower", "text": "Lovage produces tall yellow umbel flowers in June–July on stems up to 2 m. Cut flower stems off as buds form if you want to focus the plant on leaf production (the leaves are the kitchen prize). Leave flowers if you want the architectural drama (impressive specimen plant), the bees and beneficial insects that work the umbels, or the seed (lovage seed has its own celery-like flavour for use in pickles and breads)."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Harvest seed in late summer", "text": "The yellow umbel flowers develop into seed heads through August. Cut whole umbels as the seeds turn brown but before they shatter, hang upside-down inside a paper bag for 2 weeks. Lovage seed has a stronger flavour than the leaves — used in pickled vegetables, rye bread, spice mixes. One plant produces 100+ g of seed in a good year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I pick lovage?", "answer": "From March onwards — lovage is one of the earliest usable greens of the year. Young tender leaves appear in early spring and are at peak flavour through April–June. Midsummer foliage gets coarse and very strongly flavoured — too strong for most uses except long-simmered stocks. Cut the plant down hard in mid-July for a fresh flush of tender leaves through late summer and autumn. Stops cropping in late autumn when the plant dies back; returns each spring."},
    {"question": "Is lovage the same as celery?", "answer": "No — different plant, similar flavour. Lovage (Levisticum officinale) is a hardy perennial herb in the carrot family with celery-parsley flavour. Celery (Apium graveolens) is a biennial vegetable in the same family, grown for the swollen stems. They share the carrot-family aroma but are different species. Lovage substitutes well for celery in soups, stocks, and stews — one tablespoon of chopped lovage leaves replaces a whole celery stick in flavour intensity. Don't substitute the other way (lovage is too strong for fresh salads)."},
    {"question": "How big does lovage get?", "answer": "Big — 1.5–2 m tall when flowering, 1 m wide at the base. This is a back-of-the-border plant, not a herb-bed neighbour for parsley and chives. Allow 1 m² of space and don't plant near small delicate herbs that the lovage will overshadow. If your garden is small, consider whether you really need lovage — one plant is enough for a household and supermarket parsley/celery substitutes work for most cooking."},
    {"question": "Will lovage survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — very hardy (RHS H7). The above-ground foliage dies back completely in late autumn and the plant retreats to its deep taproot, returning each spring (early March in mild gardens, late March further north). Survives any UK winter without protection. Established lovage is essentially indestructible — it lives 10+ years and gets more vigorous over time. Two cautions: (1) doesn't tolerate waterlogged soil; (2) avoid transplanting mature plants (long taproot resents disturbance)."},
    {"question": "Can I grow lovage in a pot?", "answer": "Possible but limiting. Lovage has a long deep taproot and wants 1 m of root space — even a 40 cm pot restricts it. Pot lovage stays smaller (1 m tall instead of 2 m), produces fewer leaves, and may need division every 2–3 years. Better in the ground if you have space. If you only have a pot: choose the largest container you can manage (50 cm+), use deep root-friendly compost, and keep the plant smaller by cutting hard back twice a year."},
    {"question": "What does lovage taste like?", "answer": "Strong celery-parsley-anise, with a slight bitter edge. The flavour is far stronger than celery — one chopped tablespoon of lovage leaves replaces a whole celery stick in flavour intensity, so use sparingly. Classic uses: soups (especially clear chicken broth), stews, beef stocks, lentil dishes, savoury pies. Less traditional: lovage leaves can replace celery in salad if used very sparingly, or as a herb butter for fish. The seeds have an even stronger celery flavour — used in pickling."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'lovage';

-- From mangetout.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow mangetout direct from March to July, in sun in well-drained soil — they''re an edible-podded variant of the garden pea, eaten whole including the soft, sugary, immature pod before the seeds inside have filled out. Mangetout (literally "eat-all") are hardy (RHS H4–H5) but the seedlings dislike cold wet soil — wait until soil is at least 7°C in spring, or sow under cloches. Pick the pods young and flat (5–7 cm) while the peas inside are still tiny; left longer the pods get tough and stringy. Successional sowing every 3 weeks keeps fresh tender pods coming from June to September. Distinct from sugar snap peas (rounder fatter pods, also eaten whole — slightly later UK introduction). Both need a 1.5–2 m support — pea netting, twigs, or wire — and protection from pea moth and pigeons.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose mangetout or sugar snap", "text": "Mangetout: flat thin pods 5–8 cm long, eaten whole when the pod is young and the peas are still tiny. Oregon Sugar Pod is the UK standard. Tender, sweet, classic. Sugar snap: fatter rounder pods 5–7 cm, eaten whole when fully filled out (still soft, still sweet, never tough). Sugar Ann, Sugar Snap, Cascadia. Slightly newer to UK gardens, very popular. For a first crop, Oregon Sugar Pod mangetout — reliable, no stringy issue, easy to pick at the right moment."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, March to July", "text": "Wait until soil is 7°C or warmer (mid-March in southern England, late March–April in the Midlands and north). Cold wet soil = poor germination. Make a flat-bottomed drill 5 cm deep, 15 cm wide; sow seeds 5–8 cm apart in two staggered rows along the drill. Cover with soil, water lightly. Germination in 10–14 days."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Sow successionally every 3 weeks", "text": "The key to continuous mangetout. A single mangetout sowing crops heavily for 3–4 weeks then declines. Sow a fresh short row every 3 weeks from late March until early July, and you'll have tender pods from June through September. Don't sow after late July — heat causes mildew and the autumn pods get tough."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Provide a 1.5–2 m support before sowing", "text": "Mangetout climb by tendrils and need something to grip. Set up support before sowing — easier than retro-fitting around growing plants. Options: pea netting stretched on canes (cheapest, easiest); hazel twigs and pea sticks (traditional, beautiful); wire trellis. The plants grow 1.5–2 m tall."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Protect seedlings from pigeons and mice", "text": "Mice eat the seeds before germination — soak seeds in paraffin briefly before sowing, or set humane traps. Pigeons eat the emerging seedlings in March–April. Cover the sown drill with horticultural fleece or chicken wire until the plants are 15 cm tall (past the pigeon-eating stage)."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water consistently when flowering and podding", "text": "Mangetout drop flowers and produce stringy pods in dry conditions. Once flowering starts (May–June): water deeply once or twice a week in dry spells. Mulch with garden compost in spring to conserve moisture. Pot-grown mangetout need daily watering once cropping."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick young and pick often", "text": "The most important harvest rule. Pick when pods are 5–7 cm long, flat, and the peas inside are still tiny lumps. Left longer: pods get tough, stringy, and the plant stops producing new flowers. Pick at least every 2–3 days during cropping — daily during peak. The more you pick the more they produce. Cooked: 2 minutes blanched, or eaten raw in salads."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for pea moth, mildew, and aphids", "text": "Pea moth (Cydia nigricana): tiny caterpillars inside pods, June–July. Use pheromone moth traps in flowering rows from mid-May. Powdery mildew: white powder on leaves, usually late summer/heat-stressed plants. Improve airflow, water at the base, accept that late sowings often get mildewed. Pea aphids: leaf damage, occasional virus transmission. Encourage hoverflies and ladybirds; spray with horticultural soap if severe."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What's the difference between mangetout and sugar snap peas?", "answer": "Both are edible-podded peas eaten whole, but the pods are different. Mangetout (eat-all): flat, thin pods 5–8 cm long, eaten when pod is young and peas inside are tiny lumps. If left to mature the pod gets stringy. Sugar snap: fatter, rounder, fuller pods 5–7 cm, eaten when fully filled out — still sweet and crunchy, never tough. Sugar snaps are newer to UK gardens (1970s/80s) and very popular; mangetout has been grown in Britain for centuries. Both crop similarly and can be sown together."},
    {"question": "When can I pick mangetout?", "answer": "6–8 weeks after sowing, depending on conditions and variety. Pick when pods are 5–7 cm long, flat, and peas inside are tiny lumps — not when the pods feel full. Pick at least every 2–3 days during the cropping window; mangetout left on the plant beyond peak go stringy AND the plant stops flowering. The more you pick, the more they produce rule applies more strongly to mangetout than almost any other vegetable."},
    {"question": "Why are my mangetout going stringy and tough?", "answer": "Picked too late. Pods get stringy and the side-strings (along the seam) get fibrous within a few days of reaching peak. Solution: pick more often, even daily during peak production. Stringy pods can still be eaten if you strip the strings off the seam first (run a knife under each end and pull the string off). Sugar snap varieties are bred to be stringless even when fully filled out — consider switching if stringy mangetout is a recurring issue."},
    {"question": "How tall do mangetout grow?", "answer": "1.5–2 m for tall varieties like Oregon Sugar Pod and the traditional sugar snap. Compact varieties like Half Pint and Sugar Ann grow 75 cm to 1 m — good for small gardens or container culture. Always provide support before sowing — pea netting, hazel sticks, or wire trellis — set up to the variety's expected height. Without support, mangetout sprawl flat and rot in damp weather."},
    {"question": "Can I grow mangetout in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — use a compact variety. Choose Half Pint (60 cm dwarf), Sugar Ann (compact 75 cm), or Tom Thumb (50 cm). Use a 40 cm pot of peat-free compost. Sow 5–6 seeds per pot, set up a 1 m support (a wigwam of canes works well). Water daily once cropping starts. One pot gives a few weeks of pickings; sow a fresh pot every 3 weeks for continuous supply."},
    {"question": "Why didn't my mangetout germinate?", "answer": "Three common causes. (1) Soil too cold: mangetout seeds rot in soil below 7°C. Wait until soil warms (mid-March in south, April further north), or pre-warm soil under cloches. (2) Mice ate the seeds: dig down where you sowed; if seeds are missing, mice have taken them. Resow with paraffin-soaked seeds or in a covered drill. (3) Old seed: pea seed loses viability after 2–3 years; use fresh packets each year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'mangetout';

-- From marigold.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow marigold seed direct outdoors from March onwards, or in modules from February — the name marigold covers two unrelated plants in UK gardens. Calendula officinalis (pot marigold, English marigold) is a fully hardy annual (RHS H4) with edible petals that self-seeds happily across British gardens. Tagetes patula (French marigold) and T. erecta (African marigold) are frost-tender annuals (RHS H1c) usually grown for summer bedding and tomato companion-planting in greenhouses. Both are easy, prolific and reliable. Calendula in clear yellow and orange; Tagetes in fiery orange, yellow and mahogany. Tagetes suppresses root-knot nematodes in greenhouse tomato beds. Both attract pollinators and hoverflies that prey on aphids — useful allies for the wider veg patch.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick the right marigold for the job", "text": "Calendula officinalis (pot marigold) for the kitchen garden, edible petals, and self-seeding cottage-garden look. Tagetes patula (French marigold) for tomato beds and summer bedding. Tagetes erecta (African marigold) for big show flowers. Each grows differently — don't mix them up."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow Calendula direct from March", "text": "Hardy enough to sow outdoors from March in southern England, April further north. 1 cm deep, scatter or in rows. Germination in 10–14 days. Calendula will also self-sow from previous-year plants once established."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Sow Tagetes indoors from late March", "text": "Frost-tender — start in modules at 18°C. Plant out after the last frost (mid-May south, early June north). Don't be tempted to sow Tagetes outdoors early; cold soil rots the seed."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Thin to spacing", "text": "Calendula 30 cm apart for full-size plants; 15 cm for cut-and-come-again. Tagetes 20 cm apart for French types; 30 cm for African types."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pinch out the tip", "text": "When plants are 15 cm tall, pinch out the growing tip to encourage branching. Both calendula and tagetes flower far more from pinched plants."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Deadhead through summer", "text": "Cut spent flowers back to the next bud or side-shoot. Both species stop flowering when seed heads form. Picking calendula for vases or to use the petals in cooking has the same effect."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Use Tagetes as a tomato companion", "text": "Plant French marigolds in or beside greenhouse tomato beds. Tagetes patula roots secrete alpha-terthienyl which suppresses root-knot nematodes — a real, evidence-based companion-planting effect (not folklore). Particularly useful if you've grown tomatoes in the same greenhouse soil for several years."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Save calendula seed in autumn", "text": "Calendula comes true from open-pollinated seed. Let some flowers go to seed in autumn; gather the curved seed clusters when dry; store in a paper envelope. Self-sown seedlings the following spring are often the most vigorous."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What's the difference between Calendula marigolds and Tagetes marigolds?", "answer": "Two unrelated plants share the name. Calendula officinalis (pot marigold, English marigold) — hardy annual, clear yellow or orange daisy-like flowers, edible petals, traditional UK cottage-garden plant. Tagetes patula and T. erecta (French and African marigolds) — frost-tender annuals from Mexico, brighter and more varied colours, traditional bedding plants. Different family, different growing requirements; just confusingly the same name."},
    {"question": "Do marigolds really help tomatoes?", "answer": "Yes, but only Tagetes — and only against root-knot nematodes, a pest mostly affecting greenhouse tomato beds where the same soil has been used for years. Tagetes patula secretes alpha-terthienyl which kills the nematodes. The marigolds beside tomatoes general claim is partly true for greenhouse soil; less true outdoors where root-knot nematodes aren't a major UK problem. Calendula has no such effect."},
    {"question": "Are calendula petals edible?", "answer": "Yes — pluck the petals from open flowers and sprinkle on salads, rice, soups, or use to colour butter and cheese. They have a mild, slightly resinous flavour. The whole flower is edible but the centre disk gets bitter. Sometimes called poor man's saffron — colour without the cost."},
    {"question": "When should I sow marigolds in the UK?", "answer": "Calendula: March to April direct outdoors; can also be sown in autumn (September) for early flowers the following spring. Tagetes: late March to early May indoors in modules; plant out after the last frost (mid-May south, early June north). Don't sow Tagetes outdoors before mid-April; cold wet soil rots the seed."},
    {"question": "Why are my marigolds not flowering?", "answer": "Likely causes: too much shade (both species want sun for 6+ hours a day); over-feeding (rich soil pushes leaves at the expense of flowers); seed heads forming (deadhead religiously); or for Tagetes, planted out too early when nights were still cold (the check stays with the plant). They want lean, sunny, deadheaded conditions."},
    {"question": "Do marigolds self-seed?", "answer": "Calendula yes, prolifically — once you have it, you have it for the next several years from self-sown seedlings on bare soil each spring. Tagetes barely — fresh sowing each year is the norm. Calendula self-seedlings often grow more vigorously than nursery-sown plants and crop earlier."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'marigold';

-- From mint.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant mint in spring or autumn — buy small plants from a nursery rather than seed, since mint comes true only from cuttings or division. Mint is very hardy (RHS H6) and runs aggressively underground; always grow it in a sunken pot or a large container to stop it taking over the bed. Plant in moist, fertile soil in sun or part-shade. Cut back hard in late autumn or early spring; the plant regenerates from the roots every year. Pick from the top growth regularly to encourage bushiness. Spearmint (Mentha spicata) is the kitchen standard; peppermint for tea; apple mint, Moroccan mint and chocolate mint for variety. Cut back twice a season to keep new tender growth coming.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose your variety", "text": "Spearmint (Mentha spicata) is the standard kitchen mint. Peppermint for tea. Moroccan mint for North African dishes. Apple mint for desserts. Chocolate mint for novelty. Each is a distinct species or cultivar — don't seed-grow them, the seedlings rarely come true."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in a sunken container", "text": "Plant in a 25–30 cm pot, then sink the pot into the bed with the rim 5 cm proud of the soil. This contains the runners. Without containment, mint takes over."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Position", "text": "Sun or part-shade. Moist, fertile soil. Mint tolerates damp conditions better than most herbs and grows on the shadier side of a bed where other herbs sulk."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water and feed", "text": "Water regularly through summer. A light feed of liquid fertiliser monthly through the growing season keeps leaves tender."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Cut back twice a season", "text": "Hard cut in late June after the first flush flowers; second hard cut in late autumn. This forces fresh tender growth and stops the plant going woody."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick from the top", "text": "Pick the top 7–10 cm of growth regularly. The plant branches at each cut and produces more."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Divide every 3 years", "text": "Mint exhausts itself in a container after about 3 seasons. Lift, divide the rootball into 4–6 pieces in spring, replant the most vigorous segment, compost the rest."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I stop mint taking over my garden?", "answer": "Don't plant it directly in the bed. Use a sunken container (a 25–30 cm pot with the rim 5 cm above soil level), or a large standalone container, or a section of bed walled off with sunk slate or paving. Mint spreads by underground runners — once it's loose in a bed, you'll never fully get rid of it."},
    {"question": "Can I grow mint from seed?", "answer": "Most named varieties don't come true from seed — spearmint seedlings won't taste like spearmint, and most peppermint seed sold isn't true peppermint anyway. Always buy a small plant from a nursery, or take a cutting from a friend's plant in spring. One small plant gives you decades."},
    {"question": "When is the best time to pick mint?", "answer": "Just before flowering, when the leaves are largest and the oils strongest. From May to August for spearmint. Pick the top 7–10 cm of each shoot; the plant branches at each cut. Avoid picking flowering shoots — the flavour weakens. Cut the whole plant back hard once after flowering and you get a second flush of tender leaves."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between spearmint, peppermint, and apple mint?", "answer": "Spearmint (Mentha spicata) is the standard kitchen mint — for new potatoes, mint sauce, mojitos. Peppermint (Mentha × piperita) has a stronger, cooler menthol kick — for tea and confectionery. Apple mint (Mentha suaveolens) has fuzzy leaves and a fruity-sweet flavour — for desserts and Pimm's. Each is distinct; grow more than one if you have space."},
    {"question": "How do I store fresh mint?", "answer": "Treat cut stems like flowers: stand in a glass of water on the counter, change daily. Keeps 4–5 days. For longer storage, freeze whole leaves in ice cube trays with water, or chop and freeze in oil. Drying loses most of the flavour; freezing preserves it."},
    {"question": "Does mint really deter pests?", "answer": "Modestly. Mint deters cabbage white butterflies from laying on nearby brassicas (the strong smell confuses them) and discourages some aphid species. The effect is partial — netting is still more reliable for cabbage white. The pollinator-attraction effect (bees and hoverflies love the flowers) is bigger than the deterrent effect."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'mint';

-- From mizuna.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow mizuna direct from March to October, in sun or part shade in moist soil — it''s a fast-growing cool-season Japanese salad green with feathery deeply-cut leaves and a mild peppery mustard flavour, ideal for cut-and-come-again cropping. Mizuna is hardy (RHS H4–H5) and at its best in the cooler months — spring and autumn sowings produce tender leaves for weeks, while midsummer sowings bolt to flower quickly. One of the most useful UK undercover winter salads — sown in September it crops through to March from a cold polytunnel, cloche, or cold frame. Pick young leaves continuously by cutting at the base (cut-and-come-again — the plant regrows for 4–5 cuts). Slug attack is the main problem outdoors; flea beetle damages young plants in summer.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct, March to October", "text": "Mizuna is cool-season: best in spring (March–May) and autumn (August–October), with midsummer sowings often bolting before they're ready. Sow thinly in shallow drills (1 cm deep), 20–30 cm between rows. Germination in 7–14 days. For cut-and-come-again: thin to 5 cm; for full mature plants: thin to 20–30 cm. Mizuna isn't fussy about soil but prefers moisture-retentive ground."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow successionally every 3 weeks", "text": "A single mizuna sowing crops cut-and-come-again for 4–6 weeks then bolts. Sow a fresh row every 3 weeks through the cool months for continuous picking. Skip the hot midsummer weeks (mid-June to early August) — mizuna bolts immediately in heat. Resume in late August for autumn and winter cropping."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Sow under cover in September for winter salads", "text": "The best mizuna season is winter, sown in September. A row sown in late August or early September under a cloche, low polytunnel, or cold frame produces tender leaves from October through to March — one of the most useful UK winter undercover salads. Outdoor September sowings crop until first hard frost, then survive but stop growing until spring."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Pick young leaves cut-and-come-again", "text": "The classic UK technique for fast salads. Cut whole rows at 2–3 cm above the soil when leaves are 10–15 cm tall (4–6 weeks from sowing). The plant regrows in 7–10 days — repeat the cut 3–4 times before the row finally bolts. Use scissors or a sharp knife for clean cuts that re-grow quickly; tearing damages the regrowth point."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Protect from slugs", "text": "The main mizuna problem outdoors. Slugs love the tender leaves and can shred a row overnight. Three controls. (1) Wool pellets around the row — slugs dislike crossing rough fibres. (2) Nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) watered into the soil — biological control. (3) Grow under cover — cloches and polytunnels exclude slugs almost completely (a major reason mizuna is so successful undercover)."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Watch for flea beetle in summer", "text": "Flea beetle (Phyllotreta) creates tiny shot-hole damage on leaves in hot dry summers — peppers the leaves with pinprick holes. Damaged leaves are still edible but unsightly. Prevention: cover with fine insect mesh from sowing, or keep soil consistently moist (flea beetle thrives in drought-stressed plants). Mid-summer mizuna often gets flea beetle even with prevention — another reason to avoid hot-season sowings."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Use young — flavour intensifies with age", "text": "Pick young (10–15 cm leaves) for mild peppery flavour suitable for salads. Older leaves get sharper and more mustardy — still good for stir-fries, sushi rolls, mixed salad mixes, but too strong for fresh leaf salads. Use fresh within 2–3 days of picking; mizuna doesn't store well. Add to sandwiches, mixed salad bowls, stir-fries (cooks in 30 seconds)."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Let some plants flower for self-seeding (optional)", "text": "Mizuna bolts to small yellow flowers in late summer if not picked. Let one or two plants flower and set seed — the small black seeds drop where they fall and produce volunteer seedlings the following spring. Cut bolting plants if you don't want self-seeding; the slightly tougher leaves and young flower stems are still edible (use in stir-fries)."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What does mizuna taste like?", "answer": "Mild peppery mustard — much milder than mature mustard greens, but with a clear mustard-family edge. Young leaves are gentle enough for salads; older leaves get sharper and more mustardy. The flavour is between rocket and watercress — peppery but not aggressive. Excellent in mixed salad bowls (the feathery leaves add texture and visual variety), stir-fries, sushi rolls, sandwiches. Use within 2–3 days of picking — flavour and texture both fade with storage."},
    {"question": "When can I pick mizuna?", "answer": "4–6 weeks after sowing, when leaves are 10–15 cm tall. Cut-and-come-again: cut whole rows 2–3 cm above the soil, the plants regrow in 7–10 days, repeat 3–4 times before bolting. Mature plants: pull whole plants for use as a stir-fry green (leaves to 30+ cm). Most UK kitchen gardeners use the cut-and-come-again method for maximum salad supply from a small row."},
    {"question": "Why does my mizuna keep bolting?", "answer": "Heat + day length. Mizuna bolts to flower in long warm days — exactly what summer offers. Three strategies. (1) Sow in cool months: March–May for spring crops, August–October for autumn/winter. (2) Skip June and July: hot-season sowings bolt before producing usable leaves. (3) Sow under cover in September: mizuna's best season is autumn–winter undercover, when day length is short and heat isn't an issue."},
    {"question": "Is mizuna the same as mustard greens?", "answer": "Same family, different species, different flavour intensity. Mizuna (Brassica rapa var. nipposinica): mild peppery flavour, feathery leaves, the salad-friendly choice. Mustard greens (Brassica juncea — see separate mustard-greens slug): stronger peppery flavour, broader leaves, more often cooked than eaten raw. Red Giant mustard and Green-in-Snow mustard are the typical mustard greens; Mibuna is a smooth-leaved relative of mizuna. Both grow under the same UK conditions."},
    {"question": "Can I grow mizuna under a cloche in winter?", "answer": "Yes — this is mizuna's most useful UK growing window. Sow in late August or early September under a low polytunnel, cloche, or cold frame. The plants establish before frosts and crop from October through to March, growing slowly through the coldest months but consistently producing tender leaves. No competition from outdoor crops — mizuna's autumn-winter window fills a real gap in UK home-grown salad supply."},
    {"question": "How do I stop slugs eating my mizuna?", "answer": "Combine multiple controls — no single method is 100% effective on tender salad greens. Apply at sowing: (1) Wool pellets scattered along the row. (2) Nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) watered into the soil. (3) Hand-pick slugs in the evening after rain. Best long-term solution: grow mizuna under cover (cloche, cold frame, polytunnel) — slugs are largely excluded and the crop is much more reliable."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'mizuna';

-- From mustard-greens.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow mustard greens direct from March to October, in sun or part shade in moist soil — they''re fast-growing cool-season leaf brassicas with a strong peppery hot mustard flavour, used in stir-fries, salads (very young leaves), pickling, and the classic Asian condiment. Mustard greens are hardy (RHS H4–H5) and at their best in cool weather; midsummer sowings bolt to flower quickly. Red Giant (large burgundy-red leaves, hot wasabi-like flavour) and Green-in-Snow (very hardy, mild–medium heat) are the popular UK varieties. Pick young leaves for salads (mild peppery), older leaves for cooking (much hotter — cooks down to a milder kick). Cooks down dramatically — a bowl of mustard greens reduces to a few tablespoons. Sow under cover in September for winter cropping through to March.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick variety by heat level and use", "text": "Red Giant: large burgundy-red savoyed leaves, very hot wasabi-like flavour, the showy choice. Strong enough that even cooked it retains a kick. Looks dramatic in ornamental kitchen gardens. Green-in-Snow: very hardy, mid-green leaves, mild-to-medium peppery flavour — the choice for cooking and for milder eaters. Survives harder winters under cover. Wasabi mustard: intensely hot, more punchy than Red Giant, mostly used as a condiment or in sushi. Golden Frills: feathery yellow-green leaves, mild — closer to mizuna in style. For a first crop: Red Giant for visual drama and proper mustard heat."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, March to October", "text": "Cool-season crop. Spring sowings (March–May) crop until June bolt. Autumn sowings (August–October) crop into winter, especially under cover. Skip the hot midsummer (June–early August) — mustard greens bolt immediately in heat. Sow in shallow drills 1 cm deep, 25 cm between rows. Germination in 5–10 days at 15°C."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to spacing matching use", "text": "For cut-and-come-again salads: thin to 5 cm. For full mature plants (for stir-fries or pickling): thin to 25–30 cm. Mature plants reach 30–40 cm tall and can mass-crop in autumn — a single Red Giant plant produces a bowlful of leaves at maturity."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Sow under cover in September for winter cropping", "text": "The most useful UK growing window. A September sowing under a cloche, low polytunnel, or cold frame establishes before frosts and crops from October through to March. Green-in-Snow is the variety bred specifically for this — the name reflects its winter hardiness. Outdoor September sowings crop until first hard frost, then survive but stop growing until spring."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick young for salads, older for cooking", "text": "Young leaves (10–15 cm) have a mild peppery flavour suitable for mixed salads in small quantities — mustard greens are too hot on their own for most fresh salads. Mature leaves (20–30 cm) are too hot raw but cook beautifully — stir-fried, braised, in noodle soups, in dumplings. Cooking reduces the heat dramatically; even very hot raw leaves cook down to a milder kick."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Watch for flea beetle, slugs, and cabbage white", "text": "Flea beetle: pinprick shot-hole damage in summer; cover with fine mesh or sow under cover. Slugs: love mustard greens like all brassicas; apply wool pellets and nematodes. Cabbage white butterfly: caterpillars on summer plants; cover with insect mesh from sowing in April–August. The pest problems are the main reason autumn–winter undercover is the safest mustard-green growing window."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cook them, don't just salad them", "text": "The biggest mustard-greens insight for UK home cooks. Raw, the leaves are too hot for most fresh salads (just a few finely sliced young leaves in a mixed bowl). Cooked, they're transformed: stir-fry with garlic and ginger for 2 minutes; add to ramen and noodle soups; braise with chilli and soy; pickle young leaves Korean-style. The classic Indian dish saag uses mustard greens (sarson ka saag) — far more authentic than spinach."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Save seed for mustard condiment", "text": "Let one plant flower and set seed (yellow umbel flowers, then seed pods). Harvest seed pods as they brown but before they shatter (August onwards), hang upside-down in paper bags for 2 weeks, sieve out seed. Make English mustard: grind seeds, mix with water, leave 10 minutes for the enzymes to activate, season. Make brown mustard: similar but uses the brown seeds without grinding finely."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What do mustard greens taste like?", "answer": "Hot peppery mustard — much stronger than mizuna, similar to a fresh mild radish or wasabi. Heat varies dramatically by variety and age: young leaves of mild varieties (Green-in-Snow) are gently peppery; mature leaves of hot varieties (Red Giant, Wasabi mustard) are strongly hot raw. Cooking reduces the heat by 50–80% — even the hottest leaves cook down to a manageable warmth. Best uses: stir-fries, braising, pickling, Indian saag, kimchi. Use young leaves sparingly in salads."},
    {"question": "Is mustard greens the same as mizuna?", "answer": "Same family, different species, different flavour intensity. Mustard greens (Brassica juncea): broader leaves, much hotter peppery mustard flavour, often cooked. Mizuna (Brassica rapa var. nipposinica — see separate mizuna slug): feathery leaves, mild peppery, mostly eaten raw in salads. Both grow under identical conditions (cool season, direct-sow, susceptible to slugs and flea beetle). Many UK gardeners grow both for the contrast."},
    {"question": "When can I pick mustard greens?", "answer": "4–8 weeks after sowing depending on size wanted. Young leaves (10–15 cm, 4 weeks): for cut-and-come-again salads in small quantities. Mature leaves (20–30 cm, 8 weeks): for cooking. Whole plants: harvest at 8–10 weeks for stir-fries and braises. Year-round picking from autumn-undercover sowings: October to March from a September-sown crop under cover."},
    {"question": "How do I cook mustard greens?", "answer": "The most common UK preparation: stir-fry with garlic and chilli for 2 minutes — cuts the heat, retains crunch, dresses with soy sauce and rice vinegar. Braising: 10 minutes in stock with bacon or pancetta, classic American Southern style. Saag: traditional Indian preparation — long-simmer with onions, garlic, ginger, chilli, finish with cream or yoghurt. Pickling: salt-pack young leaves Korean-style for kimchi. Soup garnish: a handful of young leaves stirred into ramen or pho in the last 30 seconds."},
    {"question": "Why does my mustard greens crop bolt so fast?", "answer": "Heat + long days. Mustard greens bolt to flower in summer conditions. Sow in cool months only: March–May for spring crops, August–October for autumn–winter. Skip June and July entirely — midsummer sowings will bolt before producing usable leaves. Under cover in September is the best UK growing window — short days, cool temperatures, slow steady growth from October to March."},
    {"question": "Can mustard greens survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — particularly Green-in-Snow which is bred for it. Outdoors, mustard greens crop until first hard frost (-3°C+), then survive as small plants without growth until spring. Under a cloche or polytunnel: continuous cropping through winter, from October to March. The autumn-winter undercover window is the most useful UK growing season for mustard greens — no flea beetle, no cabbage white, no bolt-risk, slow steady supply of tender greens."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'mustard-greens';

-- From nasturtium.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow nasturtium seed direct from May, after the last frost — the seeds are large, easy to handle, and the plants hate transplanting. Nasturtiums are frost-tender annuals (RHS H1c) but ridiculously easy: push a seed 2 cm into the ground, water in, and the plant does the rest. Climbing varieties (Tall Mix, Jewel of Africa) scramble 2 m up netting or wigwams; bush forms (Tom Thumb, Empress of India) make compact mounds 30 cm tall. The flowers, leaves and young seed pods are all edible — peppery, like rocket. Nasturtiums also work as a trap crop for blackfly, pulling aphids away from beans and brassicas. Don''t over-feed; rich soil gives leaves and no flowers. Self-seeds happily in mild gardens.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct from May", "text": "Wait for the last frost. Push seeds 2 cm into the ground, 30 cm apart for climbers, 20 cm for bush types. Water in. Germination in 7–14 days."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pick climber or bush", "text": "Climbing (Tall Mix, Jewel of Africa, Spitfire): scramble 2 m up netting, wigwams, or fences. Best for height and quick cover. Bush (Tom Thumb, Empress of India, Black Velvet): 30 cm tall mounds. Suit containers, edges, and bedding."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Don't over-feed the soil", "text": "Nasturtiums flower best on lean, average-to-poor soil. Rich soil and fresh manure give endless lush leaves and almost no flowers. Don't dig in compost or feed; standard garden soil is fine."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Position in sun or light shade", "text": "Full sun for the best flowering; will tolerate light shade but flowers less. Nasturtiums in deep shade are mostly leaves."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Provide support for climbers", "text": "String or netting on a fence; a wigwam of bamboo canes; threading them through other shrubs and roses. Climbers don't grip with tendrils — they twine — so the support should have horizontal elements they can wrap around."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Plant near beans and brassicas as a trap crop", "text": "Aphids (especially blackfly) prefer nasturtium foliage to the main crop. Planting nasturtiums near runner beans, broad beans, brassicas, and courgettes draws aphids onto the nasturtiums and away from the food crops. You can then ignore the nasturtium aphids (they're decoy fodder) or hose them off."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Eat the harvest", "text": "Flowers — toss in salads, fill with cream cheese, freeze in ice cubes for summer drinks. Mild peppery flavour. Leaves — like rocket, peppery and zingy in salads. Young seed pods — green and soft, pickled in vinegar make poor man's capers — almost indistinguishable from the real thing."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Save seed in autumn", "text": "Nasturtium seed pods turn from green to pale yellow when ripe; they drop to the ground freely. Collect a few and store in a paper envelope; the rest self-seed for next year's free flowers. Open-pollinated cultivars come true from seed."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When and how do I sow nasturtium seeds?", "answer": "Direct outdoors from May, after the last frost. The seeds are large and easy to handle. Push 2 cm into the ground, 30 cm apart for climbing varieties, 20 cm for bush types. Water in. Germination in 7–14 days. Don't bother starting indoors — nasturtiums hate transplanting and root-disturbance."},
    {"question": "Why is my nasturtium all leaves and no flowers?", "answer": "The soil is too rich. Nasturtiums flower on lean, poor-to-average soil; rich beds give a riot of foliage and almost no flowers. Don't dig in manure before sowing; don't feed during the season; in containers, use peat-free compost without added feed. The plant wants to be slightly under-cared-for."},
    {"question": "Are nasturtiums edible?", "answer": "Yes — every part above ground. Flowers are mild, peppery, look beautiful in salads and ice cubes. Leaves taste like rocket — peppery, zingy, good in sandwiches and salads. Young green seed pods pickled in vinegar give poor man's capers — startlingly like the real thing. Pick young leaves and flowers; older parts toughen. Don't eat sprayed plants."},
    {"question": "Climber or bush — which type should I choose?", "answer": "Climbing (Tall Mix, Jewel of Africa) for 2 m of quick cover on fences, walls, wigwams; useful as a screen or for filling gaps in roses. Bush (Tom Thumb, Empress of India, Black Velvet) for containers, edges, the front of borders, and ground cover. Both have the same flowers, just different habits. Grow both for variety."},
    {"question": "Do nasturtiums really help with aphids?", "answer": "Yes, as a trap crop. Aphids (especially blackfly Aphis fabae) prefer nasturtium foliage to most other plants and concentrate on it. Planting nasturtiums beside vulnerable crops (beans, brassicas, courgettes) draws aphids onto the decoy and away from your dinner. You can then ignore the nasturtium aphids, hose them off, or feed them to encouraged ladybirds."},
    {"question": "How do I make nasturtium poor man's capers?", "answer": "Pick young green seed pods (still soft, before turning yellow). Soak in salted water overnight; drain. Pack into a sterilised jar; cover with hot white wine or cider vinegar; add a few peppercorns and a bay leaf. Seal. Mature for at least a month before eating. Use anywhere you'd use capers — they're remarkably similar in flavour."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'nasturtium';

-- From onion.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant onion sets from mid-March to mid-April in southern England, late April in the north — or sow seed indoors from January for transplanting in April. Onions are hardy (RHS H5) and tolerate frost as young plants. They want a sunny well-drained bed with low nitrogen; rich soil makes lush leaves and undersized bulbs. Plant sets 10 cm apart with the tips just showing above the soil. Harvest from late July to early September when the foliage yellows and topples. Dry the bulbs in the sun for a week, then store somewhere airy and frost-free; properly cured UK-grown onions keep until April or longer.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose sets or seed", "text": "Sets (small immature onions) are faster, easier, and what most UK gardeners grow. Seed gives more variety and a longer storage life but needs an early indoor start. Heat-treated sets resist bolting."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Late winter. Fork over a sunny, well-drained spot. Don't add fresh manure — high nitrogen produces lush leaves, not bulbs. Rake to fine tilth."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant the sets", "text": "Mid-March to mid-April in southern England, late April in the north. Push individual sets into the soil so just the tip shows, 10 cm apart, in rows 30 cm apart. Firm in. Birds sometimes pull them out for the wisps of dry root — net for a fortnight after planting."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Weed", "text": "Hand-weed regularly throughout the season. Onions don't compete well with weeds. Hoe carefully between rows."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water if very dry", "text": "Onions are reasonably drought-tolerant but a dry June reduces the eventual bulb size. Water deeply once a week if there's no rain."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Stop watering at maturity", "text": "When you see the foliage starting to yellow and topple in late July, stop watering. Bulbs ripen and store better in dry conditions."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Lift", "text": "Once foliage has yellowed and toppled (don't crush it to speed the process — that introduces disease), lift the bulbs with a fork and lay them on top of the soil to dry. A week of sunny weather is ideal; if it's wet, move them to an airy shed."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Cure and store", "text": "When the necks are fully dry (papery, no green), trim leaves to 5 cm and roots flush with the base. Don't wash. Store in mesh bags or woven trays in a cool, dry, well-ventilated place. Inspect monthly and remove any with soft necks."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant onion sets in the UK?", "answer": "Mid-March to mid-April in southern England, late March to early May in the Midlands, mid-April to early May in northern England and Scotland. Earlier sowings risk bolting; later ones don't have time to make a decent bulb. Heat-treated sets resist bolting and can be planted from late March even in the south."},
    {"question": "Why are my onions bolting (going to flower)?", "answer": "Bolting (premature flowering) usually means cold weather hit the sets when they were young, signalling that they've been through a winter. Heat-treated sets resist bolting. If you find one bolting, pull it; the bulb won't store but is fine to eat fresh. Other causes: planting too early, water stress, planting larger sets (smaller sets bolt less)."},
    {"question": "Sets or seed — which is better?", "answer": "Sets are easier, faster, more reliable, and what nine out of ten UK gardeners grow. Seed gives access to more cultivars (especially storage varieties like Bedfordshire Champion), often produces firmer bulbs that store longer, and avoids the bolting tendency of sets. Sow seed indoors in January to February, transplant in April. For first-year gardeners, sets are the answer."},
    {"question": "How big do UK onions get?", "answer": "Most main-crop varieties form bulbs 5–8 cm across — fist-sized. Sturon and Stuttgarter Riesen are reliable medium-sized croppers. Exhibition varieties (Kelsae, Ailsa Craig) can reach 15–20 cm with sustained feeding and watering. Smaller, dense bulbs store longer than huge fluffy ones."},
    {"question": "When are my onions ready to harvest?", "answer": "Late July to early September. Watch the foliage — when about three-quarters has yellowed and toppled, the bulbs are mature. Don't bend the necks deliberately to speed ripening; you'll wound the bulb and ruin storage life. Lift on a dry day with a fork; lay on top of the soil to dry in the sun."},
    {"question": "How long do home-grown onions store?", "answer": "Properly cured storage varieties (Bedfordshire Champion, Sturon, Red Baron) keep 6–9 months in cool dry conditions. The trick is full curing — necks must be papery and fully dry before you stack them; any green left in the neck rots downwards into the bulb. Check stored onions monthly; soft ones go to the kitchen immediately."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'onion';

-- From oregano.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant oregano in a sunny, well-drained spot in poor soil — Mediterranean origins mean it wants sharp drainage, full sun, and no feeding. Common oregano is hardy in most of the UK (RHS H5) but flavour is mild; for proper Italian and Greek cooking strength, plant Greek oregano (Origanum vulgare subsp. hirtum) which has 2–3× the essential oil content. Oregano is a wandering perennial that spreads by underground rhizomes and self-seeds prolifically; cut flower heads off in late summer to limit seedling spread. The white-pink flowers in July–August are extraordinary for bees and butterflies — many gardeners let part of the clump flower and crop the other side. Hard-prune in spring back to woody stubs to refresh the plant. Pick fresh through summer; oregano is one of the few herbs that dries well, intensifying flavour.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick Greek over common for kitchen use", "text": "Common oregano (Origanum vulgare): the UK native wild marjoram. Pretty pink-white flowers, excellent for bees, mild flavour — fine for salads but weak for pizzas and tomato sauces. Greek oregano (subsp. hirtum): the proper culinary form, white flowers, much stronger essential oil content, this is what Italian and Greek cooking demands. Pot marjoram / Sweet marjoram (O. majorana): different species, sweeter, more tender (H1c–H3) — see separate sweet-marjoram content. Many seed packets confuse the three. Buy by Latin name from a herb nursery to be sure."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in poor, dry, sunny soil", "text": "The most common cause of weak oregano flavour is rich soil. Stress and drought concentrate the essential oils. Sandy or gravelly soil in full sun is ideal; on heavier soils fork in plenty of horticultural grit, or grow in pots. Never feed oregano — feeding weakens flavour. Even pots benefit from grit mixed into peat-free compost."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Buy a young plant rather than start from seed", "text": "Seed-grown oregano varies wildly in flavour — many plants are weak culinary forms. Buy a named cultivar in 1 L pot from a herb nursery (Jekka's, Pepperpot, Hartley Botanic), or take a cutting/division from a friend's strong-flavoured plant. One plant is enough for a household and will spread."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Mulch with gravel, not bark", "text": "A 3 cm layer of gravel or pea shingle around the base keeps weeds down, prevents soil splash on the leaves, and reflects heat onto the plant (which intensifies flavour). Organic mulches hold moisture against the stem and encourage rot. The Mediterranean look is also the right look."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Hard-prune in spring", "text": "Late April, cut the whole plant back to the woody stubs — almost ground level. This stops it sprawling, encourages strong new growth, and keeps flavour concentrated. Oregano sprouts vigorously from old wood (unlike sage), so don't be timid."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Let some flower, cut others", "text": "Mid-summer flowering (July–August) is when bees descend in numbers — oregano is one of the top 10 UK garden plants for bee diversity. Let half the clump flower for wildlife; on the other half, snip flower stems off as buds form to keep leaf production going for the kitchen."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick and dry through summer", "text": "Pick whole sprigs by snipping 10 cm tips. For drying, harvest just as flower buds form (peak essential oil concentration), bunch and hang upside-down in a warm dry place for 1–2 weeks. Crumble dried leaves off the stems and store in a sealed jar. Unlike most herbs, oregano often tastes stronger dried than fresh."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Limit self-seeding", "text": "Common oregano self-seeds prolifically and can colonise gravel paths and rockeries. Cut flower stems off before seed sets (early autumn) if you want to contain it. Greek oregano (the culinary form) is less aggressive; Common is the wanderer."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "My oregano tastes bland — what went wrong?", "answer": "Two likely causes. Wrong type: most generic oregano plants sold cheaply are Origanum vulgare (common), which has mild flavour. For pizza-strength oregano, plant Greek oregano (subsp. hirtum) — buy by Latin name. Too rich, too wet, or too shady: oregano flavour concentrates under stress. Rich feeding and constant moisture produce lush leaves but bland flavour. Stop feeding, move to a sunny well-drained position, and the next season's growth will be stronger."},
    {"question": "Will oregano survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Common oregano is hardy through most UK winters (RHS H5) — it dies back to the woody base each year and returns in spring. Greek oregano is slightly less hardy (H4) and may suffer in cold wet winters on heavy soils; gritty soil and a sheltered position improve survival. The Mediterranean cultivars (golden, variegated forms) are more tender still — overwinter in pots that can move under cover."},
    {"question": "When and how should I prune oregano?", "answer": "Late April — cut the whole plant back hard to the woody stubs (almost ground level). Oregano resprouts vigorously from old wood and the spring hard-prune produces compact, well-flavoured growth for the year. A second light trim after flowering (August) tidies the plant and triggers fresh leaves for autumn cropping. Don't prune in autumn — the soft regrowth won't survive winter."},
    {"question": "Can I grow oregano in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and on heavy soils, it's the better option. A 30 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed 50/50 with horticultural grit gives the sharp drainage oregano wants. Full sun. Don't feed. Pots dry out quickly — water when the surface is bone dry, then leave alone. Stress is the secret to flavour. Refresh the compost every 2 years."},
    {"question": "How do I dry oregano?", "answer": "Harvest just as flower buds form (peak essential oil). Cut whole stems with leaves attached, bunch 6–10 stems together, hang upside-down in a warm dry airy place (a kitchen ceiling or airing cupboard) for 1–2 weeks. When the leaves crumble easily, strip them from the stems and store in a sealed jar away from light. Dried oregano lasts a year and is often stronger than fresh — many Italian and Greek cooks prefer it."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between oregano and marjoram?", "answer": "Same genus, different species, different flavour. Oregano (Origanum vulgare): pungent, peppery, stronger — for Italian and Greek cooking. Sweet marjoram (Origanum majorana): sweeter, milder, more delicate — for French cooking and meat dishes. Pot marjoram, Greek oregano, wild marjoram are all Origanum species but with their own flavour profiles. Buy by Latin name to be sure what you're growing."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'oregano';

-- From pak-choi.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow pak choi seed direct from late July to early September for autumn–winter cropping; spring sowings (March–April) work but bolt rapidly in long summer days. Pak choi is hardy (RHS H4–H5) and one of the fastest brassicas — 5–6 weeks from sowing to harvest. Plant 20 cm apart in moist fertile soil with sun for half the day. Net against cabbage white through summer and flea beetle on young leaves. Pick whole heads when 15–20 cm tall, or take outer leaves continuously for cut-and-come-again. The classic stir-fry green; juicier and milder than spring cabbage; tolerates light frost. Joi Choi, Choko, Tatsoi (a different cultivar group with darker rosette leaves), and Rosie F1 (red-stemmed) are reliable UK choices.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "Spring (March–April) for early summer cropping; main sowings late July to early September for autumn–winter cropping. Avoid mid-summer sowings — pak choi bolts fast in long days and heat. 1 cm deep drill; sow thinly; cover lightly. Germination 7–10 days."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Thin to 20 cm", "text": "When seedlings have 2 true leaves, thin to 20 cm apart for full-size heads, 10 cm for cut-and-come-again use. Eat the thinnings."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Net against flea beetle", "text": "Young pak choi leaves are heavily attacked by flea beetle (tiny black pock-marks). Cover with fleece or enviromesh for the first 4 weeks after sowing."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water consistently", "text": "Pak choi bolts in drought as well as heat. Water deeply twice a week; mulch with grass clippings to hold moisture. Container plants need daily watering in summer."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Net against cabbage white", "text": "From transplanting / early seedling stage through October. Cabbage whites lay on pak choi the same as on any brassica."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Harvest whole heads", "text": "Cut at ground level when 15–20 cm tall and the head feels firm. The whole plant cooks down to a small portion — usually 1–2 heads per person per meal. The cut stump sometimes resprouts giving a second smaller pick."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Or pick cut-and-come-again", "text": "For salad use, pick outer leaves continuously from 4 weeks after sowing. Take 2–3 outer leaves per plant weekly; the centre keeps producing for 4–6 weeks before bolting."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Stand autumn crops through winter", "text": "Hardy enough to stand light frosts. In southern England and most of the Midlands, autumn-sown plants give pickings through to December under fleece. Cold gardens lift before deep frost."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow pak choi in the UK?", "answer": "Main sowing window: late July to early September for autumn–winter cropping (best results in UK). Spring sowings (March–April) work but bolt fast as days lengthen towards mid-summer. Avoid May–July sowings — long summer days trigger bolting before heads form properly."},
    {"question": "Why does my pak choi bolt before forming a head?", "answer": "Long summer days (above 14 hours of daylight) trigger bolting in pak choi. UK summer day length is the wrong frame for it. Sow in late summer for autumn cropping when days shorten; that gives the heads time to form before flowering. Bolt-resistant cultivars (Joi Choi, Choko) help but don't eliminate the problem."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between pak choi, tatsoi and bok choy?", "answer": "Pak choi (also spelled bok choy in some regions): tall upright form with white or green stems and broad leaves. The classic Chinese stir-fry green. Tatsoi: dark green rosette form with spoon-shaped leaves; flatter habit, more cold-tolerant; same species, same general care. Joi Choi, Rosie, Joi Choi F1: cultivar names for pak choi types. All are Brassica rapa subsp. chinensis."},
    {"question": "How do I stop flea beetle on pak choi?", "answer": "The dominant young-plant pest. Cover with fleece or fine enviromesh for the first 4 weeks after sowing — the flea beetles can't reach the leaves. Yellow sticky traps catch a useful number. Established plants tolerate the damage and outgrow it; the first 4 weeks are the critical window."},
    {"question": "Can I grow pak choi in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Excellent for containers. A 30 cm pot grows 4–6 plants. Use peat-free compost; water daily in summer; net against cabbage white and flea beetle. Containers actually help with bolting because you can move them into shade in hot weather — useful for spring sowings."},
    {"question": "When and how do I harvest pak choi?", "answer": "5–6 weeks from sowing. Cut whole heads at ground level when 15–20 cm tall and firm. Or pick outer leaves continuously from 4 weeks after sowing for cut-and-come-again use. Eat fresh — pak choi doesn't store well, soft within a week of picking."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'pak-choi';

-- From parsley.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow parsley seed direct from mid-March to August in successional batches every six weeks; or start indoors in modules from February. Germination is famously slow — 4–6 weeks isn''t unusual. Pouring boiling water down the drill before sowing speeds things up; so does soaking the seed overnight first. Parsley is hardy (RHS H4) and crops right through a UK winter from a sheltered bed. It runs to seed in its second year, so sow fresh each spring and treat as an annual. Flat-leaf (Italian) parsley has the better flavour and a more open habit; curly is harder-leaved and better at standing through winter cold. Worth having two or three patches in any kitchen garden.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Soak the seed (optional)", "text": "Soak parsley seed overnight in tepid water before sowing. Speeds germination from 4–6 weeks to 2–3."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct or in modules", "text": "Mid-March to August outdoors; February onwards indoors in modules. 1 cm deep drill, sow thinly, cover lightly. Pour boiling water down the drill before sowing — an old gardener's trick that genuinely speeds germination by softening the seed coat."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to 20 cm", "text": "When seedlings are 5 cm tall, thin to 20 cm apart. Don't transplant if you can avoid it — parsley dislikes root disturbance."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Keep weed-free and moist", "text": "Hand-weed regularly. Water in dry weather; parsley bolts in drought."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick outer stems", "text": "Pick outer stems at the base; the centre keeps producing. Don't strip the plant — leave 6–8 stems each time."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Protect for winter cropping", "text": "A cloche from October keeps parsley producing through to March in most of the UK."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Replace each year", "text": "In its second year parsley bolts; flowers, sets seed, dies. Sow fresh each spring. Self-sown seedlings from last year's plants are often the most vigorous."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why doesn't my parsley seed germinate?", "answer": "Parsley seed is famously slow — 4–6 weeks isn't unusual, especially in cold soil below 10°C. Soak seed overnight before sowing; pour boiling water down the drill at sowing time (the heat softens the seed coat and triggers germination); keep the bed warm and moist. Don't give up on a batch before 6 weeks — patience is the answer."},
    {"question": "Flat-leaf or curly parsley — which is better?", "answer": "Flat-leaf (also called Italian) has the better flavour, a more open habit that's easier to pick, and is preferred by most cooks. Curly has a stronger texture, holds up to longer cooking, and is hardier in cold weather. Grow flat-leaf for everyday picking; curly for winter cropping. Both have the same growing requirements."},
    {"question": "Is parsley an annual or biennial?", "answer": "Biennial — it grows leaves in year one, runs to seed in year two, then dies. Treat as an annual: sow fresh each spring. The flower stalks in year two have a stronger flavour but the leaves become coarse and bitter. Save seed from second-year plants if you want to grow next year's batch from your own stock."},
    {"question": "Can I grow parsley in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — a 25 cm pot grows enough parsley for daily picking. Use peat-free multi-purpose compost. Water consistently; parsley wilts in dry compost and gets bitter under stress. Place in sun or part-shade. One pot per household is usually plenty; for heavy use, two pots in succession."},
    {"question": "When can I pick parsley?", "answer": "8–10 weeks after sowing for the first picks, then continuously. Pick outer stems at the base of the plant; the centre produces new growth indefinitely. Don't strip a plant — leave 6–8 stems each time. Winter-protected plants give pickings from December onwards in southern England."},
    {"question": "My parsley has gone yellow and bolted — can I save it?", "answer": "No. Bolted parsley (sending up a flower stem) is going to seed and won't produce more useful leaves. The flowering shoots themselves are coarse and bitter. Pull the plant, compost the foliage, and re-sow if there's still growing time. Bolting in spring usually means the plant is in its second year; in summer, drought stress."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'parsley';

-- From parsnip.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow parsnip seed direct from March to May in deep, stone-free, well-prepared soil; germination is slow (3–5 weeks) and unreliable, so sow fresh seed each year and mark the row with quick-germinating radishes. Parsnips are very hardy (RHS H7) and improve in flavour after frost — the cold converts starches to sugars and turns a coarse autumn root into a sweet winter one. Lift from October through to March, taking them as needed; the crop stores far better in the ground than out of it. Choose Tender and True (heritage), Gladiator (canker-resistant F1), or White Gem (heavy cropper). Don''t add manure before sowing — it makes the roots fork. The classic UK winter roast vegetable.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Use fresh seed", "text": "Parsnip seed loses viability fast — anything older than a year germinates poorly. Buy fresh seed each year. Even fresh seed is slower and patchier than most."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare deep stone-free soil", "text": "Fork the bed deeply to remove stones and break up clods. Don't add fresh manure — it makes the roots fork and split. Compost dug in the previous autumn is fine."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "March to May. Make a 2 cm drill, sow 2–3 seeds per station every 15 cm, cover lightly. Sow radish seed between the parsnip stations as a marker crop — the radishes germinate in days and show you where the row is while you wait for the parsnips."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Be patient", "text": "Germination takes 3–5 weeks even at the right temperature. Don't give up on a sowing before 6 weeks. Keep the soil moist; dry conditions during germination kill seedlings."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Thin to one per station", "text": "Once seedlings show their first true leaves, thin to the strongest single seedling per station. Eat the radish thinnings."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Keep weed-free", "text": "Hand-weed; parsnips don't compete well. Mulch with grass clippings once seedlings are established."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Lift after first frost", "text": "From October onwards, but the sweetest roots come after the first frosts. Frost converts starches to sugars and transforms the flavour. Leave most of the crop in the ground — it stores there better than anywhere else — and lift a few at a time as needed."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why won't my parsnip seed germinate?", "answer": "Parsnip seed is famously unreliable — slow (3–5 weeks), patchy, and dependent on fresh seed. Old seed (more than a year past the harvest date) germinates very poorly. Buy fresh seed each year. Sow into warm, moist soil; cover lightly; keep watered. Don't give up before 6 weeks — late seedlings still emerge."},
    {"question": "Why are my parsnips forking?", "answer": "Stones in the soil, fresh manure dug in before sowing, or compacted ground. Each obstacle the growing root meets causes a fork. Dig the bed deeply before sowing; remove stones; don't add manure (use compost from the previous autumn instead). For heavy clay, sow short varieties (Avonresister, Lancer) or grow in deep tilth raised beds."},
    {"question": "When do parsnips taste best?", "answer": "After the first hard frost. Frost triggers the plant to convert stored starches to sugars as an antifreeze response, sweetening the roots dramatically. Pre-frost parsnips are coarser and less sweet. The traditional UK harvest is from late October onwards; the best of the crop is lifted in December and January."},
    {"question": "How do I store parsnips through winter?", "answer": "The bed they grow in is the best storage. Parsnips keep firm and improve in flavour in the ground from October until March. Lift as needed. If your soil freezes hard or floods, cover the row with a thick straw mulch before the worst weather — you can fork through frost-bound soil to reach them. Lifted roots store in damp sand for 2–3 months."},
    {"question": "What variety of parsnip is best?", "answer": "Tender and True is the heritage UK standard — long, slim, good flavour. Gladiator is a modern canker-resistant F1, more uniform. White Gem is reliable and heavy-cropping. For heavy clay, choose short varieties: Avonresister (canker-resistant short) or Lancer."},
    {"question": "Are parsnip leaves safe to handle?", "answer": "Mostly yes, but parsnip foliage contains furanocoumarins that can cause phytophotodermatitis (skin reaction with sunlight) in sensitive people on hot days. Wear long sleeves and gloves when thinning, weeding or topping the foliage in summer. Wash hands after handling. The roots themselves are entirely safe."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'parsnip';

-- From pea.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow pea seed direct from March to July in successional batches every three weeks, or start in modules under cloches from February. Peas are hardy (RHS H4–H5) and young plants tolerate light frost. Choose shelling peas (Hurst Greenshaft, Kelvedon Wonder) for traditional cropping, mangetout (Oregon Sugar Pod) for flat edible pods, or sugar snap (Sugar Ann) for sweet whole pods. Sow 2–3 seeds per station, 5 cm deep, in double rows; provide pea sticks or netting from 30 cm. Pigeons strip seedlings — net the row until the plants are 30 cm tall. Pea moth lays in flowers from June; net the flowering row or accept a few maggots in early pods. Pick young and often for the sweetest peas.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct or in modules", "text": "March to July outdoors; February to early March in modules under glass. Indoors gives an earlier crop and dodges pigeons. 2 cm deep, 5 cm apart in double rows 25 cm apart. Successional sowings every 3 weeks keep peas coming through summer."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Build the support before sowing", "text": "Pea sticks (twiggy hazel or birch), netting on canes, or a wigwam. Most modern shelling peas reach 60–90 cm; mangetout and tall varieties reach 1.5–2 m. Put supports in at sowing time, not afterwards."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Protect from pigeons", "text": "Net the row from sowing until plants are 30 cm tall. Pigeons strip pea seedlings in a single visit. Use bird netting on hoops, not fine fleece (the tendrils tangle in fine mesh)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water at flowering and pod-fill", "text": "Peas are reasonably drought-tolerant before flowering but thirsty once flowers and pods are developing. Water deeply twice a week from flowering onwards. Drought at flowering reduces pod count."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Mulch heavily", "text": "Once plants are 15 cm tall, mulch with grass clippings or straw 5 cm thick. Holds moisture, suppresses weeds, keeps the roots cool."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Watch for pea moth", "text": "Cydia nigricana lays eggs on flowers from June. Larvae develop inside maturing pods. Net the row with fine enviromesh during flowering (June for spring-sown, August for later sowings) to exclude the moth. Or accept some maggoty pods; only the earliest crops are heavily affected."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick young and often", "text": "Pick when pods are full but the peas inside are still bright green and tender. Mangetout pick when the pods are 5 cm and the peas inside are barely formed. Sugar snap when pods are full and crisp. Pick every 2–3 days during the peak; standing pods on the plant slows further flowering."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow peas in the UK?", "answer": "March to July outdoors; February to early March in modules under glass or in a cold frame. Earlier sowings give earlier crops but are slug- and pigeon-vulnerable. Round-seeded cultivars (Meteor, Feltham First) are hardier for very early sowings; wrinkle-seeded cultivars (Hurst Greenshaft, Kelvedon Wonder) are sweeter but want warmer soil."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between shelling peas, mangetout and sugar snap?", "answer": "Shelling peas (Hurst Greenshaft, Kelvedon Wonder): pick when the pods are bulging; shell out the peas, discard the pod. Classic garden pea. Mangetout (Oregon Sugar Pod, Carouby de Maussane): pick when the pods are flat and barely-developed peas inside; eat the whole pod. Sugar snap (Sugar Ann, Cascadia): pick when the pods are full and crisp; eat the whole pod, peas and all. Sugar snap is the sweetest fresh from the plant."},
    {"question": "How do I stop pigeons eating my pea seedlings?", "answer": "Net the row from sowing until plants are 30 cm tall. Bird netting on hoops works best; pigeons can't reach through 5 cm mesh. Don't use fine fleece — pea tendrils tangle in it and get torn off when you remove the netting. After 30 cm, the plants are big enough to handle modest peck damage."},
    {"question": "My peas have maggots in the pods — what's wrong?", "answer": "Pea moth (Cydia nigricana). Adults lay eggs on flowers in June and July; larvae develop inside pods. Net the flowering row with fine enviromesh, or grow early (March-sown) and late (July-sown) crops that flower outside the moth's main flight period. Pheromone traps from late May give an early warning."},
    {"question": "How long do pea plants crop for?", "answer": "3–4 weeks of picking from each sowing. That's why successional sowings every 3 weeks matter — they spread the harvest from June to September. After a plant has finished cropping, cut it off at the base and leave the roots in the soil (they fix nitrogen as they decompose, feeding the next crop)."},
    {"question": "Can I grow peas in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — dwarf cultivars (Half Pint, Tom Thumb) reach only 25 cm and crop happily in 30-litre containers without staking. Sugar snap varieties (Sugar Ann) in larger containers with a small wigwam also work. Water container peas daily in summer."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'pea';

-- From pear.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant pear trees bare-root from November to March, in a sheltered, sunny, frost-free spot — pears flower earlier than apples and are vulnerable to spring frosts, so position matters more than for any other fruit. Pears are very hardy (RHS H6) but the early blossom is not. The rootstock controls the eventual size: Quince C for cordons and small gardens (2–3 m), Quince A for half-standards in larger gardens (3–4 m), pear seedling rootstock for full-size trees. Most pears need a pollination partner from the same flowering group; Conference is the UK reliable choice — partially self-fertile and crops in shaded gardens where most pears struggle. Pick pears unripe and firm in September, ripen indoors over 2–3 weeks; tree-ripened pears go mealy and brown at the core. Pear leaf blister mite causes black blisters but rarely harms the crop.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick a rootstock for your space", "text": "Quince C (most dwarfing): 2–3 m tree, suitable for cordons, espaliers, fans, and small gardens. Needs permanent staking. Quince A (semi-vigorous): 3–4 m bush tree, the standard garden pear. Pear seedling (vigorous): 5–8 m full-size tree, only for orchards and large gardens. Most garden centres sell on Quince A; specialist nurseries (Blackmoor, Bernwode, Keepers) offer the full range."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose Conference if you only plant one", "text": "Conference is the UK's safest pear — partially self-fertile, reliable cropper, takes light shade, stores 1–2 months, classic flavour. Crops well as a single tree (heavier with a pollination partner). Other reliable UK varieties: Doyenne du Comice (peak flavour, demanding), Williams' Bon Chretien (early, peeling pear), Beth (compact, very early). Beware: most pears are not self-fertile — you'll need 2–3 trees of different varieties in the same flowering group."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in a sheltered, sunny, frost-free spot", "text": "Position matters more for pears than any other fruit. Pears flower in mid-April — 2–3 weeks earlier than apples — and the blossom is vulnerable to late frosts that wipe out the crop. South or west-facing wall positions are ideal; avoid frost pockets (low-lying spots, hollows). Spacing: 4 m for bush trees on Quince A, 2 m for cordons on Quince C, 8 m for espaliers."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March while dormant. Soak the roots for an hour before planting. Plant at the same depth the tree was in the nursery — graft union (the bulge) should be 7–10 cm above soil. Stake permanently for Quince C rootstocks (they don't anchor well). Water in deeply."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Mulch and avoid grass at the base", "text": "A 5 cm mulch of garden compost or well-rotted manure in a 1 m circle around the trunk (keeping it 5 cm clear of the bark itself). Don't let grass grow within this circle in the first 3–5 years — grass roots compete heavily with young pears. After 5 years, lawn can grow back if you want."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Prune in winter for shape, summer for cordons", "text": "Bush trees: late winter (Feb–early Mar) — remove crossing branches, open the centre to a wine glass shape, shorten leaders by a third. Cordons, espaliers, fans: summer prune (August) — cut sideshoots back to 3 leaves above the basal cluster. Pears tolerate hard pruning if needed."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick at the right (early) moment", "text": "The most important pear husbandry rule. Pears do NOT ripen well on the tree — they go mealy and brown at the core. Pick when the fruit is full size, still firm, but the stalk lifts cleanly from the spur with a gentle twist (early-mid September for Williams, September for Conference, late September for Doyenne du Comice). Lay out on a tray in a cool dark place; they ripen over 2–3 weeks."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for pear leaf blister mite and scab", "text": "Pear leaf blister mite: small dark blisters on leaves, looks alarming but rarely affects fruit yield — pick affected leaves off and bin (don't compost). Pear scab: brown/black corky spots on leaves and fruit — choose resistant varieties (Concorde, Beth), prune for airflow, clear fallen leaves in autumn. Codling moth can affect pears as well as apples — pheromone traps in June or grease bands."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Do I need two pear trees for pollination?", "answer": "Most pears need a partner — Conference is the partial exception. Pears are grouped into flowering periods 1–4 and need a pollinator from the same or an adjacent group. Conference is partially self-fertile and crops decently as a single tree (more heavily with a partner). Other reliable solo plants: Williams (some self-fertility). For best crops, plant two compatible varieties (e.g. Conference + Williams + Comice = a trio that pollinates each other). Most garden centres label compatibility groups; ask if not."},
    {"question": "When do I pick pears?", "answer": "Earlier than you think. Pick when the fruit is full-size, still firm, but the stalk lifts cleanly with a gentle twist of the fruit. This is typically late August for early varieties (Williams), September for mid-season (Conference), late September for late (Doyenne du Comice). Don't wait until the pear feels soft on the tree — by then it's gone mealy and brown at the core. Pears ripen indoors over 2–3 weeks on a tray. Test for eating ripeness by gentle pressure near the stalk — it should yield slightly."},
    {"question": "Why has my pear tree never cropped?", "answer": "Three usual causes. (1) Frost on blossom: pears flower early (April) and a late frost kills the crop. Plant in a sheltered south or west-facing position, away from frost pockets. (2) No pollination partner: most pears need a partner from the same flowering group; Conference is the partial exception. Identify your variety and check pollination requirements. (3) Too young or pruned too hard: pears on Quince A take 3–4 years to start cropping; hard winter pruning reduces fruit buds (prune lightly for fruit, hard only for shape)."},
    {"question": "What's the best pear variety for the UK?", "answer": "Conference — the safest, most reliable choice. Partially self-fertile, tolerates light shade and cooler gardens, stores 1–2 months, distinctive elongated shape, classic pear flavour. Doyenne du Comice has the best flavour but needs warmth and a partner. Williams' Bon Chretien is the early melting pear (peels cleanly, the supermarket pear). Beth is the compact early variety for small gardens. Avoid old varieties unless you have a real interest in heritage fruit."},
    {"question": "What's wrong with my pear leaves — small dark blisters?", "answer": "Pear leaf blister mite (Eriophyes pyri) — small mites that cause raised pinkish-green blisters on leaves, turning black through summer. Looks alarming but rarely affects fruit yield. Pick affected leaves off in spring and bin (don't compost — eggs overwinter). For severe cases, a winter wash with horticultural oil reduces overwintering mites. Don't panic — this is a cosmetic issue, not a crisis."},
    {"question": "Can I grow a pear tree in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — on Quince C rootstock only. A 50 cm pot of peat-free compost, in a sunny sheltered spot. Pear trees in pots need: (1) permanent staking, (2) regular feeding (slow-release fertiliser in April + liquid feed monthly), (3) consistent watering (drying out causes fruit drop). Repot every 3–4 years in winter, replacing the outer compost. A pot pear gives a smaller crop than in-ground but works for cordon or stepover-trained trees on a patio."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'pear';

-- From peony.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant peony crowns from October to March, bare-root, in a sunny spot with deep fertile soil. Peonies are very hardy (RHS H6–H7) and live for 50+ years if planted right — but they need one very specific thing: the dormant buds (eyes) on the crown must sit no more than 3 cm below the soil surface. Bury them deeper and the plant gives lush foliage every spring but no flowers, ever. Once planted, peonies dislike being moved; site them carefully the first time. Flowering happens in May or June for two weeks of glory; herbaceous types die back to nothing each winter. Sarah Bernhardt (classic pink), Bowl of Beauty (anemone-form pink), Karl Rosenfield (deep red), Coral Charm (coral fading to cream). Ants on buds are normal and harmless.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy crowns from a specialist nursery", "text": "October to March is the planting season. Bare-root crowns establish far better than pot-grown — they're cheaper, give bigger plants, and arrive when the soil's ready. Buy from a peony specialist; the variety choice is much wider than at general garden centres."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose a sunny well-drained spot", "text": "Full sun ideal (6+ hours direct); tolerates light shade for part of the day. Sharp drainage essential. Once planted, peonies stay put for decades — site them carefully. Don't plant where roses, peonies or other deep-rooted shrubs have grown before; the soil may be exhausted."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Dig deep and prepare well", "text": "Fork in well-rotted manure or compost a spade deep. Peonies are heavy feeders that stay in the same spot for 50+ years — make the bed properly. Mix in grit if your soil is heavy."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant SHALLOWLY", "text": "The single most important step. The eyes (dormant buds on the crown) must be no more than 3 cm below the soil surface. Deeper and the plant won't flower, ever — it'll give lush foliage every spring and no buds. Use a stick across the hole to measure depth. Cover with soil to just above the eyes."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water in and mulch", "text": "Water heavily on planting; mulch around (not on) the crown with 5 cm of compost. Keep mulch 5 cm clear of the eyes; smothering them slows or prevents flowering."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Don't move once planted", "text": "Peonies sulk badly when moved — typically skipping flowers for 2–3 years before settling in again. Site right the first time. If you absolutely must move one, do it in October, lift the entire root ball intact, and replant at the same depth in fresh prepared ground."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Feed in March", "text": "A light balanced feed in March each year. Don't over-feed — rich nitrogen pushes leaves and floppy stems at the expense of flowers."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Stake heavy flowers", "text": "Double cultivars (Sarah Bernhardt, Karl Rosenfield) carry enormous heads that topple in spring rain. Put in ring stakes (grow-through grids) in early April before the stems get tall and heavy. Single-flowered peonies don't usually need staking."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Deadhead after flowering", "text": "Cut spent flowers back to the next leaf joint. Don't cut the foliage — it continues feeding the crown for next year's flowers through summer. Cut foliage right back to ground level in November after the leaves have yellowed."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why doesn't my peony flower?", "answer": "Almost always planting depth. The dormant buds (eyes) on the crown must be no more than 3 cm below the soil surface. Bury them deeper and you get lush foliage every spring and no flowers, year after year. Lift the plant in October, replant at the correct depth, and flowers usually return within 2 seasons. Other less common causes: too much shade, fresh manure added recently (high nitrogen), or simply a young plant still settling in."},
    {"question": "When should I plant peony crowns?", "answer": "October to March, bare-root. October–November is ideal — the crown gets winter to establish roots before spring growth. February–March is acceptable. Avoid summer planting; the plant is in full growth and resents disturbance."},
    {"question": "Can I move an established peony?", "answer": "Yes, but it'll sulk for 2–3 years. Best done in October. Cut foliage back; carefully lift the entire root ball intact (peony roots are brittle); replant immediately at the same depth (eyes no more than 3 cm below surface). Expect no flowers in the first year after moving; possibly the second. Don't move unless you have to."},
    {"question": "Are the ants on peony buds harming the plant?", "answer": "Not at all. Ants are attracted to sugary nectar secreted by the developing buds — they're feeding, not damaging. Some old gardening folklore suggested ants were necessary for the buds to open; that's not true (buds open fine without them). They simply ride along. Wash bouquet stems before bringing them indoors if you don't want ants in the house."},
    {"question": "How long do peonies live?", "answer": "50+ years routinely; some 100+ year-old plants are still cropping. Peonies are among the longest-lived garden perennials. Crowns inherited from grandparents' gardens often outlast multiple owners. They benefit from being left undisturbed — moving and dividing reduce their longevity. A well-sited peony is a generational plant."},
    {"question": "When do peonies flower in the UK?", "answer": "Herbaceous peonies (P. lactiflora) flower in May and June for about two weeks. Earlier cultivars (Buckeye Belle, Karl Rosenfield) from mid-May; later cultivars (Sarah Bernhardt, Bowl of Beauty) into June. Tree peonies (P. delavayi, P. suffruticosa) flower earlier — late April to mid-May. Itoh hybrids (herbaceous × tree crosses) flower late May–June with a longer season."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'peony';

-- From plum.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant plum trees bare-root from November to March, in a sheltered sunny spot in well-drained soil — plums flower very early (March–April), so a frost-protected position is essential or the crop is lost. Plums are hardy (RHS H6) but the early blossom is not. Victoria is the UK''s reliable choice — self-fertile, hugely productive, classic flavour, and crops in cooler gardens where other plums struggle. Never prune a plum in winter — pruning when dormant invites silver leaf disease (a fungus that enters open wounds and kills branches); prune in June–August when wounds heal fast. Rootstock controls size: Pixy for small trees and patio culture, St Julien A for half-standards (the UK standard), Brompton for full-size orchard trees. Watch for plum sawfly (worms in young fruit), plum moth, and brown rot in damp summers.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Plant Victoria if you only plant one", "text": "Victoria is the UK's safest plum — self-fertile, heavy cropping, classic dessert-and-cooking flavour, tolerates cooler gardens. Crops without a pollination partner. Other reliable self-fertile UK varieties: Czar (cooking, very hardy, the choice for colder gardens), Marjorie's Seedling (late, dessert), Opal (early, dessert). Non-self-fertile varieties (like Coe's Golden Drop) need a pollination partner from the same group — buy by name and check compatibility."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pick a rootstock for your space", "text": "Pixy (most dwarfing): 2–3 m tree, suitable for patio pots, fans, and small gardens. Needs permanent staking. St Julien A (semi-vigorous): 3.5–4.5 m bush — the standard UK garden plum. Brompton (vigorous): 5–7 m full-size tree, only for orchards and large gardens. Most garden centres sell on St Julien A; specialist nurseries (Blackmoor, Bernwode, Keepers) carry the full range."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in a sheltered, sunny, frost-free spot", "text": "Position matters as much as variety with plums. Plums flower in late March–April — earlier than apples or pears — and the blossom is vulnerable to frost. South or west-facing wall positions are ideal; avoid frost pockets and exposed hill-tops. Plums tolerate heavier soils than most fruit, including some clay, provided they don't waterlog."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March while dormant. Soak the roots for an hour. Plant at the same depth the tree was in the nursery — graft union (the bulge) should be 7–10 cm above soil. Stake permanently for Pixy rootstocks. Water in deeply."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "DON'T prune in winter — prune in June–August", "text": "The single most important plum rule. Pruning when dormant exposes the tree to silver leaf disease (Chondrostereum purpureum), a fungus that enters fresh wounds and gradually kills branches. The spores are airborne in winter and absent in summer. Prune in June, July, or early August when wounds heal fast. Light annual pruning is much better than occasional hard pruning. Remove dead, diseased, crossing branches; thin overcrowded growth; shorten leaders by a third if needed."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Thin heavy crops", "text": "A loaded plum branch breaks easily. In June, thin the developing fruit so plums are spaced 5–7 cm apart along each branch. Heavy crops also exhaust the tree and trigger biennial cropping (heavy one year, almost none the next). Thinning gives bigger fruit, healthier trees, and reliable annual crops."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Net or pick at the right moment", "text": "Wasps, birds, and squirrels all attack ripening plums. Net mature trees once fruit starts colouring (mid–late July). Pick when the fruit yields slightly to gentle pressure and pulls away easily from the spur — usually mid-August for Victoria. Pick over the tree every 2–3 days through the ripening period; plums ripen unevenly."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for plum sawfly, plum moth, and brown rot", "text": "Plum sawfly: small grubs inside young fruit (June–July) — affected plums drop early. Hang yellow sticky traps in flowering trees, or apply pheromone moth traps. Plum moth: caterpillars in ripening fruit (August). Pheromone traps from June onwards. Brown rot: fluffy white patches on damaged fruit — pick off and bin (don't compost); prune for airflow. Damp summers are worst for all three."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I prune a plum tree?", "answer": "June, July, or early August — NEVER in winter. Pruning in dormant season exposes the tree to silver leaf disease (Chondrostereum purpureum), a fungus carried by airborne spores in winter that enters fresh wounds and gradually kills branches. Summer wounds heal quickly and resist silver leaf because the spores aren't around. Light annual pruning in summer is much better than occasional hard winter pruning. Remove dead/diseased/crossing branches first, then thin to improve airflow."},
    {"question": "Do I need two plum trees for pollination?", "answer": "No, if you choose a self-fertile variety. Victoria, Czar, Opal, Marjorie's Seedling all crop without a partner. Self-fertile plums make up most of the popular UK varieties and are the right choice for a single-tree garden. If your plum is not self-fertile (e.g. Coe's Golden Drop, Cambridge Gage), you need a partner from the same flowering group — check at purchase or by variety name."},
    {"question": "Why has my plum tree never cropped — or only some years?", "answer": "Three common causes. (1) Frost on blossom: plums flower very early (March–April) and a late frost kills the crop. South or west-facing sheltered positions help. (2) Biennial bearing: an over-cropping year exhausts the tree and the next year gives almost nothing. Thin heavy crops in June (5–7 cm fruit spacing) to break the cycle. (3) Too young: plums on St Julien A take 4–5 years to crop properly; on Pixy often 3 years; on Brompton 5–7 years."},
    {"question": "What are the small dimples and grubs in my unripe plums?", "answer": "Plum sawfly damage. Sawfly larvae enter developing fruit in June, leaving a small dark dimple; the fruit usually drops to the ground while still small. Damaged fruit shouldn't be eaten (the grubs are inside). Two controls. (1) Pheromone traps for plum moth (and sticky traps for sawfly) hung in trees from late May. (2) Hygiene: pick up and bin all dropped fruit through June–August — the larvae overwinter in the soil and re-emerge next year."},
    {"question": "What's the best plum variety for the UK?", "answer": "Victoria — the safest, most productive, classic UK plum. Self-fertile, heavy cropper, dessert-and-cooking flavour, available everywhere. For colder gardens (Highlands, exposed Midlands): Czar (cooking, very hardy and reliable). For dessert plums: Opal (early), Marjorie's Seedling (late). For gages (sweeter, more aromatic plums): Cambridge Gage (needs a partner) or Oullins Gage (self-fertile)."},
    {"question": "Can I grow a plum tree in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — on Pixy rootstock only. A 50 cm pot of peat-free compost, in a sheltered sunny spot. Plum trees in pots need: (1) permanent staking, (2) slow-release fertiliser in April plus monthly liquid feed, (3) consistent watering (drying out causes fruit drop and disease), (4) summer pruning to keep the size manageable. Repot every 3 years in winter, replacing outer compost. Pot plums crop reliably though smaller — useful for patio fruit."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'plum';

-- From potato.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant seed potatoes from mid-March in southern England, mid-April in northern Scotland — earlier under fleece in mild south-coast gardens. Chit your tubers from late January by standing them in a cool light place to sprout. First earlies crop from late June; main-crops from August to October. Potatoes are frost-tender on the foliage (RHS H1c) so emerging shoots need earthing up or fleecing if a late frost is forecast. They want deep loose soil — fork it over and rake in compost before planting. Earlies in pots and bags suit small gardens; main-crops want a proper bed. Blight is the constant threat from July onwards — grow Sarpo varieties outdoors if you can''t get under cover.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Chit your seed potatoes", "text": "Chit from late January to February. Stand seed tubers in egg boxes or trays in a cool, light, frost-free place. Shoots should be 2 cm long and dark green when you plant."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the soil", "text": "Fork over a sunny bed in late winter and rake in well-rotted compost. Potatoes want deep, loose, weed-free soil. Don't add lime — they prefer slightly acidic conditions."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant", "text": "First earlies from mid-March in the south, late March in the Midlands, early April in the north. Set tubers 30 cm apart, 12 cm deep, rows 60 cm apart for earlies; 75 cm apart for main-crop. Shoots facing up."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Earth up", "text": "When shoots reach 20 cm, draw soil up the stems with a hoe so only the top leaves show. Repeat every fortnight until the foliage closes the row. Earthing up gives more tubers and stops them turning green."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water", "text": "Water deeply once flowering begins. A dry July gives a smaller crop and increases scab. Mulch around the base after the last earthing-up to hold moisture."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Lift", "text": "Earlies are ready about 12 weeks after planting, when the plants flower. Main-crops are ready when foliage yellows and dies down, usually August to October. Lift gently with a fork from the side of the row."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for blight", "text": "Foliage browning fast in warm humid weather means blight. Cut the haulm (stems) at ground level immediately to stop the disease reaching the tubers. Leave tubers in the ground for two weeks for spores to die off, then lift. Sarpo varieties are blight-resistant."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I plant seed potatoes in the UK?", "answer": "First earlies in mid-March in southern England, late March in the Midlands, early April in northern England and Scotland. Second earlies a fortnight later. Main-crop varieties from late April to mid-May. Earlier in mild south-coast gardens with fleece protection — but emerging shoots are frost-tender, so don't rush. If a frost is forecast after shoots emerge, draw soil over them or cover with fleece overnight."},
    {"question": "Do I need to chit potatoes?", "answer": "Chitting (sprouting) speeds up early varieties by 2–3 weeks and gives a slightly bigger crop. Stand seed tubers in a cool, light, frost-free place in egg boxes from late January. They're ready when shoots are 2 cm long, stocky and dark green. Main-crop varieties don't strictly need chitting — they have time to catch up — but you can chit them too. Don't use shop-bought eating potatoes; they may carry diseases and produce unpredictable crops."},
    {"question": "How deep should I plant potatoes?", "answer": "12 cm deep with 30 cm between tubers for earlies, 38 cm for main-crops. Rows 60 cm apart for earlies, 75 cm apart for main-crops. Make a trench with a draw hoe or spade; set tubers in with shoots facing up; cover with soil. Depth matters because the new tubers form on stems above the seed potato — too shallow and they turn green in light; too deep and they take longer to emerge."},
    {"question": "How do I stop potato blight?", "answer": "You can't stop it in a wet UK summer, but you can choose blight-resistant cultivars (Sarpo Mira, Sarpo Axona, Lady Balfour). Space plants well, water at the base, never the foliage. From July, check for brown patches on leaves daily. When blight strikes, cut all haulm at ground level immediately and burn it — don't compost. Leave tubers in the ground for two weeks so spores die off, then lift."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between first earlies, second earlies and main-crop?", "answer": "Days to maturity and how they store. First earlies (Charlotte, Pentland Javelin) crop in 10–12 weeks from planting — small, waxy, eat fresh. Second earlies (Maris Peer, Charlotte) take 13–14 weeks. Main-crops (Maris Piper, King Edward, Cara) take 18–20 weeks, give the heaviest crops, and store through winter. For a small garden, earlies in pots and bags give the best value; for a proper crop you want main-crops in the ground."},
    {"question": "Can I grow potatoes in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — first and second earlies do well in 40-litre potato bags or large containers. Fill the bottom third with multipurpose compost, plant 2–3 chitted tubers, cover with 10 cm compost, then top up as shoots grow until the bag is full. Water consistently — containers dry out fast. Crop is ready when the foliage yellows or the plant flowers, depending on variety."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'potato';

-- From primrose.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant primrose in autumn or early spring, in part shade in moist humus-rich soil — it''s a UK native woodland perennial that produces pale yellow saucer-shaped flowers from February through April, often before any leaves are out on the trees above. The wild primrose (Primula vulgaris) is one of the most cherished UK native flowers and a protected species in the wild (don''t dig from wild colonies — buy from nurseries). Hardy (RHS H6–H7) and long-lived where conditions suit; happy in damp meadows, woodland edges, banks, and stream-sides. Divide every 3–4 years after flowering to maintain vigour. Don''t confuse with primula hybrids — the gaudy bedding "primulas" sold in supermarkets are short-lived hybrids; true wild primrose is a long-lived garden classic. Self-seeds modestly into established colonies.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick wild primrose, not bedding primulas", "text": "Wild primrose (Primula vulgaris): UK native, pale yellow flowers, 15 cm tall, long-lived perennial, the classic woodland-edge plant. The right choice for naturalistic UK gardens. Garden hybrids (Polyanthus, F1 bedding primulas): brightly-coloured bedding plants sold by supermarkets in spring. Showy but short-lived (often only one season), prone to vine weevil. Not suitable for naturalising. Wanda primrose: heritage cultivar, deep red-purple flowers, very vigorous, longer-lived than F1 hybrids. Lady Greer: pale yellow miniature, refined. For a first primrose: wild P. vulgaris from a wildflower nursery (Naturescape, BBC Wild Flower Shop)."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in part shade in moist soil", "text": "Wild primroses are woodland-edge plants. Position under deciduous trees, alongside north-facing walls, on damp banks, in meadow plantings. Avoid full sun and dry soil — primroses sulk and die in those conditions. Moisture-retentive humus-rich soil with leaf-mould worked in. Damp meadow plantings work brilliantly — primroses naturalise into wildflower meadow grass."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in autumn or early spring", "text": "Best windows: autumn (September–October) for establishment before flowering, OR early spring (February–March) for plants flowering the same year. Don't plant in summer drought — establishment is poor. Container-grown wild primrose plants are sold by wildflower nurseries and some independent garden centres."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't dig from the wild", "text": "UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 prohibits digging up wild primroses (and any wild plant) without the landowner's permission. Even where it's legal, it's bad practice — wild primrose colonies are slow to recover from digging. Buy plants from a reputable wildflower nursery instead. Seed-grown plants are also possible (sow seed fresh on damp compost in autumn) but slow — 2 years to flowering."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Mulch annually with leaf-mould", "text": "Primroses thrive on leaf-mould. Apply a 3–5 cm mulch of leaf-mould or fine garden compost around (not on) the crowns each autumn. In wild meadow plantings: simply let leaf litter from neighbouring trees accumulate naturally — primroses thrive on this. No high-nitrogen fertiliser — encourages soft growth that flowers poorly."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Divide every 3–4 years after flowering", "text": "The single most important husbandry rule for long-term primroses. Wild primrose clumps gradually become congested and decline; division every 3–4 years maintains vigour. Timing: immediately after flowering in May or early June. Method: lift the whole clump, gently tease apart into 3–6 sections (each with leaves and roots), replant the best outer pieces in fresh leaf-mould-enriched soil. Water in deeply. Many UK gardeners divide annually for the most vigorous display."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Let some plants set seed for naturalising", "text": "Wild primrose self-seeds gently into established colonies. Don't deadhead if you want naturalising; let some plants set seed and shatter on the ground. Seedlings appear the following spring around mature plants. Manage the colony: thin overcrowded seedlings, transplant desired ones to fill gaps (young primroses move well, mature ones don't). A managed primrose colony can carpet a damp shady corner over 5–10 years."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for vine weevil", "text": "The main UK primrose pest — small grey-brown beetles whose larvae eat the roots, often killing the plant from below. Symptoms: plant collapses despite damp soil; lifted plant has eaten roots. Prevention: nematodes (Steinernema kraussei) watered into the soil in autumn — biological control. Pot-grown primroses are particularly vulnerable; treat new container plants with nematodes on arrival. Garden-grown wild primroses generally suffer less than potted hybrids."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Can I dig wild primroses from the countryside?", "answer": "No — it's illegal. The UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 prohibits digging up wild plants (including primroses) without the landowner's permission. Beyond legality: wild primrose colonies are slow to recover from disturbance, and once-common UK primrose populations have declined significantly. Buy plants from a reputable wildflower nursery (Naturescape, BBC Wild Flower Shop, Plantlife) instead. For free establishment: take a small division from a friend's well-established garden colony."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between primrose and primula?", "answer": "Same genus, but the names work differently in UK gardening. Primrose (Primula vulgaris): the UK native, pale yellow, long-lived perennial, the woodland classic. Primula is the genus — covers many species including the wild primrose, cowslip (P. veris), and oxlip (P. elatior). Primulas in garden-centre speak usually means bedding hybrids and Polyanthus — colourful spring annuals/short-lived perennials, not the same as wild primrose. For naturalistic UK gardens, plant wild primrose; for bright spring bedding, the hybrids work for one season."},
    {"question": "When do I divide primroses?", "answer": "Immediately after flowering in late May or early June. Lift the whole clump, gently tease apart into 3–6 sections (each with leaves and roots), replant the best outer pieces in fresh leaf-mould-enriched soil, water in deeply. Divide every 3–4 years to maintain vigour. Many UK gardeners divide annually for the most vigorous display. Don't divide in autumn or winter — the disturbed plants may not establish before the cold."},
    {"question": "Why are my primroses dying?", "answer": "Three common causes. (1) Too dry: primroses are damp-meadow and woodland-edge plants. Move to a moister position; mulch annually with leaf-mould. (2) Vine weevil: larvae eat the roots underground. Apply nematodes in autumn. (3) Wrong plant: garden-centre primula hybrids are often short-lived (one season only) — not the same as wild primrose. Buy wild Primula vulgaris from a wildflower nursery for plants that last."},
    {"question": "Where do primroses grow best?", "answer": "Damp shaded woodland edges, north-facing banks, alongside streams — the conditions of their wild habitat. Best UK positions: under deciduous trees (spring sunlight before canopy closes), in damp meadows, on north-facing slopes. Soil: humus-rich, moisture-retentive, slightly acid to neutral. Avoid: full sun, dry chalk soils, waterlogged ground. Primroses naturalise spectacularly in suitable conditions, gradually carpeting an area over 5–10 years."},
    {"question": "Are primrose leaves and flowers edible?", "answer": "Yes — both are traditionally edible in small quantities. Flowers: a classic UK edible garnish — scatter on salads, crystallise with caster sugar for cake decoration, use in spring nettle soup. Leaves: edible but tougher — old country use was to add young leaves to spring salads or boil briefly as a wild green. Caution: a small number of people develop contact dermatitis from primula sap; wear gloves when handling and don't eat large quantities. The flowers are far more rewarding than the leaves."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'primrose';

-- From pulmonaria.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant pulmonaria in autumn or spring, in part to full shade in moisture-retentive soil — they''re hardy perennials grown for the distinctive silver-spotted oval leaves and small bell-shaped flowers that open pink and age to blue (sometimes both colours on the same stem). Pulmonaria is hardy (RHS H6–H7) and one of the earliest flowering UK perennials, blooming from late February through April when little else is yet awake. The flowers are an important early nectar source for emerging queen bumblebees. Cut the whole plant down to 5 cm after flowering for a fresh flush of clean foliage — old summer leaves get mildewed and tatty. Sissinghurst White (pure white flowers, heavily silver-spotted leaves) is the UK garden classic. Spreads gently by underground rhizomes; divide every 3–4 years.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick a pulmonaria for flowers or foliage", "text": "Sissinghurst White: pure white flowers, heavily silver-spotted leaves — the white-garden classic. Blue Ensign: deep blue flowers without pink phase, plain green leaves — striking flower contrast. Diana Clare: blue-violet flowers, almost entirely silver leaves — foliage star. Trevi Fountain: cobalt blue flowers, silver-spotted leaves — vigorous, mildew-resistant. Opal: pale pink-fading-to-blue flowers, silver-marked leaves — classic cottage-garden form. For a first pulmonaria: Sissinghurst White or Trevi Fountain — both reliable, mildew-resistant."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in part to full shade", "text": "Pulmonarias are woodland-edge plants. Position under deciduous trees, in north-facing borders, alongside shaded paths. Full sun is bad — leaves scorch, plants sulk, mildew is much more common. Moisture-retentive soil is essential — drought-stressed pulmonaria leaves go crispy and brown."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in autumn or spring", "text": "Best time: autumn (September–October) for establishing roots before winter, OR spring (March–April) for fast growth into the flowering season. Container-grown plants widely sold by garden centres and herbaceous-perennial nurseries. Spacing: 40 cm apart — pulmonaria fills out into 50 cm ground-covering mats within 2 years."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Mulch annually with leaf-mould", "text": "Pulmonaria thrives on leaf-mould or garden compost worked into the soil at planting and applied as an annual mulch. In autumn or spring, apply 3–5 cm around (not on) the crowns. Don't feed with high-nitrogen fertiliser — encourages soft growth that mildews more readily. Steady organic feeding from leaf-mould is the right approach."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Watch the flower colour change", "text": "The classic pulmonaria magic: many varieties have flowers that open pink and age to blue as the petals mature, with both colours often visible on the same flower stem. This is a normal pH-driven colour change in the petal anthocyanins. Not all varieties show the colour shift: Sissinghurst White stays white, Blue Ensign stays blue. Bee preference: bees visit younger pink flowers (when they have nectar) and ignore the older blue ones (which have been drained)."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut whole plant down after flowering", "text": "The single most important husbandry rule. When flowers finish in late April or early May, cut the whole plant down to 5 cm above the ground. Three reasons: (1) tidies the spent flowers; (2) triggers a fresh flush of clean spotted foliage for summer display; (3) removes the older leaves that will inevitably develop powdery mildew through summer. Water deeply after the chop to support regrowth."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for powdery mildew", "text": "Pulmonaria's main UK disease. White powdery patches on leaves, often appearing late summer when conditions are dry. Prevention: (1) cut the whole plant down after flowering — fresh regrowth resists mildew better than the original spring leaves; (2) water deeply in dry spells (drought stress increases mildew); (3) choose mildew-resistant cultivars (Trevi Fountain, Raspberry Splash, Excalibur); (4) avoid overhead watering (water at the base). Mildewed plants can be cut down again in late summer for a third flush."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Divide every 3–4 years", "text": "Pulmonaria clumps spread gently by underground rhizomes and gradually become congested. Every 3–4 years, in early autumn or early spring, lift the clump, split with a sharp spade into 3–6 sections, replant the best outer pieces in fresh leaf-mould-enriched soil. Compost the dead congested centre. Free new plants for filling gaps or sharing."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I cut back pulmonaria?", "answer": "After flowering in late April or May — cut the whole plant down to 5 cm above the ground. Three reasons: (1) tidies the spent flowers; (2) triggers a fresh flush of clean spotted foliage for summer display; (3) removes the older leaves that will inevitably develop powdery mildew. Water deeply after the cut. Late summer mildew: if the regrowth gets mildewed, cut again — pulmonaria recovers from multiple hard cuts in a season."},
    {"question": "Why do pulmonaria flowers change colour from pink to blue?", "answer": "Natural pH change in the petal anthocyanins as the flower ages. Newly-opened flowers are typically pink (more acidic petal sap); older flowers turn blue (more alkaline as sap matures). Both colours often appear on the same stem at different stages — the classic two-tone effect. Bee preference: bees visit the younger pink flowers (where nectar is) and ignore the older blue ones (effectively visual signalling for pollinators)."},
    {"question": "What's the best pulmonaria for the UK?", "answer": "For white-themed gardens: Sissinghurst White — classic, vigorous, heavily silver-spotted leaves, pure white flowers. For mildew-resistance: Trevi Fountain — cobalt blue flowers, vigorous, much less prone to mildew than older cultivars. For silver foliage: Diana Clare — almost entirely silver leaves, dramatic. For pure blue: Blue Ensign — no pink phase, deep blue throughout. For a first pulmonaria, Trevi Fountain for reliability."},
    {"question": "Why are my pulmonaria leaves covered in white powder?", "answer": "Powdery mildew — the main UK pulmonaria disease. Three causes: drought stress, full sun, age of foliage. Treatment: cut the whole plant down to 5 cm — fresh regrowth is usually mildew-free. Prevention going forward: (1) move to a shadier position if currently too sunny; (2) mulch annually with leaf-mould and water deeply in dry spells; (3) replace with mildew-resistant cultivars (Trevi Fountain, Raspberry Splash, Excalibur) if persistent."},
    {"question": "Will pulmonaria grow in deep shade?", "answer": "Yes — one of the few flowering perennials that genuinely flourishes in deep shade. Pulmonaria thrives in north-facing borders, under deciduous trees, alongside shaded paths. Best display under deciduous trees where the plant gets spring sunlight before the canopy closes. In full shade: pulmonaria still flowers well; the silver leaf-spots are particularly striking against dark shade backgrounds."},
    {"question": "Is pulmonaria good for wildlife?", "answer": "Outstanding for early-emerging bumblebees. Pulmonaria is one of the first UK garden plants to flower (February–April) — exactly when queen bumblebees emerge from hibernation and desperately need nectar to start new colonies. A pulmonaria patch in late February attracts buff-tailed bumblebee queens, garden bumblebee queens, and a few honeybees on warmer days. The early nectar is critical for bumblebee colony survival; pulmonaria earns its garden place for this alone."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'pulmonaria';

-- From pumpkin.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow pumpkin seed indoors from late April; plant out after the last frost (mid-May in southern England, early June in the north). Pumpkins are frost-tender annuals (RHS H1c) and need a sunny sheltered spot in rich moist soil with plenty of room — full-size cultivars sprawl 2 m or more in every direction. Plant 1.5 m apart for standard varieties, 1 m for compact (Baby Bear, Jack Be Little). Feed weekly with high-potash liquid once fruit sets. Pick when the stem is corky and the shell sounds hollow when tapped — usually mid-October. Cure in the sun or under glass for 2 weeks to harden the skin for storage. Crown Prince and Cinderella are the eating favourites; Atlantic Giant for show; Jack-O-Lantern for Halloween carving.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow seed indoors", "text": "Late April to early May. Set one seed on its side in a 9 cm pot of multi-purpose compost. 18–22°C on a windowsill or in a propagator. Germination in 5–7 days. Pumpkin seedlings grow fast — don't sow too early."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare a rich sunny bed", "text": "Full sun, sheltered from wind, rich moisture-retentive soil. Fork in two barrowloads of well-rotted manure or compost per planting station. Pumpkins are exceptionally hungry — poor soil gives small fruit."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Harden off and plant out", "text": "After the last frost. 7–10 days of hardening off. Plant on a slight mound (helps drainage); space 1.5 m apart for standard cultivars, 1 m for compact bush types."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water deeply", "text": "Water deeply twice a week through summer; pumpkins are thirsty. Mulch heavily with straw or grass clippings to hold moisture and keep developing fruit off bare soil."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Feed weekly from flowering", "text": "A weekly high-potash liquid feed from the first flowers helps fruit set and ripening. Skip the feed if the plant is producing more leaves than flowers; switch to nothing or potash-only."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Limit fruit per plant for big pumpkins", "text": "Each plant can set 4–6 fruit if left to itself; for bigger pumpkins, pinch out all but 2–3 fruits per plant. For show-stoppers (Atlantic Giant), one fruit per plant gets all the energy."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Put a board or straw under each fruit", "text": "As fruit develops on the ground, slip a wooden board or thick straw layer underneath. Stops the bottom rotting on damp soil and discourages slugs."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick when stem is corky", "text": "Usually mid-October. Test ripeness: the stem should be corky and brown; the shell should sound hollow when tapped; the colour should be fully developed for the variety. Cut with 5–10 cm of stem attached."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Cure for storage", "text": "Lay pumpkins in the sun or under cold glass for 2 weeks to harden the skin and seal the stem. Cured pumpkins store 4–6 months at 10–15°C in a cool dry place. Uncured fruit rots in storage."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow pumpkin seeds in the UK?", "answer": "Late April to early May indoors at 18–22°C. Don't sow earlier — pumpkin seedlings grow fast and you'll have unmanageable plants by planting-out time. Don't sow direct outdoors except in late May in sheltered south-coast gardens; emergence is patchy in cold soil."},
    {"question": "How much space do pumpkins need?", "answer": "A lot. Standard cultivars (Crown Prince, Cinderella, Atlantic Giant) sprawl 2–3 m in every direction from a single plant. Compact bush varieties (Baby Bear, Jack Be Little, Sweet Lightning) stay within 1 m. For most UK gardens, the compact varieties are the practical choice; full-size pumpkins need an allotment or dedicated patch."},
    {"question": "How do I get really big pumpkins?", "answer": "Three things. (1) Limit fruit per plant — one fruit per plant for show; 2–3 for huge eating pumpkins. Pinch off all later flowers. (2) Feed heavily — high-potash liquid feed weekly; fresh manure dug in before planting. (3) Water consistently — twice a week deeply; never let the plant dry out. Atlantic Giant and Dill's Atlantic Giant are the cultivars to grow for show."},
    {"question": "When is a pumpkin ready to pick?", "answer": "Three tests, all should pass. (1) Stem corky and brown: the green stem turns woody and brown when fruit is mature. (2) Hollow sound when tapped: a ripe pumpkin sounds hollow; a green one sounds dull. (3) Full colour: fully orange, blue, white or whatever the variety should be — no green left except possibly stripes. Pick before the first hard frost."},
    {"question": "How do I cure and store pumpkins?", "answer": "Cure for 2 weeks after picking: lay the pumpkins in the sun outdoors (cover at night if frost is forecast), or under cold glass in a greenhouse. The skin hardens and the stem seals — essential for storage. Store at 10–15°C in a cool dry well-ventilated place. Don't refrigerate; don't stack pumpkins together (the points of contact rot). Cured pumpkins keep 4–6 months for most cultivars; Crown Prince keeps longest."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between pumpkin and squash?", "answer": "Mostly culinary, not botanical. Both are Cucurbita species — pumpkin is the term for round orange Halloween-style fruit (often C. pepo for thin-skinned, C. maxima for big eating types). Winter squash is the broader category that includes butternut (C. moschata, separate slug `winter-squash`), Crown Prince, Hubbard, and other long-storing types. Summer squash includes courgette and pattypan. They all need the same growing conditions; the differences are in size, shape, flesh density, and storage life."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'pumpkin';

-- From radish.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow radish seed direct from March to September in successional batches every 2–3 weeks. Radishes are hardy annuals (RHS H3–H4) and the fastest UK vegetable — summer varieties (Cherry Belle, French Breakfast, Sparkler) crop in 4–6 weeks; winter daikon types (separate `daikon` slug) take 10–12 weeks. Sow thinly in light fertile soil with sun for at least half the day; thin to 3 cm apart. Pull young (the size of a 10p coin) for the best flavour and texture — radishes left too long get woody, hot and split. The classic first crop for kids: from seed packet to plate in a month. Flea beetle pock-marks young leaves but doesn''t usually stop the crop.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H3',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "March to September outdoors; successional sowings every 2–3 weeks for continuous picking. 1 cm deep, sow thinly along a drill, cover lightly. Earliest sowings (March) under cloches in cold gardens."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Thin to 3 cm", "text": "When seedlings have 2 true leaves, thin to 3 cm apart. Don't crowd them — overcrowded radishes go all leaf and no root. Eat the thinnings (whole young plants are edible)."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Water regularly", "text": "Radishes go woody, hot, and split if they dry out. Water lightly every 2–3 days through the growing window; mulch with grass clippings to hold moisture."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Watch for flea beetle on hot dry days", "text": "Tiny black beetles pock-mark young leaves with shot-holes. Mostly cosmetic — radishes outgrow the damage in days. For severe pressure, cover with fleece for the first 2 weeks or yellow sticky traps."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pull young", "text": "Pull when roots are 1.5–2.5 cm across (the size of a 10p coin to a 50p). Beyond that they get coarse, hot, and start to split. Check the row every couple of days during the peak picking window — radishes go from perfect to past-it in a week."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Sow successionally for continuous supply", "text": "The window from perfect to woody is 2–3 weeks at most. A single sowing gives a glut and a famine. Sow a short row every 2–3 weeks from March to August for continuous fresh radishes."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Avoid mid-summer sowings", "text": "In hot weather (June–July), radishes bolt almost immediately and go hot and woody. Either skip mid-summer or choose heat-tolerant cultivars (Rudi, Mantanghong) and provide afternoon shade."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow radishes in the UK?", "answer": "March (under cloches) to September outdoors. Successional sowings every 2–3 weeks. Avoid sowing in mid-summer heat (late June through July) — radishes bolt and go woody fast in temperatures above 22°C. Best sowings are spring (March–May) and late summer (August–September)."},
    {"question": "Why are my radishes all leaves and no root?", "answer": "Three usual causes. Too crowded: thin to 3 cm apart so each plant has room to swell. Too much nitrogen: rich soil pushes leaves at root expense; don't add fresh manure before sowing. Too much shade: radishes need sun for at least half the day; full shade gives all leaf."},
    {"question": "Why are my radishes splitting or hot?", "answer": "Left in the ground too long. Radishes go from perfect to past-it in 7–10 days. Check the row every couple of days once roots reach 1.5 cm across; pull as soon as they hit 2.5 cm. Drought stress also splits them — keep them consistently watered."},
    {"question": "What variety of radish is best for the UK?", "answer": "Cherry Belle (round red, fast, reliable), French Breakfast (long red-and-white, classic French), Sparkler (round red/white), Easter Egg Mix (multicoloured) for summer. For autumn–winter: black Spanish, Mooli (the white daikon — see separate `daikon` slug). Heat-tolerant: Rudi, Saxa."},
    {"question": "Can I grow radishes in containers?", "answer": "One of the easiest container crops. A 30 cm-deep pot grows a couple of dozen radishes. Use peat-free compost; sow thinly; thin to 3 cm; water consistently. Container radishes are sweeter than ground-grown because you control watering. Brilliant first crop for kids — fast results."},
    {"question": "Are radish leaves edible?", "answer": "Yes — young leaves are edible raw in salads (a peppery rocket-like flavour). Larger leaves can be stir-fried. Don't strip the plant though — the leaves are powering the root. Eat thinnings whole; pick the youngest leaves from mature plants."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'radish';

-- From raspberry.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant raspberry canes from November to March in the dormant season — bare-root canes are cheaper than pot-grown and establish better. Raspberries are very hardy (RHS H6) and shrug off UK winters. Plant 45 cm apart in rows 1.8 m apart, in moist, fertile, slightly acidic soil with full sun or light shade. Summer-fruiting varieties (Glen Ample, Tulameen) fruit on last year''s canes — tie in new growth as it appears. Autumn-fruiting varieties (Autumn Bliss, Polka) fruit on this year''s canes — cut everything to the ground in February. Net against birds from June. Both types crop heavily for 10–12 years from one planting; the easiest soft fruit to grow in the UK.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose summer or autumn varieties", "text": "Summer-fruiting (Glen Ample, Tulameen, Glen Lyon): bigger fruit, traditional flavour, fruits June–July on last year's canes. Autumn-fruiting (Autumn Bliss, Polka, Joan J): smaller fruit, fruits Aug–Oct on this year's canes, easier to prune. Grow both for a long season."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Build the support", "text": "Two parallel rows of stout posts at each end of the row, with 2–3 horizontal wires stretched between (45, 90, 135 cm high). Canes are tied to the wires as they grow."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant the canes", "text": "November to March (bare-root). Dig a trench 30 cm deep; spread the roots out; cover with soil; firm in; water in well. Plant 45 cm apart in rows 1.8 m apart. Trim canes to 25 cm above the soil to force fresh growth."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Tie in summer-fruiting canes", "text": "Through the season, tie new (this year's) green canes to the support wires. These bear next year's fruit."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Cut out fruited canes (summer types)", "text": "After harvest, cut the canes that fruited this year right down to the ground. Leave the new green canes (this year's growth) for next year's crop."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut everything in February (autumn types)", "text": "For autumn-fruiting raspberries, in February (before new growth starts), cut every cane to ground level. The plant sends up new canes that flower and fruit on the same shoots that summer."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Net against birds", "text": "From June (summer types) or August (autumn types) until harvest finishes, net firmly on a frame. Birds take more raspberries than they leave on unnetted plants."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Mulch annually", "text": "Each February or March, top up with 5 cm of well-rotted compost or leaf mould. Holds moisture, suppresses weeds, slowly feeds the canes."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant raspberry canes?", "answer": "November to March in the dormant season — bare-root canes are cheaper, establish better, and arrive by post when the season is right. Pot-grown canes can be planted year-round but they're more expensive and don't catch up to bare-root by the following summer."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between summer-fruiting and autumn-fruiting raspberries?", "answer": "Summer-fruiting fruit on canes that grew last year (biennial canes); harvest June–July; bigger fruit, classic flavour; prune by cutting fruited canes after harvest. Autumn-fruiting fruit on canes that grew this year (annual canes); harvest August–October; smaller fruit but a longer picking season; prune by cutting everything to the ground in February. Autumn types are easier to manage."},
    {"question": "How do I prune raspberries?", "answer": "Depends on type. Summer-fruiting: after harvest, cut fruited canes (look brown and woody) to ground level; tie new green canes to the wires for next year. Autumn-fruiting: in February, cut all canes to ground level — the plant pushes up fresh canes that fruit the same year. Confusion is the commonest reason for poor crops; identify your variety before pruning."},
    {"question": "My raspberries are spreading everywhere — how do I control them?", "answer": "Raspberries spread by underground suckers and can become invasive. Bury a barrier (sunk paving slabs or pond liner) around the row 30 cm deep to stop lateral spread. Dig out unwanted suckers regularly through summer. Containers work for autumn-fruiting types (40-litre pots) but limit yield."},
    {"question": "How long do raspberry plants last?", "answer": "10–12 years from a single planting if well-mulched and properly pruned. After that, productivity drops and viruses build up; replace by buying new certified canes (don't transplant your own — virus carries over)."},
    {"question": "How do I store a glut of raspberries?", "answer": "Freeze flat on a tray (single layer, no contact), then bag once frozen — they keep separate. Use within 12 months. Jam and freezing are the two main paths; raspberry vinegar is a delicate alternative. Fresh raspberries last 2–3 days in the fridge if dry; never wash until you eat them."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'raspberry';

-- From redcurrant.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant redcurrant bushes bare-root from November to March, in sun or light shade, in any decent garden soil — they crop heavily on a permanent framework of branches for 15+ years. Redcurrants are very hardy (RHS H6–H7) and shrug off any UK winter. Don''t prune them like blackcurrants: redcurrants fruit on old wood and spurs, so train to a permanent open-goblet framework like a gooseberry. Net the ripening trusses heavily — blackbirds and squirrels strip a bush in a morning. Pick whole strigs (the strings of berries) by snipping at the top of the truss, then strip individual berries with a fork later. Jonkheer van Tets (early), Red Lake (mid), and Rovada (late, heaviest cropper) are reliable UK varieties.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Know that redcurrants are pruned like gooseberries, NOT blackcurrants", "text": "This is the single most important thing about growing redcurrants. Blackcurrants fruit on last year's wood and are pruned hard to the base each winter. Redcurrants fruit on spurs on a permanent framework of older branches, so they're pruned like a gooseberry: build a goblet shape and only renew sparingly. Pruning them like a blackcurrant removes the crop."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose your variety", "text": "Jonkheer van Tets — early, vigorous, large berries. Crops late June. Red Lake — mid-season, heavy crop, long strigs, the UK favourite. Rovada — late, the heaviest cropper of all, very long strigs, mildew-resistant, the modern standard. Stanza — late, compact bush, good for smaller gardens. A single bush of any variety crops well; there's no need for a pollination partner."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March. Plant on a slight mound to keep the crown out of waterlogging. Spacing: 1.5 m apart for bushes; 30–45 cm for cordons; 1 m for fan-trained. Redcurrants tolerate light shade — useful if you've run out of sunny spots."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Train to a permanent goblet", "text": "Year one: cut the main shoots back by half to outward-facing buds, removing any inward-pointing growth. Aim for 5–6 main branches angled outward from a short clear stem (15 cm). Year two onwards: build out from this framework, keeping the centre open."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Summer prune in late June", "text": "Cut all new sideshoots back to five leaves in late June. This exposes the developing strigs to sunlight (sweetens the berries), keeps the bush manageable, and encourages spur formation along the framework branches."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Winter prune for renewal", "text": "February. Shorten the leading shoots of each main branch by half. Cut back sideshoots already shortened in summer to two buds. Remove any really old wood (more than 4–5 years) at the base to encourage strong replacement shoots. The permanent framework largely stays — you're refreshing the spur system, not rebuilding the bush."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Net the strigs as they colour up", "text": "Birds and squirrels will strip a bush in a morning the moment the berries turn red. Net the bush from mid-June, before any colour shows. Fine mesh tucked under at the base — squirrels can climb under any gap. A walk-in fruit cage is the long-term answer if you grow multiple soft fruit bushes."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick whole strigs, strip with a fork", "text": "Pick the entire string of berries at once by snipping the stalk at the top of the strig — much faster than picking individual berries off the bush, and the strigs keep better in the fridge. Strip the berries with a kitchen fork over a bowl when you're ready to use them. The strigs are too bitter to eat; the berries make summer pudding, redcurrant jelly, and a classic accompaniment to roast lamb."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I prune a redcurrant — like a blackcurrant?", "answer": "No — and this is the most important rule. Redcurrants fruit on spurs on a permanent framework of branches, so they're pruned like a gooseberry: open goblet shape, sideshoots shortened in summer and winter, only the oldest wood (4–5+ years) renewed. Pruning a redcurrant like a blackcurrant (hard to the base) removes the entire crop. Blackcurrants fruit on last year's wood, redcurrants on older wood — different plants, opposite pruning."},
    {"question": "When are redcurrants ready to pick?", "answer": "Late June to mid-July, depending on variety. The whole strig should be bright glossy red with no green or pink berries at the tip. Pick the entire strig by snipping the top stalk rather than individual berries. The flavour is sharp — high in pectin and acid, which is why redcurrant jelly sets beautifully and pairs so well with rich meats."},
    {"question": "What's the best redcurrant variety for the UK?", "answer": "Rovada — modern Dutch variety, very late (mid-July), the heaviest cropper, very long strigs, mildew-resistant. Red Lake is the traditional UK favourite — mid-season, reliable, large berries. Jonkheer van Tets for an early crop. For a mix, plant one of each to spread the picking from late June through July."},
    {"question": "Why do my redcurrants not crop?", "answer": "Three usual causes. Wrong pruning: someone has cut the bush back hard each winter, like a blackcurrant, removing all the fruiting wood. Switch to spur pruning and the bush will crop the following year. Birds eating the buds in winter: bullfinches strip the flower buds in February–March; net from December. Frost on flowers: late May frosts can knock out the crop in cold gardens; row cover the bush if a frost is forecast during flowering."},
    {"question": "Can I grow redcurrants in shade?", "answer": "Yes — better than most fruit. Redcurrants tolerate light shade and crop reliably even on a north-facing wall (fan-trained). Crop will be lighter and ripen a week or two later than in full sun, but flavour is fine. Useful for gardens running out of sunny corners."},
    {"question": "How do I make redcurrant jelly?", "answer": "Strip the berries into a pan with a splash of water, simmer until soft, strain through a jelly bag overnight, measure the liquid, add 450 g sugar per 600 ml liquid, boil hard for 8–10 minutes until it sets on a cold saucer. Redcurrants are so high in pectin you need no other gelling agent. A glass on a roast lamb sandwich is one of the great rewards of a UK kitchen garden."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'redcurrant';

-- From rhubarb.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant rhubarb crowns from November to March in a sunny spot with deep, rich, moist soil — well-rotted manure dug in heavily before planting pays back for the next 20 years. Rhubarb is very hardy (RHS H7) and one of the few crops that survives anything a UK winter can throw at it. Don''t crop in year one (let the crown build up); pull stalks from April onwards in year two and beyond. Force one crown each year for early sweet pink stalks (cover with a tall pot in January for stalks in March). Mulch heavily in November with manure or compost; rhubarb is one of the hungriest crops in the garden. Crowns last 20+ years.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose crowns or seed", "text": "Buy crowns from a nursery; seed is possible but takes 2–3 years to reach productive size and the seedlings vary in quality. Reliable UK varieties: Timperley Early (the standard for forcing), Victoria (large red stalks), Champagne (sweet, slim stalks)."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Full sun, deep, fertile, moisture-retentive soil. Fork in two barrowloads of well-rotted manure or compost per crown — rhubarb is exceptionally hungry. Once planted, it stays in the same spot for 20+ years, so make the bed properly."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant", "text": "November to March. Dig a hole big enough for the crown with the buds 2 cm below soil level. Firm in. Water if the soil is dry."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't crop in year one", "text": "Resist the temptation. Let the crown build up reserves through its first summer. Pull only one or two thin stalks for cooking, if any."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Mulch heavily each November", "text": "Apply a thick (10 cm) mulch of well-rotted manure or compost around the crown each November. Don't bury the crown itself — keep mulch 5 cm clear of the buds. This annual top-up feeds the plant for the entire next season."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pull stalks from April", "text": "From year two onwards, pull stalks from April through to July. Grasp at the base, twist and pull — don't cut. Stop picking by mid-July to let the plant build up for next year. Leaves are toxic (oxalic acid) — compost them, don't eat them."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Force a crown for early stalks (optional)", "text": "In late January, cover one crown with a large bucket, terracotta forcer, or even a tall plastic pot — exclude all light. The plant produces pale pink, very tender stalks in 6–8 weeks. The forced crown then gets a year off; don't pick from it that summer."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Remove flower stalks", "text": "Rhubarb sometimes sends up tall flower stalks in summer (the plant is trying to seed). Cut them off at the base; flowering reduces the next year's crop."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Divide every 5–7 years", "text": "Mature crowns get crowded. In November, lift the crown, divide it with a spade into 3–4 pieces (each piece needs at least one strong bud), replant the best ones in fresh soil. Discard the woody central core."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I start picking my rhubarb?", "answer": "Year two onwards. Don't pull stalks in the first season after planting — let the crown build up. From year two, pull stalks from April through to mid-July. Stop picking from mid-July to let the plant restore itself for the following year. A mature crown gives 30–50 stalks a season for 20+ years."},
    {"question": "Are rhubarb leaves really poisonous?", "answer": "Yes, the leaves contain oxalic acid in concentrations high enough to cause illness or worse. Strip the leaves off the stalks in the garden and compost them; only eat the stalks. The stalks contain small amounts of oxalic acid too but at safe levels — like spinach or chard. The general rule with rhubarb: stalks yes, leaves no."},
    {"question": "How do I force rhubarb for early sweet stalks?", "answer": "In late January, cover one crown completely with a tall opaque container — a traditional terracotta forcer, a large bucket, or a tall plastic pot. Exclude all light. The plant pushes up pale pink, tender, very sweet stalks in 6–8 weeks. Pick when stalks are 25–30 cm long, then remove the cover. The forced crown must rest the following summer — don't pick from it again until the year after."},
    {"question": "Why is my rhubarb sending up flower stalks?", "answer": "Stress or maturity. Hot dry weather, lack of feeding, or simply an old plant. Cut the flower stalks off at the base as soon as they appear — they take energy from the crown that would otherwise go into next year's stalks. Mulch heavily and water well. If a crown is still flowering after attention, it's probably time to divide it."},
    {"question": "How long do rhubarb crowns last?", "answer": "20+ years, often much longer. Crowns inherited from old gardens can be 50+ years old and still productive. Divide every 5–7 years to keep them vigorous; replace only when even divided sections produce poorly."},
    {"question": "When does rhubarb come into season in the UK?", "answer": "Forced rhubarb: March (the famous Yorkshire forced rhubarb is harvested by candlelight in dark sheds). Outdoor rhubarb: April through to early July. Don't pick after mid-July — stalks get tough and stringy, and the plant needs time to build for next year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'rhubarb';

-- From rocket.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow rocket direct from March to September, in sun or part shade in moist soil — it''s one of the fastest UK salad crops, ready to pick 3–4 weeks from sowing as cut-and-come-again leaves with their distinctive peppery mustard flavour. Rocket is hardy (RHS H4–H5) but bolts to flower in heat — successional sowing every 3 weeks is essential, and most UK gardens grow rocket best in spring and autumn rather than midsummer. Two species: garden rocket (Eruca vesicaria) is the fast-growing annual, ready in 3 weeks; wild rocket (Diplotaxis tenuifolia) is the smaller-leaved, hotter, more pungent perennial-ish form that takes longer but is more bolt-resistant. Cut at the base with scissors when leaves are 8–10 cm — the plant regrows for 3–4 cuts before bolting. Flea beetle is the main pest — fine mesh covers, or sow under cover.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick garden or wild rocket", "text": "Garden rocket (Eruca vesicaria): smooth oval leaves, fast-growing (3 weeks to pick), milder flavour, true annual — bolts after 4–6 weeks. The salad-bag rocket. Wild rocket (Diplotaxis tenuifolia): deeply-cut narrow leaves, slower (5–6 weeks to pick), hotter and more pungent flavour, perennial-ish (can re-grow from cut roots for a season), more bolt-resistant. Esmee and Buzz are popular garden-rocket varieties; Wild Rocket is sold as the wild type. For a first crop: garden rocket (Esmee or basic packet) — quickest reward."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, March to September", "text": "Cool-season preference: best in spring (March–May) and autumn (August–September). Skip the hot weeks (mid-June to mid-August) — heat triggers immediate bolting. Direct-sow in shallow drills 1 cm deep, 20 cm between rows. Germination in 5–10 days. For cut-and-come-again: thin to 5 cm; for mature plants: thin to 15 cm."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Sow successionally every 3 weeks", "text": "The key to continuous rocket. Each sowing crops for 3–4 weeks then bolts. Fresh row every 3 weeks through the cool months for non-stop supply. Many UK kitchen gardens overlap rocket sowings with mizuna and mustard-greens for a diverse Asian-style salad."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Sow under cover in September for winter cropping", "text": "The most useful UK growing window. A September sowing under a cloche, low polytunnel, or cold frame establishes before frosts and crops from October through to March. Wild rocket is particularly suited to under-cover winter cropping — slow steady growth, peppery flavour, no flea beetle issues in cold months."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Cover with insect mesh from sowing", "text": "Flea beetle is the dominant rocket pest — small dark beetles that pepper leaves with pinprick shot-hole damage in warm weather. Fine insect mesh (1 mm mesh) over the row from sowing through cropping excludes flea beetle entirely. Mesh is essential for outdoor summer rocket; September–March crops generally escape flea beetle."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut whole rows cut-and-come-again", "text": "Cut at 2–3 cm above the soil when leaves are 8–10 cm tall (3–4 weeks from sowing for garden rocket, 5–6 weeks for wild). The plant regrows in 7–10 days — repeat 3–4 times before final bolt. Use scissors for clean cuts that re-grow quickly; tearing damages the regrowth point. Eat fresh — rocket wilts in hours after picking and loses heat over a few days."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Water consistently", "text": "Rocket bolts faster in drought. A weekly soak in dry spells keeps the leaves tender and the plant in leaf-production mode. Mulch with fine compost along the row to conserve moisture. Pot-grown rocket needs daily watering once growing."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Let some bolted plants flower for self-seeding", "text": "Rocket flowers are edible — small four-petalled cream-yellow (garden rocket) or yellow (wild rocket) blooms with a stronger version of the leaf flavour. Let one or two bolted plants flower and set seed; the seeds drop and produce volunteer rocket the following year. The flowers are tasty in salads and garnishes — strong peppery punch, attractive on the plate."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Why does my rocket keep bolting?", "answer": "Heat + long days. Rocket bolts to flower in summer conditions. Five strategies. (1) Sow in cool months only: March–May for spring, August–September for autumn. (2) Skip June, July, early August: midsummer sowings will bolt before producing leaves. (3) Plant wild rocket — more bolt-resistant than garden rocket. (4) Sow in part shade in summer — cooler position delays bolting. (5) Water consistently — drought triggers bolting faster than heat alone."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between garden rocket and wild rocket?", "answer": "Different species, different growth and flavour. Garden rocket (Eruca vesicaria): smooth oval leaves, fast (3 weeks from sowing), milder flavour, annual, bolts quickly. The classic supermarket-bag rocket. Wild rocket (Diplotaxis tenuifolia): deeply-cut narrow leaves, slower (5–6 weeks), hotter and more pungent flavour, more perennial-ish, more bolt-resistant. Most UK gardeners grow both — garden rocket for quick salad bulk, wild rocket for stronger flavour and reliability through summer."},
    {"question": "When can I pick rocket?", "answer": "3–4 weeks after sowing for garden rocket; 5–6 weeks for wild rocket. Cut whole rows at 2–3 cm above soil when leaves are 8–10 cm tall. Cut-and-come-again: the plant regrows in 7–10 days; repeat 3–4 cuts before the row finally bolts. Use fresh — rocket wilts within hours of picking and the peppery heat fades over a few days in the fridge."},
    {"question": "Why does my rocket have tiny holes in the leaves?", "answer": "Flea beetle damage. Tiny dark beetles jump like fleas when disturbed and feed on brassica leaves, leaving pinprick shot-hole damage. Cosmetic damage — leaves are still edible. Prevention: cover the row with fine insect mesh (1 mm) from sowing through cropping. Sow under cover in September for winter crops — flea beetle is absent in cold months. Wild rocket is also slightly more flea-beetle resistant than garden rocket."},
    {"question": "Can I grow rocket in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — pots make slug control and positioning easier. A 25 cm pot of peat-free compost holds 30–40 rocket plants. Sow thinly, thin to 3–5 cm. Position in part shade in summer (delays bolting) or full sun in cooler months. Water consistently. For winter use: position pots on a sunny windowsill or in a cold greenhouse — rocket crops slowly through winter indoors. Continuous supply: sow a fresh pot every 3 weeks; three pots in rotation gives continuous rocket."},
    {"question": "Are rocket flowers edible?", "answer": "Yes — and they're better than the leaves for some uses. The small four-petalled flowers (cream-yellow on garden rocket, brighter yellow on wild rocket) have a stronger version of the leaf flavour — peppery, mustardy, with floral notes. Scatter on salads as a garnish — striking visual contrast. Pickle in white wine vinegar for a strongly-flavoured condiment. Pluck individual flowers rather than eating them on the stem (stems are tough). Don't pick all flowers; leave some to set seed for self-sown volunteers."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'rocket';

-- From rose.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant roses from November to March (bare-root) or year-round (container), in any decent soil with sun for at least half the day. Roses are mostly hardy in the UK (RHS H4–H6 depending on type) and far easier than their reputation suggests if you choose disease-resistant cultivars and plant the right rose for the spot. David Austin English roses combine old-rose form with modern repeat-flowering and shrug off most diseases; older hybrid teas demand more spraying. Plant deeper than the pot — bury the graft union 5 cm below soil level to protect against frost and rose suckers. Prune in February for shape and to remove dead wood. Feed in March and after the first flush. Mulch with compost annually. A well-chosen rose flowers for 30+ years.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose the right rose type", "text": "Bush (hybrid tea single-stem, floribunda multi-flower spray), shrub, climber, rambler, English (David Austin). Match to your space. For most UK gardens, David Austin English roses are the best all-rounders — disease-resistant, repeat-flowering, traditional form."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Full sun or at least 4–6 hours direct. Any decent garden soil; fork in compost. Roses tolerate clay but want it improved with grit and compost. Avoid replanting where roses have grown before (rose replant disease — soil becomes hostile to new roses)."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant deep", "text": "Bare-root from November to March, container year-round. Bury the graft union (the knobbly bit at the base of the stems where the rose was joined to its rootstock) 5 cm below soil level. Protects against frost damage and rose suckers from the rootstock."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water in and mulch", "text": "Water heavily on planting (a bucket per rose), then weekly through the first summer in dry weather. Mulch with 5 cm of well-rotted compost or manure each March, kept 5 cm clear of the stem."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Prune in February", "text": "Open the centre by removing crossing branches. Shorten remaining stems by a third for shrub roses; by half for hybrid teas and floribundas; tie in new growth horizontally for climbers; ramblers are pruned after flowering, not in winter. Cut to outward-facing buds."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Feed in March and after first flush", "text": "A balanced rose feed in March; high-potash feed in late June after the first flush of flowers. Replenishes the energy for the second flush."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Deadhead through summer", "text": "Cut spent blooms back to a five-leaf set; encourages repeat-flowering on most cultivars. Don't deadhead ramblers or species roses — they're once-flowering anyway and produce decorative hips for autumn."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for black spot, mildew, rust", "text": "Pick up infected fallen leaves and bin them (don't compost). Improve air circulation by pruning open centres. Choose resistant cultivars from the start — David Austin English roses, modern floribundas like Iceberg, and most species roses all resist disease far better than older hybrid teas."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant roses in the UK?", "answer": "Bare-root roses from November to March, while the plants are dormant — they're cheaper, establish better, and have a wider variety choice than container roses. Container-grown roses can be planted year-round but are more expensive. Avoid planting in waterlogged or frozen soil; heel plants in temporarily if conditions are wrong."},
    {"question": "Why does my rose have black-spotted leaves?", "answer": "Black spot (Diplocarpon rosae) — fungal, common in wet UK summers. Pick up infected fallen leaves and bin them (don't compost; the fungus survives composting). Improve air circulation. Choose resistant cultivars — David Austin English roses, Iceberg, Munstead Wood, Lady of Shalott all resist far better than older hybrid teas. Sulphur sprays available for severe cases."},
    {"question": "When and how do I prune my rose?", "answer": "Depends on type. Bush roses (hybrid tea, floribunda): February. Shorten stems by half to two-thirds. Open the centre. Shrub roses (including David Austin): February. Shorten by a third; remove dead wood; light prune. Climbers: February or after flowering; tie new growth horizontally to encourage side-shoots. Ramblers: After flowering only (June–July); remove old wood, tie in new long shoots for next year. Never prune ramblers in winter or you lose the next year's flowers."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between bush, shrub, climber and rambler roses?", "answer": "Bush roses (hybrid tea, floribunda): 1–1.5 m, repeat-flowering, formal beds. Shrub roses (English roses, old roses, modern shrubs): 1–2 m, more relaxed habit, often the best for mixed borders. Climbers: 2–4 m, repeat-flowering on established frameworks, suit walls and pergolas. Ramblers: 4–8 m, once-flowering (huge June display), best for growing through trees or large pergolas."},
    {"question": "Are David Austin English roses worth the price?", "answer": "For most UK gardens, yes. Bred to combine old-rose form (full, fragrant, romantic) with modern characteristics (repeat-flowering, disease resistance, manageable size). Pay roughly twice the price of a basic hybrid tea but get a plant that crops far longer, looks better, and stays healthy without spraying. Munstead Wood, Lady of Shalott, Olivia Rose Austin, and Gertrude Jekyll are reliable starters."},
    {"question": "How do I stop greenfly (aphids) on roses?", "answer": "Ladybirds and hoverflies eat them in volume — encourage by planting flowering herbs (dill, coriander, fennel) nearby. Wash off small infestations with a hose. Soft soap spray (a teaspoon of washing-up liquid in a litre of water) on calm dry days for bigger numbers. Healthy well-fed roses resist aphid damage; weak or stressed plants get hit hardest."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'rose';

-- From rosemary.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant rosemary in spring or autumn, ideally as a young plant rather than from seed — seed-raised plants are slow and unpredictable. Rosemary is hardy in most of the UK (RHS H4) but resents wet feet — the commonest cause of dead rosemary is heavy clay in a cold wet winter, not cold itself. Choose a sunny well-drained spot; raised beds and gravel gardens suit it. Salvia rosmarinus ''Miss Jessopp''s Upright'' is the most reliable UK cultivar — bigger, hardier and more upright than the prostrate forms. Cut stems for cooking year-round. Prune lightly each spring to keep the shape; never cut back into old bare wood, as rosemary rarely regrows from it. A well-sited plant lasts 15–20 years.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy a young plant", "text": "Don't bother with seed. Buy a young rosemary plant (preferably Miss Jessopp's Upright for UK reliability) in a 1–2 litre pot. Cuttings from a friend's plant in spring or autumn root readily."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose a sunny well-drained spot", "text": "Full sun, well-drained soil. South-facing wall, raised bed, gravel garden, or large container. Rosemary tolerates poor soil but not waterlogged conditions."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in spring or autumn", "text": "March–April or September–October. Dig a hole twice the rootball width; add grit if your soil is heavy. Don't bury the stem below its original level. Water in well, then let nature take over."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Prune lightly each spring", "text": "After flowering (usually April–May), shorten last year's growth by a third. Never cut back into old bare wood; rosemary rarely regrows from it. Light annual pruning keeps the shape; neglect for several years and the plant gets leggy."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick year-round", "text": "Cut sprigs whenever you cook with it. The plant tolerates regular light picking. Avoid picking heavily in mid-winter; cold-damaged tips happen on over-picked plants."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Take cuttings as insurance", "text": "At year 10–15, take cuttings in spring or autumn so you have replacements ready before the original declines. Even healthy rosemary eventually exhausts itself."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Will rosemary survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes, in most of the UK if planted in a well-drained sunny spot. Rosemary is rated RHS H4 (−5 to −10°C). It survives the cold; what kills it is waterlogged roots in winter — heavy clay soil holding water around the roots in cold weather. Drainage is the key. Raised beds, gravel gardens, and south-facing walls give the best winter survival."},
    {"question": "Why is my rosemary dying?", "answer": "Almost always waterlogged roots. Rosemary hates cold wet feet — the combination kills more rosemary than frost ever does. Symptoms: browning needles from the bottom up, sudden collapse after a wet spell. Move the plant to a better-drained site, or replace and improve drainage with grit. Other causes: pruning into old bare wood (rosemary rarely regrows from it), or simply the plant having reached its 15–20 year lifespan."},
    {"question": "Can I grow rosemary in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and it solves the drainage problem for heavy-soil gardens. Use a 30 cm clay pot (clay breathes better than plastic for Mediterranean herbs), peat-free compost mixed with grit, and a south-facing position. Water modestly through summer; let the compost surface dry between waterings. In winter, move to a sheltered spot or unheated greenhouse — pots are less hardy than ground plants because the rootball can freeze through."},
    {"question": "How and when should I prune rosemary?", "answer": "Light prune each spring after flowering (April–May). Shorten last year's growth by a third with secateurs. Never cut back into the woody base — rosemary rarely regrows from old bare wood, and you'll kill the plant. If a plant has become leggy and bare at the base, layer a low branch or take cuttings rather than trying to rejuvenate the original."},
    {"question": "What's the best rosemary variety for the UK?", "answer": "Salvia rosmarinus Miss Jessopp's Upright is the standard UK choice — bigger, hardier, more upright, and longer-lived than the wild-type or prostrate forms. Tuscan Blue is another reliable upright. Avoid prostrate cultivars (Severn Sea and similar) for UK ground planting except in very mild south-coast gardens — they're less hardy and rot more readily."},
    {"question": "How do I take rosemary cuttings?", "answer": "Spring or autumn. Cut 10 cm shoots from non-flowering side-shoots. Strip the lower leaves, dip the base in rooting hormone (optional), push into a pot of gritty multi-purpose compost. Water in; cover loosely with a plastic bag to keep humidity up. Roots in 6–8 weeks. Pot on the following spring."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'rosemary';

-- From runner-bean.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow runner bean seed indoors from mid-April, or direct outdoors from late May. Plant out after the last frost: mid-May in southern England, early June in the north. Runners are tender annuals (RHS H1c) needing 2.5 m of vertical space and a sunny sheltered spot. Build a wigwam or row of canes set in deep rich soil with plenty of compost dug in. Pick the pods young and often from late July — leaving them on the plant shuts down production. Pollination depends on bumblebees, and hot dry weather can give a poor set even on otherwise healthy plants. The most reliable climbing bean for UK conditions.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Build the support", "text": "Late April. Tie 2.5 m bamboo canes or hazel poles into a wigwam (5–6 canes around a 1 m diameter) or a row of crossed pairs. Sink each cane 30 cm into the ground for stability."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "Late April indoors in deep modules (root-trainers if you have them); late May direct outdoors. Indoors: one seed per pot, 5 cm deep, 18°C. Outdoors: two seeds per cane, 5 cm deep, thin to the strongest after germination."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Fork compost or well-rotted manure into the planting area. Runners are gross feeders and need rich, moisture-retentive soil."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant out", "text": "After the last frost. Indoor-raised plants harden off for 7–10 days first. Set one plant at the base of each cane. Water in well."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Train and tie in", "text": "Twine the young growth around the cane in the direction it wants to go (clockwise for most). Tie loosely with twine if it won't grip. They climb 30 cm a week once warm."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water and pinch", "text": "Water deeply every few days from flowering onwards. Once the leading shoot reaches the top of the cane, pinch it out to encourage side-shoots and more flowers."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick young and often", "text": "From late July. Pick beans at 15–20 cm before the pods get stringy. Picking daily during the peak keeps the plant producing through to September."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow runner beans in the UK?", "answer": "Indoors mid- to late April, outdoors late May (south) to mid-June (north). Runner beans are frost-tender — sowing too early means the seedlings sit in cold soil and rot. A late April indoor sowing gives plants ready to go out around the last frost; a late May direct sowing avoids the hardening-off step. Both work."},
    {"question": "Why are my runner bean flowers dropping without setting?", "answer": "Hot dry weather, lack of pollinators, or poor watering. Runner beans need bumblebees and consistent moisture. In a heatwave, mist the flowers in the cool of the morning, mulch heavily, water deeply. Plant nearby annual flowers (cosmos, calendula, sweet peas) to draw pollinators. Some varieties (white-flowered Czar, Moonlight) set more reliably than scarlet-flowered classics."},
    {"question": "How tall do runner beans grow?", "answer": "2.5 to 3 m on a proper support. Stop them at 2.5 m by pinching the leading shoot — taller is unstable in wind, and the upper pods become hard to reach. Dwarf cultivars (Hestia, Pickwick) only reach 45–60 cm and grow without support, suitable for containers and exposed gardens."},
    {"question": "How do I store a glut of runner beans?", "answer": "Pods slice and freeze well — slice diagonally, blanch for 90 seconds, plunge in cold water, drain, freeze flat on trays then bag. They keep their texture better than french beans. Pickling and chutney are traditional alternatives. Beans left on the plant to swell can be shelled, dried, and used as stored beans for winter — but each pod left back shuts down further flowering."},
    {"question": "Can I grow runner beans in pots?", "answer": "Bush varieties (Hestia, Pickwick) in 40-litre containers with a wigwam of short canes. Climbing varieties need too much root space for containers to be reliable. Water containers daily in summer; feed weekly with high-potash liquid feed from flowering."},
    {"question": "When is the harvest window for runner beans in the UK?", "answer": "Late July to early October in southern England; mid-August to mid-September further north. Earlier sowings under cover or with fleece protection can extend the start by 2–3 weeks. Production stops at the first frost."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'runner-bean';

-- From sage.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant sage in a sunny, well-drained spot — Mediterranean origins mean it wants poor, gritty soil and full sun, and it sulks (or rots over winter) in rich or wet ground. Common sage is hardy in most of the UK (RHS H5) and stays evergreen through normal winters; the ornamental coloured forms (purple sage, tricolor sage, golden sage) are tender (H4) and may lose leaves in cold gardens. Buy a young plant in spring — sage is slow from seed and most named varieties don''t come true anyway. Replace the bush every 4–5 years before it gets woody and bare at the base. Hard-prune in spring after the last frost to keep it bushy. Pick fresh leaves year-round; the strongest flavour is in early summer before flowering.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose your sage", "text": "Common sage (Salvia officinalis): grey-green leaves, the toughest, the standard culinary form. The reliable backbone. Purple sage (Purpurascens): purple-flushed leaves, slightly more tender (H4), good flavour, attractive. Tricolor sage: pink-white-green variegated, ornamental, less hardy still, weaker flavour. Golden sage (Icterina): yellow-green variegated, less hardy, good flavour. Pineapple sage (S. elegans) — different species, half-hardy, fruity-pineapple scent, ornamental and culinary. For a kitchen herb, plant common sage. The coloured forms are ornamental first, edible second."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Buy a plant in spring, not seed", "text": "Sage germinates slowly and unreliably from seed, and named cultivars don't come true. Buy a young plant in 9 cm or 1 L pot from a garden centre or herb nursery in April–May. One plant is enough for a household."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in poor, well-drained, sunny soil", "text": "The single biggest cause of sage failure in UK gardens is rich wet soil — it rots over winter. Sandy or gravelly soil is ideal; clay soils need plenty of horticultural grit forked in. Full sun. Avoid feeding — lush growth is weak growth that won't survive winter."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "In heavy soils, grow in a pot", "text": "A 30 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed 50/50 with horticultural grit gives perfect drainage. Stand it in full sun. Pots are also useful in cold gardens — move under cover (shed, conservatory) in deep winter to protect tender variegated forms."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Hard-prune in spring", "text": "After the last frost (April–May), cut the bush back hard — by about a third to half. This stops it getting leggy and woody, encourages dense new growth, and keeps the bush compact for another year. Never cut into old bare wood — sage rarely shoots from old stems."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Don't cut in autumn", "text": "Pruning in late summer or autumn leaves soft new growth exposed to frost, which kills it. Stop cutting (other than picking small amounts for the kitchen) from August onwards. Let the bush harden off for winter."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick fresh year-round", "text": "Sage is evergreen — pick fresh leaves any time, even in January. Strongest flavour is on fully-grown leaves before flowering (June). Older leaves at the base are tougher; pick fresher growth from the tips."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Replace every 4–5 years", "text": "Even with regular pruning, sage gets woody at the base after about 5 years and stops producing usable leaves. Take 10 cm semi-ripe cuttings in late summer to start replacement plants (they root easily in gritty compost), or buy a new plant. Compost the old bush — it's done its work."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Will sage survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Common sage (grey-green) is hardy through most UK winters (RHS H5) — surviving down to about -15°C. In a sheltered, well-drained spot it stays evergreen. The coloured variegated forms (purple, tricolor, golden) are less hardy (H4) and may lose leaves or die back to the base in a cold winter, particularly on heavy or wet soils. For cold gardens, grow tender forms in pots that can move under cover."},
    {"question": "Why has my sage gone leggy and bare at the base?", "answer": "That's its natural ageing pattern. Without regular hard spring pruning, sage extends its branches outward each year and the base becomes increasingly woody and leafless. Two fixes. (1) Hard-prune in spring — cut back by a third to half each April–May to keep it bushy. (2) Replace every 4–5 years before it gets terminally woody. Take cuttings in late summer to grow replacements."},
    {"question": "When should I prune sage?", "answer": "Late spring — April or early May, after the last frost. Cut back the whole bush by a third to half, into youngish wood (never into old leafless stems). One annual hard prune is all sage needs. Avoid pruning after August — the soft regrowth won't survive winter."},
    {"question": "Can I grow sage in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and on heavy or wet soils, it's the better option. Use a 30 cm pot with peat-free compost mixed 50/50 with horticultural grit. Full sun. Don't overwater — sage hates wet roots far more than it hates drought. A potted sage lives 4–5 years like an in-ground one and gives you the option to move it under cover in severe winter weather."},
    {"question": "Should I let sage flower?", "answer": "Up to you. Sage flowers (mauve-purple spikes) in June, are extremely good for bees, and don't harm the plant. The leaves are slightly less strongly flavoured after flowering, so for kitchen use some gardeners cut flower stems off as they form to keep leaf production going. Letting it flower is the better choice for wildlife; cutting flowers keeps the harvest stronger."},
    {"question": "How do I take sage cuttings?", "answer": "Late summer (August–early September). Cut 10 cm semi-ripe shoots — the lower half woody, the top still green. Strip the lower leaves. Push the bare stem into a 50/50 mix of compost and horticultural grit. Water once and cover with a clear plastic bag. Roots form in 4–6 weeks. Pot up individually in spring; plant out the following year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sage';

-- From salad-burnet.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant salad burnet from a young plant in spring or autumn, or sow seed direct in March–April, in sun or part shade in well-drained soil — it''s a hardy UK native perennial that gives a delicate cucumber-flavoured leaf for salads, herb butters, and cool summer drinks. Salad burnet is very hardy (RHS H6–H7) and evergreen — the rosette of small toothed leaflets stays usable through mild winters, providing fresh greens when little else is. Pick the young inner leaves continuously; older outer leaves get tough. Cut flower stems off as buds form to keep leaf production going and prevent self-seeding (though salad burnet seeds itself modestly). Tolerates the chalk and dry conditions that defeat many herbs — found wild on UK chalk grassland. An old-fashioned UK herb less grown today but unmatched for cucumber-fresh winter salads.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy a plant or sow direct", "text": "From plant (faster): a 9 cm or 1 L plant from a herb nursery (Jekka's, Pepperpot, RHS Wisley) in March–May. One plant is enough; divides easily once established. From seed (cheaper, slower): direct-sow in March–April or August–September; germinates in 14–21 days. Seed-grown plants reach productive size in year 2."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in sun or part shade", "text": "Salad burnet is happy in full sun or part shade. Well-drained soil is essential — tolerates dry, chalky, sandy conditions perfectly (the wild plant grows on UK chalk grassland). Doesn't like waterlogged ground. Spacing 30 cm. Ideal positions: front of border, herb bed edge, gravel garden, dry chalky slope, rockery."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pick young inner leaves continuously", "text": "Cut young leaves with scissors at the base, starting from the inside of the rosette where the youngest growth is. The flavour is in the young tender leaves — older outer leaves get tough and slightly bitter. Pick continuously to keep the plant producing new growth. Crush a leaf between your fingers before tasting; the cucumber scent confirms you have salad burnet (rather than its giant relative Sanguisorba officinalis, the great burnet, which has medicinal but not culinary use)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Cut flower stems off as buds form", "text": "The key husbandry technique. Salad burnet produces small round pinkish-red flower-heads on stems 60–80 cm tall from June onwards. Cut flower stems off as buds form — this keeps leaf production going (otherwise leaves get coarser) and limits self-seeding. Leave a few stems if you want the architectural button-flowers (visually distinctive) or some self-seeding for replacement plants."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Use fresh for cucumber-cool summer drinks", "text": "The classic UK use: a few leaves in a glass of Pimm's or a jug of cucumber-water alongside mint. The cool cucumber flavour is at its best on a hot day. Salad use: sparingly in green salads, particularly with cool ingredients (cucumber, lettuce, soft cheeses). Herb butter: chopped salad burnet leaves blended into softened butter. Vinegar: steep fresh leaves in white wine vinegar for a week for a delicate herb vinegar."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick through winter (one of the few evergreens)", "text": "Salad burnet is evergreen in mild UK gardens — the rosette stays green and usable through normal winters, giving fresh leaves when most other herbs are dormant. Pick lightly in winter (don't strip a plant), let it rebuild in spring. In hard winter weather, cover with horticultural fleece to keep the leaves usable."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Divide every 4–5 years in spring", "text": "Salad burnet clumps gradually become congested. In early spring (March), lift the clump, split with a sharp spade into 3–4 sections, replant the best outer pieces in fresh soil. Compost the dead centre. Free replacement plants and a rejuvenated original. Most UK gardens never need more than 2–3 plants total."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Don't confuse with great burnet", "text": "Salad burnet (Sanguisorba minor) is the culinary herb on this page — small toothed leaflets, 30–60 cm rosette, cucumber flavour. Great burnet (Sanguisorba officinalis) is a different species — taller (1 m), larger leaves, deeper red bottle-brush flowers, medicinal but not culinary use. Buy by Latin name to be sure, or check leaf size and flower colour. Most herb nurseries label correctly."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What does salad burnet taste like?", "answer": "Delicate cool cucumber — a clear cucumber flavour with a slight nutty edge, from the young tender leaves. Older outer leaves get tougher and slightly bitter — always pick the youngest inner growth. Cucumber-like enough that salad burnet was traditionally added to wine and water in the era before cucumbers were widely grown in UK gardens. Use fresh — the flavour fades quickly with drying or cooking. Add at the end of preparation, just before serving."},
    {"question": "Will salad burnet survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — very hardy and evergreen (RHS H6–H7). The rosette of leaves stays green and usable through normal UK winters in most regions, dying back only in the hardest winters or extreme cold (Highland Scotland, exposed sites). One of the few culinary herbs that gives usable leaves in January and February — earning its place in any kitchen herb collection for that reason alone. Pick lightly in winter so the rosette can rebuild in spring."},
    {"question": "When and how do I pick salad burnet?", "answer": "Year-round in most UK gardens — most heavily from April to October, lightly through winter. Cut young tender leaves at the base with scissors, starting from the inside of the rosette. Don't pick whole stems — that disturbs the rosette structure. Cut flower stems off as they form (June onwards) to keep leaf production going. Stop heavy picking in late autumn so the plant has a winter reserve of leaves."},
    {"question": "Is salad burnet the same as great burnet?", "answer": "No — different species, different uses. Salad burnet (Sanguisorba minor) is the small culinary herb — 30–60 cm rosette, fine toothed leaflets, delicate cucumber flavour, pinkish-red button flower-heads. Great burnet (Sanguisorba officinalis) is a larger meadow perennial — 1 m tall, larger oval leaflets, deeper red bottle-brush flower-heads, traditional medicinal use (haemostatic — staunching bleeding) but not really culinary. Both are UK natives but the culinary one is the small species. Buy by Latin name."},
    {"question": "Can I grow salad burnet in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — useful for kitchen garden access. A 25 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed with grit, in sun or part shade. Salad burnet in pots gives the same evergreen winter leaves as in the ground, with the convenience of moving the pot near the kitchen door for easy picking. Water consistently in summer (pots dry faster than open ground). Refresh top compost annually. A pot salad burnet lasts 4–5 years before needing division or replacement."},
    {"question": "Is salad burnet really an old-fashioned UK herb?", "answer": "Yes — once common, now rare in modern UK gardens. Mentioned in herbals from Tudor and Stuart times, salad burnet was a standard kitchen-garden plant in pre-greenhouse Britain, valued specifically because it gave fresh cucumber flavour through winter when cucumbers themselves weren't yet available. Francis Bacon wrote in 1625 that the air perfumed with burnet was one of the most pleasant flavours of the spring. Modern UK gardens largely overlook it; supermarket cucumber availability removed the need. But it earns its place for the winter leaf supply and the chalk-grassland tolerance."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'salad-burnet';

-- From salsify.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow salsify direct from late March to early May, in sun in deep, stone-free, well-drained soil — it''s a hardy biennial root vegetable grown for the long pale tap roots harvested in autumn and winter. Salsify is very hardy (RHS H6–H7) — the roots stand in the ground through winter and are lifted as needed from October through to early spring. The famous "oyster" nickname comes from the subtle delicate flavour of the cooked root, said to resemble oysters (a stretch, but the flavour is genuinely unusual). Like parsnip, salsify needs fresh seed each year — viability drops sharply after one year. Pull deep stones before sowing; the roots fork around obstructions, ruining the harvest. An old-fashioned UK veg that''s been almost forgotten — worth growing for the unusual flavour and the satisfaction of a traditional winter root crop.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Prepare deep, stone-free, light soil", "text": "The single most important salsify rule. Like parsnip, salsify produces a long (30 cm) pale tap root that forks around any stone or obstruction, ruining the harvest. Dig the bed deeply (30+ cm) before sowing; remove all stones; break up clay clods. Don't add fresh manure in the season of sowing — fresh organic matter also causes forking. Sandy, stone-free loam is the perfect salsify soil; on clay, raise a bed of imported sandy soil or grow in deep containers."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, late March to early May", "text": "Don't transplant — salsify resents disturbance of the developing taproot. Sow direct in shallow drills 1 cm deep, 30 cm between rows. Use fresh seed from this year's packet — salsify seed loses viability quickly after one year. Germination takes 14–21 days in cool soil. Salsify-only variety: Sandwich Island (mauve-flowered, white-skinned roots) is the UK standard."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to 15 cm apart", "text": "Once seedlings are 5 cm tall (3–4 leaves), thin to one plant every 15 cm. Crowded plants produce smaller, thinner, forked roots. Use the thinnings in salads (very young salsify leaves are mild and edible). Keep the row weed-free — competition reduces root size."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water in summer dry spells", "text": "Salsify produces longer, fatter roots with consistent moisture. Weekly deep watering in dry weather through July and August. Mulch with compost along the row to conserve moisture. Drought stress causes splitting and woody texture in the roots."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Wait for autumn — and a first frost — before lifting", "text": "Don't lift salsify too early. Roots reach full size by September but flavour improves dramatically after the first hard frost (typically late October–November in the UK), which converts starch to sugar in the root. Begin lifting from late October onwards as needed. Lift carefully with a fork — pushed deep alongside the row to lift the long taproots without snapping."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Leave roots in the ground over winter", "text": "Salsify is one of the few winter-storage crops that prefers in-ground storage to a shed. Roots stand in the ground through the worst UK winters (RHS H6–H7) and remain fresh, sweet, and crisp. Lift as needed from October through to March. In hard frost: a mulch of straw or bracken over the row makes lifting easier (frozen ground around the crowns). In cold areas (Highlands, exposed sites), lift before Christmas and store in damp sand in a cool shed."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Cook simply to taste the unusual flavour", "text": "Salsify's oyster flavour is subtle and easily masked. The classic approach: scrub roots, scrape skin (wear gloves — the milky sap stains skin), drop into water with lemon juice (prevents browning), boil 15–20 minutes until tender. Serve with butter and parsley, or in a white sauce. Roasted salsify is wonderful: peeled chunks tossed in oil and roasted alongside potatoes. Salsify soup with leek and cream is the traditional French dish."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Let one or two roots flower and seed for next year", "text": "Save seed. Salsify is biennial — first-year roots are the crop; second-year flowers and seed. Let one or two leftover plants overwinter and flower in May–June of their second year. Beautiful large mauve flowers open at sunrise and close by midday (the Jack-go-to-bed-at-noon common name). Seed heads look like giant dandelion clocks; harvest seed before they shatter. Use fresh next spring — salsify seed loses viability after one year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I harvest salsify?", "answer": "October to March, after the first hard frost. Roots reach full size by September but flavour is much improved by frost (starch converts to sugar). Lift as needed from late October onwards; salsify stands in the ground all winter without deterioration. Use a fork pushed deep alongside the row to lift the long roots whole — snapping a root in half wastes most of it. Use within a week of lifting for best flavour; older lifted roots get woody."},
    {"question": "Why is my salsify forked or stunted?", "answer": "Soil obstructions or fresh organic matter. Salsify's long taproot grows straight only in deep, stone-free, settled soil. Causes of forking: (1) stones in the root zone — dig deeply and remove all stones before sowing; (2) fresh manure in the planting year — apply manure the previous autumn, not the season of sowing; (3) compacted soil — fork the bed to 30+ cm depth; (4) transplanted seedlings — always direct-sow."},
    {"question": "Does salsify really taste like oysters?", "answer": "Subtly, with imagination. The oyster plant nickname is a stretch — salsify has a delicate, slightly sweet, earthy flavour with a faint shellfish note, especially in white sauce or a stew. It's not a precise oyster substitute. Better description: a delicate winter root somewhere between parsnip, artichoke heart, and a faint maritime note. Cook simply (boiled with butter and parsley) for the clearest taste of the unusual flavour."},
    {"question": "How does salsify differ from scorzonera?", "answer": "Different species, same use. Salsify (Tragopogon porrifolius): white-skinned roots, mauve flowers, the more common UK choice. Scorzonera (Scorzonera hispanica): black-skinned roots, yellow flowers, sometimes called black salsify. Both are grown identically, both have the subtle oyster flavour, both store in the ground over winter. Scorzonera roots are slightly stronger-flavoured and have firmer texture. Many UK gardeners grow both for variety; for a single choice, salsify (white) is the easier find at seed suppliers."},
    {"question": "Will salsify survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — exceptionally hardy (RHS H6–H7). Roots stand in the ground through the worst UK winters without protection. Frost actually improves flavour by converting starch to sugar in the root. In hard frost: a mulch of straw or bracken makes lifting easier (otherwise the soil around the crown freezes solid). In very cold regions (Highland Scotland): lift roots before Christmas and store in damp sand in a cool shed (lasts 2–3 months)."},
    {"question": "Why isn't salsify more popular?", "answer": "Largely a matter of unfamiliarity and unfashionable status. Salsify was widely grown in Victorian UK kitchen gardens, fell out of fashion in the 20th century, and never recovered. It's not inherently difficult — easier than parsnip or carrot in many ways (similar care, less prone to root fly, more cold-hardy). The unusual flavour divides modern eaters; some love it, some find it bland. Most UK seed suppliers stock Sandwich Island; specialist nurseries (Real Seeds, Pennard Plants) often have a wider range."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'salsify';

-- From salvia.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant ornamental salvias in spring, in full sun in well-drained soil — Mediterranean origins mean they want poor, gritty conditions and sulk in rich wet ground. Hardy salvias (RHS H5–H6, Salvia nemorosa, S. × sylvestris and their hybrids) form tidy mounds 40–80 cm tall topped with dense spikes of small two-lipped flowers in violet, purple, pink, or white from May through August. Cut the whole plant down to 10 cm after the first flush of flowers fades (the Chelsea chop applied in late June) — this triggers a second, longer flush of flowers lasting into September. Salvias are top-tier bee plants and the showiest are top-tier garden classics: Caradonna (deep violet, near-black stems), Mainacht / May Night (deepest indigo), Amistad (taller half-hardy magenta-purple). Avoid feeding — rich growth flops and flowers less.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick a hardy or half-hardy salvia", "text": "Hardy ornamental salvias (Salvia nemorosa, S. × sylvestris, S. × superba hybrids — RHS H5–H6): 40–80 cm mounds, violet to pink spike flowers, fully hardy, the safe garden choice. Caradonna is the most popular — near-black stems, deep violet flowers, 60–70 cm. Half-hardy salvias (Salvia × jamensis, S. greggii, Amistad and similar — H3–H4): larger shrubby plants, brighter colours (red, magenta, hot pink), more tender — may need winter protection or cuttings. Annual / tender salvias (Salvia splendens, S. farinacea — H1c): bedding plants, treat as summer annuals. For long-term garden value, plant hardy S. nemorosa types."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in full sun and well-drained soil", "text": "Mediterranean origins: salvias want full sun (6+ hours), sharp drainage, and poor-to-ordinary soil. Rich soil = flopping plants + reduced flowers. On clay soils, fork in plenty of horticultural grit at planting. Sandy, chalky, or stony soils suit them perfectly. Spacing: 40 cm for mound-forming hardy salvias, 60 cm for larger shrubby forms."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in spring, after last frost", "text": "Container-grown plants are typically sold in 1–2 L pots from April onwards. Plant after the last frost in your area (mid-April in southern England, late April–May in the Midlands and north). Water in deeply, then leave alone — salvias resent overwatering once established."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't feed", "text": "Feeding is the most common cause of salvia disappointment. Rich soil and fertiliser produce lush growth that flops, blocks airflow, encourages mildew, and reduces flowering. No fertiliser at planting, no fertiliser during the season. A 3 cm gravel mulch around the base keeps weeds down, reflects heat, and avoids the organic-mulch moisture that salvias dislike."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Apply the Chelsea Chop in late June", "text": "The critical husbandry technique for hardy salvias. Once the first flush of flowers fades (late June), cut the whole plant down to 10–15 cm above ground. The plant resprouts and produces a second, longer flush of flowers from August through September. Without the Chelsea Chop, salvias flower once (May–June) and look tatty for the rest of summer. With it, you get 4 months of flowering instead of 6 weeks."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Stake taller varieties in exposed sites", "text": "Most S. nemorosa hybrids (40–70 cm) need no staking. Taller varieties like Amistad (1.2 m) or vigorous Mainacht on rich soil may flop in summer storms — a discreet pea-stake or grow-through plant support placed early lets them grow up through it."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Leave the seedheads or cut for tidiness", "text": "After the second flush fades in September, you have a choice. Leave the dark seedheads for winter structure and bird food (goldfinches eat the seeds). Cut to 10 cm for a tidier border. Don't cut hard in autumn on the half-hardy varieties (Amistad, jamensis types) — the woody stems protect the crown from frost; cut them in late spring instead."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Take cuttings of half-hardy varieties for insurance", "text": "Half-hardy salvias (Amistad, S. greggii, S. × jamensis) may not survive a hard UK winter. Take 8 cm tip cuttings in late summer (August–September): strip lower leaves, push into gritty compost, keep in a cool light spot through winter. Roots form in 3–4 weeks. By spring you have insurance plants in case the parents don't make it through."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I cut back a salvia?", "answer": "Twice a year for the longest display. First cut (the Chelsea Chop) in late June: cut the whole plant down to 10–15 cm after the first flush of flowers fades. This triggers a second flush in August–September. Second cut in late autumn (after second flush): trim to 10 cm for hardy varieties, OR leave the woody stems intact for half-hardy varieties (they protect the crown from frost — cut them in late spring instead)."},
    {"question": "Will salvia survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Hardy varieties (S. nemorosa, S. × sylvestris, S. × superba and their hybrids — RHS H5–H6) survive most UK winters, dying back to the crown each autumn and returning each spring. Half-hardy varieties (Amistad, S. greggii, S. × jamensis — H3–H4) often don't survive a cold wet winter, especially on heavy soils. Two protections for half-hardy salvias: (1) leave the woody stems on through winter (cut in spring) — they protect the crown; (2) take cuttings in August–September as insurance. In colder gardens, grow half-hardy salvias as annuals or in pots that can move under cover."},
    {"question": "Why is my salvia not flowering?", "answer": "Three usual causes. (1) Too rich: salvias flower better in poor soil. Stop feeding, fork grit into the soil. (2) Not enough sun: salvias need 6+ hours direct sun. Move to a sunnier spot. (3) No Chelsea Chop: after the first flush in May–June, the plant goes to seed and stops flowering. Cut it back hard in late June to trigger the second flush."},
    {"question": "What's the best salvia variety for the UK?", "answer": "Caradonna is the modern classic — S. nemorosa cultivar, deep violet flowers on near-black stems, 60–70 cm, very hardy, repeat-flowers reliably. Other excellent hardy choices: Mainacht / May Night (deepest indigo, vigorous), Ostfriesland / East Friesland (compact 40 cm violet), Schwellenburg (pink). For taller late-season colour: Amistad (half-hardy, magenta-purple, 1.2 m, take cuttings as insurance)."},
    {"question": "Can I grow salvia in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — particularly useful for half-hardy varieties. A 30 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed 30% with horticultural grit. Full sun. Don't feed beyond the slow-release in the compost. Water when the surface is bone dry. The pot advantage: half-hardy salvias (Amistad, greggii) can move under cover in winter, surviving where they'd die outdoors. Repot every 2–3 years; refresh top compost annually. Salvias in pots flower as well as in the ground if drainage is right."},
    {"question": "Are salvias good for bees?", "answer": "Outstanding — top tier UK garden plant for pollinators. The two-lipped flower shape is specifically adapted for bumblebees, which are the main pollinators. Long-tongued bumblebees (garden bumblebee, common carder) work the flowers from the front; short-tongued bumblebees sometimes rob by piercing the side of the flower for nectar. Honeybees visit too. Caradonna and similar deep-purple varieties are particularly bee-attractive. A salvia border in full flower is a constant buzz from May through September."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'salvia';

-- From shallot.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant shallot sets from late February to mid-March (or in November–December for autumn-planted overwintering types). Shallots are hardy (RHS H5–H6) and one of the easiest UK alliums — set one bulb in the ground in spring, lift a cluster of 6–12 daughter bulbs in July. They want a sunny well-drained bed with low nitrogen (rich soil makes leaves not bulbs). Plant 15 cm apart with the tip just showing above the soil; allium leaf miner is the main pest now widespread across the UK, so net from March onwards. Lift when the foliage yellows and topples; cure in the sun for two weeks before storing in mesh bags. Shallots keep 8–10 months — well into the following spring. Banana / Echalion shallots (Longor, Pikant) give long elegant bulbs; round shallots (Golden Gourmet, Red Sun) give heavier yields.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose sets or seed", "text": "Sets (small immature bulbs): easier, faster, what most UK gardeners grow. Each set produces a cluster of 6–12 daughter bulbs. Seed: cheaper per plant, gives access to more cultivars, but slower — sow indoors February for transplanting April. Sets are the default choice."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare a sunny well-drained bed", "text": "Full sun, well-drained soil. Fork in compost from the previous autumn but not fresh manure (high nitrogen makes lush leaves and small bulbs). Slightly alkaline (pH 6.5–7.0) is ideal."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant the sets", "text": "Late February to mid-March for spring planting; November–December for overwintering autumn types (Red Sun, Yellow Moon are hardy enough). Push each set into the soil so just the tip shows above the surface, 15 cm apart in rows 30 cm apart. Firm in well."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Net against allium leaf miner", "text": "From March onwards. Phytomyza gymnostoma tunnels into shallot, onion, leek and chive stems and is now widespread across the UK. Net with 5 mm enviromesh on hoops from before adults are active (March) until lifting."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Watch out for birds", "text": "Birds sometimes pull newly-planted sets out, looking for grubs in the dry roots. Net for a fortnight after planting if birds are a problem in your garden."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Hand-weed", "text": "Hand-weed throughout the season; shallots compete poorly with weeds. Hoe carefully between rows; don't disturb the developing clusters."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Stop watering when foliage yellows", "text": "From late June, watch for the foliage to yellow and topple — the sign that bulbs have stopped swelling and are ripening. Stop watering at this point; bulbs cure best in dry conditions."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Lift, cure, and store", "text": "Lift the clusters with a fork in July when foliage has fully yellowed. Separate the bulbs gently by hand; don't break the necks. Lay on top of the soil to dry in the sun for a week if dry; otherwise move to an airy shed for 2 weeks. When necks are fully papery, trim foliage to 5 cm; store in mesh bags or trays in a cool dry place. Keep 8–10 months."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant shallot sets in the UK?", "answer": "Late February to mid-March is the main spring planting window. Earlier-planted sets risk rotting in cold wet soil; later-planted sets don't have time to mature properly. Hardy overwintering varieties (Red Sun, Yellow Moon) can be planted in November–December for an earlier July crop."},
    {"question": "How many shallots do I get from one set?", "answer": "6–12 daughter bulbs per set, depending on cultivar and growing conditions. Banana / Echalion shallots tend to produce fewer, longer bulbs (5–8); round shallots produce more, smaller bulbs (8–12). One small bag of sets typically yields 5–10 kg of shallots from a 5-metre row."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between shallots and onions?", "answer": "Both are Allium cepa but different cultivar groups. Shallot (Aggregatum Group): one set produces a cluster of bulbs; sweeter, milder flavour; longer storage life (8–10 months); easier to grow. Onion (Cepa Group): one set produces one bulb; stronger flavour; varied storage life. Both have the same general care; shallots are arguably easier and more forgiving."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between banana shallots and round shallots?", "answer": "Banana / Echalion shallots (Longor, Pikant, Eschalote Grise): long elongated bulbs, 4–6 per cluster, premium kitchen variety. Round shallots (Golden Gourmet, Red Sun, Mikor): traditional round bulbs, 8–12 per cluster, heavier overall yield. The flavours are similar; the choice is about size and yield-per-set."},
    {"question": "Why are my shallots small and undersized?", "answer": "Most likely: too much nitrogen (rich soil), too much shade, planted too late, or allium leaf miner damage. Don't fork in fresh manure before planting; sun for at least 6 hours a day; plant by mid-March in spring; net against leaf miner from March onwards."},
    {"question": "How long do shallots store?", "answer": "8–10 months for well-cured properly-stored bulbs — well into the following spring. The trick is full curing (necks fully papery and dry before stacking) and cool dry storage. Round shallots typically store longer than banana types. Inspect monthly; soft ones go to the kitchen first."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'shallot';

-- From snowdrop.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant snowdrops "in the green" in February–March (just after flowering, while leaves are still up) rather than as dry bulbs in autumn — they''re one of the few bulbs that establish much better from actively-growing plants than from dormant bulbs, which often desiccate before planting. Snowdrops are very hardy (RHS H7) and the iconic UK winter flower — pure white nodding bell-shaped flowers from late January through February, often appearing through snow. The common snowdrop (Galanthus nivalis) is the UK native naturalised wide; "S. Arnott" is the modern reliable favoured single; the world of named snowdrop cultivars ("galanthophilia") supports a passionate UK following. Plant in damp shade or part shade — under deciduous trees is ideal. Self-seeds and bulb-divides into spreading drifts over 5–10 years; don''t disturb established colonies.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H7',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Plant in the green, not as dry autumn bulbs", "text": "The single most important snowdrop rule. Most autumn bulb suppliers sell snowdrops as dry bulbs, but dry snowdrop bulbs desiccate easily and establish poorly — typically 30–60% loss in the first year. In the green plants (small clumps of actively growing snowdrops with foliage attached, sold in February–March) establish at near 100%. Buy in the green from specialist suppliers (Avon Bulbs, Beth Chatto, Pottertons) for reliable establishment."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pick a snowdrop type", "text": "Common snowdrop (Galanthus nivalis): the UK native (and widely naturalised), 10–15 cm, classic single-flowered, the standard for naturalising in lawns and woodland. The right first snowdrop. Giant snowdrop (G. elwesii): larger flowers, 15–20 cm, broader grey-green leaves, more vigorous, slightly earlier. S. Arnott: a vigorous single cultivar, larger flowers than the species, RHS Award of Garden Merit, the reliable modern choice. Flore Pleno: double-flowered common snowdrop — frilly, full-petalled. Speciality cultivars (Lady Beatrix Stanley, Magnet, Atkinsii): the world of galanthophilia — collected by enthusiasts."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in damp part shade", "text": "Best position: under deciduous trees, alongside shaded paths, in damp grass or wildflower meadow. Snowdrops naturalise best in conditions that get spring sunlight before tree canopy closes, then summer shade. Avoid: deep dry shade (under conifers), full hot sun in dry soil. Soil: moisture-retentive, humus-rich, with leaf-mould worked in."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant at the same depth as the soil line on the leaves", "text": "For in the green plants: plant at exactly the same depth as the green-to-white boundary on the bulb (you can see where the leaves were emerging from soil). For dry bulbs (if you must): plant 7–10 cm deep, blunt end down. Spacing: 5–7 cm apart for natural drifts; 10 cm apart for spaced display."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Let them naturalise — don't disturb established colonies", "text": "Snowdrops spread slowly by both self-seeding and bulb division. An established colony is best left undisturbed — moving snowdrops sets them back. Naturalisation takes 5–10 years but the result is a spreading drift that flowers more impressively every year. Don't mow the area until the snowdrop foliage has died back naturally in May."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Let foliage die back naturally", "text": "The single rule for keeping snowdrops returning: don't cut, mow, or tidy the foliage until it dies back naturally in May. The leaves photosynthesise to fuel next year's flowers — cut them off and you lose next year's bloom. In lawns: skip mowing the snowdrop area until at least mid-May. In borders: leave the yellowing leaves alone; tuck them gently under emerging perennials if they look tatty."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Divide every 5–7 years if congested", "text": "Mature snowdrop clumps can get so congested that flowering drops. Divide in February-March (in the green, after flowering): lift the whole clump with a fork, gently tease apart into smaller clumps (5–10 bulbs each), replant immediately at the same depth in fresh leaf-mould-enriched soil. Water in. This is also how to spread an established colony to new areas of the garden."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Join the galanthophile world (or don't)", "text": "Snowdrop collecting is a serious UK garden subculture — enthusiasts (galanthophiles) collect hundreds of named cultivars, some single bulbs trading for £1,000+. Special snowdrop gardens: Welford Park, Painswick Rococo Garden, Anglesey Abbey, and others open in February for the snowdrop season. For ordinary gardeners: a generous drift of common snowdrop and S. Arnott is more rewarding than a collection of unusual ones — quantity and naturalisation beats rarity for visual effect."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant snowdrops?", "answer": "February to March — in the green, not as dry autumn bulbs. In the green plants (small clumps with active foliage, sold immediately after flowering) establish at near 100%. Dry bulbs sold in autumn suffer 30–60% losses — dry snowdrop bulbs desiccate quickly. Buy from specialist suppliers (Avon Bulbs, Beth Chatto, Pottertons) in the green for reliable establishment. Plant at exactly the same depth as the green-to-white boundary on the leaves indicates."},
    {"question": "When do I cut snowdrop foliage down?", "answer": "Never — let it die back naturally in May. Cutting snowdrop leaves before they yellow naturally starves next year's flower (the leaves photosynthesise to fuel the bulb). In lawns: skip mowing the snowdrop area until at least mid-May, when leaves have yellowed and collapsed. In borders: simply leave the yellowing leaves alone, tuck them gently under emerging summer perennials. The annual decision is when to mow — wait for May."},
    {"question": "Why aren't my snowdrops flowering?", "answer": "Most common cause: dry bulbs that established poorly. Dry snowdrop bulbs lose 30–60% before they establish, and survivors often flower weakly for 2–3 years before recovering. Replant the area with in the green plants for reliable establishment. Other causes: too dry a position (snowdrops want moisture-retentive shade); mowing too early (cuts foliage before it can fuel next year's bloom); congested clumps (divide every 5–7 years)."},
    {"question": "Will snowdrops naturalise in my lawn?", "answer": "Yes — exactly where they thrive. Lawn snowdrops are one of the great UK garden classics. Best lawns: damp grass under deciduous trees, particularly in old or relaxed lawns where conditions match woodland-edge. Plant in the green: scatter small clumps across the lawn in February, plant 5 cm deep into slits cut with a spade. Don't mow the lawn until mid-May. Over 5–10 years the colony spreads into a drift."},
    {"question": "What's the best snowdrop variety for the UK?", "answer": "For naturalising: common snowdrop (Galanthus nivalis) — UK native, vigorous, the right plant for drifts in lawns and under trees. For garden display: S. Arnott — RHS Award of Garden Merit, larger flowers, vigorous, the modern reliable favourite. For something different: Flore Pleno (double common snowdrop) or Magnet (long pedicel making the flowers swing in the breeze). For collectors: hundreds of named cultivars exist — start with the basics, develop preferences over time."},
    {"question": "Can I grow snowdrops in pots?", "answer": "Yes — pots showcase the early flowers brilliantly. Use a 25 cm deep pot of peat-free compost mixed with leaf-mould. Plant in the green in February–March; 10–15 bulbs per pot for a dense display. Move the pot to a doorstep or porch when flowering for close-up enjoyment, return to a shady cool corner for summer. Water consistently through the flowering season. Repot every 2–3 years in February, dividing the clump and refreshing compost."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'snowdrop';

-- From sorrel.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow sorrel direct from March to May, or plant a young clump in spring, in sun or part shade — it''s a hardy perennial leaf herb with a sharp lemony-sour flavour, from the wood-sorrel family (not citrus). Sorrel is very hardy (RHS H6) and crops from April right through to the first hard frosts. Cut flower stems off as soon as they appear — flowering stops leaf production and makes existing leaves tough. Pick young leaves continuously for fish sauces, soups (the classic French sorrel velouté), salads in small quantities, and an unmistakable lemony note in stir-fries. Eat in moderation: sorrel is high in oxalic acid (the source of the sour taste) which is best eaten with care if you have kidney issues or gout. Divide clumps every 4 years. Buckler-leaf sorrel (Rumex scutatus) is a smaller, milder species.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose common or buckler-leaf sorrel", "text": "Common sorrel (Rumex acetosa): the standard UK kitchen sorrel. Large arrow-shaped leaves, sharp lemony flavour, vigorous perennial 60 cm tall when flowering. The functional choice for sauces and soups. Buckler-leaf sorrel (Rumex scutatus): smaller (30 cm), shield-shaped leaves, milder less sharp flavour, more compact and ornamental. Good for salads. Spreads more aggressively than common. Red-veined sorrel (Rumex sanguineus): striking burgundy veins on green leaves, mild flavour, mostly grown for the leaf appearance in salads."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct or plant a clump", "text": "From seed: surface-sow direct in March–May where you want the plant, or in modules. Germinates in 10–14 days at 10–15°C. From plant: a 9 cm or 1 L pot in spring. One plant gives a household more sorrel than it can use; two for a longer picking window."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in sun or part shade, in moisture-retentive soil", "text": "Adaptable to most UK soils provided they don't dry out completely. Sorrel sulks and runs to flower fast in drought; moisture-retentive soil enriched with garden compost keeps leaves tender for longer. Light shade in southern England keeps the plant producing through midsummer; full sun in cooler regions."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Pick young leaves continuously", "text": "Cut whole leaves at the base, starting from the outside of the clump. Pick the youngest tender leaves; older leaves get tough and very high in oxalic acid. Continuous picking is the rhythm — the more you cut, the more it produces. A single clump crops from April through to October."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Cut all flower stems immediately", "text": "The single most important husbandry. Sorrel sends up tall reddish flower spikes from May onwards. Cut them at the base as soon as you see them, before they open. Flowering stops leaf production and makes the remaining leaves tough and bitter. With diligent flower-stem removal, the plant produces tender leaves all season."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water in dry spells", "text": "Sorrel goes bitter and tough in drought. A weekly soak in dry spells keeps the leaves tender and mild-flavoured. Mulch the clump in spring with garden compost to conserve moisture."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Divide every 4 years", "text": "After 4 years the clump gets congested and the centre dies out. Lift in early spring or autumn, split with a spade into 4–6 sections, replant the best outer pieces in fresh soil. Compost the dead centre. Free new plants."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Use in moderation", "text": "The sharp lemon flavour comes from oxalic acid, which inhibits calcium absorption and can be problematic in large quantities for people with kidney issues, gout, or a tendency to oxalate kidney stones. Healthy adults: small quantities (a handful of young leaves in a sauce or salad) are fine and traditional. Cooked sorrel: turns olive-grey and softens dramatically — the classic French sorrel velouté uses several handfuls. Children, pregnant women, and people with kidney/gout issues: very small amounts only, or avoid."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I start picking sorrel?", "answer": "From April onwards — sorrel is one of the first usable herbs each year, often producing leaves before frosts have stopped. Pick young leaves from the outside of the clump. By May the plant is in full production. Continuous picking through summer keeps it generating tender new growth. Stops cropping after first hard autumn frosts (late October–November)."},
    {"question": "Why is my sorrel bitter or tough?", "answer": "Three causes. (1) Flowering: the plant has sent up flower stems and is no longer producing tender leaves — cut every flower stem off at the base as soon as it appears. (2) Drought: sorrel turns tough in dry spells — water weekly in summer. (3) Old leaves: pick only young leaves from the centre; outer older leaves get tough and high in oxalic acid. With weekly flower-stem removal and watering, a sorrel plant produces tender leaves from April to October."},
    {"question": "Is sorrel safe to eat?", "answer": "Yes, in moderation, for healthy adults. Sorrel contains oxalic acid (the source of its lemon-sour flavour), which inhibits calcium absorption and can contribute to kidney stones in susceptible people. Small culinary quantities (a handful of young leaves in soup or sauce) are traditional and fine for most adults. Avoid or use very sparingly if you have kidney issues, gout, a tendency to kidney stones, are pregnant, or feeding small children. Cooked sorrel retains the oxalic acid — cooking doesn't neutralise it."},
    {"question": "Will sorrel survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — fully hardy (RHS H6). The foliage dies back to the crown after hard frosts and the plant returns from the rootstock each spring (March in mild years, April further north). Survives any UK winter without protection — sorrel is a tough native UK plant found wild in meadows. In a mild winter you may keep picking small leaves through to January."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between common sorrel and French sorrel?", "answer": "Common sorrel (Rumex acetosa): the UK native, large arrow-shaped leaves, sharp lemon flavour, vigorous 60 cm clump. The standard kitchen sorrel. French sorrel is a confusing name — usually means buckler-leaf sorrel (Rumex scutatus), a smaller (30 cm) species with shield-shaped leaves and milder flavour, used in classical French cooking. The Cookery sense of French sorrel is often just good sorrel cooked the French way, though, regardless of species. Either works for sorrel velouté."},
    {"question": "How do I make sorrel sauce or soup?", "answer": "Classic French sorrel velouté: sweat 4 large handfuls of young sorrel leaves in butter with a chopped shallot for 5 minutes (it'll wilt to almost nothing and turn olive-grey). Add 500 ml chicken or vegetable stock, simmer 10 minutes, blend smooth, stir in 100 ml double cream and a squeeze of lemon. Serve with poached or grilled white fish. The olive colour looks unappealing but the flavour is delicate, lemony, and unmistakable."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sorrel';

-- From spinach.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow spinach seed direct from March to May for spring crops and from August to September for autumn and winter. True spinach is hardy (RHS H4) but bolts fast in heat and long days — UK summer is exactly when you can''t grow it, so skip June and July outdoor sowings. Sow thinly in moist, fertile soil; thin to 15 cm apart for full-size plants. Pick outer leaves continuously from 6 weeks after sowing. Slugs are the dominant problem on damp ground — apply nematodes and check at dusk. For a summer crop, grow chard or perpetual spinach instead — both are Beta vulgaris relatives that don''t bolt the way true spinach does. Winter-hardy varieties (Medania, Giant Winter) overwinter outdoors in southern England.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "March to May for spring crops; August to September for autumn and winter. Avoid June and early July — spinach bolts in heat. 2 cm deep drill, sow thinly, cover lightly."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Thin to 15 cm", "text": "When seedlings are 5 cm tall, thin to 15 cm apart for full-size plants. Closer (5 cm) for cut-and-come-again baby leaves. Eat the thinnings."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Water regularly", "text": "Spinach bolts in dry conditions as well as in heat. Water deeply twice a week; mulch with grass clippings to hold moisture."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Protect from slugs", "text": "Spinach is among the slug favourites. Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) to warm moist soil two weeks before sowing. Beer traps, hand-picking at dusk, copper rings around seedlings."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick outer leaves continuously", "text": "From 6 weeks after sowing. Pick the outer leaves at the base of the plant; the centre keeps producing. Don't strip a plant — leave 4–6 leaves to power further growth."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Sow winter varieties for over-wintering", "text": "For winter cropping, sow Medania or Giant Winter in August–September. They overwinter outdoors in southern England (cloche-protected further north) and give an early spring pick before bolting."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Consider chard for summer", "text": "If you want spinach-like leaves through the UK summer, grow chard (also called Swiss chard or perpetual spinach) instead — same family, but doesn't bolt."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow spinach in the UK?", "answer": "March to May for spring crops; August to September for autumn and winter. Skip June and early July — spinach bolts in long summer days and heat. A late-July sowing of a bolt-resistant cultivar can sometimes work in the cool north. For continuous picking, sow every 3 weeks in spring and again in autumn."},
    {"question": "Why is my spinach bolting (going to seed)?", "answer": "Heat (above 25°C), long summer days, drought, or simply the natural lifecycle. True spinach bolts as days lengthen towards mid-summer no matter what — that's why UK summer is the wrong time for it. Once a plant bolts, pull it; the leaves get bitter. For summer leaf, grow chard instead."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between spinach and chard?", "answer": "Spinacia oleracea (true spinach) is the classic baby-leaf or hearting type — quick-growing, tender, but bolts fast. Beta vulgaris subsp. cicla (chard, Swiss chard, perpetual spinach) is a different species despite the name — bigger leaves, thicker midribs, much slower to bolt. Use chard for UK summer; true spinach for spring and autumn."},
    {"question": "How do I stop slugs eating my spinach?", "answer": "Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) to warm moist soil two weeks before sowing — they kill slugs from the inside. Beer traps near the bed catch a useful number. Hand-pick at dusk on damp evenings. Copper rings around seedlings work in the early stages. Pellets approved for organic use (ferric phosphate) for severe pressure."},
    {"question": "Can I grow spinach in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — a 30 cm pot grows 6–8 cut-and-come-again plants. Use peat-free compost; water daily in warm weather; position in part-shade in summer to delay bolting. Container spinach is the easiest way to grow it in summer because you can move it to cool conditions."},
    {"question": "When can I pick spinach?", "answer": "From about 6 weeks after sowing for baby leaves; 8–10 weeks for full-size leaves. Pick outer leaves regularly; the centre keeps producing for several weeks before the plant bolts. Plants give 4–8 weeks of cropping before they need replacing. Winter-sown varieties pick from October through to March in southern England."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'spinach';

-- From spring-onion.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow spring onion seed direct from March to September, every 3 weeks, in sun in any decent garden soil — they''re one of the easiest UK kitchen-garden crops and a steady supply takes minimal effort. Spring onions are hardy (RHS H4–H5) and a late-summer or early-autumn sowing overwinters to give the earliest fresh onion of the year in March–April. White Lisbon is the UK workhorse — reliable, mild, classic. White Lisbon Winter Hardy is the autumn-sowing variant for overwintering. Pull when stems are pencil-thick (15–20 cm tall) — leaving longer makes them tough. Successional sowing every 3 weeks is the only way to keep tender spring onions coming all season. They''re useful companion plants for carrots — the onion scent confuses carrot fly.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H4',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H5',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick the right variety for the season", "text": "White Lisbon: the UK standard — pencil-thin stems, mild flavour, fast-growing, the choice for spring–summer sowing. White Lisbon Winter Hardy: bred for autumn sowing and overwintering — sow August–September, pull March–April for the earliest fresh onions of the year. Red Beard / Apache: red-skinned, ornamental and tasty in salads. Welsh onion (A. fistulosum — true bunching onion, perennial): forms clumps that divide year after year; pull a few stems at a time, the clump regrows."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow direct, in a sunny spot", "text": "Spring onions don't transplant well (small fragile roots). Sow direct in shallow drills 1 cm deep, 15 cm between rows. Thin sowing — aim for seeds 1–2 cm apart along the drill. Germination in 14–21 days at 10–15°C. Sunny position, ordinary garden soil — no need for special preparation."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Sow successionally every 3 weeks", "text": "The key to continuous spring onions. Sow a fresh short row every 3 weeks from mid-March until mid-September. Each row is ready 8–10 weeks after sowing. Skip the very hot midsummer weeks if your garden gets dry — heat-stressed spring onions bolt to flower rather than producing usable stems."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Sow in August for overwintering", "text": "The most useful sowing window for early-spring picking. Sow White Lisbon Winter Hardy (or similar hardy variety) in late August. The seedlings overwinter as small plants (covered by snow if necessary — they're fine), then resume growth from early March and are pullable from late March onwards. Earliest fresh onions of the year, when little else is yet usable."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Thin to 2–3 cm apart", "text": "Once seedlings have 3–4 leaves, thin to one plant every 2–3 cm. Don't pull thinnings — eat them (baby spring onions, salad-tender, perfect for garnishing). The remaining plants will grow to full size with proper spacing."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Water in dry spells", "text": "Spring onions bolt to flower and turn bitter in drought. Water deeply weekly in dry weather to keep stems tender. Mulch with fine compost along the row to conserve moisture. Pot-grown spring onions need daily watering once growing strongly."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pull when pencil-thick", "text": "Pull (don't cut) when stems are 15–20 cm tall and pencil-thick — typically 8–10 weeks from sowing. Slip a fork under the row to loosen the soil before pulling — saves snapping off stems. Use within 3–4 days — spring onions wilt fast. Trim the roots and the very top of the green leaves; the whole rest of the plant is edible."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Companion plant with carrots", "text": "The classic UK companion: alternate spring onion rows with carrot rows. The onion scent confuses carrot root fly (which detects carrots by smell), giving significant pest reduction without chemicals. Plant 1 row of spring onions between every 2 rows of carrots. Don't plant near peas or beans — alliums slow legume growth."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I pull spring onions?", "answer": "8–10 weeks after sowing, when the stems are 15–20 cm tall and pencil-thick. Don't wait longer — overgrown spring onions get tough, fibrous, and develop a stronger flavour that's less pleasant in salads. Slip a fork under the row to loosen the soil before pulling — snapping the stem off saves the root in the ground but loses the white base. Pull whole row at peak, use what you need, fridge the rest (3–4 days max)."},
    {"question": "How do I get spring onions in March and April?", "answer": "Sow White Lisbon Winter Hardy in late August. The seedlings establish before winter, overwinter as small plants under snow if necessary, and resume growth in early March. First pulling: late March in southern England, mid-April in the Midlands, late April in Scotland. This gives you fresh-pulled spring onions weeks before any spring-sown crop is ready. The earliest fresh kitchen-garden onion of the year."},
    {"question": "Why are my spring onions bolting (flowering)?", "answer": "Heat stress + dry soil. Spring onions bolt to flower (a single tall flowering stem rises) in hot dry conditions, then leaf production stops and the bulb base toughens. Three preventatives. (1) Water consistently in dry weather. (2) Skip the hottest midsummer sowing window if your garden is dry. (3) Pull at the right size (pencil-thick) rather than letting them stand. Bolted spring onions are still edible — the flavour just becomes stronger and the stem tougher."},
    {"question": "What's the best spring onion variety for the UK?", "answer": "White Lisbon — the workhorse of UK gardens, mild and reliable for March–September sowings. White Lisbon Winter Hardy — the autumn-sowing variant for March–April pulling. Red Beard or Apache for red-skinned salad colour. Welsh onion for a perennial bunching onion that crops year after year from the same clump (different species — Allium fistulosum). For a single variety, White Lisbon covers most needs."},
    {"question": "Can I grow spring onions in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — perfect for pots. A 25 cm pot of peat-free compost holds 20–30 spring onions. Sow thinly, thin to 2–3 cm apart, water consistently. Pot-grown spring onions pull at the same size as in-ground ones. Continuous supply: sow a fresh pot every 3 weeks. A pot lasts about 6 weeks from sowing to last pull. Three pots in rotation gives a household continuous spring onions from May to October."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between spring onions and Welsh onions?", "answer": "Different species, different growth pattern. Spring onions (Allium cepa varieties): single stem from a single seed, pulled whole, annual crop — must re-sow. Welsh onion / bunching onion (Allium fistulosum): perennial, forms clumps that divide year after year, pull individual stems from the clump while it regrows. Welsh onions are slightly stronger flavoured and very low-maintenance once established. Many UK kitchen gardens grow both — Welsh onion as a permanent clump, spring onions for the milder annual crop."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'spring-onion';

-- From strawberry.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant strawberry runners in late summer to early autumn (August to September) for a full crop the following June. The plant itself is hardy (RHS H5) but the flowers are frost-tender, so cover with fleece if a late April frost is forecast. Plant 30 cm apart in rich, well-drained soil in full sun. Choose early (Honeoye), mid (Cambridge Favourite, Sonata), late (Symphony) or perpetual (Albion, Mara des Bois) varieties for a long picking season. Mulch with straw or matting once flowers appear to keep fruit clean and deter slugs. Net the plants from late May — birds strip a row overnight. Replace plants every 3–4 years; older plants exhaust and crop less.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy certified virus-free runners", "text": "Late summer (August to September) is the best planting time. Buy certified virus-free runners from a nursery, not from a friend's diseased patch. Cold-stored runners can be planted spring through June for a smaller late-summer crop."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare the bed", "text": "Full sun, well-drained, slightly acidic soil. Fork compost or well-rotted manure into the bed. Strawberries hate waterlogged ground."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant", "text": "30 cm apart in rows 75 cm apart. Set the crown level with the soil surface — neither buried nor exposed. Water in well."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Mulch with straw", "text": "When flowers appear in April, tuck clean straw or strawberry matting under the plants. Keeps fruit off the soil, deters slugs, suppresses weeds."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Protect flowers from late frost", "text": "Strawberry flowers are killed by frost — black centres after a cold night mean no fruit. Cover with fleece from mid-April onwards if frost is forecast."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Net against birds", "text": "From late May, net the plants firmly on hoops at least 30 cm above the foliage. Birds strip a row in a day. Use proper bird netting with 1 cm mesh; nothing smaller (birds and hedgehogs get caught in fine mesh)."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick ripe", "text": "Pick when fully red with a sweet smell. Take with the calyx attached (no green stem in the punnet). Pick every other day during peak — over-ripe fruit invites grey mould (botrytis) which spreads."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Remove runners (or use them)", "text": "Through summer, the parent plant sends out runners with baby plants. Cut them off for bigger fruit on the parent; or peg the first plantlet on each runner into a pot of compost to root your next year's stock."},
    {"step": 9, "name": "Replace every 3–4 years", "text": "Productivity drops sharply after year 3. Take young plants from the runners of the best parents, plant a fresh bed each year, retire the oldest."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant strawberries in the UK?", "answer": "Late summer (August to September) for the biggest crop the following June. Spring-planted bare-root or cold-stored runners give a smaller crop in their first year. Autumn-planted strawberries establish over winter and crop fully their first summer."},
    {"question": "Why have my strawberry flowers gone black in the middle?", "answer": "Frost damage. Strawberry flowers are tender — a single light frost during flowering kills the developing fruit, leaving the centre black. Cover with fleece from mid-April onwards on clear nights. Lost flowers don't re-form on the same truss."},
    {"question": "How long do strawberry plants last?", "answer": "3–4 years productively. Year one (autumn-planted) gives a strong first crop; years two and three are peak; from year four cropping declines and plants get diseased. Replace stock continuously by rooting runners from your best parents."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between June-bearing and perpetual strawberries?", "answer": "June-bearing (also called summer-fruiting): one big flush in June and early July. Honeoye is early, Cambridge Favourite mid-season, Symphony late. Perpetual (everbearing): smaller fruit but cropping in waves from June through to October. Albion and Mara des Bois are perpetual. Most allotments grow both for a long season."},
    {"question": "How do I stop slugs eating my strawberries?", "answer": "Mulch with clean straw or strawberry matting to keep fruit off the ground. Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) to warm moist soil in spring. Beer traps around the bed. Pick fully-ripe fruit promptly. Hanging baskets and tabletop grow-bags side-step the slug problem entirely."},
    {"question": "Can I grow strawberries in containers in the UK?", "answer": "Excellent for containers. Hanging baskets, strawberry pots, troughs, grow-bags — all work, and elevation cuts slug damage to near zero. Use peat-free multi-purpose compost; feed weekly with high-potash liquid from first flowers; water daily in summer. Container plants need replacing every 2 years (less root space accelerates decline)."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'strawberry';

-- From sunflower.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow sunflower seed direct from late April outdoors or in modules indoors from March. Sunflowers are frost-tender annuals (RHS H1c) and want a sunny sheltered spot in any decent garden soil. Tall varieties (Russian Giant, Mongolian Giant) reach 3–4 m and need staking; medium types (Velvet Queen, Italian White) reach 1.5–2 m and stand on their own; dwarf varieties (Teddy Bear, Sunspot) reach 45–60 cm and suit containers. Plant 30–60 cm apart depending on type. Slugs strip seedlings — protect for the first 4 weeks. Pick stems for cut flowers when the petals have just opened and the back of the head is still green. Leave seed heads on tall plants in autumn for the birds.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "Late April direct outdoors in sheltered gardens; mid-March to mid-April indoors in modules elsewhere. 2 cm deep, 2 seeds per station, thin to the strongest. Germination in 7–10 days at 15°C+."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Protect seedlings from slugs", "text": "Slugs and snails strip emerging sunflowers in a single night. Use nematodes, beer traps, hand-picking at dusk, or copper rings around new transplants. Pressure drops sharply once stems are 30 cm tall."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant out after the last frost", "text": "Outdoor-sown plants are fine in situ; module-grown seedlings transplant after the last frost (mid-May south, early June north). Space 30 cm for dwarf, 60 cm for medium-to-tall varieties."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Stake tall varieties early", "text": "Russian Giant, Mongolian Giant, Titan reach 3–4 m and topple in wind. Put in a tall cane (2.5 m) at planting; tie the stem in every 30 cm as it grows. Tie loosely with soft twine."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water deeply through summer", "text": "Sunflowers are reasonably drought-tolerant once established but a dry July gives smaller flowers. Water deeply every 5–7 days in dry weather. Mulch the base."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut for vases or leave for seeds", "text": "For cut flowers: cut stems with secateurs when the petals have just opened and the back of the head is still green. They'll last 5–7 days in water. To leave heads on the plant for autumn seed: don't cut; the seeds ripen as the head turns brown and droops."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Leave seed heads for birds", "text": "In October–November, when seed heads have turned brown and dry, leave them on the plant. Goldfinches, tits, and house sparrows feast on them through autumn. Cut the dried heads when most seeds are eaten or store somewhere dry to feed birds through winter."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow sunflower seeds in the UK?", "answer": "Late April to early May direct outdoors in southern England; mid-March to mid-April indoors in modules for transplanting. Don't sow direct outside before mid-April — cold wet soil rots the seeds. A sheltered sunny spot is essential; sunflowers in the shade are leggy and stunted."},
    {"question": "How tall do sunflowers grow?", "answer": "Varies hugely by cultivar. Dwarf (Teddy Bear, Sunspot, Pacino): 45–60 cm — ideal for containers and small gardens. Medium (Velvet Queen, Italian White, Vanilla Ice): 1.5–2 m — most flower beds. Tall (Russian Giant, Mongolian Giant, Titan): 3–4 m — back of a border with staking. Multi-headed (Soraya, Lemon Queen): 1.5–2 m with many smaller heads on one plant for cut flowers."},
    {"question": "How do I stop slugs eating sunflower seedlings?", "answer": "The first 4 weeks after emergence are the danger zone. Apply nematodes (Phasmarhabditis hermaphrodita) to warm moist soil before sowing. Beer traps near the bed. Hand-pick at dusk in damp weather. Copper rings on bare soil around each seedling. Once stems are 30 cm tall and starting to lignify, slug pressure drops sharply."},
    {"question": "Why is my sunflower not following the sun?", "answer": "Mature flower heads don't track the sun — only young growth does (a phenomenon called heliotropism, driven by uneven cell elongation on the shaded side). Once the bud opens, the flower fixes facing east (where it warms first in the morning), and stays there. Mature sunflower fields all face the same direction. Nothing wrong with your plant."},
    {"question": "When should I cut sunflowers for vases?", "answer": "Just as the petals open and the back of the head is still green. Earlier than this and they don't fully open; later, they're too far gone for a long vase life. Cut in the cool of the morning, plunge straight into water, recut underwater before arranging. Sunflowers last 5–7 days in clean water (change daily)."},
    {"question": "Can I save sunflower seeds for next year?", "answer": "Yes — let one or two heads mature fully on the plant. When the back of the head turns brown and dry and the seeds are plump and loose, cut the whole head and hang upside down in an airy dry place for 2 weeks to fully dry. Rub or pick seeds out, store in a paper envelope in a cool dry place. F1 hybrids don't come true from saved seed; open-pollinated varieties (Russian Giant, Mammoth Russian) do."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sunflower';

-- From swede.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow swede seed direct from May to early June for autumn–winter harvest. Swede is very hardy (RHS H6) and the orange-fleshed staple of Scottish neeps and tatties — slower-growing than turnip, hardier, and bigger-rooted. Sow thinly in deep stone-free soil; thin to 30 cm apart. Watch for flea beetle on young leaves (fleece protection or a slightly later sowing dodges the worst). Lift from late October onwards; frost actually improves the flavour. Most allotment growers lift in November and store in damp sand for winter; in milder gardens swedes can be left in the ground under straw mulch and pulled as needed. Marian (the UK standard) and Brora (newer, club-root resistant) are the reliable choices.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "May to early June. Don't sow earlier — May-sown swedes outgrow flea beetle damage faster than April sowings. 1 cm deep drill, sow thinly, cover lightly."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prepare deep firm soil", "text": "Full sun, firm fertile soil, slightly alkaline (lime if below pH 6.5). Fork in compost from the previous autumn; don't add fresh manure (it makes the roots fork). Stone-free deep tilth gives the cleanest roots."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Thin to 30 cm", "text": "When seedlings have 4 true leaves, thin to 30 cm apart in rows 45 cm apart. Eat the thinnings — small swede leaves are excellent as greens."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Protect young leaves from flea beetle", "text": "Cover with fleece or enviromesh from sowing through the first 4–5 weeks. Adult flea beetles pock-mark young leaves; established plants outgrow the damage."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water through dry spells", "text": "Swedes need consistent moisture for the first 6 weeks. Drought makes them woody and split. Water deeply once a week if no rain; mulch with grass clippings or straw."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Watch for club root", "text": "Soil-borne disease (Plasmodiophora brassicae) that distorts roots and stunts growth. Once present, almost impossible to eradicate. Lime the bed to pH 7+ before planting; raise seedlings in modules so they have strong root systems before transplanting; consider club-root-resistant cultivars (Invitation F1) for severely-affected beds."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Lift after first frosts", "text": "From late October. Frost converts starches to sugars and improves the flavour dramatically. Lift gently with a fork; trim tops 2 cm above the root; brush off loose soil. Don't wash."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Store or leave in ground", "text": "Store: layer roots in damp sand in a wooden box in a frost-free shed. Keep until March. Leave in ground: in milder gardens (southern England, mild south-coast), swedes hold in the ground under straw mulch and you pull as needed. In cold gardens lift before deep frost."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow swede in the UK?", "answer": "May to early June. Don't sow earlier than May — April-sown swedes face the worst of the flea beetle generation and grow slowly through cold weather. May-sown plants outgrow the pest faster. Single sowing covers the season; swedes are not a successional crop."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between swede and turnip?", "answer": "Different species. Swede (Brassica napus, subsp. rapifera): yellow-orange flesh, dense and slightly waxy when raw, slow-growing (24+ weeks from sowing), very hardy (RHS H6), classic winter storage root. Turnip (Brassica rapa): white flesh, fast-growing (6–12 weeks), tender when young, hardy (H5). Swedes are the neeps in Scottish neeps and tatties. Different in flavour, hardiness, and growing schedule."},
    {"question": "Why are my swedes small and woody?", "answer": "Drought stress during the first 6 weeks after sowing is the usual cause. Swedes need consistent moisture to develop full-sized roots; once they check, they don't catch up. Other causes: club-root infection, sowing too late, or a cultivar wrong for UK conditions."},
    {"question": "How do I stop club root on brassicas?", "answer": "Lime the soil to pH 7+ before planting. Raise transplants in modules so they have a strong root system before going in. Use brassica rotation (don't plant brassicas in the same bed two years running). If your bed is heavily infected, the only solution is to skip brassicas in that bed for 7+ years; or grow in containers of fresh compost. Club-root-resistant cultivars (Invitation F1 swede, Kilaton F1 cabbage) are useful tools."},
    {"question": "When are swedes ready to harvest?", "answer": "From late October onwards, but frost-sweetened roots are best — wait until after the first hard frost in November for the transformation in flavour. The first lifts can be cricket-ball-sized; later lifts can be tennis-ball or larger. Smaller is sweeter; over-large swedes get woody."},
    {"question": "How long do swedes store?", "answer": "Lifted and stored in damp sand in a frost-free shed: 4–5 months, until March. Left in the ground under straw mulch in milder gardens: until February or March, lifted as needed. They hold up far better than turnips in storage and are one of the most useful UK storage vegetables."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'swede';

-- From sweet-cherry.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant sweet cherry trees bare-root from November to March, in a sheltered sunny spot in well-drained soil — and plan how you''ll net them before you plant, because blackbirds and starlings will strip an unprotected cherry tree in a day. Sweet cherries are hardy (RHS H6) but the early blossom (April) is vulnerable to spring frosts. Stella is the UK''s reliable choice — fully self-fertile, heavy cropper, classic dark red dessert flavour. Like plums, never prune sweet cherries in winter (silver leaf disease) — prune in June–August. The Gisela 5 rootstock revolutionised UK cherry growing — produces small (2.5–3 m) trees that fit small gardens and can be netted as a single unit. Pick when fully coloured (almost black for dark cherries, deep red for Stella) and the stalk pulls away cleanly with the fruit.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose Stella if you only plant one", "text": "Stella is the UK's safest sweet cherry — fully self-fertile (the first commercial self-fertile cherry), reliable cropping, dark red dessert flavour. Other reliable self-fertile UK varieties: Sunburst (very dark, large fruit, vigorous), Lapins (red-orange, late, heavy cropper), Sweetheart (heart-shaped, late, very sweet). All four crop without a partner. Non-self-fertile cherries (Bigarreau Gaucher, heritage varieties) need a partner from the same flowering group."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pick Gisela 5 rootstock for almost any garden", "text": "The rootstock that transformed UK cherry growing. Gisela 5 (semi-dwarfing): 2.5–3 m tree, easily nettable, productive from year 3. Colt (semi-vigorous): 3.5–4.5 m bush, heavier crop but harder to protect from birds. F12/1 (vigorous): 6–8 m, traditional orchard, very long-lived but unmanageable for nets. Most UK garden cherries should be on Gisela 5."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in a sheltered, sunny, frost-free spot", "text": "Position matters as much as variety. Cherries flower in April — earlier than apples — and the blossom is vulnerable to frost. South or west-facing wall positions are ideal; avoid frost pockets. Well-drained soil — cherries hate waterlogged ground far more than most fruit. On clay, plant on a raised mound."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plan bird protection before planting", "text": "Sweet cherries without netting yield almost nothing for the gardener. Blackbirds and starlings strip a tree in a single morning when the fruit ripens. Plan one of: (1) a full walk-in fruit cage (the long-term answer), (2) a temporary nylon net thrown over the whole tree once berries colour, or (3) planting only on Gisela 5 which keeps the tree small enough to drape with mesh from a frame. Without a plan to net, sweet cherries are a frustrating crop."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March while dormant. Soak the roots for an hour. Plant at the same depth the tree was in the nursery. Stake permanently for Gisela 5 rootstocks. Water in deeply."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "DON'T prune in winter — prune June–August", "text": "Same rule as plum. Pruning when dormant exposes the tree to silver leaf disease (Chondrostereum purpureum). Prune in June, July, or early August when wounds heal fast. Light annual pruning is much better than occasional hard cuts. Remove dead/diseased/crossing branches; thin overcrowded growth; shorten leaders by a third on young trees. Mature cherries need very little pruning."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Net the tree once berries colour", "text": "Hang netting over the tree as soon as the first berries start turning red (mid-June for early varieties, July for Stella, late July for late varieties). Use fine mesh (1 cm) to exclude both birds and the occasional cherry slug (sawfly larvae). Tuck the mesh under at the base — birds find any gap. Remove after the final pick to allow the tree to breathe."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick at full colour", "text": "Cherries don't ripen off the tree — pick when fully coloured. Stella: dark red (not just red). Sunburst: nearly black with a slight glow. Lapins: orange-red darkening to deep red. Sweetheart: deep red. The stalk should pull away from the spur with the fruit (the spur stays on the tree). Pick over the tree every 2–3 days through the ripening window; cherries split in heavy rain and spoil within days of full ripeness."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When do I prune a sweet cherry tree?", "answer": "June, July, or early August — NEVER in winter. Same rule as plums: pruning in dormant season exposes the tree to silver leaf disease, an airborne fungus that enters fresh wounds and gradually kills branches. Summer wounds heal fast and resist infection. Mature sweet cherries need very little pruning — just remove dead, diseased, or crossing branches and thin to improve airflow. Avoid hard pruning; cherries don't tolerate it as well as apples or plums."},
    {"question": "Do I need two cherry trees for pollination?", "answer": "No, if you choose a self-fertile variety. Stella, Sunburst, Lapins, Sweetheart all crop without a partner — these four self-fertile varieties cover most UK garden needs. Self-fertile cherries are the obvious choice for a single-tree garden. If your cherry is not self-fertile (e.g. Early Rivers, heritage varieties), you need a partner from the same flowering group — check the variety details at purchase."},
    {"question": "How do I protect cherries from birds?", "answer": "Net the tree completely. Three approaches. (1) Walk-in fruit cage with 1 cm mesh — the long-term answer for multiple soft fruit and cherries. Expensive but solves the problem permanently. (2) Temporary net thrown over the whole tree once fruit starts colouring, tucked under at the base. Works on Gisela 5 trees up to 3 m. (3) Mesh cone or frame over a small tree. Without netting, blackbirds and starlings strip the crop in a day — there's no way around this."},
    {"question": "My cherries split before they ripen — what's wrong?", "answer": "Sudden heavy rain after a dry spell. The fruit absorbs water through the skin and splits because the flesh expands faster than the skin can stretch. Three preventatives. (1) Consistent watering: regular weekly watering in dry spells avoids the boom-and-bust pattern. (2) Pick early: as soon as fruit is fully coloured, before the next rain. (3) Cover the tree with plastic sheet over a frame when rain is forecast just before ripening (commercial growers do this routinely)."},
    {"question": "What's the best sweet cherry variety for the UK?", "answer": "Stella — the safest, most reliable. Fully self-fertile, heavy cropper, classic dark red dessert flavour, available everywhere. Other excellent self-fertile options: Sunburst (very large dark fruit), Lapins (very heavy cropper, late), Sweetheart (deep flavour, very late). Plant on Gisela 5 rootstock for a manageable 2.5–3 m tree. Avoid heritage varieties unless you have a real interest in old fruit — they're often hard to find pollinators for."},
    {"question": "Can I grow a sweet cherry in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — on Gisela 5 rootstock only. A 50 cm pot of peat-free compost, in a sheltered sunny spot. Pot cherries need: (1) permanent staking, (2) slow-release fertiliser in April plus monthly liquid feed, (3) consistent watering (drying out causes fruit drop and splitting), (4) summer pruning to keep size manageable, (5) bird protection — a fine mesh draped over the whole pot tree from a frame. Pot cherries crop reliably though smaller — perfect for a sunny patio."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sweet-cherry';

-- From sweet-chestnut.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant sweet chestnut bare-root from November to March, in well-drained acid to neutral soil — only if you have space, because mature trees reach 20–30 m and live 500+ years. Sweet chestnut is hardy (RHS H6) and well-naturalised in the UK (Roman introduction rather than true native), thriving on sandy or loamy soils but failing on chalk and shallow soils. Plant two different varieties for cross-pollination — sweet chestnut is mostly self-incompatible, and the wind-pollinated catkin pollen needs a different cultivar within range. The edible nuts ripen in October, encased in fiercely spiny green burrs that split open to reveal 2–3 glossy mahogany-brown nuts. Don''t confuse with horse chestnut (Aesculus hippocastanum, the conker tree) — horse chestnut nuts are not edible. Roast over an open fire in late autumn, or stuff into the Christmas turkey.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Don't confuse with horse chestnut", "text": "Critical to get right. Sweet chestnut (Castanea sativa) is the edible nut tree — long narrow leaves with sharp serrated edges, catkins in summer, spiny green burrs containing glossy brown edible nuts. Horse chestnut (Aesculus hippocastanum, the conker tree) is NOT edible — palmate leaves (5–7 fingers spreading from one point), conical pink-white flower candles, spiny green husks containing glossy brown conkers. Conkers cause stomach upset if eaten. The leaf shape is the easiest distinguishing test — long single leaves = sweet chestnut; palmate clustered fingers = horse chestnut."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Check you have the right soil", "text": "Sweet chestnut wants acid to neutral, well-drained, deep soil — sandy or loamy, typical of UK lowland woods. It fails on chalk, alkaline soils, shallow soils over chalk, and waterlogged ground. Test pH (£5 kit) before planting. If your soil is alkaline or shallow, plant walnut instead — easier on UK chalk soils, similar nut crop."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant at least two varieties for cross-pollination", "text": "Sweet chestnut is mostly self-incompatible — own pollen doesn't fertilise own flowers. Plant 2 different varieties within 50 m for proper cross-pollination. Marigoule and Marsol are the modern UK pairing — heavy cropping, large nuts, good flavour. Bouche de Bétizac is the heaviest cropper. Maraval is a reliable pollinator. For a single specimen with no nut crop, the species form (Castanea sativa unselected) gives a magnificent tree with smaller nuts that's still ornamentally valuable."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter, allow real space", "text": "November to March while dormant. Sweet chestnut establishes faster from bare-root than container plants. Spacing: 12–15 m apart for nut-cropping orchards; 20 m for free-standing specimens; don't plant within 10 m of a building or path. A mature sweet chestnut reaches 20–30 m tall and 15–20 m crown spread — this is a tree for parks, large gardens, and orchards, not small suburban plots."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Be patient — first crop in 5–10 years", "text": "Sweet chestnut from a grafted variety crops from year 5–7 onwards. Seedling trees (cheaper but unselected) take 10–15+ years to first crop and may produce smaller nuts. Be patient — once established, sweet chestnuts crop reliably and heavily for 100+ years from a single tree."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Watch the catkins in midsummer", "text": "Sweet chestnut flowers in late June–July — the catkins are visible as long yellowish-cream pendulous strings. Hot calm conditions during catkin-shedding give the best pollination; cool wet weather can reduce the nut crop substantially. Nothing you can do about the weather, but if a poor flowering year happens, expect a smaller crop in October."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick burrs from October, wear gloves", "text": "The spiny green burrs ripen in October, splitting open on or around the tree to reveal 2–3 glossy mahogany-brown nuts inside. Wear thick leather gloves — the spines are vicious. Pick from the ground (where they fall when ripe) rather than from the tree (most are out of reach anyway). Or shake branches gently. The nuts are best eaten fresh; they can be stored 2–3 weeks at room temperature, longer in the fridge."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Roast or boil before eating", "text": "Sweet chestnut nuts must be cooked — they're starchy and inedible raw. Roasting (the classic): cut a cross in the flat side of each nut with a sharp knife, roast at 200°C for 20–25 minutes, or on a chestnut roasting pan over an open fire for 15–20 minutes. The shells split, peel them off (warm — they're easier hot), eat the soft sweet kernels. Boiling: simmer 25–30 minutes; useful for stuffing and savoury dishes. Marrons glacés: candied chestnuts, a French Christmas treat — labour-intensive but exquisite."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Are sweet chestnuts and horse chestnuts the same thing?", "answer": "No — totally different species, only one is edible. Sweet chestnut (Castanea sativa): long narrow serrated leaves, catkin flowers, spiny burrs containing edible nuts — the chestnut for roasting and Christmas stuffing. Horse chestnut (Aesculus hippocastanum, the conker tree): palmate leaves (5–7 fingers from one point), conical pink-white flower candles, spiny husks containing conkers — NOT edible, causes stomach upset. The leaf shape is the easiest field test: long single leaves = sweet chestnut; clustered hand-like fingers = horse chestnut."},
    {"question": "How big does a sweet chestnut tree get?", "answer": "Massive — 20–30 m tall and 15–20 m wide at maturity, over centuries (sweet chestnuts live 500+ years). This is not a tree for a small garden. Allow space: 20 m clear of any structure, 12–15 m apart in orchards. If you want chestnuts but lack space, walnut (15–20 m) is more compact and tolerates UK chalk soils better. For tiny gardens, give up on chestnut — plant a hazel instead for nuts."},
    {"question": "When are sweet chestnuts ready to pick?", "answer": "October. The spiny green burrs split open on the tree and the glossy mahogany-brown nuts fall to the ground. Pick from the ground rather than from the tree (mostly out of reach; tree-picking risks injury from the spines). Wear thick leather gloves to handle burrs that haven't fully opened. A 20-year-old grafted sweet chestnut produces 20–50 kg of nuts a year in a good season. Use fresh, or store 2–3 weeks at room temperature, or 1–2 months in the fridge."},
    {"question": "Why isn't my sweet chestnut tree producing nuts?", "answer": "Three usual causes. (1) Too young: grafted trees first crop in year 5–7; seedling trees take 10–15+ years. (2) No pollination partner: sweet chestnut is mostly self-incompatible — needs a different variety within 50 m. Plant Marigoule and Marsol together for reliable cross-pollination. (3) Poor flowering weather: catkins shed pollen in late June–July; cool wet weather reduces pollination, giving small crops. Nothing you can do about the weather; expect occasional poor years."},
    {"question": "Can sweet chestnuts grow in UK chalk soil?", "answer": "No — they fail on chalk and shallow soils over chalk. Sweet chestnut needs deep acid to neutral well-drained soil (sandy, loamy, typical of UK lowland woods). On chalk soils, leaves turn yellow with green veins (lime-induced chlorosis), trees fail to thrive, and nut crops are poor or absent. Alternative for chalk soils: walnut (Juglans regia) tolerates alkaline conditions much better and produces a comparable autumn nut crop."},
    {"question": "How do I roast chestnuts?", "answer": "The classic UK Christmas method. Cut a deep cross in the flat side of each nut with a sharp knife — this prevents them exploding from internal steam. Roast in a hot oven (200°C / 400°F) for 20–25 minutes, or in a chestnut roasting pan over an open fire for 15–20 minutes turning every few minutes. The shells split open along the cross. Peel them off while warm (the shells stick once cold). Eat the soft, sweet, slightly buttery kernels. Roast chestnuts traditionally eaten with mulled wine on Christmas Eve."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sweet-chestnut';

-- From sweet-marjoram.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow sweet marjoram indoors in March or buy young plants in May, in full sun in well-drained soil — it''s a tender Mediterranean perennial that''s nearly always grown as a half-hardy annual in the UK because it doesn''t survive cold wet winters. Sweet marjoram is more tender than its cousin oregano (RHS H1c–H3) and dies in a UK winter outdoors except in the mildest gardens or under cover. The sweeter, more delicate flavour (vs the punchy oregano) makes it the classic French and Italian herb for lamb, omelettes, and tomato dishes — fresh is unmatched. Pinch out flowering buds to keep the bush producing tender leaves. Grow in a pot that moves indoors over winter for a perennial supply, or sow fresh each spring as an annual. Knotted marjoram is the same plant (named for the knot-like flower buds).',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H3',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Confirm you have sweet marjoram, not oregano", "text": "Three Origanum species are easily confused. Sweet marjoram (Origanum majorana): the herb on this page — tender (H1c–H3), sweet delicate flavour, small grey-green leaves, white/pink knot-like flower buds. The flavour is what makes it distinct. Oregano / wild marjoram (O. vulgare): hardy perennial (H4–H5), stronger flavour, the pizza herb — see separate oregano slug. Pot marjoram (O. onites): half-hardy semi-shrub, intermediate flavour, less common. Buy by Latin name from a reputable herb nursery to be sure."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow indoors in March or buy a young plant in May", "text": "From seed (cheaper, slower): surface-sow on damp peat-free compost in February–March (needs light to germinate, don't cover). Keep at 18–21°C. Germinates in 14–21 days. Pot up individually when seedlings have 4 true leaves. From plant (faster): buy a 9 cm or 1 L plant from a herb nursery (Jekka's, Pepperpot, garden centre) in May. One plant is enough for a household."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant out after the last frost", "text": "Plant outside only after the last frost — mid-May in southern England, late May–early June in the Midlands and north. Sweet marjoram is killed by even light frost. Full sun, well-drained or gritty soil, sheltered position. For perennial culture: grow in a pot that moves indoors over winter (see Step 7)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't feed, don't over-water", "text": "Mediterranean origins: sweet marjoram flowers and flavours best in poor, dry conditions. No fertiliser. Water only when the soil/compost surface is dry — overwatering causes weak floppy growth and reduces flavour intensity. A gravel mulch around the base keeps weeds down without holding moisture."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pinch out flower buds for continuous leaves", "text": "The trade-off: flowers vs leaves. Once sweet marjoram starts flowering (the distinctive knot-like buds appear from June–July), leaf production slows and existing leaves get coarser. Pinch out flower buds as they form to keep the plant producing tender leaves for the kitchen. For seed-saving or wildlife: let some flowers open (bees enjoy them) — accept the trade with leaf quality."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick young shoots through summer", "text": "Snip 10 cm tips of young growth — the plant branches and re-grows. Strongest flavour is on tender new shoots before any flowering. Use fresh — sweet marjoram dries less well than oregano (the volatile oils are softer and more delicate, so they fade with drying). For winter use: chop fresh leaves and freeze in ice cubes with olive oil."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Overwinter the plant indoors as a perennial (optional)", "text": "The UK trick for year-round sweet marjoram. Grow in a 25 cm pot through summer. Before the first frost (mid-October in most of the UK), move the pot indoors to a cool but frost-free position: a porch, conservatory, unheated greenhouse, or bright kitchen windowsill. Water sparingly through winter (just enough to prevent total dry-out). Cut back hard in March, top-dress with fresh compost, return outdoors after last frost. A pot-grown marjoram can live 4–5 years this way."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Take cuttings as insurance in late summer", "text": "Insurance against winter loss. In August–September, take 8 cm tip cuttings of soft new growth, strip lower leaves, push into gritty compost in a propagator (cool, light position). Roots form in 3–4 weeks. Overwinter the rooted cuttings on a kitchen windowsill or in an unheated greenhouse — by spring you have new young plants to replace the parent if it doesn't make it through."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What's the difference between sweet marjoram and oregano?", "answer": "Same genus, different species, different flavour. Sweet marjoram (Origanum majorana): tender perennial (H1c–H3), small grey-green leaves, sweet delicate flavour with floral notes — the classic French and Italian herb for lamb, omelettes, sauces. Oregano (O. vulgare): hardy perennial (H4–H5), broader green leaves, stronger more peppery flavour — the pizza herb. Pot marjoram (O. onites): intermediate. Sweet marjoram is grown as an annual in most of the UK because it doesn't survive winter outdoors; oregano is fully hardy."},
    {"question": "Will sweet marjoram survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Mostly no — it dies in a UK winter outdoors except in the mildest gardens. Sweet marjoram is tender (RHS H1c–H3) and hates cold wet winters. Three options. (1) Grow as an annual — sow or buy fresh each May, accept it dies at the first frost. (2) Grow in a pot that moves indoors for winter (porch, conservatory, kitchen) — survives as a perennial. (3) Take cuttings in August as insurance — root indoors over winter, plant out next May."},
    {"question": "When and how do I pick sweet marjoram?", "answer": "From late June through to first frost. Snip 10 cm tips of young shoots with scissors — the plant branches and re-grows. Strongest flavour is on tender new growth before flowering (typically June). Pinch out flower buds as they form to keep the plant producing tender leaves through summer. Use fresh — sweet marjoram doesn't dry as well as oregano (volatile oils fade quickly). For winter use: chop and freeze fresh leaves in ice cubes with olive oil."},
    {"question": "Can I grow sweet marjoram from a supermarket pot?", "answer": "Possible but disappointing. Supermarket herb pots are typically overcrowded clumps of weak seedlings grown for fast leaf production, not long life. Two approaches if you want to try: (1) Split the supermarket pot into 3–4 smaller clumps, pot each into fresh compost — at least one usually survives and bulks up over a few months. (2) Take small cuttings from the supermarket plant and root them in compost — keeps the variety going. For a real sweet marjoram, buy a named plant from a herb nursery."},
    {"question": "Why is my sweet marjoram bland?", "answer": "Three usual causes. (1) Too rich soil or too much feeding: like other Mediterranean herbs, sweet marjoram concentrates essential oils under stress. Stop feeding, add grit to the soil. (2) Not enough sun: needs 6+ hours direct sun for full flavour. (3) Wrong species: pot marjoram and supermarket marjoram labels are often weak. Buy true Origanum majorana from a reputable herb supplier to be sure."},
    {"question": "Is knotted marjoram the same as sweet marjoram?", "answer": "Yes — same plant, different name. Knotted marjoram refers to the small tight knot-like flower buds that form before opening — a distinctive visual feature of Origanum majorana. The two names are completely interchangeable in UK gardening and herb literature. The Latin name majorana (= majoram in Old English/French) gives the modern English form."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sweet-marjoram';

-- From sweet-pea.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow sweet pea seed in October–November (autumn) for the biggest, earliest plants, or January–March (spring) for a shorter season. Sweet peas are hardy annuals (RHS H3–H4) — autumn-sown seedlings overwinter in a cold frame or sheltered cold greenhouse and produce far stronger plants than spring sowings. Soak seeds overnight before sowing to speed germination. Pinch out the growing tip at 4–6 leaves to force branching from the base. Climbing — build a wigwam, obelisk, or hazel pea-sticks 2 m tall. Plant out 20 cm apart after the last hard frost. Pick continuously from May–June — flowering stops the moment seed pods form. Spencer-type varieties for show; old-fashioned grandifloras (Cupani, Matucana) for fragrance.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H3',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H4',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Soak seed overnight", "text": "Soak sweet pea seed in tepid water for 12–24 hours before sowing. The hard seed coat softens and germination speeds from 2–3 weeks to about 1. Any seeds still floating after soaking should be discarded."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Sow in deep modules or pots", "text": "October–November for autumn sowing; January–March for spring sowing. Use deep root-trainers or 9 cm pots — sweet peas hate root disturbance and develop long tap-roots fast. Sow 2 seeds per cell, 2 cm deep, in peat-free multi-purpose compost."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Overwinter autumn sowings cool", "text": "Keep autumn-sown plants in an unheated cold frame, cold greenhouse, or against a sheltered north-facing wall. They want air, light, and protection from frost but NOT heat — warm conditions make them leggy. Pinch out the growing tip when seedlings have 4–6 true leaves."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Build the support before planting out", "text": "Wigwam of bamboo canes or hazel pea-sticks 2 m tall. An obelisk in a border. A length of netting on canes. Sweet peas climb 2 m and need substantial support from the start."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Plant out after the last hard frost", "text": "Late March in southern England (autumn-sown); mid-April for spring-sown. Space 20 cm apart around the support. Harden plants off for 7–10 days before planting if they've been under cover."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pinch out, train, and tie in", "text": "If you didn't pinch the tip earlier, do it now. Tie new growth gently to the support with soft twine as the plants climb; they'll grip with tendrils but tying speeds them up."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick continuously", "text": "Cut flowers regularly — the more you pick, the more they flower. The instant seed pods start forming, flowering stops — so pick all flowers as they open, even if you don't have a use for them. A handful of stems in a small jug on a kitchen table fills the room with scent."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Feed weekly from flowering", "text": "A weak high-potash liquid feed weekly from the first flowers. Water deeply twice a week in dry weather. Sweet peas crop until late August in southern England; cut back hard once spent, and the plant may give a second flush."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I sow sweet peas in the UK?", "answer": "Two main windows. Autumn (October–November): the traditional approach, gives biggest plants and earliest flowers from May. Overwinter in a cold frame or sheltered cold greenhouse. Spring (January–March): start indoors at 10–15°C, transplant out from April. Spring sowings give a shorter season (June to August). Autumn-sown almost always crop better than spring-sown if you can manage the overwintering."},
    {"question": "Do I need to soak sweet pea seed?", "answer": "Yes — significantly speeds and improves germination. Soak in tepid water for 12–24 hours. Discard any seeds still floating after soaking (they're not viable). Some growers nick the seed coat with a sharp knife to speed soaking further; not essential."},
    {"question": "Why aren't my sweet peas flowering much?", "answer": "Most likely cause: not picking enough. Sweet peas stop flowering the instant seed pods form — the plant's whole purpose is to set seed, and once it has, it shuts down. Pick all flowers as they open, even if you give them away. Other causes: too much nitrogen (fresh manure pushes leaves not flowers — switch to high-potash feed); not enough sun; too late a sowing."},
    {"question": "Spencer or grandiflora — what's the difference?", "answer": "Spencer types: large modern blooms with frilly waved petals, long stems, the show-bench standard. Less fragrant than grandifloras. Grandiflora / old-fashioned: smaller blooms with plain petals, shorter stems, intensely fragrant. Cupani (the original, from 1699), Matucana (deep maroon-purple), Painted Lady (pink and white) are the classic grandifloras. Grow grandifloras for scent; Spencers for cutting."},
    {"question": "How tall do sweet peas grow?", "answer": "Standard varieties reach 1.8–2.4 m on supports. Dwarf cultivars (Bijou, Snoopea, Cupid) reach 45–60 cm and need no support — suit containers and edges. Cordon-trained sweet peas (single-stem, side-shoots removed) for showing can reach 2.5–3 m. Standard climbers are the most useful for gardens."},
    {"question": "How do I store a glut of sweet pea seeds?", "answer": "Save your own from grandiflora or open-pollinated cultivars (Spencer varieties are often F1 and don't come true). Let a few flowers go to seed at the end of the season; cut pods when they turn brown and brittle; pop the seeds out; dry on a tray for a week; store in a paper envelope in a cool dry place. Viable for 3–4 years. F1 hybrids must be re-bought each year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sweet-pea';

-- From sweet-pepper.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow sweet pepper seed indoors from late February to mid-March in a heated propagator; the long UK season makes an early start essential. Peppers are frost-tender (RHS H1c) and want consistent warmth above 15°C to flower and fruit, which in practice means a greenhouse, polytunnel, conservatory or warm windowsill — outdoor crops in pots are possible in sheltered south-facing positions from June to September but rarely match indoor yields. F1 hybrids like Bell Boy, Redskin (compact) and Sweet Banana suit UK conditions. Pinch out the growing tip at 30 cm to encourage branching. Pick green or wait for full colour (red, yellow or orange) — colour develops over the last 2–3 weeks and concentrates the sugars. One plant gives 6–12 fruit.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow seed indoors", "text": "Late February to mid-March in a heated propagator at 21–25°C. Sweet peppers need a long season; later sowings rarely ripen before autumn cools off. Sow 2 seeds per 9 cm pot, 5 mm deep; thin to the strongest seedling after germination."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Pot on as roots fill", "text": "Move to 1-litre pots when roots fill the smaller; then 5-litre pots once the plant is 30 cm tall. Don't let pepper seedlings get root-bound — they check growth and never fully recover."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Choose UK-suitable F1 hybrids", "text": "Bell Boy (classic blocky bell), Redskin (compact dwarf for containers), Sweet Banana (long, mild, very productive), Marconi (Italian pointed type). F1 hybrids out-perform open-pollinated peppers in UK conditions by a wide margin."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant in final position by late May", "text": "Greenhouse / polytunnel border or 7.5-litre containers. Outdoor in sheltered south-facing pots from June only. Indoor positions get earlier and bigger crops."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pinch out the growing tip", "text": "When plants are 30 cm tall, pinch out the top growing point. This encourages branching and gives a bushier plant with more fruit. Bell Boy and dwarf F1s self-branch and need less pinching."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Feed and water", "text": "Feed weekly with a high-potash liquid feed from the first flowers. Water consistently — peppers are prone to blossom-end rot from erratic watering. Avoid splashing the foliage to reduce mildew."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pick green or wait for colour", "text": "Sweet peppers are fully edible when green but sweeten as they turn colour over the final 2–3 weeks. Picking green keeps the plant productive for longer (more fruit, smaller); leaving fruit to colour gives fewer, sweeter peppers. Most UK growers do both."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for aphids and mildew under glass", "text": "Greenhouse pepper plants attract aphids; encourage ladybirds and hoverflies, or wash off with soapy water weekly. Powdery mildew strikes in late summer — improve airflow by removing some leaves around fruit."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Can I grow sweet peppers outdoors in the UK?", "answer": "Marginally and only in southern England in a sheltered south-facing position, ideally in pots that hold heat. Even there, outdoor yields are 30–50% of greenhouse yields. North of the Midlands, outdoor sweet peppers rarely ripen fully — grow under cover instead. The plants need consistent warmth above 15°C and protection from wind."},
    {"question": "When should I sow sweet pepper seeds?", "answer": "Late February to mid-March in a heated propagator. Earlier sowings (January) lead to leggy plants by planting-out time; later (April) leaves too little growing season. Sweet peppers need 18–22 weeks from sowing to first ripe fruit, so an early start is essential in our short summer."},
    {"question": "Why are my peppers tiny and not setting?", "answer": "Cold weather. Peppers stop flowering and fail to set fruit below 15°C — common in early UK summer. Move plants to a warmer position, keep them sheltered from cold nights, and be patient. Once temperatures climb consistently above 18°C through July, set rates improve dramatically."},
    {"question": "What's the best variety for the UK?", "answer": "For greenhouse / polytunnel: Bell Boy (classic blocky), Marconi (Italian pointed), Sweet Banana (long mild, very prolific). For containers and small spaces: Redskin and Mohawk are compact F1s that crop heavily. Avoid heritage open-pollinated cultivars — they need warmer summers than the UK provides."},
    {"question": "When can I pick sweet peppers?", "answer": "Green peppers are pickable from late July onwards once they reach full size. For coloured (red, yellow, orange) peppers, leave them on the plant another 2–3 weeks after reaching full size — they turn colour and sweeten dramatically. The last fruit can be picked into October from greenhouse crops."},
    {"question": "How do I store sweet peppers?", "answer": "Refrigerated, 1–2 weeks in the salad drawer. Sliced and frozen on trays then bagged — keeps 6 months; texture softens but flavour holds for cooking. Roasted, peeled and preserved in olive oil — classic Mediterranean approach for a glut."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sweet-pepper';

-- From sweetcorn.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow sweetcorn seed indoors in late April or early May; plant out in late May or early June after the last frost. Sweetcorn is frost-tender (RHS H1c) and a borderline UK crop — most reliable in southern England and the Midlands, marginal further north outdoors. Choose UK-suited F1 cultivars (Lark, Swift, Earlibird, Incredible) for the best cropping. The most important rule: plant in a block, not a row. Sweetcorn is wind-pollinated, and rows give poor pollination resulting in patchy cobs. A 4×4 or 5×5 grid 45 cm apart fills the cobs evenly. Harvest when silks turn brown and a kernel squirts milky juice when pressed. Eat within hours of picking — sugars convert to starch fast.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow indoors", "text": "Late April to early May. Use deep modules or 9 cm pots (root-trainers ideal — sweetcorn dislikes root disturbance). One seed per pot, 3 cm deep, 18–22°C. Germination in 7–10 days."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose F1 hybrids for the UK", "text": "Standard varieties don't ripen reliably in UK summers. F1 hybrids bred for cooler climates (Lark, Swift, Earlibird, Incredible, Sundance) are essential. Don't mix sweet and supersweet cultivars in the same block — cross-pollination produces starchy cobs."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Prepare a sunny sheltered bed", "text": "Full sun, sheltered from wind, fertile well-drained soil. Fork in compost. The bed should be roughly square for the block planting."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant out in a BLOCK", "text": "After the last frost: mid-May in southern England, early June in the north. Plant in a square grid 45 cm apart — minimum 4×4, ideally 5×5 or bigger. Rows of sweetcorn produce poor pollination and patchy cobs; the block ensures wind-carried pollen reaches every silk."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Water deeply at flowering", "text": "Once tassels (male flowers at the top) appear in July, water deeply twice a week. Drought at flowering and silking gives gappy cobs. Mulch heavily."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Earth up if rocking", "text": "Tall stems can be loosened by wind. Draw soil up around the base of each stem when plants are 1 m tall to add stability."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Test for ripeness", "text": "Cobs are ready when the silks turn brown and dry. Test by peeling back the husk slightly and pressing a kernel with a thumbnail — a milky juice means ready; clear juice means not ready; pasty means past-ripe. Each plant produces 1–2 cobs."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Eat the same day", "text": "Sugars convert to starch within hours of picking. Pick, husk, and eat within 1–2 hours for the sweetest cobs. Refrigerate immediately if you can't eat straight away; freezing whole cobs preserves more sugar than fridge storage."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow sweetcorn in the UK?", "answer": "Indoors in late April to early May; plant out late May (south) to early June (north) after the last frost. Don't sow direct in cold soil — emergence is patchy and slow. The indoor head-start is essential for ripening before autumn cools off."},
    {"question": "Why are my sweetcorn cobs only half-filled with kernels?", "answer": "Poor wind pollination. Sweetcorn is wind-pollinated — pollen from the tassel (male flower at the top) must land on each silk (female stigma) for a kernel to develop. Rows give poor pollination because pollen blows past most silks; a block of 4×4 or 5×5 plants ensures pollen reaches all silks regardless of wind direction. Hand-pollinating helps: at midday in dry weather, shake the tassels above the silks."},
    {"question": "Can I grow sweetcorn in the north of England or Scotland?", "answer": "Outdoors, marginally — even with F1 hybrids and a south-facing sheltered site, summers further north are often too cool for cobs to ripen fully. Under cover (polytunnel, walk-in greenhouse) is more reliable. Earlibird and Swift are the cultivars to choose for shorter seasons. Highland gardens are essentially outside sweetcorn's range without serious protection."},
    {"question": "Can I plant sweetcorn next to courgette or beans?", "answer": "Yes — and it's a traditional Three Sisters combination (sweetcorn, climbing beans, squash). Beans climb the corn stalks; squash sprawls underneath suppressing weeds; sweetcorn provides the structure. Works modestly in UK conditions; not as productive as separate beds in our climate but visually striking and decent if space is tight."},
    {"question": "How do I store a glut of sweetcorn?", "answer": "Freezing is the only worthwhile preservation. Husk and de-silk the cobs immediately after picking, blanch whole cobs for 7 minutes or strip kernels and blanch for 3 minutes, plunge in cold water, drain, freeze. Keeps 12 months. Drying for cornmeal works but isn't worth the effort for small gardens. Pickling whole baby cobs is the only other option."},
    {"question": "When is sweetcorn ready to pick?", "answer": "Watch the silks at the top of each cob. Once they've turned brown and dry, peel back the husk slightly and press a kernel with a thumbnail. Milky juice = ripe; clear watery juice = not ready; thick pasty starch = past it. Usually 18–22 weeks from sowing depending on cultivar and weather. Most UK crops ripen mid-August to mid-September."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'sweetcorn';

-- From thyme.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant thyme in spring or autumn as a young plant; seed is possible but slow and unreliable. Thyme is hardy (RHS H5) and shrugs off UK winters as long as it has drainage — like rosemary, the killer is cold wet roots, not cold itself. Plant in full sun, ideally between paving slabs or in a raised bed of gritty soil. Common thyme (Thymus vulgaris) is the kitchen standard; lemon thyme adds citrus to chicken and fish; creeping thyme makes edible carpets between paving. Pick sprigs year-round; the flavour is strongest just before flowering in May–June. Cut back lightly after flowering to keep the plant compact. Slugs love young plants — protect for the first season; established thyme is robust.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Buy young plants", "text": "Thyme seed germinates slowly and the seedlings are easy to lose. Buy young plants in 9 cm pots — much faster, and you can see what variety you're getting."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose a hot dry spot", "text": "Full sun, well-drained soil. Mediterranean herbs hate UK clay in winter. Raised beds, gravel paths, dry-stone walls, between paving slabs — anywhere drainage is sharp."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant out", "text": "March–April or September–October. Dig in grit if the bed is heavy. Space 30 cm apart for common thyme; 15 cm for creeping types. Water in well."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Protect young plants from slugs", "text": "Thyme is slug-vulnerable in its first season. Apply nematodes, copper rings, or hand-pick at dusk. Established plants are too woody for slug damage."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick year-round", "text": "Cut sprigs whenever you cook with it. Pick more heavily in May–June (just before flowering) when the oils are strongest. Avoid heavy picking in mid-winter."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut back after flowering", "text": "Shear lightly after flowering (late June–July) to keep the plant compact and force fresh growth. Don't cut into old bare wood; thyme regrows from it but slowly."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Replace every 4–5 years", "text": "Common thyme gets woody and unproductive after 4–5 years. Take cuttings or layer low branches in summer to have replacements ready."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Will thyme survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes, in most of the UK with good drainage. Thyme is RHS H5 (−10 to −15°C) and tolerates the cold itself. What kills it is waterlogged roots — heavy clay holding water around the crown in cold wet winters. Plant in raised beds, gravel gardens, between paving slabs, or in containers; sharp drainage is more important than insulation."},
    {"question": "Can I grow thyme between paving slabs?", "answer": "Excellent position for it. Creeping thymes (Thymus serpyllum, T. praecox) tolerate light foot traffic and release their fragrance when crushed. Plant in the gaps between slabs with extra grit worked into the soil. They form weed-suppressing carpets and need almost no maintenance. Common thyme is too upright for paving but works alongside it."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between common, lemon and creeping thyme?", "answer": "Common thyme (Thymus vulgaris) is the standard kitchen thyme — upright, grey-green, strong savoury flavour. Lemon thyme (Thymus citriodorus) has a citrus note that suits fish, chicken, and Mediterranean dishes. Creeping thymes (Thymus serpyllum, T. praecox) are low-growing mat-formers used between paving and as ground cover; they're edible but with a weaker flavour. Grow common thyme for cooking; the others for variety and ground cover."},
    {"question": "When is the best time to pick thyme?", "answer": "Year-round for everyday cooking; most flavourful just before flowering in May–June. Pick whole sprigs about 7–10 cm long from the soft top growth. Don't strip a plant — leave plenty of growth to support it. In winter, pick sparingly; cold-damaged shoots happen if you over-pick."},
    {"question": "How do I dry thyme?", "answer": "Cut sprigs in dry weather just before flowering. Tie loose bundles of 6–8 sprigs with string and hang upside down in a warm dry place for 2–3 weeks. Strip the leaves once fully dry; store in airtight jars away from light. Thyme dries better than most herbs and keeps its flavour for a year."},
    {"question": "Why has my thyme gone woody and bare in the middle?", "answer": "Age and lack of pruning. After 3–5 years, thyme gets woody at the base and dies out in the centre. Annual light pruning after flowering delays this; without it, the plant declines faster. The fix is replacement — take cuttings or layer low branches in summer, root them over winter, and plant out fresh stock the following spring. Common thyme rarely lives more than 6–7 years."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'thyme';

-- From tomato.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow tomato seeds indoors from late February to early April — earlier under glass, later for outdoor crops. South-coast gardeners can start in mid-February; upland Scotland from early April. Plant out once the last frost has passed: mid-May in southern England, early June in the Midlands and the north, end of June in Highland gardens. Tomatoes are frost-tender (RHS H1c) and the foliage will die at the first touch of frost, so timing matters. They want a sunny, sheltered spot with deep, rich, well-drained soil and consistent watering once flowering begins. Outdoor crops fruit from late July to October; under cover, picking starts a fortnight earlier and runs until the first cold nights see them off. Cordon varieties give the longest pick; bush types are best where the season is short.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H1c',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H1c',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow seed", "text": "Sow tomato seed indoors from late February for greenhouse crops, mid-March to mid-April for outdoor. 18–22°C in a propagator or warm windowsill. Germination takes 7–10 days."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Prick out", "text": "Prick out into 9 cm pots when the first true leaves appear, about three weeks after sowing. Handle the seedlings by their seed leaves, never the stem. Keep above 15°C."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Pot on", "text": "Pot on to 1-litre pots when roots fill the smaller pots. Water when the surface dries. Move to a cooler windowsill once seedlings are sturdy — too much heat makes them leggy."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant out", "text": "Plant out after the last frost: mid-May in southern England, early June further north. Harden plants off for 7–10 days first — outside in the daytime, in at night, gradually lengthening the outdoor time. Space 60 cm apart for bush varieties, 45 cm for cordons. Stake cordons at planting time so you don't damage roots later. Water in well."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pinch out side-shoots (cordons only)", "text": "Pinch out side-shoots weekly on cordon varieties from late June. Side-shoots appear in the V between leaf and stem — snap them off with your fingers while they're small. Bush varieties don't need this."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Feed and water", "text": "Feed weekly with a high-potash liquid feed once the first truss sets fruit. Water at the base of the plant, never on the foliage. Mulch heavily to keep moisture even — erratic watering causes blossom-end rot and split fruit."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Watch for blight", "text": "Watch for blight from mid-July, especially after warm humid nights. Brown patches on leaves and stems mean it's already there — remove affected material immediately and pick any green fruit to ripen indoors. Blight-resistant cultivars (Crimson Crush, Mountain Magic, Lizzano) reduce the risk on outdoor crops."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant out my tomatoes in the UK?", "answer": "Wait for the last frost in your area — mid-May in southern England, late May in the Midlands, early June in the north of England and southern Scotland, and end of June in Highland gardens. Harden tomatoes off for 7–10 days before planting out by leaving them outside for longer each day. Plant 60 cm apart for bush varieties and 45 cm for cordons. Water them in well and stake cordons at planting time, so you don't damage the roots later."},
    {"question": "Do tomatoes need full sun in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — tomatoes need at least six hours of direct sun a day to fruit reliably. They will grow in partial shade but the crop will be small and slow to ripen. South-facing walls and sheltered south-facing patios are the best UK positions, especially north of the Midlands where summer warmth is unreliable. Under glass or polytunnel cover, tomatoes will fruit further north than they would outdoors. East-facing gardens manage; north-facing rarely do."},
    {"question": "What's the best tomato variety to grow outdoors in the UK?", "answer": "Choose a blight-resistant variety if you grow outdoors anywhere damp. Crimson Crush, Mountain Magic and Lizzano are bred specifically to resist Phytophthora infestans. For flavour and a long pick, Sungold (cherry) and Gardener's Delight (cherry) are the long-running UK favourites and crop reliably outdoors in southern England. Plum varieties like San Marzano need a warm summer and tend to do better under glass. Bush (determinate) varieties suit shorter seasons because they ripen all at once."},
    {"question": "Why are my tomato leaves curling?", "answer": "Leaf curl is usually heat stress or uneven watering, not disease. Tomato leaves curl upwards in hot dry conditions to reduce water loss — they recover overnight. Persistent downward curl means erratic watering: tomatoes want consistent moisture from a deep mulch and regular base-watering, never on the foliage. If the curl comes with yellow streaks, mottling or stunted growth, suspect a virus and remove the plant to stop it spreading."},
    {"question": "How do I stop blight on my outdoor tomatoes?", "answer": "You can't stop blight in a wet UK summer, but you can buy time. Choose blight-resistant cultivars (Crimson Crush, Mountain Magic, Lizzano). Space plants 60 cm apart so air moves between them. Water at the base only, never on the foliage. Remove the lower leaves up to the first truss to keep splash-back off the plant. Once you see brown patches on stems and leaves, the crop is on borrowed time — pick everything green and ripen it indoors before the rot spreads."},
    {"question": "When is the harvest window for tomatoes in the UK?", "answer": "Outdoor tomatoes start cropping from late July in southern England, mid-August further north, and run until the first cold nights — typically early October down south, mid-September in Scotland. Under glass or polytunnel cover, you can pick from early July through to October. Pick fruit when the colour is fully developed but the texture is still firm; tomatoes continue ripening off the vine. The last green fruit will ripen on a windowsill or in a paper bag with a banana."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'tomato';

-- From tulip.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant tulip bulbs in November to early December (later than other spring bulbs) — the cold soil reduces tulip fire disease risk; earlier planting in warm autumn soil encourages this fungal disease. Tulips are hardy (RHS H6) and the most variable spring bulb, with thousands of cultivars from dwarf species types to elegant lily-flowered to flamboyant doubles and parrots. Most Dutch hybrid tulips are short-lived in UK gardens — they flower brilliantly year 1, weakly year 2, often disappear by year 3 (lift, store, and replant for reliable repeat performance, OR treat as annuals). Species tulips and Darwin hybrids are the exceptions — these perennialise and naturalise. Plant 10–15 cm deep in well-drained soil and full sun. Squirrels eat tulip bulbs — protect with deep planting and wire mesh.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose perennials or annuals", "text": "Dutch hybrid tulips (most named cultivars sold by garden centres): brilliant year 1, weak year 2, disappear by year 3. Treat as annuals — buy fresh bulbs each year for spectacular display, accept they won't reliably return. Darwin hybrid tulips (Apeldoorn, Pink Impression, Daydream): the most perennial of the large-flowered groups — return for 3–5 years with proper management. Species tulips (T. tarda, T. clusiana, T. saxatilis, T. turkestanica): truly perennial, naturalise into drifts, much smaller flowers but reliable year after year. For a first tulip: mixed Darwin hybrids for impact + a separate planting of species tulips for perennial return."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in November to early December", "text": "Later than other spring bulbs — the cool soil reduces tulip fire disease (a serious fungal disease that thrives in warm soil). September–October plantings (typical for daffodils) often produce fire-infected tulips. November is the sweet spot: cold enough to suppress fire, warm enough for some root establishment before deep winter. In Scotland and the north: late October still works because soil is cooler."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant deep — 10–15 cm or more", "text": "Deeper than commonly advised. Standard advice is 10 cm; 15–20 cm gives better perennialisation and protects from squirrels. Most UK gardeners under-plant tulips — leading to bulbs that flower once and disappear. Plant pointed end up, blunt end down. Spacing: 10 cm apart for normal varieties; 5 cm for species tulips."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Plant in full sun in well-drained soil", "text": "Tulips are sun-and-drainage specialists. Best position: full sun (6+ hours), south or west-facing borders. Soil: well-drained loam, sandy soil, chalk all work; clay needs grit forking in or raised beds. Avoid: shade (reduces flowering), waterlogged soil (rots bulbs). For pots: peat-free compost mixed 30% with horticultural grit."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Protect against squirrels", "text": "Squirrels dig and eat tulip bulbs — particularly Dutch hybrid types. Three defences. (1) Deep planting (15–20 cm) — squirrels rarely excavate that deep. (2) Chicken wire under planting: bury 2 cm mesh wire 5 cm below the bulbs; tulip stems grow through, squirrels can't dig down. (3) Mulch with sharp gravel at the surface — discourages digging. Species tulips are less attractive to squirrels than Dutch hybrids."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Deadhead promptly, leave foliage", "text": "Same rule as daffodils: deadhead spent flowers (snap off at the base of the seed pod) to prevent seed-setting that drains the bulb, but leave the leaves alone for at least 6 weeks until they yellow naturally. In lawns: don't mow until mid-June. In borders: tuck yellowing leaves under emerging perennials. Cutting foliage early is the #1 cause of tulips that flower poorly or go blind."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Lift Dutch hybrids each summer (or treat as annuals)", "text": "For reliable Dutch hybrid repeat performance: lift bulbs after foliage has yellowed (typically late June). Brush off soil, store dry in a cool dry shed, replant in November. Most UK gardeners don't do this and accept Dutch hybrids as one-season displays. The alternative: plant Darwin hybrids and species tulips that perennialise without lifting."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Watch for tulip fire disease", "text": "The serious UK tulip disease. Symptoms: distorted, withered, brown-spotted leaves and flowers; collapse of plants; brown lesions on emerging shoots. Cause: Botrytis tulipae fungus, thrives in warm wet conditions. Prevention: plant in November (cold soil), full sun, well-drained position; remove and bin affected plants (don't compost); avoid replanting tulips in infected areas for 3 years. Once established, fire spreads through colonies."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When should I plant tulips?", "answer": "November to early December — later than other spring bulbs. Two reasons: (1) cold autumn soil suppresses tulip fire disease (Botrytis tulipae), which thrives in warm September–October soil; (2) tulips don't need an early start for spring flowering. Plant 10–15 cm deep (deeper than usually advised — 15–20 cm gives better perennialisation and protects from squirrels). In Scotland and the north: late October is fine because soil is cooler by then."},
    {"question": "Why don't my tulips come back the next year?", "answer": "Most likely two causes. (1) Dutch hybrid tulips are short-lived in UK gardens — most named cultivars flower brilliantly year 1, weakly year 2, disappear by year 3. Solution: plant Darwin hybrids (Apeldoorn, Pink Impression) or species tulips, which perennialise reliably. (2) Planted too shallow — at 5–8 cm depth bulbs split into many small non-flowering offsets rather than one large bulb. Plant 15–20 cm deep. (3) Foliage cut too early — leaves need 6+ weeks after flowering to refill the bulb."},
    {"question": "How deep should I plant tulip bulbs?", "answer": "Deeper than usually advised — 15–20 cm. Standard advice (10 cm) often leads to bulbs that split into many small non-flowering offsets and disappear within 2–3 years. Deeper planting (15–20 cm) keeps bulbs intact and producing flowering-sized growth year after year. Bonus benefit: deeper bulbs are out of squirrel reach. For species tulips: 10 cm is fine (smaller bulbs, shallower naturally)."},
    {"question": "Will tulips naturalise in my lawn?", "answer": "Mostly no — but species tulips will. Dutch hybrid tulips rarely naturalise in lawns — they need lifting, drying, and replanting to perform reliably, which is incompatible with lawn cultivation. Species tulips (T. tarda, T. clusiana, T. saxatilis) do naturalise into drifts in well-drained sunny lawns, similar to crocuses but slower. For naturalising: species tulips at 10 cm depth, in sunny meadow-style lawns, don't mow until mid-June."},
    {"question": "What's the best tulip variety for the UK?", "answer": "For perennial return: Darwin hybrid tulips (Apeldoorn red, Pink Impression, Daydream) — the most reliable repeat-flowerers among the large Dutch types. For naturalising: species tulips like T. tarda (yellow with white margins, multi-headed), T. clusiana (Lady Tulip, pink-and-white), or T. turkestanica (white with yellow centres, very early). For impact (treated as annuals): any Dutch hybrid in mass planting. For pots: dwarf species tulips, T. greigii hybrids, T. kaufmanniana hybrids."},
    {"question": "Can I grow tulips in pots?", "answer": "Yes — pots showcase tulips brilliantly. Use a 30 cm pot of peat-free compost with grit for drainage. Plant 10–15 bulbs in November, 10 cm deep. Move pots under cover in deep winter if hard frost is forecast (especially clay pots which can crack); bring out for flower display in spring. After flowering: most pot tulips are best treated as annuals (compost the bulbs after foliage dies back); or plant exhausted bulbs in the garden border for them to try again next year."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'tulip';

-- From turnip.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Sow turnip seed direct from March to August in successional batches every three weeks. Turnips are hardy (RHS H5–H6) and one of the quickest UK vegetables — early varieties (Snowball, Tokyo Cross) crop in 6–8 weeks; main-crop types (Golden Ball, Manchester Market) take 10–12 weeks and store through winter. Pull young (golf-ball sized) for the best flavour; older turnips get coarse and woody. Different species from swede (Brassica napus) despite the kitchen overlap. Flea beetle is the main pest on young leaves — fleece protection or a quick crop window dodges the worst of it. Late-summer sowings (July–August) crop into late autumn and store well in damp sand.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Sow direct", "text": "March to August in successional batches every 3 weeks. 1 cm deep drill, sow thinly, cover lightly. Early sowings (March) under cloches; April onwards direct. Turnips germinate fast — 7–14 days."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Thin to 15 cm", "text": "When seedlings have 2 true leaves, thin to 15 cm apart. Eat the thinnings (turnip tops are like spring greens)."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Protect young leaves from flea beetle", "text": "Tiny black beetles that pock-mark young brassica leaves. Apply enviromesh or fleece from sowing through the first 4 weeks; the plants then outgrow the damage. Yellow sticky traps catch a useful number."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Water consistently", "text": "Turnips bolt or go woody in drought. Water deeply once a week in dry spells; mulch with grass clippings to hold moisture."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pull young for tender roots", "text": "Pull when roots are golf-ball sized (5–7 cm across) for the best flavour. Larger roots get coarse, fibrous, and bitter. The early varieties (Snowball, Purple Top Milan, Tokyo Cross) are designed to be eaten small."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Sow main-crop for storage", "text": "For winter storage, sow July to early August. These take longer to mature (10–12 weeks) and store well. Lift before the first hard frost; cut tops 2 cm above the root; store in damp sand in a frost-free shed."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Don't confuse with swede", "text": "Turnip (Brassica rapa) and swede (Brassica napus) are different species despite similar appearance and use. Swede is hardier (H6), slower-growing, and the orange-fleshed root of neeps. Different sowing schedule too — see the separate swede content."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "When can I sow turnips in the UK?", "answer": "March under cloches in southern England; April onwards direct outdoors across the UK. Successional sowings every 3 weeks until early August give continuous picking. July–August sowings are for autumn–winter storage crops. Avoid sowing in mid-summer heat — turnips bolt fast above 25°C."},
    {"question": "How do I stop flea beetles on turnips?", "answer": "Cover young plants with fleece or enviromesh for the first 4 weeks after sowing — the flea beetles can't reach the leaves. Yellow sticky traps placed near the row catch adults. Established plants (after 4–5 leaves) tolerate the damage and grow through it. Sowing in cooler weather (early spring, late summer) bypasses peak flea-beetle activity."},
    {"question": "How big should I let turnips grow?", "answer": "Golf-ball to tennis-ball (5–7 cm across) is the sweet spot for flavour and texture. Beyond that they get coarse, woody, and bitter. Pull alternate plants from a row first — gives the remaining plants more room to keep growing. Early types are bred to be eaten small; main-crop types can grow a bit larger."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between turnips and swedes?", "answer": "Different species. Turnip (Brassica rapa): white-fleshed root, fast-growing (6–12 weeks), eaten young, more tender. Swede (Brassica napus): yellow-fleshed root, slow-growing (24+ weeks), bigger, hardier, more robust flavour. Swede is the neeps in Scottish neeps and tatties. Different sowing dates and care — they're not interchangeable in the garden even if they're occasionally swapped in recipes."},
    {"question": "Can I eat turnip tops?", "answer": "Yes — the leaves are excellent as spring greens, steamed or wilted like chard. Pick young leaves regularly from your thinnings, or sow turnip specifically for tops (without thinning to one per station). Italian cuisine uses cima di rapa (essentially turnip-top broccoli) extensively."},
    {"question": "How do I store turnips through winter?", "answer": "Main-crop varieties (Golden Ball, Manchester Market) lift in October–November before the first hard frost. Cut tops 2 cm above the root; layer roots in damp sand in a wooden box; store in a frost-free shed. Keep firm until March. Smaller early-variety turnips don't store as well — use fresh."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'turnip';

-- From watercress.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Grow watercress in a pot stood in a saucer of fresh water, kept topped up daily, in light shade — it''s a perennial aquatic herb that wants constantly damp roots and cool conditions. Watercress is hardy (RHS H6) but UK home growers don''t usually attempt the running-stream cultivation it really wants. The pot-in-saucer method works well for kitchen use. Sow seed in late spring (March–May) onto compost, keep wet, and pick young shoots within 6–8 weeks. Don''t gather wild watercress — UK wild stocks may carry liver fluke (Fasciola hepatica) and should not be eaten raw. Land cress (Barbarea verna) is the soil-grown UK alternative with similar peppery flavour, sown March to September and cropped winter through to spring.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Choose your method", "text": "Pot in saucer (recommended for UK home use): a 20 cm pot of compost stood in a saucer that's kept topped up with water. Simple, controllable, suits small kitchens. Running water bed: traditional commercial method needing a continuous clean water flow — impractical for most UK gardeners. Land cress (separate species Barbarea verna): similar flavour, grown in damp soil like spinach. The easiest UK alternative."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Buy seed or cuttings", "text": "Watercress seed is widely available; cuttings from a bunch of supermarket watercress also root readily in water. Land cress (American land cress) seed is sold separately — sometimes labelled winter cress."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Sow on damp compost", "text": "March to May for outdoor pots; year-round indoors on a kitchen windowsill. Surface sow onto damp peat-free compost; press lightly; mist to keep the surface moist. Germination in 7–14 days."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Keep constantly wet", "text": "Stand the pot in a saucer that's always full of fresh water. Top up daily in summer; replace the water weekly to keep it fresh. Watercress wants its roots in water; don't let the saucer dry out."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Position in light shade", "text": "Watercress wilts in strong direct sun and loves cool conditions. Light shade or an east-facing windowsill; outdoors in summer choose a sheltered shady spot."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Pick young shoots", "text": "From 6–8 weeks after sowing. Pick the top 5–7 cm of growth regularly. The plant branches at each cut and produces more. Don't let it flower (small white flowers) — flavour weakens; cut flower stems off as soon as they appear."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Replace plants every 6 months", "text": "Watercress in pots exhausts itself after about 6 months. Take cuttings from the best plants (the cut stems root in water in days) and start fresh pots. The parent plant can go on the compost."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "DON'T eat wild watercress", "text": "UK wild watercress may carry liver fluke (Fasciola hepatica), a parasite that can cause serious illness in humans. Never gather and eat wild watercress raw. Commercially-grown watercress from supermarkets is safe; home-grown watercress in containers using mains water is safe."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Can I grow watercress at home in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — the pot-in-saucer method works well for kitchen use. A 20 cm pot of compost stood in a saucer of fresh water, kept topped up daily, gives a useful crop within 6–8 weeks. Don't try to grow watercress in a normal garden bed; without constantly wet roots it sulks and bolts."},
    {"question": "Is wild watercress safe to eat?", "answer": "No — not raw. UK wild watercress may carry liver fluke (Fasciola hepatica), a parasite that can infect humans and cause serious illness. The eggs are laid by infected sheep and cattle and can persist in the stream or surrounding wet ground. Wild watercress should only be eaten well-cooked; even then it's safer to grow your own. Commercially-grown watercress is safe to eat raw."},
    {"question": "What's the difference between watercress and land cress?", "answer": "Watercress (Nasturtium officinale): true aquatic perennial; needs constantly wet roots; classic peppery flavour. Land cress (Barbarea verna, sometimes called American land cress): grows in normal damp soil; similar peppery flavour; much easier in UK gardens. Land cress is a hardy biennial — sow March–September, crop October through to spring. The practical UK substitute for watercress when you don't have running water."},
    {"question": "How do I grow watercress from supermarket sprigs?", "answer": "Stand a bunch of supermarket watercress in a glass of fresh water; change the water daily. Roots form within a week. Once 2–3 cm of roots have grown, plant the rooted sprigs in damp compost in a pot, stand the pot in a saucer of water, treat as for seed-grown. A free start, and the supermarket bunch was someone else's cultivar selection."},
    {"question": "When can I pick watercress?", "answer": "6–8 weeks after sowing (or 2–3 weeks after planting rooted sprigs). Pick the top 5–7 cm of shoots; the plant branches and produces more from each cut. Continuous picking is the rhythm; the more you cut the more it grows. Cut flower stems off as soon as they appear to keep the foliage tender."},
    {"question": "Why is my watercress yellowing and tough?", "answer": "Three usual causes. Roots not wet enough: the saucer dried out. Keep it topped up daily. Too much sun: watercress wilts in strong direct sun; move to light shade. Flowering: the plant is mid-life and trying to set seed; cut the flower stems off and pick more aggressively, or replace with fresh cuttings."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'watercress';

-- From whitecurrant.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant whitecurrant bushes bare-root from November to March, in sun or light shade, in any decent garden soil — they are the white-fruiting cultivar form of the same species (Ribes rubrum) as redcurrant, grown identically. Whitecurrants are very hardy (RHS H6–H7) and shrug off any UK winter. Prune like a gooseberry, not a blackcurrant — they fruit on spurs on a permanent framework. The berries are translucent golden-white when ripe, noticeably sweeter and lower in acid than redcurrants, less attractive to birds (which is the practical advantage over reds in unnetted gardens). Eat fresh, add to summer pudding for a paler version, or make a delicate amber jelly. White Versailles, White Pearl, and Blanka are the reliable UK varieties.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Treat whitecurrants exactly like redcurrants", "text": "Whitecurrants are Ribes rubrum — the same species as redcurrant, selected for the absence of red pigment in the berry. Every aspect of cultivation is identical to redcurrant: planting, pruning, feeding, picking. The only practical differences are flavour (sweeter, less acid), appearance (translucent gold-white), and bird damage (slightly less because birds spot red faster than white)."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Choose your variety", "text": "White Versailles — the traditional UK variety, early-to-mid, reliable, long strigs of pale gold berries. White Pearl — modern, very pale (almost white), good cropper, decent disease resistance. Blanka — Czech variety, late, the heaviest cropper, very long strigs, the modern standard if you can find it. White Grape — small bush, useful for containers. A single bush crops well; no pollination partner needed."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant bare-root in winter", "text": "November to March, while dormant. Plant on a slight mound to keep the crown out of waterlogging. Spacing: 1.5 m for bushes; 30–45 cm for cordons; 1 m for fan-trained. Whitecurrants take light shade as happily as redcurrants — fan-train against a north or east wall if running out of sunny spots."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Train to a permanent goblet", "text": "Year one: cut the main shoots back by half to outward-facing buds, removing inward growth. Aim for 5–6 main branches angled outward from a 15 cm clear stem. Year two onwards: extend the framework, keep the centre open."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Summer prune in late June", "text": "Cut all new sideshoots back to five leaves in late June. Exposes the strigs to sunlight, keeps the bush manageable, encourages spurs along the framework branches. Same timing as redcurrant and gooseberry."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Winter prune for renewal", "text": "February. Shorten leading shoots of each main branch by half. Cut back the sideshoots already shortened in summer to two buds. Remove any wood older than 4–5 years at the base. The permanent framework largely stays; the spur system gets refreshed."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Net (lightly) against birds", "text": "The big practical advantage of whitecurrants over reds: birds find pale fruit harder to spot, so a light netting often suffices where reds need a full fruit cage. Still net once the berries start to colour — squirrels and starlings will find them given time. Fine mesh, tucked under at the base."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Pick whole strigs", "text": "Whitecurrants are fully ripe when the berries are translucent — you can see the seeds through the skin — and the strig looks like strands of small pale grapes. Pick the entire strig by snipping the stalk at the top. The taste is sweet enough to eat off the bush, unlike redcurrants. They keep well in the fridge for 2–3 days; freeze on trays for longer storage."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "Is a whitecurrant a different plant from a redcurrant?", "answer": "No — same species (Ribes rubrum), selected for the absence of red pigment in the berry. Every aspect of cultivation is identical to redcurrant: same hardiness, same pruning, same pests, same planting calendar. Whitecurrants are sweeter and lower in acid than redcurrants, and slightly less attractive to birds — which is the practical case for growing them."},
    {"question": "Are whitecurrants sweeter than redcurrants?", "answer": "Yes — noticeably. Whitecurrants have lower acidity than red, which makes them sweet enough to eat raw off the bush. Redcurrants are sharp and almost always cooked or made into jelly. If you want soft fruit you can eat by the handful in July, plant whitecurrants over reds. If you want jelly for roast lamb, plant reds."},
    {"question": "Where can I buy whitecurrant bushes?", "answer": "Specialist soft-fruit nurseries (Blackmoor Nurseries, Pomona Fruits, Ken Muir, Suttons) stock several varieties bare-root from November. Garden centres rarely carry whitecurrants — they're a connoisseur's fruit, not a mass-market line. Order in October–November for delivery during the dormant planting season."},
    {"question": "Do birds eat whitecurrants?", "answer": "Less than they eat redcurrants, because pale fruit is harder for birds to spot than glossy red. But squirrels and starlings will find them given time, and a heavy crop still needs netting once the berries start to translucent up. The bullfinch problem (flower buds in winter) is identical to redcurrants — net from December if bullfinches visit your garden."},
    {"question": "How do I prune a whitecurrant bush?", "answer": "Identical to redcurrant — and like a gooseberry, NOT like a blackcurrant. Whitecurrants fruit on spurs on a permanent framework. Build an open goblet, summer prune the sideshoots back to five leaves in late June, winter prune them to two buds in February, renew only the oldest wood (4–5+ years). Pruning hard to the base like a blackcurrant removes the crop."},
    {"question": "What can I make with whitecurrants?", "answer": "Eat fresh (the main pleasure — they're a true dessert fruit). Whitecurrant jelly is pale amber and delicate, lovely with white meats and game birds. Mix with redcurrants in summer pudding for a paler more subtle version. Add to a salad in late summer for sweetness and visual contrast. Freeze surplus on trays for winter desserts. The flavour is gentler than red — think of a sweet white wine vs a bold red."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'whitecurrant';

-- From wild-garlic.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant wild garlic bulbs in autumn in a damp, lightly shaded spot, or sow seed after collecting it from established colonies — it''s a native UK woodland perennial with broad green leaves and white star-shaped flowers in April–May, with a powerful garlic flavour to leaves, flowers, bulbs, and seed pods. Wild garlic is very hardy (RHS H6), thrives in the conditions that defeat most cultivated herbs (damp shade, leaf-mould soil), and spreads slowly by seed and bulb division to form a carpet over 5–10 years. Critical safety note: wild garlic leaves are sometimes confused with lily-of-the-valley (Convallaria majalis) which is highly toxic — always crush a leaf and confirm the garlic smell before eating. Pick leaves from March to May, flowers in April–May, both raw or cooked (pesto, butter, omelettes, soup). After flowering the plant disappears underground until next spring.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H6',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H7',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Identify it without doubt — the lily-of-the-valley trap", "text": "The most important rule. Wild garlic leaves can superficially resemble lily-of-the-valley (Convallaria majalis) which is highly toxic — even small quantities can cause heart issues. Safe identification test: crush a leaf between your fingers. Wild garlic smells unmistakably of garlic. Lily-of-the-valley smells of nothing or faintly sweet. Always do the crush-and-smell test before picking, even on plants you think you know. Lords-and-ladies (Arum maculatum) is another woodland look-alike — also toxic, also no garlic smell."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Plant in damp shade", "text": "Wild garlic wants the conditions of UK deciduous woodland: dappled shade under trees, moisture-retentive leaf-mould-rich soil, sheltered from wind. Avoid full sun (leaves scorch) and dry soil (poor growth). Under fruit trees, alongside a north-facing wall, or in a damp wild corner are ideal positions."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant bulbs in autumn", "text": "Bulbs (in the green): plant bulbs in September–October, 5 cm deep and 10 cm apart. Pot-grown plants in growth (in the green): plant in March–April when the leaves are up but before flowering — the most reliable establishment method. From seed: trickier; needs cold stratification (winter outdoors), patience (often takes 2 years to flower), and source-collected seed (it doesn't dry-store well)."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't dig from the wild", "text": "UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 makes it illegal to dig up wild plants without the landowner's permission. Even where it's legal, collecting wild bulbs damages woodland ecosystems. Buy from a specialist supplier (Crocus, Sarah Raven, Cotswold Garden Flowers) or take a small division from a friend's established patch. Foraging the leaves (not the bulbs) from public woodland is generally legal but check local bylaws."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick leaves March–May", "text": "Cut whole leaves at the base from when the plant emerges in March. Pick before flowering for the best flavour (April onwards in southern gardens) — leaves get tougher and slightly bitter after flowers open. Don't pick all leaves from a single plant; take a leaf or two from each clump so the colony continues photosynthesising for the bulb to bulk up."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Use the flowers and seed pods too", "text": "The white star-shaped flowers (April–May) are edible and have a milder garlic flavour — scatter on salads, butter, soups. The green seed pods that follow (May–June) are intensely garlicky — pickle them whole as wild garlic capers or finely chop into dressings. Every part of wild garlic except the roots is edible."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Let it die back undisturbed", "text": "After flowering (late May–June), wild garlic dies back completely and the bed looks bare until the following spring. Don't dig the area in summer (you'll damage the dormant bulbs) and don't worry about the disappearance — it's natural. Mulch with leaf-mould or compost in autumn to feed the next season's growth."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Make wild garlic pesto in May", "text": "The classic use. Recipe: 100 g wild garlic leaves, 50 g grated parmesan (or pecorino), 50 g toasted pine nuts (or walnuts), 100 ml extra virgin olive oil, juice of half a lemon, salt to taste. Blitz in a food processor. Keeps 1 week in the fridge or 3 months frozen in ice cube trays. Use on pasta, bruschetta, roast potatoes, fish. One large picking gives 4–6 jars for the freezer."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "How do I tell wild garlic from lily-of-the-valley?", "answer": "The crushed-leaf test is the only safe identification. Wild garlic (Allium ursinum) smells unmistakably of garlic when a leaf is crushed. Lily-of-the-valley (Convallaria majalis) is highly toxic — even small quantities can cause serious heart problems — and smells of nothing or faintly sweet when crushed. Always crush and smell, even on plants you think you know. Visual cues are unreliable (both have broad green leaves), but lily-of-the-valley leaves come from a single point and curl around each other, while wild garlic leaves come individually from the soil. If unsure, don't eat."},
    {"question": "Is wild garlic invasive?", "answer": "Slowly. Wild garlic spreads by both seed and underground bulb division but at a stately pace — a small clump becomes a metre-square patch over 5–10 years. It's nothing like the aggressive runner-spread of mint or lemon balm. In the right damp shaded conditions it will gradually carpet an area, which is generally what UK gardeners want. To contain: deadhead flowers before they set seed, and dig out spreading bulbs at the edge of the colony in autumn (don't try in summer when bulbs are dormant — you can't find them)."},
    {"question": "When is wild garlic at its best?", "answer": "March to May, with the absolute peak in April just before flowering. From March, the first leaves appear and are tender and mild. April leaves are at peak flavour. By May the flowers are out (also edible, milder). June onwards the leaves yellow and the plant retreats underground until next spring. Make pesto in April for the year's freezer stash — that's when the harvest is most rewarding."},
    {"question": "Can I forage wild garlic legally in the UK?", "answer": "Yes — for leaves, with conditions. Under the UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981, you can pick parts of wild plants (leaves, flowers) for personal use without the landowner's permission, but it's illegal to dig up any wild plant. Forage sustainably: take a few leaves from each clump rather than stripping a single plant. Check local bylaws — some SSSIs (Sites of Special Scientific Interest) and nature reserves prohibit any foraging. Don't sell foraged wild garlic without proper permits."},
    {"question": "Can I grow wild garlic in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — useful where garden conditions are wrong. A 30 cm wide pot of peat-free compost mixed with leaf-mould, in a shady spot, kept moist. Wild garlic in pots is less vigorous than in-ground but produces enough for kitchen use. Top-dress with fresh leaf-mould each autumn. The pot can dry out completely in summer dormancy (June–February) — that's fine; the bulbs are sleeping."},
    {"question": "How do I preserve wild garlic for use through the year?", "answer": "Three methods, in order of best results. (1) Wild garlic pesto frozen in ice cube trays — drops out into pasta sauces all year. (2) Wild garlic butter: blend chopped leaves into softened butter, roll in clingfilm, freeze in logs, slice off as needed. (3) Pickled wild garlic seed pods: collect green seed pods in May–June, pickle in white wine vinegar with peppercorns and bay — keeps a year, intensely garlicky. Drying is NOT recommended — wild garlic flavour fades dramatically when dried."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'wild-garlic';

-- From winter-savory.md
UPDATE plant_species
SET
  description = 'Plant winter savory from a young plant in spring, in full sun in well-drained gritty soil — it''s a hardy Mediterranean semi-shrubby perennial that gives year-round peppery thyme-like leaves and clouds of white-pink flowers in July–August. Winter savory is hardy in most UK gardens (RHS H5) and stays evergreen through normal winters; the related summer savory (S. hortensis) is an annual with softer flavour. The traditional "bean herb" — savory is the classic UK culinary partner for broad beans, runner beans, and lentils, and is thought to reduce the digestive after-effect of legumes. Cut hard back in spring (April) to keep it bushy. The flowers are excellent for bees and the dense low evergreen mound (30–40 cm) makes useful low edging for herb beds.',
  rhs_hardiness_min = 'H5',
  rhs_hardiness_max = 'H6',
  howto_steps = $$[
    {"step": 1, "name": "Pick winter or summer savory", "text": "Winter savory (Satureja montana): hardy perennial (H5), 30–40 cm semi-shrubby evergreen mound, leaves stronger and more peppery, year-round availability, lives 5–8 years. The functional kitchen choice. Summer savory (Satureja hortensis): hardy annual (H4), 30 cm soft upright plant, softer milder flavour, dies in autumn. Used by some cooks who prefer the softer flavour for delicate dishes. For a UK garden, winter savory is the obvious choice — perennial, more flavour, less work."},
    {"step": 2, "name": "Buy a young plant in spring", "text": "Buy a 1 L plant from a herb nursery (Jekka's, Pepperpot, RHS Wisley) in April–May. From seed is possible but slow (germination takes 3–4 weeks, plants reach productive size in year 2) and most named cultivars come from cuttings. One plant is enough for a household."},
    {"step": 3, "name": "Plant in full sun and well-drained soil", "text": "Mediterranean origins: winter savory wants 6+ hours direct sun and sharp drainage. Rich or wet soil = winter death: the main UK failure mode is waterlogged clay rotting the crown over winter. On heavy soils: plant in a raised bed, fork in plenty of horticultural grit, or grow in a pot. Sandy, chalky, or stony soils suit it perfectly. Spacing 40 cm."},
    {"step": 4, "name": "Don't feed", "text": "Feeding is the most common cause of winter savory disappointment. Rich soil produces lush growth that floops, gets mildewy, and loses flavour. No fertiliser at planting; no fertiliser during the season. A gravel mulch around the base keeps weeds down without adding nutrients. Stress concentrates the essential oils."},
    {"step": 5, "name": "Pick fresh year-round", "text": "Winter savory is evergreen — pick fresh leaves any time of year, even in January. Strongest flavour is on tender new growth in late spring and early summer before flowering. Older lower leaves get woody; pick from the tips. Use fresh — winter savory keeps its flavour well when dried, useful for winter when fresh picking is reduced."},
    {"step": 6, "name": "Cut hard back in April", "text": "The single most important husbandry technique. In April after the last frost, cut the whole plant back hard — by half to two-thirds, into the soft greenish wood (don't cut into the old grey woody base — it rarely re-shoots). This stops the bush getting leggy and woody, encourages dense compact new growth, and triggers the strongest flush of flavoured leaves for the summer kitchen. Don't prune in autumn — soft regrowth won't survive winter."},
    {"step": 7, "name": "Pair with bean and lentil dishes", "text": "Winter savory is THE bean herb in traditional UK and European cooking. Add a sprig to: broad bean dishes (whether soup or salad), runner bean recipes, butter bean stews, lentil soups, sausages and pulses, summer vegetable terrines. The reputed digestive effect (reducing the gas-producing effect of legumes) may or may not be scientifically robust, but the flavour pairing is unbeatable. Don't overdo it — winter savory is potent, a few small sprigs per dish is plenty."},
    {"step": 8, "name": "Replace every 5–8 years", "text": "Winter savory gradually gets woody and bare-based after about 7–8 years. Take 8 cm semi-ripe cuttings in late summer (August) to start replacement plants: strip lower leaves, push into gritty compost, root in 4–6 weeks. Plant out the rooted cuttings in spring. Or buy a new plant when the original gets tired. The cuttings often produce stronger-flavoured plants than the parent."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  faqs = $$[
    {"question": "What's the difference between winter and summer savory?", "answer": "Different species, different lifespans, different flavours. Winter savory (Satureja montana): hardy perennial (H5), 30–40 cm woody-based evergreen mound, year-round availability, stronger more peppery flavour, lives 5–8 years. Summer savory (Satureja hortensis): hardy annual (H4), 30 cm soft upright, dies in autumn, softer milder flavour, must be re-sown each year. For a year-round low-effort kitchen herb, plant winter savory. Many UK gardeners grow both for the flavour variation; most plant only winter savory."},
    {"question": "Will winter savory survive a UK winter?", "answer": "Yes — hardy in most UK gardens (RHS H5), staying evergreen through normal winters with leaves available for picking even in January. Main failure mode: cold WET winters on heavy soils rot the crown. Prevention: plant on well-drained or gritty soil; on clay, plant on a raised mound or grow in a pot. Pot-grown plants can move under cover in deep winter for extra protection. Established plants survive temperatures down to about -15°C in dry conditions."},
    {"question": "When and how do I prune winter savory?", "answer": "Hard prune in April, after the last frost. Cut the whole plant back by half to two-thirds, but only into the soft greenish wood — winter savory rarely re-shoots from old grey woody stems. This triggers a strong flush of fresh tender leaves with peak flavour for the summer kitchen. Don't prune in autumn — the soft regrowth won't survive winter. A light tidying trim after the July–August flowering removes spent flower stems and keeps the plant compact."},
    {"question": "Why is winter savory called the bean herb?", "answer": "Traditional UK and European cooking pairs winter savory with all legume dishes. The herb's peppery thyme-like flavour complements broad beans, runner beans, butter beans, lentils, and pulses generally. The reputed digestive benefit — savory is thought to reduce the gas-producing effect of beans — is widely repeated in herbal literature but isn't strongly supported by modern research. What's certain is the flavour pairing: a small sprig of winter savory in any bean dish adds a perfect peppery edge. Use sparingly; the flavour is potent."},
    {"question": "Can I grow winter savory in a pot?", "answer": "Yes — and on heavy soils, it's the better option. A 25 cm pot of peat-free compost mixed 30% with horticultural grit, in full sun. The pot advantage: perfect drainage protects against winter crown rot, and the pot can move under cover in deep winter weather. Don't feed. Water only when the surface is bone dry. Refresh the top compost annually. Pot winter savory lives 4–5 years and gives a steady year-round supply."},
    {"question": "How does winter savory taste compared to thyme?", "answer": "Sharper, more peppery, with a hint of pine-like resinous edge. Both are aromatic Mediterranean herbs, both pair well with savoury dishes, but winter savory has a more peppery punch where thyme is softer and more lemony. Don't substitute equal quantities of one for the other — use less savory than thyme in a recipe to start, then adjust. Winter savory works particularly well where you'd want both thyme AND black pepper; thyme stands alone better."}
  ]$$::jsonb,
  date_published = COALESCE(date_published, CURRENT_DATE),
  date_modified = CURRENT_DATE
WHERE slug = 'winter-savory';

COMMIT;
